51} G. E. Schulze (the author of {\i
Aenesidemus}, the first skeptical attack on Kant) was very likely an important catalyst in this distancing process. In 1801 Schulze had published a two-volume opus under the title of {\i
Critique of Theoretical Philosophy} in which he again defended the standpoint of common sense and of theoretical skepticism. Hegel reviewed it in 1802, and Schulze responded to his review in the subsequent year with an anonymous essay entitled \u8220?Aphorisms Concerning the Absolute.\u8221? In the essay Schulze skillfully parodied the Identity Philosophy of Schelling to which Hegel still clearly adhered at the time of the review. He pretended to be a disciple of Schelling and pretended to rely on Schellingian principles to criticize what was in fact his own skepticism. He argued, quite consequentially, that since in intuition there is no distinction between subject and object, and yet consciousness requires this distinction, the aim of the philosopher is to achieve a kind of semi-consciousness, a dreamy state so to speak, in which all distinctions are overcome and all doubts therefore disappear. This is the state of mind which Hegel himself was later to deride in the Preface to his {\i
Phenomenology of Spirit} (1807). Using language strongly reminiscent of Schulze's, Hegel described it as a \u8220?night in which all cows are black\u8221? (GW 9, 17.28\u8211?29). Among the many factors that contributed to Hegel's becoming deliberately aware that he was parting company with Schelling, this anonymous publication of Schulze might well have been the most decisive. 
