{\ql
The history of translation\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ Hegel's Logic has a long tradition of translation into English. In the {\i
Secret of Hegel} (1898), Stirling included a translation of the section on \u8220?quality,\u8221? as well as loose renditions of substantial excerpts from \u8220?quantity,\u8221? notably Remark I on calculus.{\ul
}{\super
1} On the American side, there already was the translation of a brief excerpt taken from the concluding chapter of the Subjective Logic (GW 12.236.3\u8211?20; 25.10\u8211?13) in the {\i
Journal of Speculative Philosophy} (1869). It came as the Appendix to what appears to be a pr\u233?cis of the Encyclopedia Logic intended for a course of instruction.{\ul
}{\super
2} We also know from the testimony of William T. Harris that the St. Louis Hegelian Henry C. Brockmeyer, to whom the same Harris dedicated his {\i
Hegel's Logic}, had translated the whole Logic in 1859 and 1860. Apparently this translation was Harris's first exposure to Hegel. Harris had copied it out and, as he says, it \u8220?still exists, but has never been printed, any portion of it.\u8221?{\ul
}{\super
3} Back in Great Britain, a translation of the Subjective Logic was published in two volumes at Oxford by H. S. Macran, in 1912 and 1929.{\ul
}{\super
4} And it was also {\ul
}in 1929 that a first complete translation finally became available, produced by W. H. Johnston and L. G. Struthers. It was published in both London and New York.{\ul
}{\super
5} This text remained the standard English translation of the Logic until it was replaced in 1969 by that of Arthur V. Miller.{\ul
}{\super
6} As for the Encyclopedia Logic, a translation was published at Oxford in 1873 by William Wallace. It was republished in revised form in 1892{\ul
}{\super
7} and again, but with only minor variations and with an Introduction by J. N. Findlay, in 1975.{\ul
}{\super
8} A completely new translation by T. F. Geraets, W. A. Suchting, and W. H. Harris was published in 1991.{\ul
}{\super
9} Finally, Stephen Houlgate has included a portion of the German text of Book One of the Greater Logic (up to the end of Section 1, Chapter 2), together with an edited version of the Miller translation of the same text, in his 2006 commentary.{\ul
}{\super
10} In preparing the present text, I have of course consulted and profited from the results of all previous efforts. Throughout I have also used as control the excellent Italian translation of Arturo Moni, first published in 1924\u8211?1925 and revised in 1968 by Claudio Cesa.{\ul
}{\super
11} The present translation is, however, completely new, and, since I have departed from standing conventions on several key terms, a few words of explanation are now in order. {\s3 \afs28
{\b
{\ql
Issues of translation\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ The Logic's range of vocabulary is extremely narrow, as one would expect of any logic. The few terms which are key to it present, however, considerable difficulty. In this translation, I have taken as my starting point the Glossary {\ul
}of the Geraets/Suchting/Harris translation of the Encyclopedia Logic, but I have taken to heart, and have often agreed with, the Suchting minority report.{\ul
}{\super
12} I have steadfastly refused to resort to mechanical devices in cases where different words in German need to be kept distinct but are normally translated by the same word in English. Artificial constructs (unless they are in the original languages) are not translations but rather open admissions of failure in translation. Below is a list of terms that presented special difficulty, along with a brief explanation of my decisions on how to handle them. {\b
{\i
an (in) sich}}, {\b
{\i
an (in) sich selbst}}, {\b
{\i
an (in) ihm (ihr)}}, {\b
{\i
an (in) ihm (ihr) selbst}}, {\b
{\i
f\u252?r sich}}, {\b
{\i
f\u252?r sich selbst}}, {\b
{\i
f\u252?r ihm (ihr) selbst}}. These are simple expressions, but difficult to translate and all the more challenging because there are important conceptual distinctions riding on them, as I now must explain. There are two syntactic distinctions at work in them. One is between pronouns in indirect objective form ({\i
ihm}, {\i
ihr}) and in subjective or reflective form ({\i
sich}). The difference between {\i
an (in) sich} and {\i
an (in) ihm (ihr)}, and between {\i
f\u252?r sich selbst} and {\i
f\u252?r ihm (ihr) selbst}, is, therefore, syntactic in origin ({\i
sich} refers to the subject of a sentence; {\i
ihm (ihr)} to a third term). But Hegel uses the contrast between {\i
an (in) sich} and {\i
an (in) ihm (ihr)} also to convey a conceptual difference. A determination is present in a concept {\i
an ihm} when it accrues to it because of its external context (cf. GW 21, 112.9\u8211?11: \u8220?In so far as that which something is {\i
in itself} ({\i
an sich}) is also {\i
in it} ({\i
an ihm}), the something is affected with being-for-other\u8221?; also, GW 21, 108.10\u8211?11). It may well be that the determination already {\i
implicitly} belongs to the internal logic of the concept (that is, is present in the concept {\i
an sich}), and that a logician would already be able to recognize it as thus belonging to it on the basis of its external usage and its genesis as concept. (Cf. 21, 219.13\u8211?31.) As yet, however, the determination is not {\i
said} by the concept itself \u8211? that is to say, the concept has not as yet expressly appropriated it. It remains, therefore, still external to it. When the concept explicitly expresses the determination, then the latter is present in it {\i
f\u252?r ihm}, or, conversely, the concept possesses it {\i
f\u252?r sich}. The other distinction is between {\i
sich} understood as subjective/reflective pronoun and {\i
selbst} (\u8220?self\u8221?) understood demonstratively. It is in this demonstrative sense, that is, in order to reinforce the {\i
sich} and the {\i
ihm (ihr)} rhetorically, that Hegel uses the {\i
selbst} in {\i
an (in) sich selbst}, {\i
an (in) ihm (ihr) selbst}, etc. To further complicate things, {\i
selbst} can be used in German, just as it {\ul
}can in English, both in this just cited meaning of demonstrative pronoun or as a noun. Now a problem of translation arises because English does not {\i
verbally} distinguish between objective and subjective pronouns but, when necessary, adds \u8220?-self\u8221? to a pronoun in order to make it subjective/reflective. Nor does English verbally distinguish between this subjective/reflective form and the same form but used in demonstrative function. (Cf.: \u8220?He did it to himself\u8221?; \u8220?He did it himself.\u8221?) The net result is that, if we were to translate the German expressions literally, we would come up with the awkward \u8220?in it itself,\u8221? \u8220?in itself itself,\u8221? \u8220?for it itself,\u8221? \u8220?for itself itself,\u8221? or with variations thereof. The problem is how to avoid this awkwardness and at the same time make sure that, in using \u8220?self,\u8221? we do not surreptitiously introduce in English the word's meaning as noun whereas in German the corresponding {\i
selbst} is purely demonstrative. The problem is especially acute in rendering the distinction between {\i
an ihm selbst} and {\i
an sich selbst}. I concede that there is no single way of dealing with this problem, and that each carries its own difficulties. I have opted for simply dropping the demonstrative {\i
selbst}. In English we only need to say \u8220?in it,\u8221? \u8220?in itself,\u8221? \u8220?for it,\u8221? \u8220?for itself.\u8221? I have rejected the current widespread convention of translating {\i
an ihm selbst} as \u8220?in its own self,\u8221? for two reasons. For one thing, the formula is more of a gloss than a translation. The German phrase that it translates would be {\i
an seinem eigenen Selbst}, an expression that carries the connotations of \u8220?on its own account,\u8221? exactly the formula sometimes used to translate Hegel's {\i
f\u252?r sich sein}. Mind you, no German is likely to say {\i
an seinem eigenen Selbst}; but neither would an English person say \u8220?in its own self.\u8221? Another reason is that here more than anywhere else the use of \u8220?self\u8221? as noun can be misleading because it suggests a subjectivity which is not in the German text. Regarding {\i
f\u252?r sich selbst}, I have avoided as a general rule the currently often used \u8220?on its own account\u8221? \u8211? not because the formula is necessarily wrong (I have occasionally used it myself) but because it is unduly verbose and not necessary; the simpler \u8220?for itself\u8221? suffices. I admit that the use of \u8220?in it\u8221? as a translation of {\i
an ihm selbst} was often problematic. It failed in context to identify its referent unambiguously. {\ul
}I have often replaced it, therefore, with \u8220?within it\u8221? or adverbially simply with \u8220?within,\u8221? and occasionally with \u8220?internally\u8221? \u8211? in all cases hoping to retain the contrast with the \u8220?out,\u8221? the \u8220?without,\u8221? or the \u8220?external other,\u8221? which is in Hegel's mind. Despite these difficulties, one advantage is that the English text has been rid of otherwise innumerable instances of the \u8220?self\u8221? used as noun whereas its counterpart in German is only demonstrative. I have often replaced \u8220?in itself\u8221? with \u8220?within itself.\u8221? {\i
An sich} and {\i
f\u252?r sich} can often be translated conveniently as simply \u8220?implicitly\u8221? and \u8220?explicitly\u8221? respectively. I saw no way of rendering in English the distinction between {\i
an} and {\i
in} consistently. Besides, I am not sure that there is a clear difference in German. Finally, {\i
in sich (selbst)}, used in connection with {\i
Reflexion} (for instance, as in {\i
Reflexion-in-sich-selbst}) contrasts in meaning \u8211? though not always explicitly so \u8211? with {\i
mit sich (selbst)}, this last used in connection with such term as \u8220?equality\u8221? or \u8220?unity\u8221? to express a state of immediacy (for instance, as in {\i
Gleichheit mit sich}). I have translated the {\i
in sich (selbst)} with \u8220?immanent\u8221? (cf. GW 12, 35.1\u8211?2, where Hegel uses {\i
immanente Reflexion}) and the {\i
mit sich (selbst)} with \u8220?self-\u8221? as, for instance, in \u8220?self-equality.\u8221? {\b
{\i
Ansichsein}}. \u8220?Being-in-itself\u8221? is the normal translation, especially when contrasted with {\i
F\u252?rsichsein}, \u8220?being-for-itself.\u8221? But I have also rendered {\i
Ansichsein} as \u8220?the in-itself\u8221? and \u8220?the in-itselfness.\u8221? {\b
{\i
aufheben}}, {\b
{\i
Aufhebung}}. The commonly accepted translations of these terms are \u8220?to sublate\u8221? and \u8220?sublation.\u8221? These are terms of art which were originally coined by Stirling precisely for the purpose of translating the corresponding German words but have now made their way into the {\i
OED}. Much as I have tried to replace them with words that are just as common in English as the German equivalents are in German, and having even experimented for a while with Suchting's suggestion of \u8220?to suspend\u8221? and \u8220?suspension,\u8221? I finally had to give up and return to the traditional translations of \u8220?to sublate\u8221? and \u8220?sublation.\u8221? The fact is that the only common English word which would somewhat adequately render the double meaning of the German {\i
aufheben} is the lowly \u8220?to take up\u8221? (which, incidentally, is the translation of the Latin {\i
tollere} from which \u8220?to sublate\u8221? was coined). \u8220?To take something up\u8221? means \u8220?to take it away\u8221? while at the same time to \u8220?appropriate it.\u8221? But \u8220?to take\u8221? is in English an all too widely used word, and with too many shades of different meanings, to allow the technical narrowing that Hegel has in mind. {\b
{\i
Begriff}}. Following Geraets/Suchting/Harris, I have departed from long-standing usage and have translated {\i
Begriff} as \u8220?concept\u8221? rather than as \u8220?notion.\u8221? B. C. Burt also used \u8220?concept\u8221? in his 1896 translation of Erdmann's {\i
Outlines of Logic and Metaphysics}, for the very good reason that \u8220?notion\u8221? carries the connotation of being a subjective representation.{\ul
}{\super
13} Its meaning is also much too vague. It should be reserved for precisely such contexts as require a term without too precise a meaning. \u8220?Concept\u8221? {\ul
}has the further advantage of being patently connected with \u8220?to conceive,\u8221? just as {\i
Begriff} is connected with {\i
greifen}, and can easily be expanded into \u8220?conceptual\u8221? and \u8220?conceptually grasped\u8221? or replaced, if need be, with \u8220?comprehension\u8221? and \u8220?conceptually comprehended.\u8221? {\b
{\i
Beziehung}} and {\b
{\i
Verh\u228?ltnis}} and the related verbs {\b
{\i
sich beziehen}} and {\b
{\i
sich verh\u228?lten}}. This distinction is conceptually very important and crucial to the development of the Logic. Quite generally, {\i
Beziehung} affects the terms that enter into it externally or reflectively, whereas {\i
Verh\u228?ltnis} affects them substantially. To differentiate the two terms by translating them as \u8220?relation\u8221? and \u8220?relationship\u8221? respectively is not wrong. \u8220?Relationship,\u8221? moreover, might also well convey the right nuance of \u8220?family [as contrasted with \u8216?external\u8217?] relation.\u8221? But all this is much too vague. I have followed, therefore, the convention already adopted in {\i
The Jena System, 1804\u8211?5: Logic and Metaphysics} (1985) by reserving \u8220?relation\u8221? (\u8220?relating,\u8221? \u8220?relating oneself\u8221?) for {\i
Verh\u228?ltnis} and \u8220?connection\u8221? for {\i
Beziehung}. To \u8220?connection,\u8221? however, I have also added \u8220?reference\u8221? and \u8220?connecting reference.\u8221? In this I follow Stirling who renders {\i
Beziehung} as \u8220?reference,\u8221? and then glosses the term in his usual rich language with \u8220?be-drawing\u8221? or \u8220?drawing together\u8221? or \u8220?connection\u8221? ({\i
The Secret of Hegel}, p. 479). It is instructive to note that as the Logic progresses from the immediacy of the categories of \u8220?being\u8221? to the reflectivity of those of \u8220?essence,\u8221? {\i
Beziehung} naturally shifts in nuance of meaning from plain \u8220?connection\u8221? to reflective \u8220?reference\u8221? or \u8220?connecting reference.\u8221? {\i
Verh\u228?ltnis} comes into its own only in connection with \u8220?concept\u8221? and \u8220?idea.\u8221? It must be added that {\i
Verh\u228?ltnis} also connotes \u8220?behavior\u8221? or \u8220?comportment\u8221? ({\i
sich verhalten} means \u8220?to behave\u8221? or \u8220?to comport oneself\u8221?), and that there are passages in which this nuance of meaning needs to be brought out. For a passage in which {\i
Beziehung} and {\i
Verh\u228?ltnis} are used in contrast to each other, cf. GW 11, 353.32\u8211?354.6. In mathematical contexts, especially in connection with Hegel's discussion of calculus, {\i
Verh\u228?ltnis} is to be translated as \u8220?ratio.\u8221? {\b
{\i
Dasein}} and {\b
{\i
Existenz}}. The widely accepted translation of {\i
Dasein} is \u8220?determinate being.\u8221? This is not incorrect, but it is not sufficiently precise. All {\i
Dasein} is \u8220?determinate being,\u8221? but not all \u8220?determinate being\u8221? is merely {\i
Dasein}. Moreover, using \u8220?determinate being\u8221? makes the task of translating such derivatives as {\i
seiend}, {\i
Seiendes}, and {\i
Daseiendes}, practically impossible or at least very cumbersome. One must fall back on long circumlocutions. The \u8220?thereness\u8221? and \u8220?there-being\u8221? in Geraets/Suchting/Harris (to which Suchting, however, objects, pp. xxxvi--xxxviii) were also used by Stirling and Burt. They are not wrong, but the problem with them, besides their unusualness and the difficulties that they too create in translating {\ul
}derivative terms, is that they give rise to bizarre constructions, as for example \u8220?the proof of God's thereness.\u8221? The dictionary translation of {\i
Dasein}, and also the one that would spontaneously occur to non-philosophers, is \u8220?existence.\u8221? I see no reason for not using this term, especially because it is easily inflected as \u8220?existent\u8221? and \u8220?existing.\u8221? I have adopted it as the translation of {\i
Dasein}. If it is objected that the term has acquired specialized meaning for existentialist philosophers or for Thomists of the Gilson/Maritain school, then my rejoinder is that it is high time that it be philosophically repossessed according to its common meaning. For something \u8220?to exist\u8221? means in English that it is simply there to be pointed at \u8211? exactly what it means in German. As for the need to differentiate {\i
Dasein} from the Latinate {\i
Existenz}, Hegel himself gives us the clue. {\i
Existenz} is an ontological term; it is the counterpart of {\i
essentia} ({\i
Wesen}) and the reflective parallel of the more immediate {\i
Dasein}. As Hegel says, \u8220?For being which is {\i
mediated}, we shall reserve the expression {\i
Existenz}\u8221? (GW 21, 80.11\u8211?12; also, 12.130.10\u8211?12). But mediation is what generates concreteness (GW 21, 12ff.). For this reason I have translated {\i
Existenz} with \u8220?concrete existence,\u8221? except in such phrases as \u8220?the proofs of God's existence [{\i
Existenz}]\u8221? where the ontological meaning of \u8220?existence,\u8221? or at least that \u8220?existence\u8221? is a product of reflective mediation, is clear from the context. But see also Note 30 to 21.80. {\b
{\i
Differenz}}. Occasionally Hegel uses this Latinate form instead of the common German {\i
Unterschied} (see the discussion of {\i
Unterschied} below). When Hegel resorts to words derived from Latin, this is normally in order to make a point that would otherwise be lost in their German counterparts. In the present case, the point that Hegel wants to stress is that two things, in being different, are not thereby indifferent to each other \u8211? that their difference, in other words, entails a mutually significant relation, whether a comparison or a contrast. {\i
Differenz} and the adjective {\i
different} can be rendered in English, therefore, with \u8220?non-indifference\u8221? and \u8220?non-indifferent.\u8221? (In this I am following a suggestion of Suchting, p. xlvi.) The drawback of this solution is that {\i
Indifferenz} and {\i
indifferent} would then have to be translated as \u8220?non-non-indifference\u8221? and \u8220?non-non-indifferent,\u8221? obviously not a happy consequence. But fortunately {\i
Indifferenz} and {\i
indifferent}, unlike the common {\i
Gleichg\u252?ltigkeit} and {\i
gleichg\u252?ltig}, hardly ever appear in the Logic, and when they appear, they do so in contrast to {\i
Differenz} and {\i
different}. And this is a contrast that can be rendered well in English with \u8220?indifference\u8221? and \u8220?indifferent\u8221? without any loss of meaning or stress. I must, however, call attention to one instance in which Hegel, so far as I can tell, uses {\i
Differenz} to mean \u8220?immediate differentiation,\u8221? that is, precisely the {\ul
}opposite of what one would expect. In that one case, I have translated it as \u8220?differentiation\u8221? (GW 21, 144.8\u8211?9). {\b
{\i
Einzeln}} and {\b
{\i
Individuum}}. {\i
Einzeln} is normally translated as \u8220?individual,\u8221? but I have regularly used \u8220?singular\u8221? instead in order to reserve \u8220?individual\u8221? for {\i
Individuum}. The latter signifies in Hegel's usage a special kind of individuality, one which is best realized in a person but for which we can see at least a first delineation in any internally organized object such as a living being. (Cf. GW 12, 144.3\u8211?11, where Hegel gives a broad definition of {\i
Individuum}; also 12, 31ff., where the term is applied to living things, and 12, 189.21\u8211?25, where it is used in contrast to {\i
Einzelnheit}.) I recognize that \u8220?singular\u8221? and \u8220?singularity\u8221? sometimes tend to denote \u8220?peculiar\u8221? and \u8220?peculiarity,\u8221? but they do not have to, and in context do not. {\b
{\i
erkennen}} and {\b
{\i
wissen}}, and related nouns {\b
{\i
Erkenntnis}} and {\b
{\i
Wissen}}. The difference in German between the two verbs is basically idiomatic. Both verbs can be used transitively and intransitively, but in different contexts. It is helpful to think of {\i
erkennen} as a kind of \u8220?recognition\u8221?; for {\i
wissen}, the English of the King James Bible can help: \u8220?Wist ye not that I must be about my Father's business?\u8221? (Luke 2:49). The idiomatic difference points to a difference in meaning nuance that can be lost if one translates both with \u8220?to know,\u8221? and, since Hegel bases a conceptual difference on this nuance, it must be protected. {\i
Wissen} carries the connotation of \u8220?being aware of\u8221?; {\i
erkennen}, of \u8220?being acquainted with.\u8221? Accordingly, Hegel uses {\i
Erkenntnis} in the specialized meaning of ratiocinative knowledge. By contrast, {\i
Wissen}, like both \u8220?wisdom\u8221? and \u8220?faith,\u8221? connotes direct apprehension. It signifies the product or the origin, rather than the process, of reason. I have therefore followed the now common practice of translating {\i
erkennen} and {\i
Erkenntnis} with \u8220?cognize\u8221? and \u8220?cognition\u8221? respectively, though I do it reluctantly because \u8220?cognize\u8221? and \u8220?cognition\u8221? tend to make the already ponderous prose of the Logic even more ponderous. In places, where meaning is not at stake, I have not hesitated to replace inflected verbal forms such as \u8220?cognizing\u8221? with the plain \u8220?knowing.\u8221? {\b
{\i
Gegenstand}} and {\b
{\i
Objekt}}. I have already indicated in the body of the Introduction the difference between these two terms. The clue for how to differentiate them in English can be obtained from what amounts basically to an idiomatic difference. In English, we say \u8220?the subject matter of a science\u8221?; the Germans say \u8220?the object ({\i
Gegenstand}) of a science.\u8221? \u8220?Subject matter\u8221? renders quite well the meaning of {\i
Gegenstand} precisely as \u8220?material object,\u8221? {\i
objectum quod}. This is how I have translated it, reserving \u8220?object\u8221? for {\i
Objekt}. In a few cases, where the context is clearly phenomenological, I have used \u8220?intended object\u8221? for {\i
Gegenstand}, and in yet others, where {\ul
}Hegel refers to mathematical entities or where he cites other philosophers (notably Kant), I have used \u8220?object.\u8221? There is no other way of referring to a \u8220?triangle,\u8221? for instance, than as a \u8220?mathematical object.\u8221? And as for the other philosophers whom Hegel cites and often criticizes, the difficulty is precisely that in the context of their philosophy it is impossible to draw the precise distinction between {\i
Gegenstand} and {\i
Objekt} that Hegel has in mind. Regarding {\i
Objektivit\u228?t}, which I translate as \u8220?objectivity,\u8221? two important texts can be found in GW 11, 324.19\u8211?22 and 12, 126ff. For the adjectival form, {\i
gegenst\u228?ndlich}, I had no choice but to use \u8220?objective.\u8221? {\b
{\i
das Ideelle}}. Miller translates the term as \u8220?the ideal,\u8221? but enters the German in square brackets; Geraets/Suchting/Harris also translate it as \u8220?the ideal,\u8221? but enter an explanatory note in the Glossary. I have tried to get away from \u8220?ideal\u8221? because the common meaning of this English word, just like that of its German counterpart ({\i
das Ideal}), is misleading. {\i
Ideell} has nothing to do, except in a derivative sense, with an ideal state or situation which is archetypical or \u8220?perfect in its kind\u8221? ({\i
OED}, {\i
sub voce}; see also GW 21, 137 and Hegel's note). An {\i
Ideelles} is rather an object which, in being {\i
in itself} and even {\i
for itself}, at the same time refers to an {\i
other} that transcends it \u8211? an \u8220?other\u8221? with respect to which it is both identical yet differentiated. Hegel's typical example of an {\i
Ideelles} is \u8220?the finite.\u8221? Something finite is a reality whose whole truth lies in the \u8220?infinite\u8221? that transcends it \u8211? a transcendent \u8220?infinite\u8221? which is nevertheless present in it, inherent in its very structure as \u8220?finite.\u8221? However, that such is the truth of anything finite is made explicit only in the conceptual medium of spirit's reflection. The {\i
Ideelles} is an {\i
Objekt}, the product or achievement of spiritual activity (GW 21, 142\u8211?150). I have translated it with \u8220?idealization\u8221? for several reasons: (1) because the word, just like {\i
Ideelles}, denotes an achievement rather than just a physical state of being; (2) because an \u8220?idealization\u8221? stands to the idealized in the relation just defined, that is, as bringing to light what is essential to the latter; (3) because the word retains the connection with \u8220?representation\u8221? which Hegel also has in mind (cf. GW 21, 143.5); (4) and because, just like its German counterpart, the word also carries the pejorative meaning of \u8220?{\i
mere} idealization\u8221? from which Hegel wants to rescue it. {\b
{\i
Sache}}. In non-technical contexts, the term can and should be translated in a variety of ways, such as \u8220?substance,\u8221? or even \u8220?thing.\u8221? As a category, however, \u8220?fact\u8221? seems to be the best rendering. {\i
Sache}, like \u8220?fact,\u8221? denotes a thing or a situation which we understand to implicitly contain all the factors required for an explanation of its existence. Its presence cannot therefore be doubted even when those factors have yet to be made explicit. The related word, {\i
Tatsache}, was first coined by the theologian Johann {\ul
}Joachim Spalding precisely in order to translate the English term \u8220?matter of fact\u8221? as used by Joseph Butler in {\i
The Analogy of Religion} (1736).{\ul
}{\super
14} {\b
{\i
Schein}}. Hegel says: \u8220?Der Schein is dasselbe, was die {\i
Reflexion} ist; aber er ist die Reflexion als {\i
unmittelbar}\u8221? (GW 11, 249.21\u8211?22). {\i
Schein} is generated by reflection inasmuch as the movement of the latter, in arriving at a would-be term, immediately turns back from it ({\i
kehrt z\u252?ruck}), annuls it as its end-term, and thereby returns to that from which it had begun \u8211? only, however, in order to perform there the same move of turning back and returning. \u8220?Die Reflexion. . ., die Bewegung des Nichts durch Nichts zu sich selbst z\u252?ruck, ist das Scheinen {\i
seiner} in einem {\i
andern}\u8221? (GW 11, 292.11\u8211?12). It is a movement which, on the face of it, offers no fixed point of reference. Just like a play of lights, it might be taken as an illusion. But there is nothing illusionary about it because, on the contrary, its reality is totally exhausted by what it is {\i
simply on the face of it} \u8211? that is, it is movement simply {\i
as} movement, self-referring {\i
as} referring to an other. Anyone as sensitive to literature as Hegel was would have associated the term with the quip from an early play of Lessing that Mendelssohn cited to Jacobi as an example of the kind of doubt that common sense cannot countenance. \u8220?Oh yes! Ghosts have their fun! That's no real flame there. It only looks like a flame! It's not burning; it only seems to burn! It's not shining; it only seems to shine. [{\i
Das scheint nicht, das scheint nur zu scheinen}.] There is no lighting a real light from such a ghostly one!\u8221? ({\i
Die Matrone von Ephesus}, Act I, Scene 2). {\i
Schein} can be, however, the source of delusions, notably of the mistake of thinking that there must be for it some stabilizing factor outside it. Hegel's subsequent logical moves consist precisely in demonstrating that any stabilizing determination must be sought within the very movement of reflection of which {\i
Schein} is the immediate presentation. There is no simple or single way of translating the term in English. In German, {\i
Schein} and {\i
scheinen} can mean \u8220?shine\u8221? and \u8220?to shine,\u8221? but \u8220?semblance\u8221? and \u8220?to seem\u8221? just as well. I have chosen \u8220?shine,\u8221? both as noun and intransitive verb, in an effort to retain the metaphor of \u8220?light\u8221? which is at least partly behind Hegel's choice of {\i
Schein}. (Cf. GW 11, 391.38--392; 11, 393.13\u8211?15: \u8220?Just as the {\i
light} of nature is not a something, nor is it a thing, but its being is rather only its shining, so manifestation is self-identical absolute actuality.\u8221?) Admittedly, \u8220?shine\u8221? and \u8220?to shine\u8221? do not sit well in a procession of logical categories. For this reason, I have normally accompanied the terms with \u8220?reflective\u8221? and \u8220?reflectively\u8221? {\ul
}respectively \u8211? except in contexts where Hegel himself explicitly uses the terms in connection with \u8220?reflection\u8221? \u8211? in order to signal the fact that the terms are used technically, in a specifically contrived sense. In very few instances, as required by context, I have also used \u8220?semblance\u8221? and \u8220?to seem\u8221? (for instance, in GW 11, 251.31; 12, 133.14; 12, 177.16). {\b
{\i
selbstst\u228?ndig}}. I have translated this term with \u8220?self-subsistent,\u8221? except in mathematical contexts, and also in connection with \u8220?relation,\u8221? where I have used \u8220?independent.\u8221? {\b
{\i
Unterschied}} and {\b
{\i
Verschiedenheit}}. The problem with this pair of terms is that the area of meaning that they cover is the same as is covered in English by the three terms \u8220?difference,\u8221? \u8220?distinction,\u8221? and \u8220?diversity.\u8221? It is difficult to correlate the two German terms with the three English ones. I agree with Suchting (versus Harris/Geraets, p. xlv) that the natural way of translating {\i
Unterschied} is \u8220?difference.\u8221? We say \u8220?specific difference\u8221? where the Germans say {\i
spezifischer Unterschied}. {\i
Verschiedenheit}, for its part, is naturally translated as \u8220?diversity.\u8221? However, although this distinction is easy to pin down in principle, it does not always hold in fact. In different contexts, {\i
Unterschied} also calls for \u8220?distinction,\u8221? and {\i
Verschiedenheit} for \u8220?difference.\u8221? In English, \u8220?distinction\u8221? tends to be a difference {\i
in dictu}; \u8220?difference,\u8221? one {\i
in re}. I have used this rough rule as a guide when translating {\i
Unterschied} as \u8220?distinction,\u8221? though I must admit that on occasions I had to rely simply on my intuitive sense of the text. It was more difficult to come up with even a rough rule for {\i
Verschiedenheit}. \u8220?Diversity\u8221? tends to stress the plurality and variety of the things (or the moments of one single object) that are different, their \u8220?being versed in different directions,\u8221? so to speak, and therefore standing apart, each reflected into itself (cf. GW 11, 267.5\u8211?6); \u8220?difference\u8221? tends to stress what makes them different. This is the image that I have kept in mind when translating {\i
Verschiedenheit} with \u8220?difference\u8221? rather than with the more canonical \u8220?diversity.\u8221? But here, more so than in the case of {\i
Unterschied}, I often had to rely on intuition. {\b
{\i
Vorstellung}}. The normal translation of this term is \u8220?representation.\u8221? But Hegel uses it in different contexts in two quite distinct senses \u8211? in a generic sense that would include the specific type of representation which is the concept; and in a narrower sense which is normally associated with \u8220?imagination\u8221? and is opposed to \u8220?concept.\u8221? In this last sense, it can be translated in a variety of ways \u8211? for instance, as \u8220?ordinary representation,\u8221? \u8220?figurative representation,\u8221? or \u8220?representation of the imagination.\u8221? {\b
{\i
zu Grunde gehen}}. This is a common German expression normally translated as \u8220?to perish.\u8221? But Hegel often plays on the word {\i
Grund} (\u8220?ground\u8221? or \u8220?foundation\u8221?) in order to make a conceptual point. In an effort at saving {\ul
}the image behind the German expression, I have translated it as \u8220?to founder\u8221?; \u8220?to founder to the ground\u8221?; and \u8220?to founder, thus sinking to the ground.\u8221? {\s3 \afs28
