General division of the logic\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ It follows from what has been said regarding the {\i
concept} of this science and where its justification lies that the general {\i
division} of it can be only {\i
provisional} here \u8211? can be given, as it were, only in so far as the author is already acquainted with the science and is consequently {\i
historically} in a position to indicate in advance the main distinctions in which the concept assumes determination as it develops. Still, the attempt can be made to elicit in advance some general understanding of what is required for {\i
performing the division}, although even here recourse must be made to a procedural method which will attain full disclosure and justification only within the science. \u8211? One must thus be reminded, first and foremost, that presupposed here is that the {\i
division} must be connected with the concept, or rather must lie in the concept itself. The concept is not indeterminate but is {\i
determinate} {\i
within}; the division, however, expresses this {\i
determinateness} of the concept {\i
in developed form}; it is the {\i
parting} of the concept {\i
in judgment},{\ul
}{\super
21} not a judgment {\i
about} some subject matter or other picked out externally, but the judging, that is, the {\i
determining}, of the concept within it. Right-angularity, acute-angularity, etc., or equilaterality, which are the determinations according to which triangles are divided, do not lie in the determinateness of the triangle itself, that is, not in what is usually called the concept of a triangle, no more than in the concept of animal in general, or of mammal, bird, etc., one can find the determinations according to which animal in general is divided into mammal, bird, etc., and these classes are then divided into further genera. Such determinations are taken from elsewhere, from empirical intuition; they come to those so-called concepts from without. In the philosophical treatment of division, the concept must show that it itself holds the source of the determinations. But in the Introduction, the concept of logic 21.45was itself presented as the result of a science that transcends it, and hence as equally a {\i
presupposition} here. Accordingly, logic was defined as the science of pure thought \u8211? the science that has {\i
pure knowledge} for its principle and is a unity which is not {\ul
}abstract but living and concrete, so that the opposition of consciousness between {\i
a being subjectively existing for itself}, and another but objectively {\i
existing} {\i
such being}, has been overcome in it, and being is known to be in itself a pure concept and the pure concept to be true being. These, then, are the two {\i
moments} contained in logic. But they are now known to exist {\i
inseparably}, not as in consciousness, where each {\i
exists for itself}; it is for this reason and this reason alone, because they are at the same time known to be {\i
distinct} (yet not to exist for themselves), that their unity is not abstract, dead and inert, but concrete. This unity also constitutes the logical principle as {\i
element},{\ul
}{\super
22} so that the development of the distinction which is from the start present in it proceeds only {\i
inside} this element. For since the division is, as we said, the {\i
parting} or the {\i
judgment} of the concept \u8211? is the positing of the determination which is already immanent in it and therefore the positing of its distinction \u8211? this positing must not be understood as resolving that concrete unity back into its determinations, as if these were to exist on their own, for this would be here a vacuous return to the previous standpoint, to the opposition of consciousness. But this opposition has vanished; the unity remains the element, and the distinctions of the division and of the development in general no longer transgress that unity. Therefore the earlier determinations which (on the {\i
pathway to truth}) {\i
existed for themselves}, as for instance that of subjective and objective, or also of thought and being, of concept and reality, no matter from what standpoint they were determined, are {\i
now in their truth}, that is, in their unity, {\i
reduced to forms}. In their difference they therefore implicitly remain, {\i
in themselves}, the whole concept, and this concept is posited in the division only under its own determinations. Thus it is the whole concept which we must consider, first as {\i
existent} concept, and then {\i
as concept}; in the one case it is concept only {\i
implicitly}, {\i
in itself}, the concept of reality or being; in the other, it is the concept as such, the {\i
concept that exists for itself} (in more concrete forms, the concept as it is in the human being, who is endowed with thought, and also in the sentient animal and in general in organic individuality, although, of course, in these last it is not {\i
conscious} and still less {\i
known}; it is concept {\i
in itself} only in inorganic nature). \u8211? Accordingly, the first division must be between the logic of the {\i
concept} as 21.46{\i
being} and of the concept {\i
as concept}, or (if we want to avail ourselves of otherwise familiar, but very indeterminate and therefore very ambiguous expressions) in {\i
objective} and {\i
subjective} logic. {\ul
}However, in accordance with the elemental unity which is immanent in the concept as basis, and hence in accordance with the inseparability of the concept's determinations, such determinations, even as {\i
differentiated} (the concept is posited in their {\i
difference}), must also stand at least in {\i
reference} to each another. There results a sphere of {\i
mediation}, the concept as a system of {\i
reflected determinations}, that is, of being as it passes over into the in-itselfness of the concept \u8211? a concept which is in this way not yet posited for itself {\i
as such} but is also fettered by an immediate being still external to it. This sphere is {\i
the doctrine of essence} that stands between the doctrine of being and of the concept. \u8211? In the general division of logic in this work, it has been included in {\i
objective logic} because, although essence is indeed already inwardness, the character of {\i
subject} is to be reserved {\i
nominatim} for the concept. Recently Kant{\ul
}{\super
e} has opposed to what has usually been called \u8220?logic\u8221? 21.47another, namely a {\i
transcendental logic}.{\ul
