{\b
Chapter 2 Existence\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ Existence is {\i
determinate} being; its determinateness is {\i
existent} determinateness, {\i
quality}. Through its quality, {\i
something} is opposed to an {\i
other}; it is {\i
alterable} and {\i
finite}, negatively determined not only towards an other, but absolutely within it. This negation in it, in contrast at first with the finite something, is the {\i
infinite}; the abstract opposition in which these determinations appear resolves itself into oppositionless infinity, into {\i
being-for-itself}. The treatment of existence is therefore in three divisions: A. existence as such B. something and other, finitude C. qualitative infinity. \~ \~ {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
A. Existence as such\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ In existence (a) {\i
as such}, its determinateness is first (b) to be distinguished as {\i
quality}. The latter, however, is to be taken in both the two determinations of existence as {\i
reality} and {\i
negation}. In these determinacies, however, existence is equally reflected into itself, and, as so reflected, it is posited as (c) {\i
something}, an existent. 21.97 {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
a. Existence in general\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ Existence proceeds from becoming. It is the simple oneness of being and nothing. On account of this simplicity, it has the form of an {\i
immediate}. Its mediation, the becoming, lies behind it; it has sublated itself, and existence therefore appears as a first from which the forward move is made. It is at first in the one-sided determination of {\i
being}; the other determination which it contains, {\i
nothing}, will likewise come up in it, in contrast to the first. It is not mere being but {\i
existence}, or {\i
Dasein} [in German]; according to its [German] etymology, it is being ({\i
Sein}) in a certain {\i
place} ({\i
da}). But the {\ul
}representation of space does not belong here. As it follows upon becoming, existence is in general {\i
being} with a {\i
non-being}, so that this non-being is taken up into simple unity with being. {\i
Non-being} thus taken up into being with the result that the concrete whole is in the form of being, of immediacy, constitutes {\i
determinateness} as such. The {\i
whole} is likewise in the form or {\i
determinateness} of being, since in becoming being has likewise shown itself to be only a moment \u8211? something sublated, negatively determined. It is such, however, {\i
for us}, {\i
in our reflection}; not yet as {\i
posited} in it. What is posited, however, is the determinateness as such of existence, as is also expressed by the {\i
da} (or \u8220?there\u8221?) of the {\i
Dasein}. \u8211? The two are always to be clearly distinguished. Only that which is {\i
posited} in a concept belongs in the course of the elaboration of the latter to its content. Any determinateness not yet posited in the concept itself belongs instead to our reflection, whether this reflection is directed to the nature of the concept itself or is a matter of external comparison. To remark on a determinateness of this last kind can only be for the clarification or anticipation of the whole that will transpire in the course of the development itself. That the whole, the unity of being and nothing, is in the {\i
one-sided determinateness} of being is an external reflection; but in negation, in something and {\i
other}, and so forth, it will become {\i
posited}. \u8211? It was necessary here to call attention to the distinction just given; but to comment on all that reflection can allow itself, to give an account of it, would lead to a long-winded anticipation of what must transpire in the fact itself. Although such reflections may serve to facilitate a general overview and thus facilitate understanding, they also bring the disadvantage of being seen as unjustified assertions, unjustified grounds and foundations, of 21.98what is to follow. They should be taken for no more than what they are supposed to be and should be distinguished from what constitutes a moment in the advance of the fact itself. Existence corresponds to {\i
being} in the preceding sphere. But being is the indeterminate; there are no determinations that therefore transpire in it. But existence is determinate being, {\i
something concrete}; consequently, several determinations, several distinct relations of its moments, immediately emerge in it. {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
b. Quality\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ On account of the immediacy with which being and nothing are one in existence, neither oversteps the other; to the extent that existence is existent, to that extent it is non-being; it is determined. Being is not the {\i
universal}, {\ul
}determinateness not the {\i
particular}.{\ul
}{\super
42} Determinateness {\i
has yet to detach itself} from {\i
being}; nor will it ever detach itself from it, since the now underlying truth is the unity of non-being with being; all further determinations will transpire on this basis. But the connection which determinateness now has with being is one of the immediate unity of the two, so that as yet no differentiation between the two is posited. Determinateness thus isolated by itself, as {\i
existent} determinateness, is {\i
quality} \u8211? something totally simple, immediate. {\i
Determinateness} in general is the more universal which, further determined, can be something quantitative as well. On account of this simplicity, there is nothing further to say about quality as such. Existence, however, in which nothing and being are equally contained, is itself the measure of the one-sidedness of quality as an only {\i
immediate} or {\i
existent} determinateness. Quality is equally to be posited in the determination of nothing, and the result is that the immediate or existent determinateness is posited as distinct, reflected, and the nothing, as thus the determinate element of determinateness, will equally be something reflected, a {\i
negation}. Quality, in the distinct value of {\i
existent}, is {\i
reality}; when affected by a negating, it is {\i
negation} in general, still a quality but one that counts as a lack and is further determined as limit, restriction. 21.99 Both are an existence, but in {\i
reality}, as quality with the accent on being an existent, that it is determinateness and hence also negation is concealed; reality only has, therefore, the value of something positive from which negating, restriction, lack, are excluded. Negation, for its part, taken as mere lack, would be what nothing is; but it is an existence, a quality, only determined with a non-being. {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
Remark\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ Reality may seem to be an ambiguous word, since it is used in different, even opposite determinations. In philosophical usage, for instance, one speaks of {\i
mere empirical} reality as of a worthless being. But when it is said of thoughts, concepts, theories, that they {\i
have no reality}, this means that there is no {\i
actuality} to them. Of the idea of a Platonic republic, for instance, it is said that it might well be true {\i
in itself} or in its concept. Here the idea is not denied its value and is even allowed room {\i
alongside} reality. However, as against the so-called {\i
mere} ideas, the {\i
mere} concepts, \u8220?the real\u8221? counts as alone true. \u8211? The sense in which external existence is made the criterion of the truth of a content is for its part just as one-sided as when {\ul
}the idea, the essence, or even inner feeling, is represented as indifferent to external existence and is even held to be all the more estimable the more remote it is from reality. In connection with the term \u8220?reality,\u8221? mention must be made of the former metaphysical {\i
concept of God} that was once made the foundation of the so-called ontological proof of God's existence.{\ul
}{\super
43} God was defined as {\i
the sum-total} {\i
of all realities}, and of this sum-total it was said that it contained no contradiction within, that none of the realities canceled any other, for a reality is to be taken only as a perfection, as {\i
something affirmative} that contains no negation. Consequently, as it was said, the realities are not in opposition and do not contradict one another. On this concept of reality, the assumption is that the latter still remains after all negation has been thought away; however, to do this is to 21.100remove all determinateness from reality. Reality is quality, existence; it therefore contains the moment of the negative and is the determinate being that it is only through it. Taken in the so-called {\i
eminent sense}, or as {\i
infinite} in the ordinary meaning of the word \u8211? as we are said we should \u8211? reality is expanded into indeterminateness and loses its meaning. God's goodness is supposed to be such not in the ordinary sense but eminently; it is not different from justice but is rather {\i
tempered} by it (an expression of mediation of Leibnizian origin){\ul
}{\super
44} just as contrariwise justice is tempered by goodness; and so neither is goodness goodness any longer, nor justice justice. Power should be tempered by wisdom \u8211? but is then no longer power as such, for it is subject to wisdom. \u8211? Wisdom should be expanded into power, but then it vanishes as end and measure setting wisdom. The true concept of the infinite and of its {\i
absolute} unity that will later emerge{\ul
}{\super
45} is not to be understood as a {\i
tempering}, {\i
a mutual restricting} or {\i
blending} \u8211? a superficial, nebulous connection that can only satisfy mindless representation. \u8211? When reality, taken in the sense of a determinate quality as in the said definition of God, is made to transgress its determinateness, it ceases to be reality; it becomes abstract being; God as the {\i
pure} reality in all realities, or as the {\i
sum-total} of all realities, is the same empty absolute, void of determination and content, in which all is one. If, on the contrary, reality is taken in its determinateness, then, since it essentially contains the moment of the negative, the sum-total of all realities becomes just as much a sum-total of all negations, the sum-total {\ul
}of all contradictions, a sort of first absolute {\i
power} in which everything determinate is absorbed. However, since reality only exists in so far as it still has over against it something which it has not sublated, by being thought expanded in this way into an accomplished power void of restrictions, it becomes the abstract nothing. The said reality in everything real, the {\i
being} in all existence that should express the concept of God, is nothing else than abstract being, the same as nothing. 21.101 That determinateness is negation posited as affirmative is Spinoza's proposition: {\i
omnis determinatio est negatio},{\ul
}{\super
46} a proposition of infinite importance. Only, negation as such is formless abstraction. However, speculative philosophy must not be accused of taking negation or nothing as an ultimate: negation is as little an ultimate for it as reality is for it the truth. {\i
The unity of Spinoza's substance}, or that there is only one substance, is the necessary consequence of this proposition, that determinateness is negation. Spinoza had of necessity to posit {\i
thought} and {\i
being} or extension, the two determinations, namely, which he had before him, as one in this unity,{\ul
}{\super
47} for as determinate realities the two are negations whose infinity is their unity;{\ul
}{\super
48} according to Spinoza's definition, about which more later on,{\ul
}{\super
49} the infinity of something is its affirmation. He therefore conceived them as attributes, that is, such as do not have a particular subsistence, a being-in-and-for-itself, but only are as sublated as moments; or rather, since substance is the total void of internal determinateness, they are not even moments; the attributes, like the modes, are distinctions made by an external understanding. \u8211? Also the substantiality of individuals cannot hold its own before that substance. The individual refers to itself by setting limits to every other; but these limits are therefore also the limits of its self; they are references to the other; the individual's existence is not in the individual. True, the individual is {\i
more} than just restrictions on all sides; but this {\i
more} belongs to another sphere, that of the concept; in the metaphysics of being, the individual is an absolutely determinate something; and against this something, against this finite that would be in and for itself as such, determinateness asserts itself essentially as negation, dragging it into the same negative movement of the understanding that makes everything vanish into the abstract unity of substance. Negation stands immediately over against reality; further on, in the sphere proper to reflected determinations, it will be 21.102opposed to the {\i
positive}, {\ul
}which is reality reflecting upon negation \u8211? the reality in which the negative, still hiding in reality as such, shines forth.{\ul
}{\super
50} Quality specifically is a {\i
property} only when, in an {\i
external connection}, it manifests itself as an {\i
immanent determination}. By properties of herbs, for instance, we understand determinations which are not just {\i
proper} to a something but are such that, in virtue of them, the something {\i
holds its own} while referring to others and will not give in to the alien influences posited in it by them; on the contrary, it {\i
imposes} its own determinations in the other \u8211? though it does not keep it at a distance. On the other hand, more stable determinacies such as figure or shape are not called properties, nor even qualities, for they are thought of as alterable and therefore as not identical with {\i
being}. \u8220?{\i
Qualierung}\u8221? or \u8220?{\i
Inqualierung},\u8221? an expression of Jacob Boehme's profound but also profoundly turbid philosophy, signifies the movement within a quality (sourness, bitterness, fieriness, etc.) inasmuch as in its negative nature (in its {\i
Qual} or torment) the quality posits itself, securing itself from another; it signifies in general the internal unrest of quality by which it produces and preserves itself only in conflict.{\ul
}{\super
51} {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
c. Something\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ In existence its determinateness has been distinguished as quality; in this quality as something existing, the distinction {\i
exists} \u8211? the distinction of reality and negation. Now though these distinctions are present in existence, they are just as much null and sublated. Reality itself contains negation; it is existence, not indeterminate or abstract being. Negation is for its part equally existence, not the supposed abstract nothing but posited here as it is in itself, as existent, as belonging to existence. Thus 21.103quality is in general unseparated from existence, and the latter is only determinate, qualitative being. This sublating of the distinction is more than the mere retraction and external re-omission of it, or a simple return to the simple beginning, to existence as such. The distinction cannot be left out, for it {\i
is}. Therefore, what {\i
de facto} is at hand is this: existence in general, distinction in it, and the sublation of this distinction; the existence, not void of distinctions as at the {\ul
}beginning, but as {\i
again} self-equal {\i
through the sublation of the distinction}; the simplicity of existence {\i
mediated} through this sublation. This state of sublation of the distinction is existence's own determinateness; existence is thus {\i
being-in-itself}; it is {\i
existent}, {\i
something}. Something is the {\i
first negation of negation}, as simple existent self-reference. Existence, life, thought, and so forth, essentially take on the determination of an existent being, a living thing, a thinking mind (\u8220?I\u8221?), and so forth. This determination is of the highest importance if we do not wish to halt at existence, life, thought, and so forth, as generalities \u8211? also not at {\i
Godhood} (instead of God). In common representation, {\i
something} rightly carries the connotation of {\i
a real thing}. Yet it still is a very superficial determination, just as {\i
reality} and {\i
negation}, existence and its determinateness, though no longer the empty being and nothing, still are quite abstract determinations. For this reason they also are the most common expressions, and a reflection that is still philosophically unschooled uses them the most; it casts its distinctions in them, fancying that in them it has something really well and firmly determined. \u8211? As {\i
something}, the negative of the negative is only the beginning of the subject \u8211? its in-itselfness is still quite indeterminate. It determines itself further on, at first as {\i
existent-for-itself} and so on, until it finally obtains in the concept the intensity of the subject. At the base of all these determinations there lies the negative unity with itself. In all this, however, care must be taken to distinguish the {\i
first} negation, negation as negation {\i
in general}, from the second negation, the negation of negation which is concrete, {\i
absolute} negativity, just as the first is on the contrary only {\i
abstract} negativity. {\i
Something} is an {\i
existent} as the negation of negation, for such a negation is the restoration of the simple reference to itself \u8211? but the something is thereby equally the {\i
mediation of itself with itself}. Present in the simplicity of something, and then with greater determinateness in being-for-itself, in the subject, and so forth, this mediation of itself with itself is also already present in becoming, but only as totally abstract mediation; mediation 21.104with {\i
itself} is {\i
posited} in the something in so far as the latter is determined as a simple {\i
identity}. \u8211? Attention can be drawn to the presence of mediation in general, as against the principle of the alleged bare immediacy of a knowledge from which mediation should be excluded. But there is no further need to draw particular attention to the moment of mediation, since it is to be found everywhere and on all sides, in every concept. This mediation with itself which something is {\i
in itself}, when taken only as the negation of negation, has no concrete determinations for its sides; thus it collapses into the simple unity which is {\i
being}. Something {\i
is}, and {\ul
}{\i
is} therefore also an existent. Further, it is {\i
in itself} also {\i
becoming}, but a becoming that no longer has only being and nothing for its moments. One of these moments, being, is now existence and further an existent. The other moment is equally an existent, but determined as the negative of something \u8211? an {\i
other}. As becoming, something is a transition, the moments of which are themselves something, and for that reason it is an {\i
alteration} \u8211? a becoming that has already become {\i
concrete}. \u8211? At first, however, something alters only in its concept; it is not yet {\i
posited} in this way, as mediated and mediating, but at first only as maintaining itself simply in its reference to itself; and its negative is posited as equally qualitative, as only an {\i
other} in general. {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
B. Finitude\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ (a) Something and other: at first they are indifferent to one another; an other is also an immediate existent, a something; the negation thus falls outside both. Something is {\i
in itself} in contrast to its {\i
being-for-other}. But the determinateness belongs also to its {\i
in-itself}, and (b) the {\i
determination} of this in-itself in turn passes over into {\i
constitution}, and this latter, as identical with determination, constitutes the immanent and at the same time negated being-for-another, the {\i
limit} of something which (c) is the immanent determination of the something itself, and the something thus is the {\i
finite}. \~ In the first division where {\i
existence} in general was considered, this existence had, as at first taken up, the determination of {\i
an existent}. The moments of its development, quality and something, are therefore of equally affirmative determination. The present division, on the contrary, develops the negative determination which is present in 21.105existence and was there from the start only as negation in general. It was then the {\i
first} negation but has now been determined to the point of the {\i
being-in-itself} of the something, the point of the negation of negation. {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
a. Something and an other\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ 1. Something and other are, first, both {\i
existents} or {\i
something}. Second, each is equally an {\i
other}. It is indifferent which is named first, and just for this reason it is named {\i
something} (in Latin, when they occur in a proposition, both are {\i
aliud}, or \u8220?the one, the other,\u8221? {\i
alius alium}; in the case of an alternating relation, the analogous expression is {\i
alter alterum}). {\ul
}If of two beings we call the one A and the other B, the B is the one which is first determined as other. But the A is just as much the other of the B. Both are {\i
other} in the same way. \u8220?This\u8221? serves to fix the distinction and the something which is to be taken in the affirmative sense. But \u8220?this\u8221? also expresses the fact that the distinction, and the privileging of one something, is a subjective designation that falls outside the something itself. The whole determinateness falls on the side of this external pointing; also the expression \u8220?{\i
this}\u8221? contains no distinctions; each and every something is just as good a \u8220?this\u8221? as any other. By \u8220?this\u8221? we {\i
mean} to express something completely determinate, overlooking the fact that language, as a work of the understanding, only expresses the universal, albeit {\i
naming} it as a single object. But an individual name is something meaningless in the sense that it does not express a universal. It appears as something merely posited and arbitrary for the same reason that proper names can also be arbitrarily picked, arbitrarily given as well as arbitrarily altered.{\ul
}{\super
52} Otherness thus appears as a determination alien to the existence thus pointed at, or the other existence as {\i
outside} this one existence, partly because the one existence is determined as other only by {\i
being compared} by a Third, and partly because it is so determined only on account of the other which is outside it, but is not an other for itself. At the same time, as has been remarked, even for ordinary thinking every existence equally determines itself as an other existence, so that there is no existence that remains determined simply as an existence, none which is not outside an existence and therefore is not itself an other. 21.106 Both are determined as {\i
something} as well as {\i
other}: thus they are {\i
the same} and there is as yet no distinction present in them. But this {\i
sameness} of determinations, too, falls only within external reflection, in the comparison of the two; but the {\i
other}, as posited at first, though an other with reference to something, is other also {\i
for itself apart from the something}. Third, the {\i
other} is therefore to be taken in isolation, with reference to itself, has to be taken {\i
abstractly} as the other, the 

