{\b
Chapter 3 21.144Being-for-itself\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ In {\i
being-for-itself}, {\i
qualitative being} is {\i
brought to completion}; it is infinite being; the being of the beginning is void of determination; existence is sublated but only immediately sublated being; it thus contains, to begin with, only the first negation, itself immediate; being is of course retained as well, and the two are united in existence in simple unity; for this reason, however, each is in itself still {\i
unlike} the other, and their unity is still {\i
not posited}. Existence is therefore the sphere of differentiation, of dualism, the domain of finitude. Determinateness is determinateness as such; being which is relatively, not absolutely, determined. In being-for-itself, the distinction between being and determinateness, or negation, is posited and equalized. Quality, otherness, limit, as well as reality, in-itselfness, ought, and so forth, are the incomplete configurations of negation in being which are still based on the differentiation of the two. But since in finitude negation has passed over into infinity, in the {\i
posited} negation of negation, negation is simple self-reference and in it, therefore, the equalization with being \u8211? absolutely {\i
determinate being}. First, being-for-itself is immediately an existent-for-itself, the {\i
one}. Second, the one passes over into a {\i
multiplicity of ones \u8211? repulsion} or the otherness of the one which sublates itself into its ideality, {\i
attraction}. Third, we have the alternating determination of repulsion and attraction in which the two sink into a state of equilibrium; and quality, driven to a head in being-for-itself, passes over into {\i
quantity}. 21.145 \~ {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
A. Being-for-itself as such\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ The general concept of being-for-itself has come to light. The justification for using the expression \u8220?being-for-itself\u8221? for that concept would depend on showing that the representation associated with the expression corresponds to the concept. So indeed it appears to do. We say that something is for itself inasmuch as it sublates otherness, sublates its connection and community {\ul
}with other, has rejected them by abstracting from them. The other is in it only {\i
as} something sublated, as {\i
its moment}; being-for-itself consists in having thus transcended limitation, its otherness; it consists in being, as this negation, the infinite {\i
turning back} into itself. \u8211? In {\i
representing} to itself an intended object which it feels, or intuits, and so forth, consciousness already contains in itself as consciousness the determination of being-for-itself; that is, it has {\i
in it} the content of that object, which is thus an {\i
idealization}; even as it intuits, or in general becomes involved in the negative of itself, in the other, it {\i
abides with itself}. Being-for-itself is the polemical, negative relating to the limiting other and, through this negation of the other, is being-reflected-within-itself \u8211? even though, {\i
side by side} with this immanent turning back of consciousness and the ideality of its object, the {\i
reality} of this object is {\i
also} retained, for the object is {\i
at the same time} known as an external existence. Consciousness is thus {\i
phenomenal},{\ul
}{\super
64} or it is this dualism: on the one side, it knows an external object which is other than it; on the other side, it is for-itself, has this intended object in it as idealized, abides not only by this other but therein abides also with itself. {\i
Self-consciousness}, on the contrary, is {\i
being-for-itself} {\i
brought to completion} and {\i
posited}; the side of reference to another, to an external object, is removed. Self-consciousness is thus the nearest example of the presence of infinity \u8211? granted, of a still abstract infinity, but one which is of a totally different, concrete determination than the being-for-itself in general, whose infinity still has only qualitative determinateness. 21.146 {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
a. Existence and being-for-itself\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ As already mentioned, being-for-itself is infinity that has sunk into simple being; it is {\i
existence} in so far as in the now posited form of the {\i
immediacy} of being the negative nature of infinity, which is the negation of negation, is only as negation in general, as infinite qualitative determinateness. But in such a determinateness, wherein it is existence, being is at once also distinguished from this very being-for-itself which is such only as infinite qualitative determinateness; nevertheless, existence is at the same time a moment of being-for-itself, for the latter certainly contains being affected by negation. So the determinateness which in existence as such is an {\i
other}, and a {\i
being-for-other}, is bent back into the infinite unity of being-for-itself, and the moment of existence is present in the being-for-itself as {\i
being-for-one}. {\ul
}{\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
b. Being-for-one\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ This moment gives expression to how the finite is in its unity with the infinite or as an idealization. Being-for-itself does not have negation {\i
in it} as a determinateness or limit, and consequently also not as reference to an existence other than it. Although this moment is now being designated as {\i
being-for-one}, there is yet nothing at hand for which it would be \u8211? there is not the one of which it would be the moment. There is in fact nothing of the sort yet fixed in being-for-itself; that for which something (and there is no something here) would be, what the other side in general should be, is likewise a moment, itself only being-for-one, not yet a one. \u8211? What we have before us, therefore, is still an undistinguishedness of two sides that may suggest themselves in the being-for-one; there is only {\i
one} being-for-another, and since this is only {\i
one} being-for-another, it is also only being-for-one; there is only the one ideality, of that for which or in which there should be a determination as moment, and of that which should be the moment in it. {\i
Being-for-one} and {\i
being-for-itself} do not therefore constitute two genuine determinacies, each as against the other. Inasmuch as the distinction is momentarily assumed and we speak of {\i
a-being-for-itself}, it is this very being-for-itself, as 21.147the sublated being of otherness, that refers itself to itself as to the sublated other, is therefore {\i
for-one}; in its other it refers itself only to itself. An idealization is necessarily {\i
for-one}, but it is not for an {\i
other}; the one, for which it is, is only itself. \u8211? The \u8220?I,\u8221? therefore, spirit in general, or God, are idealizations, because they are infinite; as existents which are for-themselves, however, they are not ideationally different from that which is for-one. For if they were different, they would be only immediate, or, more precisely, they would only be existence and a being-for-another; for if the moment of being for-one did not attach to them, it is not they themselves but an other that would be that which is for them. God is therefore {\i
for himself}, in so far he is himself that which is {\i
for him}. Being-for-itself and being-for-one are not, therefore, diverse significations of ideality but essential, inseparable, moments of it. {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
Remark\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ The German expression for querying the quality of a thing, {\i
\u8220?}Was f\u252?r ein {\i
Ding etwas sey},\u8221? [or, \u8220?What {\i
for a thing} is this or that\u8221?], though strange at first, reflectively brings out the moment here considered. This expression is idealistic in origin, since it does not ask what this thing {\i
A} might be {\i
for another thing B}, not what this human being might be for another human {\ul
}being; it asks, rather, what {\i
this is for a thing}, {\i
for a human being}, so that this \u8220?being-for-one\u8221? is at the same time taken back into this thing, into this human being; or that {\i
which is} and that {\i
for which it is} are one and the same \u8211? an identity, such as ideality must also be considered to be. Ideality at first attaches to the sublated determinations as distinguished from that {\i
in which} they are sublated, which by contrast can be taken as the real. The result is that the idealization is again one of the moments and the real the other; ideality, however, consists in both determinations being equally only {\i
for one} and having only the value of {\i
a one}, and this {\i
one} ideality, thus undifferentiated, is reality. In this sense, self-consciousness, spirit, God, are each, as an infinite reference purely to itself, an idealization \u8211? the \u8220?I\u8221? is for the \u8220?I,\u8221? both are the same thing, the \u8220?I\u8221? is named twice, but in such a way that each of the two is for-one, ideally; spirit is only for spirit, 21.148God only for God, and this unity alone is God, God as spirit. \u8211? Self-consciousness, however, as consciousness, incurs the difference of {\i
itself} and an {\i
other} \u8211? or of its ideality (in which it is representational) and of its reality, since its representation has a determinate content which, as non-sublated negativity, as existence, still has the side of being known. However, to call thought, spirit, God, {\i
only} an idealization, presupposes the standpoint according to which finite existence counts as the real, and idealization or the being-for-one has only a one-sided meaning. The principle of idealism was stated in an earlier Remark{\ul
}{\super
65} where it was said that in a philosophy all depends on how far this principle is implemented. Regarding the manner of this implementation, one further comment can still be made in connection with the categories that we are considering now. This implementation depends first of all on whether finite existence still remains standing on its own alongside the being-for-itself, but, besides that, on whether the moment of the {\i
for-one}, a relation of idealization to itself as idealization, has already been posited in the infinite itself. Thus the being of the Eleatics or the Spinozistic substance are only the abstract negation of all determinateness, without ideality being posited in them \u8211? in the case of Spinoza, as it will be further mentioned below, infinity is only the absolute {\i
affirmation} of a thing,{\ul
}{\super
66} consequently only the unmoved unity; substance does not therefore ever attain the determination of being-for-itself, even less so of subject and of spirit. The idealism of the noble Malebranche is in itself more explicit. It contains the following basic thoughts: because God includes within itself all eternal truths, the {\ul
}ideas and the perfections of all things, so that they are {\i
his and only his}, we see them only in him; God awakens our sensations of objects in us through an action that has nothing of the sensuous, whereby we imagine to ourselves that we gain of an intended object not only the idea depicting its essence but also the sensation of its existence ({\i
De la recherche de la Verit\u233?}, {\i
Eclaircissement Sur la nature des id\u233?es etc}.).{\ul
}{\super
67} Thus, like the eternal truths and the ideas (essentialities) of things, the existence of these things is in God, is an idealization, not an actual existence; even though, as our intended objects, the same things are only {\i
for one}. This moment of explicit and concrete idealism, lacking in Spinoza, is present here, in absolute ideality being determined as knowledge. Pure and profound as this idealism is, these relations contain nevertheless, on the one hand, much which is still indeterminate for thought and, on the other hand, a content which is at the same time much too concrete (sin and redemption, and so forth, also enter into them); the logical determination of infinity that would have to be their foundation is not elaborated on its own, and as a result such a noble and rich idealism, though indeed the product of pure speculative spirit, is not yet the product of pure speculative thinking, the kind which alone would truly give it a foundation. 21.149 Leibnizian idealism lies more within the confines of the abstract concept. \u8211? The Leibnizian {\i
ideating} being, the monad, is essentially an idealization.{\ul
}{\super
68} Ideation is a being-for-itself in which the determinacies are not limits and therefore not an existence but rather only moments. Ideation is doubtless also a more concrete determination, but it has here no further meaning than that of ideality, since for Leibniz even the things that lack consciousness are representational, perceptual.{\ul
}{\super
69} In this system, therefore, otherness is sublated; spirit and body or the monads in general are not an other for each other, do not limit each other, have no effect on each other; all relations based on an existence fall away in general. The manifold is such only ideally and internally, the monad persists in it only as referred to itself, alterations unfold within it and entail no references of the one monad to others. What is taken in real determination to be an actually existent reference of monads to each other is an independent, only {\i
simultaneous}, becoming which is enclosed in the being-for-itself of each. \u8211? That there is a {\i
plurality} of monads, that they are thereby determined as others, is not {\ul
}the affair of the monads but of a reflection external to them, of a third; {\i
in themselves} they are not {\i
others to each other}; the being-for-itself is kept pure, without the {\i
alongside} of an existence.{\ul
}{\super
70} \u8211? But herein equally lies the incompleteness of this system. The monads are such ideating beings {\i
only in themselves} or {\i
in God}, who is the monad of monads, or also {\i
within the system}. Otherness is present in the same manner; it happens wherever one wishes, in representation itself or according to how the third that considers them as others, as many, is determined. The plurality of their existence is only excluded, and that only momentarily, for the monads are posited as the non-others that they are only through abstraction. If it is a third that posits their otherness, so it is also a third that sublates this otherness; but this whole {\i
movement that makes them idealizations} falls outside them. It may be pointed out to the contrary that this movement of thought happens itself only within an ideating monad. But the rejoinder must then be that the very {\i
content} of such a thought is {\i
within itself external to itself}. 21.150One proceeds immediately, without conceptualization (by means of the image of creation), from the unity of the absolute ideality (the monad of monads) over to the categories of the abstract {\i
plurality} (void of references) of existence, and from this plurality just as abstractly back to that unity. Ideality, representing in general, remains something formal, as does also representing raised to consciousness. Just as in Leibniz's above-mentioned fiction{\ul
}{\super
71} \u8211? that of a magnetic needle which, if it had consciousness, would see its northerly direction as a determination of its freedom \u8211? consciousness is thought only as a one-sided form indifferent to its determination and content, so is the ideality in the monads a form that remains external to their plurality. Ideality is supposed to be immanent in them, their nature is to represent; but, on the one hand, they are in a relation of harmony, but a harmony that does not fall within their determinate being and is therefore pre-established;{\ul
}{\super
72} on the other hand, this {\i
existence} of theirs is not grasped as being-for-other, even less as ideality, but is rather determined only as abstract plurality; the ideality of the plurality and its further determination as harmony do not become immanent in the plurality itself, do not belong to it. Other forms of idealism, as for example the idealism of Kant and Fichte, do not go beyond the {\i
ought} and the {\i
infinite progress} but remain in the dualism of existence and being-for-itself.{\ul
}{\super
73} It is true that in these systems the thing-in-itself or the principle of infinite resistance immediately enters {\ul
}into the \u8220?I\u8221? and becomes something only {\i
for-it}; but it proceeds from a free otherness which perpetuates itself as negative being-in-itself. The \u8220?I\u8221? is therefore indeed determined as idealization, as a-being-for-itself, as infinite reference to itself; but the {\i
being-for-one} is not completed to the point where the beyond of that in-itself, or the direction to the beyond, vanishes. {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
c. The one\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ Being-for-itself is the simple unity of itself and its moments, of the being for-one. There is only one determination present, the self-reference itself of the sublating. The {\i
moments} of being-for-itself have sunk into an 21.151{\i
indifferentiation} which is immediacy or being, but an {\i
immediacy} that is based on the negating posited as its determination. Being-for-itself is thus {\i
an existent-for-itself}, and, since in this immediacy its inner meaning vanishes, it is the totally abstract limit of itself \u8211? {\i
the one}. Attention may be drawn in advance to the difficulties that lie ahead in the exposition of the {\i
development} of the one, and to the source of these difficulties. The {\i
moments} that constitute the {\i
concept} of the one as being-for-itself occur in it {\i
one outside the other}; they are (1) negation in general; (2) {\i
two} negations that are, therefore, (3) {\i
the same}, (4) absolutely opposed; (5) self-reference, identity as such; (6) {\i
negative} reference which is nonetheless {\i
self-reference}. These moments occur here apart because the form of {\i
immediacy}, of {\i
being}, enters into the being-for-itself as existent-for-itself; because of this immediacy, {\i
each} moment is {\i
posited} as {\i
a determination existent on its own}, and yet they are just as {\i
inseparable}. Hence, of each determination the opposite must equally be said; it is this contradiction that causes the difficulty that goes with the abstract {\i
nature of the moments}. {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
B. The one and the many\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ The one is the simple reference of being-for-itself to itself in which its moments have fallen together \u8211? in which, therefore, being-for-itself has the form of {\i
immediacy} and its moments, therefore, are now {\i
there as existents}. As the self-reference of the {\i
negative}, the one is a determining \u8211? and, as {\i
self}-reference, it is infinite {\i
self-}determining. However, because of the present immediacy, these {\i
distinctions} are no longer only moments of one and the same self-determination but are at the same time posited as {\i
existents}. The {\i
ideality} of the being-for-itself as a totality thus turns at first into {\i
reality} \u8211? a reality, moreover, of the most fixed and abstract kind, as a {\i
one}. In the {\i
one}, the being-for-itself is {\i
the posited} unity of being and existence, as the absolute {\ul
}union of the reference to another 21.152and the reference to itself; but also the determinateness of being then enters {\i
into opposition} to the determination of the {\i
infinite negation}, to self-determination, so that what the one is {\i
in itself}, it {\i
is} that now only {\i
in it}, and the negative consequently is an other distinct from it. What shows itself to be {\i
present} as distinct from the one is the one's own self-determining; its unity with itself, as thus distinct from itself, is demoted to {\i
reference}, and, as {\i
negative} unity, it is negation of itself as {\i
other}, the {\i
excluding} of the one as an {\i
other} from itself, from the one. {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
a. The one within{\ul
}{\super
74}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ Within it, the one just {\i
is}; this, its being, is not an existence, not a determination as reference to an other, not a constitution; it is rather its having negated this circle of categories. The one is not capable, therefore, of becoming any other; {\i
it is unalterable}. It is indeterminate, yet no longer like being; its indeterminateness is the determinateness of self-reference, absolutely determined being; {\i
posited in-itselfness}. As negation which, in accordance with its concept, is self-referring, it has distinction in it: it directs away from itself towards another, but this direction is immediately reversed, because, according to this moment of self-determining, there is no other to which it would be addressed, and the directing reverts back to itself. In this simple immediacy, even the mediation of existence and ideality, and with it all diversity and manifoldness, have vanished. In the one there is {\i
nothing}; this {\i
nothing}, the abstraction of self-reference, is here distinguished from the in-itselfness of the one; it is a {\i
posited nothing}, for this in-itselfness no longer has the simplicity of the something, but, as {\i
mediation}, has rather the determination of being concrete; taken in abstraction, it is indeed identical with the one, but different from its determination. So this nothing, posited as {\i
in the one}, is the nothing as the {\i
void}. \u8211? The void is thus the {\i
quality} of the one in its immediacy. 21.153 {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
b. The one and the void\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ The one is the void as the abstract self-reference of negation. But the void, as nothing, is absolutely diverse from the simple immediacy of the one, from the being of the latter which is also affirmative, and because the two stand in one single reference, namely to the one, their diversity is {\i
posited}; {\ul
}however, as distinct from the affirmative being, the nothing stands as void {\i
outside} the one as existent. Being-for-itself, determined in this way as the one and the void, has again acquired an {\i
existence}. \u8211? The one and the void have their negative self-reference as their common and simple terrain. The moments of being-for-itself come out of this unity, become external to themselves; for through the {\i
simple} unity of the moments the determination of {\i
being} comes into play, and the unity itself thus withdraws to {\i
one} side, is therefore lowered to existence, and there it is confronted by its other determination standing over against it, negation as such and likewise as the existence of the nothing, as the void. {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
Remark\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ In this form of existence, the one is the stage of the category that made its appearance among the ancients as the {\i
principle of atomism}, according to which the essence of things is the {\i
atom} and the {\i
void} 

