}Only the need to give a foundation to the fluxional calculus, given its importance, could have brought Newton to deceive himself about such a proof. Other forms which Newton employed in the derivation of differentials are bound up with concrete meanings, all referring to movement, of the elements and their powers. \u8211? In connection with the use of the {\i
serial form} which is of course the signature of his method, it is all too tempting to say that it is always in someone's power to assume as {\i
exact} a magnitude {\i
as one needs} by the addition of further terms, and that the omitted terms are {\i
relatively insignificant}, the result being quite in general only an {\i
approximation}; it is all too tempting in order not to believe that here also Newton would have been satisfied with the same justification for omitting the higher powers which, by his method of solving equations of higher degree by approximation, arise in the given equations from the substitution of any established but still inexact value \u8211? namely, on the crude ground that they are small. See Lagrange, {\i
Equations Num\u233?riques}, p. 125.{\ul
}{\super
100} The {\i
error} into which Newton fell in solving a problem by the omission of essential higher powers, an error which gave his opponents the occasion 21.262for a triumph of their method over his, and of which Lagrange has traced the true origin in his recent investigations ({\i
Th\u233?orie des fonct. Analyt}., 3rd P., Ch. IV),{\ul
}{\super
101} is indicative of the {\i
formalism} and the {\i
uncertainty} which still prevailed in the use of that instrument. Lagrange shows that Newton fell into his error because he ignored the term of the series containing the power on which the specific task depended. Newton had kept to the formal, superficial principle of omitting terms because of their relative smallness. \u8211? For example, it is well known that in mechanics a {\i
determinate meaning} is assigned to the terms of the series in which the function of a movement is developed, so that the first term or the first function refers to the moment of velocity, the second to the accelerating force, and the third to the resistance of forces. Here, then, the terms of the series are not to be seen as {\i
parts} of a sum, but as {\i
qualitative moments of a conceptual whole}. In this way, the {\i
omission} of the remaining terms acquires a completely {\i
different meaning} than their omission on the ground of their {\i
relative {\ul
}smallness}.{\ul
}{\super
k} 21.263The error in Newton's solution was in ignoring, {\i
not} the terms of the series only as {\i
parts of a sum}, but the {\i
term containing the qualitative determination} on which all depends. In this example, the procedure is made to depend on the qualitative {\i
meaning}. In this connection the general assertion can at once be made that the whole difficulty of the principle would be set aside if, instead of the formalism of defining the differential simply by the problem that gives its {\i
name} to it (the problem, that is, of the {\i
difference} in general between a function and its {\i
variation} after its variable magnitudes have received an {\i
increment}), the {\i
qualitative} meaning of this principle were stated and the operation were made to depend on it. In this sense the differential of {\i
