n}}. So, that the rest of the terms are ignored is not on account of their relative smallness; there is no supposition here of an inexactitude, of a 21.264fault or error which would be {\i
compensated for} {\ul
}and {\i
corrected} by another error \u8211? a view on which Carnot especially relies for justifying the ordinary method of the infinitesimal calculus.{\ul
}{\super
103} Since at issue here is a {\i
relation} and not a {\i
sum}, the differential is completely given {\i
by the first term}; and where there is the need of further terms, of differentials of higher orders, their determination does not involve the continuation of a series as {\i
sum}, but the {\i
repetition} rather of one and the same {\i
relation}, the only one wanted and the one which is, therefore, already {\i
completely} determined in the {\i
first term}. The need for the {\i
form} of a {\i
series}, its summation and all that goes with it, must then be totally separated from this {\i
interest of the relation}. The explanations which Carnot offers concerning the method of infinitesimal magnitudes contain the most limpid and most clearly expounded exposition of the upshot of the preceding reflections. However, when the transition is made to the actual operations of calculus, the usual representations about the infinite {\i
smallness} of the omitted terms {\i
with respect to} the others more or less creep in again.{\ul
}{\super
104} Carnot justifies the method rather because, as a matter of fact, its {\i
results} are the correct ones, and because of the {\i
benefits} which the introduction of {\i
incomplete} equations (as he calls them, that is, those in which this arithmetically incorrect omission has taken place) brings to the simplification and the abbreviating of calculus. Lagrange has famously taken up Newton's original method again, that of the series, in an effort to rise above the difficulties inherent in the representation of the infinitely small, as well as in the method of the first and final ratios and limits. Of his functional calculus, of which the other advantages in the matter of precision, abstraction and generality, are sufficiently known, this much alone need be said for present purposes, namely that he rests his case on the fundamental principle that the difference, without becoming zero, {\i
can be taken to be so small that each term of the series exceeds in magnitude the sum of the following terms}.{\ul
}{\super
105} \u8211? In this method, too, the start is made from the categories of {\i
increment} and from the {\i
difference} of the function, the variable magnitude of which receives the {\i
increment} (whence the troublesome series) 21.265from the original function. Also in the sequel, the terms of the series to be omitted come in for consideration only as constituting a {\i
sum}, and the reason for omitting them is assumed in the relativity of their {\i
quantum}. Here too, therefore, on the one hand the omission is not brought back to the standpoint which prevails in some of {\ul
}the applications where (as was noted above){\ul
}{\super
106} the terms of the series are supposed to have a determinate {\i
qualitative meaning}, and terms are disregarded, not because they are insignificant in magnitude, but because they are qualitatively insignificant; on the other hand, the omission itself ceases to have any place in the essential standpoint which, as regards the so-called differential coefficient, acquires a specifically prominent position only with Lagrange, in the so-called {\i
application} of the calculus \u8211? a point which will be discussed in detail in the following Remark. The {\i
general qualitative character} of the form of magnitude here under discussion, as demonstrated in what in that form is called the infinitesimal, is most immediately found in the category of {\i
limit of the ratio} referred to above{\ul
}{\super
107} and, when carried out in calculus, has received the stamp of a method of its own. Of Lagrange's two criticisms of this method \u8211? that it lacks ease of application, and that the expression {\i
limit} carries no determinate idea \u8211? we shall take up only the second here, in order to examine more closely what is being said about its analytical meaning. Now the conception of limit does indeed imply the stated true category of the {\i
