146} They are quoted from his {\i
Neue Principien des Fluentencalculs} (Braunschweig, 1826), and concern a matter which has contributed substantially to the issue of obscurities and unscientific claims in differential calculus; moreover, they accord with what we have said concerning the general orientation of the {\i
theory} of this calculus. To quote: \u8220?Purely {\i
arithmetical investigations} \u8211? admittedly those which more than all other have direct bearing on differential calculus \u8211? have not been separated from this calculus proper. Indeed, they have even been taken, as by Lagrange, for what is {\i
of the essence} of the calculus while calculus itself has been regarded as only the {\i
application} of them. These arithmetical investigations include the rules of differentiation, the derivation of Taylor's theorems, and so on, even the various methods of integration. But {\i
the case is quite the reverse}, for it is these {\i
applications} that make up the {\i
subject matter} of differential calculus {\i
proper}, which {\i
presupposes} all those arithmetical developments and operations from analysis.\u8221? \u8211? We have shown how, with Lagrange, the separation of the so-called application from the procedure of the general part which takes its start from the series serves precisely to bring to light the {\i
proper subject} of differential calculus. However, it is strange that the author, despite entertaining the interesting view that it is precisely the so-called {\i
applications} that constitute the {\i
subject matter} of differential calculus {\i
proper}, would then himself divagate (ibid.) into the formalistic metaphysics of {\i
continuous magnitudes}, {\i
becoming}, {\i
flow}, etc., and would want to add new ballast to old. These determinations are {\i
formal}, in the sense that they are only general categories which fail to give precisely {\i
that which is specific to the subject matter}. But this is what was to be recognized in, and be abstracted from, the concrete propositions, the applications. \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} \~ {\ql
