{\b
Section III Actuality11.369\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} Actuality is the {\i
unity of essence and concrete existence}; in it, {\i
shapeless} essence and {\i
unstable} appearance \u8211? or subsistence without determination and manifoldness without permanence \u8211? have their truth. Although concrete existence is the immediacy that has proceeded from ground, it still does not have form explicitly posited in it; inasmuch as it determines and informs itself, it is {\i
appearance}; and in developing this subsistence that otherwise only is a reflection-into-other into an immanent reflection, it becomes {\i
two worlds}, {\i
two totalities of content}, one determined as {\i
reflected into itself} and the other as {\i
reflected into other}. But the essential relation exposes the formality of their connection, and the consummation of the latter is the {\i
relation of the inner and the outer} in which the content of both is equally only one {\i
identical substrate} and only {\i
one identity of form}. \u8211? Because this identity has come about also in regard to form, the form determination of their difference is sublated, and that they are {\i
one} absolute totality is {\i
posited}. This unity of the inner and outer is {\i
absolute actuality}. But this actuality is, {\i
first}, the absolute as such \u8211? in so far as it is posited as a unity in which the form has sublated itself, making itself into the {\i
empty or external distinction} of an outer and inner. {\i
Reflection} relates to this absolute as {\i
external to it}; it only contemplates it rather than being its own movement. But it is essentially this movement and is, therefore, as the absolute's negative turning back into itself. {\i
Second}, it is {\i
actuality} proper. {\i
Actuality}, {\i
possibility}, and {\i
necessity} constitute {\i
the formal moments} of the absolute, or its reflection. {\i
Third}, the unity of the absolute and its reflection is the {\i
absolute relation}, or rather the absolute as relation to itself, {\i
substance}. {\page } {\ul
} {\s3 \afs28
{\b
Chapter 1 The absolute11.370\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ The simple solid identity of the absolute is indeterminate, or rather, every determinateness of {\i
essence} and {\i
concrete existence}, or of {\i
being} in general as well as of {\i
reflection}, has dissolved itself into it. Accordingly, the {\i
determining of what is the absolute} appears to be a negating, and the absolute itself appears only as the negation of all predicates, as the void. But since it must equally be spoken of as the position of all predicates, it appears as the most formal of contradictions. In so far as that negating and this positing belong to {\i
external reflection}, what we have is a formal, unsystematic dialectic that has an easy time picking up a variety of determinations here and there, and is just as at ease demonstrating, on the one hand, their finitude and relativity, as declaring, on the other, that the absolute, which it vaguely envisages as totality, is the dwelling place of all determinations, yet is incapable of raising either the positions or the negations to a true unity. \u8211? The task is indeed to demonstrate what the absolute is. But this demonstration cannot be either a determining or an external reflection by virtue of which determinations of the absolute would result, but is rather the {\i
exposition of the absolute}, more precisely the absolute's {\i
own exposition}, and only a {\i
displaying of what it is}. \~ {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
A. The exposition of the absolute\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ The absolute is not just {\i
being}, nor even {\i
essence}. The former is the first unreflected immediacy; the latter, the reflected immediacy; further, each is explicitly a totality, but a determinate totality. Being emerges in essence as {\i
concrete existence}, and the connection of being and essence develops into the relation of {\i
inner} and {\i
outer}. The {\i
inner} is {\i
essence}, but as a totality whose essential determination is to be {\i
referred to being} and to be being immediately. The {\i
outer} is {\i
being}, but with the essential determination of being immediately {\i
connected with reflection} and, equally, in a relationless {\ul
}identity with essence. The absolute itself is the absolute 11.371unity of the two; it is that which constitutes in general the {\i
ground} of the essential relation which, as only relation, has yet to return into this its identity and whose ground is not yet {\i
posited}. It follows that the determination of the absolute is to be {\i
absolute form}, but at the same time not as an identity whose moments only are simple determinacies, but, on the contrary, as an identity whose moments are each explicitly the {\i
totality} and hence, indifferent with respect to the form, the complete {\i
content} of the whole. But, conversely, the absolute is absolute content in such a way that this content, which is as such indifferent plurality, explicitly has the negative connection of form by virtue of which its manifold is only {\i
one} substantial identity. Thus the identity of the absolute is for this reason absolute identity, because each of its parts is itself the whole or each determinateness is the totality, that is, because determinateness has become as such a thoroughly transparent reflective shine, a difference {\i
that has disappeared in its positedness}. {\i
Essence}, {\i
concrete existence}, {\i
the world existing in itself}, {\i
whole}, {\i
parts}, {\i
force} \u8211? these reflected determinations appear to representation as true being valid in and for itself; but against them the absolute is the ground into which they have foundered. \u8211? Because in the absolute the form is now only simple self-identity, the absolute does not {\i
determine} itself, for the determination is a difference of form which is valid as such from the start. But because the absolute at the same time contains every difference and form determination in general, or because it is itself absolute form and reflection, the {\i
difference of content} must also come into it. But the absolute itself is the {\i
absolute identity}; to be this identity is its {\i
determination}, for the manifoldness of the world-in-itself and of the phenomenal world has all been sublated in it. \u8211? In the absolute itself there is no {\i
becoming}, since the absolute is not being; nor does the absolute determine itself r{\i
eflectively}, for it is not the essence which determines itself only inwardly; and it also does not {\i
externalize itself}, for it is the identity of inner and outer. \u8211? But in this way the movement of reflection stands {\i
over against} its absolute identity. The movement is sublated in this identity and is thus only its {\i
inner}; but consequently its {\i
outer}. \u8211? At first, therefore, the movement consists only in sublating its act in the absolute. It is the beyond of the manifold differences and determinations and of their movement, a beyond that {\i
lies at the back} of the absolute. It is thus the {\i
negative exposition} of the absolute earlier alluded to.{\ul
}{\super
1} \u8211? In its true presentation, this exposition is the preceding whole of the logical {\ul
}movement of the spheres of {\i
being and essence}, the content of which has not been gathered in from outside as something given and contingent; nor has it been 11.372sunk into the abyss of the absolute by a reflection external to it; on the contrary, it has determined itself within it by virtue of its inner necessity, and, as being's own {\i
becoming} and as the reflection of essence, has returned into the absolute as into its ground. But this exposition has itself also a {\i
positive} side, for in foundering to the ground the finite demonstrates that its nature is to be referred to the absolute, or to contain the absolute within. However, this side is not as much the positive exposition of the absolute as it is rather the exposition of the {\i
determinations}, namely that these have the absolute for their abyss, but also for their {\i
ground},{\ul
}{\super
2} or that that which imparts subsistence to them, to their reflective shine, is {\i
the absolute} itself. \u8211? Being as shine is not {\i
nothing but reflection}, reference to the absolute; or it {\i
is} a shine inasmuch as that which {\i
shines in it is the absolute}. This positive exposition thus halts the finite just before its disappearing: it considers it an expression and a copy of the absolute. But this transparency of the finite that lets only the absolute transpire through it ends up in complete disappearance, for there is nothing in the finite which would retain for it a difference over against the absolute; as a medium, it is absorbed by that through which it shines. This positive exposition of the absolute is therefore itself only a reflective shine, for the true positive, that which contains the exposition and the expounded content, is the absolute itself. Whatever the further determinations that may occur, the form in which the absolute reflectively shines is a nullity which the exposition gathers up from {\i
outside} and in which it gains for itself {\i
a starting point} for its activity. Any such determination has in the absolute, not its beginning {\i
but its end}. This expository process, therefore, though it is an absolute act because of its {\i
reference} to the absolute into which it {\i
returns}, is not so at its {\i
starting point} which is a determination external to the absolute. But in actual fact the exposition of the absolute is the absolute's {\i
own} doing, an act that {\i
begins from itself and arrives at itself}. The absolute, only as absolute identity, is absolute {\i
in a determined guise}, that is, as {\i
identical absolute}; it is {\i
posited} as such by reflection over against opposition and manifoldness; or it is only the {\i
negative} of reflection and determination in general. \u8211? It is not just the exposition of the absolute which is therefore something incomplete, but this {\i
absolute} itself which is only {\i
arrived} at. Or again, the absolute which is only as {\i
absolute identity} is only {\i
the absolute of {\ul
}an external reflection}. It is, therefore, not the absolutely absolute but the absolute in a determination, or it is {\i
attribute}. 11.373 But the absolute is not attribute just because it is the {\i
subject matter} of an external reflection and is consequently something determined by it. \u8211? Or, reflection is not only {\i
external} to it; but, precisely because it is {\i
external} to it, it is {\i
immediately internal} to it. The absolute is absolute only because it is not abstract identity but is the identity of being and essence, or the identity of the inner and the outer. It is therefore itself the absolute form that makes it reflectively shine within itself and determines it as attribute. {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
B. The absolute attribute\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ The expression which we have used, \u8220?the absolute absolute,\u8221? denotes the absolute which in {\i
its form} has {\i
returned} back into itself or whose form is equal to its content. The attribute is just the {\i
relative absolute}, a combination which only signifies the absolute in a {\i
form determination}. For at first, before its complete exposition, the form is {\i
only internally} or, which is the same, {\i
only externally}; it is at first {\i
determinate} form in general or negation in general. But because form is at the same time as the form of the absolute, the attribute is the whole content of the absolute; it is the totality which earlier appeared as a {\i
world}, {\i
or as one of the} sides of the {\i
essential relation}, each of which is itself the whole. But both worlds, the phenomenal world and the world that exists in and for itself, were supposed to be {\i
opposed} to each other in their essence. Each side of the essential relation was indeed equal to the other \u8211? the whole as much as the parts, the expression of force the same content as force itself, and the outer everywhere the same as the inner. But these sides were at the same time supposed each to have still an {\i
immediate} subsistence of its own, the one side as existent immediacy and the other as reflected immediacy. In the absolute, on the contrary, these different immediacies have been reduced to a reflective shine, and the {\i
totality} that the attribute is is {\i
posited as its true and single subsistence}, while the {\i
determination} in which it is is posited as {\i
unessential subsistence}. The absolute is attribute because, as simple absolute identity, it is in the determination of identity; now to the determination as such other determinations can be attached, for instance, also that there are {\i
several} attributes. But because absolute identity has only this meaning, that not only all determinations have been sublated but that reflection itself has also sublated itself, all determinations are thus {\i
posited} in it {\i
as sublated}. Or the totality is posited as absolute totality. Or again, the attribute has {\ul
}the absolute 11.374for its content and subsistence and, consequently, its form determination by which it is attribute is also posited, posited immediately as mere reflective shine; the negative is posited as negative. The positive reflective shine that the exposition gives itself through the attribute \u8211? in that it does not take the finite in its limitation as something that exists in and for itself but dissolves its subsistence into the absolute and expands it into attribute \u8211? sublates precisely this, that the attribute is attribute; it sinks it and its differentiating act into the {\i
simple absolute}. But since reflection thus reverts from its differentiating act only to the {\i
identity} of the absolute, it has not at the same time left its externality behind and has not arrived at the true absolute. It has only reached the indeterminate, abstract identity, which is to say, the identity in the {\i
determinateness} of identity. \u8211? Or, since reflection determines the absolute into attribute as {\i
inner} form, this determining is something still distinct from externality; the inner determination does not penetrate the absolute; the attribute's expression, as something merely posited, is to disappear into the absolute. The form by virtue of which the absolute would be attribute, whether it is taken as outer or inner, is therefore posited as something null in itself, an external reflective shine, or a mere {\i
way and manner}. {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
C. The mode of the absolute\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ The attribute is {\i
first} the absolute in simple self-{\i
identity}. {\i
Second}, it is {\i
negation}, a negation which is {\i
as such} formal immanent reflection. These two sides constitute at first the two {\i
extremes} of the attribute, the {\i
middle term} of which is the attribute itself, since it is both the absolute and the determinateness. \u8211? The second of these extremes is the {\i
negative as negative}, the reflection {\i
external} to the absolute. \u8211? Or inasmuch as the negative is taken as the {\i
inner} of the absolute and its {\i
own} determination is to posit itself as mode, it is then the self-externality of the absolute, the loss of itself in the changeability and contingency of being, its having passed over into its opposite {\i
without turning} back into itself, the manifoldness of form and content determinations that lacks totality. \u8211? But the mode, the {\i
externality} of the absolute, is not just this. It is rather externality {\i
posited} as externality, a mere {\i
way and manner}, hence the reflective shine as reflective shine, or {\i
the reflection of form into itself}; hence, {\i
the self-identity which is the absolute}. In actual fact, therefore, the absolute is first posited as absolute identity only in the mode; it is what it 11.375is, namely self-identity, only as self-referring negativity, as {\i
reflective shining} which is posited {\i
as reflective shining}. {\ul
}Hence, in so far as the {\i
exposition} of the absolute begins from its absolute identity and passes over to the attribute and from there to the mode, it has therein exhaustively run through its moments. But {\i
first}, in this course it does not just behave negatively towards these determinations; {\i
its act} is rather the {\i
reflective movement itself}, and it is only as such a movement that the {\i
absolute truly is absolute identity}. \u8211? {\i
Second}, the exposition does not thereby deal with mere {\i
externality}, and the mode is not only the most external externality. Rather, since the mode is reflective shine as shine, it is an immanent turning back, the self-dissolving reflection, and it is in being this reflection that the absolute is absolute being. \u8211? {\i
Third}, the reflective act of exposition seems to begin from its own determinations and from something external, to take up the modes or even the determinations of the attribute as if they were {\i
found} outside the absolute and its contribution were only to reduce them to undifferentiated identity. But it has in fact found the determinateness from which it begins in the absolute itself. For as {\i
first} undifferentiated identity, the absolute is itself only the {\i
determinate absolute}, or attribute, because it is the unmoved, still unreflected absolute. This {\i
determinateness}, since it is determinateness, belongs to the reflective movement, and it is through this movement alone that the absolute is determined as the {\i
first identity}; through it alone that it has absolute form and does not just {\i
exist as self-equal} but {\i
posits itself as self-equal}. Accordingly the true meaning of mode is that it is the absolute's own reflective movement; it is a {\i
determining} by virtue of which the absolute would become, not an {\i
other}, but what it already {\i
is}; a transparent externality which is a {\i
pointing} to itself; a movement {\i
out} of itself, but in such a way that {\i
being outwardly} is just as much inwardness, and consequently equally a positing which is not mere positedness but absolute being. When therefore one asks for a {\i
content} of the exposition, for {\i
what} the absolute manifests, the reply is that the distinction of form and content in the absolute has been dissolved; or that just this is the content of the absolute, {\i
that it manifests itself}. The absolute is the absolute form which in its diremption of itself is utterly identical with itself, is the negative {\i
as} negative or the negative that rejoins itself and in this way alone is the absolute self-identity which equally is {\i
indifferent towards its distinctions} or is absolute {\i
content}. The content is therefore only this exposition itself. As this self-bearing movement of exposition, as a {\i
way} and {\i
manner} which is its absolute identity with itself, the absolute is expression, not of an inner, nor over against an other, but simply as absolute manifestation of itself for itself. Thus it is {\i
actuality}. 11.376{\ul
} {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
Remark\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ {\i
Spinoza's concept of substance} corresponds to the concept of the absolute, and to the relation of reflection to it, as presented here. Spinozism is a deficient philosophy because {\i
reflection} and its manifold determining is in it {\i
an external thinking}. \u8211? The substance of this system is {\i
one substance}, one indivisible totality; there is no determinateness which would not be contained in this absolute and be dissolved into it; and all that is important is that anything that to the natural way of representing and to the determining of the understanding appears vaguely to occur as self-subsistent is totally reduced in this necessary concept to a mere positedness. \u8211? \u8220?{\i
Determinateness is negation}\u8221?{\ul
}{\super
3} is the absolute principle of Spinozist philosophy; this true and simple insight is at the basis of the absolute unity of substance. But Spinoza stops short at {\i
negation} as {\i
determinateness} or quality; he does not advance to the cognition of it as absolute, that is, {\i
self-negating negation}; therefore {\i
his substance does not contain the absolute form}, and the cognition of it is not a cognition from within. Of course, substance is the absolute unity of {\i
thought} and being or extension; it therefore contains thought itself, but only in its {\i
unity} with extension, that is to say, not as {\i
separating} itself from extension and hence, in general, not as determining and informing, nor as a movement of return that begins from itself. For this reason, on the one hand substance lacks the principle of {\i
personality} \u8211? a defect that has especially aroused indignation against Spinoza's system{\ul
}{\super
4} \u8211? and, on the other hand, cognition is an external reflection that fails to comprehend what appears as finite \u8211? that is, the determinateness of the attribute and the mode, and in general itself as well \u8211? by not deriving them from substance; it behaves like an external understanding, taking up the determinations as {\i
given} and {\i
reducing} them to the absolute but not taking their beginning from it. The concepts that Spinoza gives of substance are that it is the {\i
cause of itself}, that its {\i
essence includes concrete existence within itself}, that the concept of the absolute is {\i
in no need of the concept of an other} by which it would have to be formed.{\ul
}{\super
5} These concepts, however profound and correct, are {\ul
}{\i
definitions} that are {\i
immediately} assumed in the science from the start. Mathematics and other subordinate sciences must begin with something {\i
presupposed} that constitutes their element and positive substrate. But the absolute cannot be a first, an immediate. Essentially the absolute is rather {\i
its result}. 11.377 The further {\i
definition of the attribute} is introduced in Spinoza {\i
simply following} that of the absolute. Spinoza defines the attribute as {\i
the manner in which the understanding conceives the essence of the absolute}.{\ul
}{\super
6} Leaving aside the fact that the {\i
understanding} is assumed to be by nature posterior to the attribute (for Spinoza defines it as {\i
mode}),{\ul
}{\super
7} the attribute, or determination as determination of the absolute, is made to {\i
depend on an other}, namely the understanding, which simply occurs over against substance externally and immediately. Spinoza further defines the attributes as {\i
infinite} \u8211? infinite also in the sense of an {\i
infinite multiplicity}.{\ul
}{\super
8} But only {\i
two} are named in what follows, {\i
thought and extension}, and no indication is given of how the infinite multiplicity necessarily reduces to opposition, specifically this opposition of thought and extension. \u8211? These two attributes are for this reason {\i
empirically} assumed. Thought and being display the absolute in a determination; the absolute itself is their absolute unity, so that the two are only unessential forms; the order of things is the same as the order of representations or thoughts, and the one absolute is viewed only in external reflection (by a mode) under both the two determinations, once as a totality of representations, and once again as a totality of things and their alterations. It is this external reflection that both generates the distinction of representation and being and reduces it to absolute identity, dissolving it there. But this whole movement goes on outside the absolute. True, the absolute is itself also {\i
thought}, and therefore the movement is only in the absolute; but, as just remarked, it is in the absolute only as one with extension, and hence not as {\i
this} movement, which is essentially also the moment of opposition. \u8211? Spinoza makes the sublime demand on thought that {\i
it consider everything under the form of eternity}, {\i
sub specie \u230?terni},{\ul
}{\super
9} that is, as it is in the absolute. But in an absolute which is only unmoved identity, the attribute, like the mode, is only as {\i
disappearing}, not as {\i
becoming}, so that this disappearing also makes its positive beginning only from without. {\ul
}{\i
The third}, {\i
the mode}, is according to Spinoza an {\i
affection} of substance,{\ul
}{\super
10} the determinate determinateness, that which is {\i
in an other} and is comprehended {\i
through this other}. Strictly speaking, the only determination of the attributes is indeterminate difference; each {\i
is supposed} to express the totality of substance and be comprehended by itself; but since each is the absolute as determined, it contains otherness and cannot be comprehended {\i
by itself}. It is only in the mode, therefore, that the determination of the attribute is truly posited. Further, this third element remains mere mode; on the one hand, it is immediately {\i
given}; on the other hand, its nothingness is not recognized as reflection into itself. \u8211? 11.378Consequently, Spinoza's exposition of the absolute is indeed {\i
complete} in so far as it begins with the absolute, lets the attribute follow therefrom, and ends with the mode. But these three are only enumerated {\i
one after the other}, without the inner chain of development, and the third is not negation {\i
as} negation, not the negatively self-referring negation by virtue of which it would {\i
explicitly} be the turning back to the first identity and this identity be in turn truly identity. What is lacking, therefore, is the necessity of the progression of the absolute to inessentiality, as well as the dissolution in and for itself of the latter into identity; or again, missing are both the becoming of the identity and its determinations. Similarly in the {\i
oriental} representation of {\i
emanation}, the absolute is the self-illuminating light. But it does not just illumine itself; it also {\i
emanates}. Its emanations are {\i
distancings} from its unclouded clarity; whatever is generated after is less perfect than that which precedes it and from which it arises. Emanation is taken only as a {\i
happening}, becoming only as a progressive loss. Being thus becomes progressively obscured, and the night, the negative, which is the final term in the progression, does not revert back to the original light. The lack of {\i
immanent reflection} that affects both the Spinozist exposition of the absolute and the doctrine of emanation is remedied in Leibniz's concept of the {\i
monad}.{\ul
}{\super
11} \u8211? It is common for the one-sidedness of a philosophical principle to be countered by the opposite one-sidedness of another, and for a totality to emerge in them, taken together, as a sort of {\i
scattered completeness}. \u8211? The {\i
monad} is a {\i
one}, a negative reflected into itself; it is the totality of the content of the world; in it the differentiated manifold has not only disappeared but is {\i
preserved} in a negative manner. Spinoza's substance is the unity of the content; but this manifold content of the world {\i
is} not as such in it but in external reflection. The monad is, therefore, essentially {\ul
}{\i
representational}; however, although indeed finite, it has no {\i
passivity} but the alterations and the determinations in it are rather manifestations within it of itself. It is an {\i
entelechy}; to manifest is its distinctive act. \u8211? The monad is thereby also {\i
determined}, {\i
differentiated from others}; the determinateness falls in the particular content and in the way and manner of the manifestation. Consequently the monad is the totality {\i
in itself}, according to its substance and not {\i
in its manifestation}. This {\i
limitation} of the monad necessarily falls, not in the {\i
self-positing} or {\i
representational} monad, but in its in-itself; or it is absolute limit, a {\i
predestination} which is posited through another being than itself. Moreover, since anything limited 11.379exists only with reference to other limited things, but the monad is at the same time a self-contained absolute, the {\i
harmony} of these limits, that is, the reciprocal references of the monads, falls outside them and is likewise pre-established by another being or {\i
in itself}. It is clear that although the {\i
principle of immanent reflection} that constitutes the fundamental determination of the monad generally removes otherness and external influence, and by virtue of it the alterations of the monad are its own positing, the side of passivity due to an other is however only transformed into an absolute limitation, into a limitation of the {\i
in-itself}. Leibniz ascribes to the monads a {\i
certain} completeness in themselves, a {\i
kind} of self-subsistence; they are {\i
created} beings.{\ul
}{\super
12} \u8211? When their limitation is more closely examined, the result of Leibniz's account is that the self-manifestation which belongs to them is the {\i
totality of form}. It is a concept of the utmost importance that the alterations of the monad be represented as actions free of passivity, as self-{\i
manifestations}, and that the principle of immanent reflection and of {\i
individuation} stand out as essential. And it is necessary to have finitude consist in the fact that the content or the {\i
substance is distinguished from the form}, and further, also that the one is limited while the other is infinite. But then we should find in the concept of the {\i
absolute monad} not only that absolute unity of form and content but also the nature of reflection \u8211? which is, as self-referring negativity, to repel itself from itself \u8211? by which it posits and creates. And in the system of Leibniz we find indeed also the further point, that {\i
God} is {\i
the source of concrete existence} and {\i
of the essence of the monads},{\ul
}{\super
13} that is, that the absolute limitations in the in-itself of the monads are not anything in and for themselves but vanish in the absolute. But in these determinations we still only find the common representations, left philosophically undeveloped without being raised to the speculative concept. So the principle of individuation does {\ul
}not receive the more profound elaboration which is its due. The concepts concerning the distinctions between the various finite monads and their relation to the absolute do not originate out of this being itself, do not originate absolutely, but belong rather to ratiocinative, dogmatic reflection and have not therefore attained inner coherence. {\ql
