{\b
Chapter 2 Actuality11.380\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ The absolute is the unity of inner and outer as a {\i
first implicitly existent} unit. The {\i
exposition} appeared as an {\i
external} reflection which, for its part, has the immediate as something it has found, but it equally is its movement and the reference connecting it to the absolute and, as such, it leads it back to the latter, determining it as a mere \u8220?way and manner.\u8221? But this \u8220?way and manner\u8221? is the determination of the absolute itself, namely its {\i
first identity} or {\i
its mere implicitly existent unity}. And through this reflection, not only is that first in-itself posited as essenceless determination, but, since the reflection is negative self-reference, it is through it that the in-itself becomes a mode in the first place. It is this reflection that, in sublating itself in its determinations and as a movement which as such turns back upon itself, is first truly absolute identity and, at the same time, the determining of the absolute or its modality. The mode, therefore, is the externality of the absolute, but equally so only its reflection into itself; or again, it is the absolute's {\i
own manifestation}, so that this externalization is its immanent reflection and therefore its being in-and-for-itself. So, as the {\i
manifestation} that it is nothing, that it has no content, save to be the manifestation of itself, the absolute is {\i
absolute form}. {\i
Actuality} is to be taken as this reflected absoluteness. {\i
Being} is not yet actual; it is the first immediacy; its reflection is therefore becoming and {\i
transition into an other}; or its immediacy is not being-in-and-for-itself. Actuality also stands higher than {\i
concrete existence}. It is true that the latter is the immediacy that has proceeded from ground and conditions, or from essence and its reflection. {\i
In itself or implicitly}, it is therefore what actuality is, {\i
real reflection}; but it is still not the {\i
posited} unity of reflection and immediacy. Hence concrete existence passes over into {\i
appearance} as it develops the reflection contained within it. It is the ground that has foundered to the ground; its determination, its vocation,{\ul
}{\super
14} is to restore this ground, and {\ul
}therefore it becomes essential relation, and its final reflection is that its immediacy be posited as immanent reflection and conversely. This unity, in which concrete existence or immediacy and the in-itself, the ground or the reflected, are simply moments, is now {\i
actuality}. 11.381The actual is therefore {\i
manifestation}. It is not drawn into the sphere of {\i
alteration} by its externality, nor is it the {\i
reflective shining} of itself in {\i
an other}. It just manifests itself, and this means that in its externality, and only in {\i
it}, it is {\i
itself}, that is to say, only as a self-differentiating and self-determining movement. Now in actuality as this absolute form, the moments only are as sublated or formal, not yet realized; their differentiation thus belongs at first to external reflection and is not determined as content. Actuality, as itself {\i
immediate} form-unity of inner and outer, is thus in the determination of {\i
immediacy} as against the determination of immanent reflection; or it is an {\i
actuality} as against {\i
a possibility}. The connection of the two to each other is the {\i
third}, the actual determined both as being reflected into itself and as this being immediately existing. This third is {\i
necessity}. But {\i
first}, since the actual and the possible are {\i
formal distinctions}, their connection is likewise only {\i
formal}, and consists only in this, that the one just like the other is a {\i
positedness}, or in {\i
contingency}. {\i
Second}, because in contingency the actual as well as the possible are a {\i
positedness}, because they have retained their determination, {\i
real actuality} now arises, and with it also real {\i
possibility} and {\i
relative necessity}. {\i
Third}, the reflection of relative necessity into itself yields {\i
absolute necessity}, which is absolute {\i
possibility} and {\i
actuality}. \~ {\s6 \afs21
