{\ql
A. Contingency{\ul
}{\super
15} or formal actuality, possibility, and necessity\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ 1. Actuality is formal inasmuch as, as a first actuality, it is only {\i
immediate}, {\i
unreflected} actuality, and hence is only in this form determination but not as the totality of form. And so it is nothing more than a {\i
being}, or concrete {\i
existence} in general. But because {\i
by essence} it is not mere concrete existence but is the form-unity of the in-itselfness or inwardness and externality, it immediately contains {\i
in-itselfness} or {\i
possibility}. {\i
What is actual is possible}. 11.382 2. This possibility is actuality reflected into itself. But this {\i
reflectedness}, itself a first, is equally something formal and consequently only {\i
the determination of self-identity} or of the in-itself in general. {\ul
}But because the determination is here {\i
totality of form}, this in-itself is determined as {\i
sublated} or essentially only with reference to actuality; as the negative of actuality, it is {\i
posited} as negative. Possibility entails, therefore, {\i
two} moments. It has first the positive moment of being a being-reflected-into-itself. But this being-reflected-into-itself, since in the absolute form it is reduced to a moment, no longer has the value of {\i
essence} but has rather the {\i
negative} meaning that possibility is (in a second moment) something deficient, that it points to an other, to actuality, and is completed in this other. According to the first, merely positive side, possibility is therefore the mere form determination of {\i
self-identity}, or the form of essentiality. As such it is the relationless, indeterminate receptacle of everything in general. \u8211? In this formal sense of possibility, {\i
everything is possible that does not contract itself}; the realm of possibility is therefore limitless manifoldness. But every manifold is {\i
determined in itself} and {\i
as against an other}: it possesses negation within. Indifferent {\i
diversity} passes over as such into {\i
opposition}; but opposition is contradiction. Therefore, all things are just as much contradictory and hence {\i
impossible}. \u8211? When we therefore say of something that \u8220?it is possible,\u8221? this purely formal assertion is just as superficial and empty as the principle of contradiction, and any content that we put into it, \u8220?A is possible,\u8221? says no more than \u8220?A is A.\u8221? Left undeveloped, this content has the form of {\i
simplicity}; only after being resolved into its determinations, does {\i
difference} emerge within it. To the extent that we stop at that simple form, the content remains something self-identical and hence {\i
a possible}. But we do not say anything by it, just as we do not with the principle of identity. Yet the possible amounts to more than just the principle of identity. The possible is {\i
reflected immanent reflectedness}; or the identical simply as {\i
a moment} of the totality, hence also as determined not {\i
to be in itself}; it therefore has the second determination of being {\i
only} a possible and the {\i
ought-to-be} of the totality of form. Without this ought-to-be, possibility is {\i
essentiality} as such; but the absolute form entails this, that essence itself is only a moment and that it has no truth without being. Possibility is this mere essentiality, but so {\i
posited} as to be only a moment, to be disproportionate with respect to the absolute form. It is the in-itself, determined as only a {\i
posited} or, equally, 11.383as {\i
not to be in itself}. \u8211? Internally, therefore, possibility is contradiction, or it is {\i
impossibility}. This finds expression at first in this way, that possibility as {\i
form determination posited as sublated} possesses a {\i
content} in general. As possible, this content is an in-itself which is at the same time something sublated or an {\ul
}{\i
otherness}. But because this content is only a possible, an {\i
other} opposite to it is equally {\i
possible}. \u8220?A is A\u8221?; then, too, \u8220?\u8211? A is \u8211? A.\u8221? These two statements each express the possibility of its content determination. But, as identical statements, they are indifferent to each other; that the other is also added, is not {\i
posited} in either. Possibility is the connection comparing the two; as a reflection of the totality, it implies that the opposite also is possible. It is therefore the {\i
ground} for drawing the connection that, {\i
because} A equals A, \u8211? A also equals \u8211? A; entailed in the possible A there is also the possible not-A, and it is this reference itself connecting them which determines both as possible. But this connection, in which the one possible also contains its other, is as such a contradiction that sublates itself. Now, since it is determined to be reflective and, as we have just seen, reflectively self-sublating, it is also therefore an immediate and it consequently becomes {\i
actuality}. 3. This actuality is not the first actuality but reflected actuality, {\i
posited as unity of itself and possibility}. What is actual is as such possible; it is in immediate positive identity with possibility; but the latter has determined itself as {\i
only possibility}; consequently the actual is also determined {\i
as only a possible}. And because possibility is {\i
immediately} contained in actuality, it is immediately in it as sublated, as {\i
only} possibility. Conversely, actuality which is in unity with possibility is only sublated immediacy; or again, because formal actuality is only {\i
immediate} first actuality, it is only a moment, only sublated actuality, or only {\i
possibility}. With this we also have a more precise expression of the extent to which {\i
possibility is actuality}. Possibility is not yet {\i
all} actuality; there has been no talk yet of real and absolute actuality. It is still only the possibility as it first presented itself, namely the formal possibility that has determined itself as being {\i
only} possibility and hence the formless actuality which is only {\i
being} or concrete existence in general. Everything possible has therefore in general a {\i
being} or a {\i
concrete existence}. This unity of possibility and actuality is {\i
contingency}. \u8211? The contingent is an actual which is at the same time determined as only possible, an actual whose other 11.384or opposite equally is. This actuality is, therefore, mere being or concrete existence, but posited in its truth as having the value of a positedness or a possibility. Conversely, possibility is {\i
immanent reflection} or the {\i
in-itself} posited as positedness; what is possible is an actual in this sense of actuality, that it has only as much value as contingent actuality; it is itself something contingent. The contingent thus presents these two sides. First, in so far as it has possibility {\i
immediately} in it, or, what is the same, in so far as this possibility {\ul
}is sublated in it, it is {\i
not positedness}, nor is it mediated, but is {\i
immediate} actuality; it has {\i
no ground}. \u8211? Because this immediate actuality pertains also to the possible, the latter is determined no less than the actual as contingent and is likewise {\i
groundless}. But, {\i
second}, the contingent is the actual as what is {\i
only} possible, or as a {\i
positedness}; thus the possible also, as formal in-itself, is only positedness. Consequently, the two are both not in and for themselves but have their immanent reflection in an other, {\i
or they do have a ground}. The contingent thus has no ground because it is contingent; and for that same reason it has a ground, because it is contingent. It is the {\i
posited}, immediate {\i
conversion} of inner and outer, or of immanently-reflected-being and being, each into the other \u8211? {\i
posited}, because possibility and actuality both have this determination in them by being moments of the absolute form. \u8211? So actuality, in its {\i
immediate} unity with possibility, is only concrete existence and is determined as groundless, something {\i
only posited} or {\i
only possible}; or, as reflected and determined {\i
over against} possibility, it is separated from possibility, from immanent reflectedness, and then, too, is no less immediately {\i
only} a possible. \u8211? Likewise possibility, as {\i
simple} in-itself, is something immediate, {\i
only} an existent in general; or, {\i
opposed} to actuality, it equally is an in-itself without actuality, {\i
only} a possible, but, for that very reason, again only a concrete, not immanently reflected, existence in general. This {\i
absolute restlessness} of the {\i
becoming} of these two determinations is {\i
contingency}. But for this reason, because each determination immediately turns into the opposite, in this opposite each equally {\i
rejoins itself}, and this {\i
identity} of the two, of each in the other, is {\i
necessity}. The necessary is an {\i
actual}; as such it is immediate, {\i
groundless}; but it equally has its actuality {\i
through an other} or in its ground and is at the same time the positedness of this ground and 11.385its reflection into itself; the possibility of the necessary is a sublated one. The contingent is therefore necessary because the actual is determined as a possible; its immediacy is consequently sublated and is repelled into the {\i
ground} or the {\i
in-itself}, and into the {\i
grounded}, equally because its {\i
possibility}, this {\i
ground-grounded-connection}, is simply sublated and posited as being. What is necessary {\i
is}, and this existent is {\i
itself the necessary}. At the same time it is {\i
in itself}; this immanent reflection is an {\i
other} than that immediacy of being, and the necessity of the existent is {\i
an other}. Thus the existent is not the necessary; but this in-itself is itself only positedness; it is sublated and itself immediate. And so actuality, in that from which it is distinguished, in possibility, is identical with itself. As this identity, it is necessity. {\ul
}{\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
B. Relative necessity or real actuality, possibility, and necessity\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ 1. The necessity which has resulted is {\i
formal} because its moments are formal, that is, simple determinations which are a totality only as an immediate unity, or as an immediate conversion of the one into the other, and thus lack the shape of self-subsistence. \u8211? The unity in this formal necessity is therefore simple at first, and indifferent to its differences. As the {\i
immediate} unity of the form determinations, this necessity is {\i
actuality}, but an actuality which, since its unity {\i
is now determined as indifferent} to the {\i
difference of} the form determinations, has a {\i
content}. This content as an indifferent identity contains the form also as indifferent, that is, as a mere {\i
variety} of determinations, and is a {\i
manifold} content in general. This actuality is {\i
real actuality}. Real actuality is {\i
as such} at first the thing of many properties, the concretely existing world; but it is not the concrete existence that dissolves into appearance but, as actuality, it is at the same time an in-itself and immanent reflection; it preserves itself in the manifoldness of mere concrete existence; its externality is an inner relating only to {\i
itself}. What is actual {\i
can act}; something announces its actuality {\i
by what it} 11.386{\i
produces}. Its relating to an other is the manifestation {\i
of itself}, and this manifestation is neither a transition (the immediate something {\i
refers} to the other in this way) nor an appearing (in this way the thing only is {\i
in relation} to an other); it is a self-subsistent which has its immanent reflection, its determinate essentiality, in another self-subsistent. Now real actuality likewise has {\i
possibility} immediately {\i
present in it}. It contains the moment of the in-itself; but, since it is in the first instance only {\i
immediate} unity, it is in {\i
one} of the determinations of form and hence distinguished, as immediate existent, from the in-itself or possibility. 2. This possibility, as the in-itself of {\i
real} actuality, is itself {\i
real possibility}, at first the in-itself {\i
full of content}. \u8211? Formal possibility is immanent reflection only as abstract identity, the absence of contradiction in a something. But when we delve into the determinations, the circumstances, the conditions of a fact in order to discover its possibility, we do not stop at this formal possibility but consider its real possibility. This real possibility is itself {\i
immediate concrete existence}, but no longer because possibility as such, as a formal moment, is immediately its opposite, a non-reflected actuality, but because this determination pertains to it by the very fact of being {\i
real} possibility. The real possibility of a fact is therefore the immediately existent manifoldness of circumstances that refer to it. {\ul
}This manifoldness of existence is therefore indeed both possibility and actuality, but their identity is at first only the {\i
content} which is indifferent to these form determinations; they therefore constitute the form, {\i
determined} as against their identity. \u8211? Or the {\i
immediate} real actuality, because it is immediate, is determined as against its possibility; as this determinate and hence reflected actuality, it is {\i
real possibility}. This real possibility is now indeed the posited {\i
whole} of the form, but of the form in the determinateness of actuality as formal or immediate and equally of possibility as the abstract in-itself. This actuality, therefore, which constitutes the possibility of a fact, is not {\i
its own possibility} but the in-itself of an {\i
other} actual; itself, it is the actuality that ought to be sublated, the possibility as {\i
only} possibility. \u8211? Real possibility thus {\i
constitutes} the {\i
totality of conditions}, a dispersed actuality which is not reflected into itself but is determined to be the in-itself of an other and intended in this determination to return to itself. 11.387 What is really possible is, therefore, something formally identical according to its {\i
in-itself}, free of contradiction because of its {\i
simple} content determination; but, as self-identical, this something must also not contradict itself according to its developed and differentiated circumstances and all else connected with it. But, {\i
secondly}, because it is manifold in itself and in manifold connection with others, and variety inherently passes over into opposition, it is contradictory. Whenever a possibility is in question, and the issue is to demonstrate its contradiction, one need only fasten on to the multiplicity that it contains as content or as its conditioned concrete existence, and from this the contradiction will easily be discovered. \u8211? And this contradiction is not just a function of comparing; on the contrary, the manifold of concrete existence is {\i
in itself} this, to sublate itself and to founder to the ground: in this it explicitly has the determination of {\i
being only a possibility}. \u8211? Whenever all the conditions of a fact are completely present, the fact is actually there; the completeness of the conditions is the totality as in the content, and the {\i
fact} is {\i
itself} this content determined as being equally actual as possible. In the sphere of the conditioned ground, the conditions have the form (that is, the ground or the reflection that stands on its own) {\i
outside them}, and it is this form that makes them moments of the fact and elicits concrete existence {\i
in them}.{\ul
}{\super
16} Here, on the contrary, the immediate actuality is not determined to be condition by virtue of a presupposing reflection, but the supposition is rather that the immediate actuality is itself the possibility. {\ul
}In self-sublating real possibility, it is a twofold that is now sublated; for this possibility is itself the twofold of actuality and possibility. (1) The actuality is formal, or is a concrete existence which appeared to subsist immediately, and through its sublating becomes reflected being, the moment of an other, and thus comes in possession of the {\i
in-itself}. (2) That concrete existence was also determined as {\i
possibility} or as the {\i
in-itself}, but of an other. As it sublates itself, this in-itself of the other is also sublated and passes over into {\i
actuality}. \u8211? This movement of self-sublating real possibility thus produces {\i
the same moments that are already present}, but each as it comes to be out of the other; in this negation, therefore, the possibility is also not a {\i
transition} but {\i
a self-rejoining}. \u8211? In formal possibility, if something was possible, then an {\i
other} than it, not {\i
itself}, was also possible. Real possibility no longer has {\i
such an other} over against it, for it is real in so far as it is itself also actuality. Therefore, as its {\i
immediate concrete existence}, {\i
the circle of conditions}, sublates itself, it makes itself into the {\i
in-itselfness} which it already is, namely the {\i
in-itself} of an other. 11.388And conversely, since its moment of in-itselfness thereby sublates itself at the same time, it becomes actuality, hence the moment which it likewise already is. \u8211? What disappears is consequently this, that actuality was determined as the possibility or the in-itself of an {\i
other}, and, conversely, the possibility as an actuality which is {\i
not that} of which it is the possibility. 3. The {\i
negation} of real possibility is thus {\i
its self-identity}; inasmuch as in its sublating it is thus within itself the recoiling of this sublating, it is {\i
real necessity}. What is necessary {\i
cannot be otherwise}; but what is only {\i
possible} can be, for possibility is the in-itself which is only positedness and hence essentially otherness. Formal possibility is this identity as transition into the other as such; but real possibility, since it has the other moment of actuality within it, is already itself necessity. Hence what is really possible can no longer be otherwise; under the given conditions and circumstances, nothing else can follow. Real possibility and necessity are, therefore, only {\i
apparently} distinguished; theirs is an {\i
identity} that does not first {\i
come to be} but is {\i
already} presupposed at their base. Real possibility is therefore a connection {\i
full of content}, for the content is that identity, existing in itself, which is indifferent to form. But this necessity is at the same time {\i
relative}. \u8211? For it has a {\i
presupposition} from which it begins; it takes its {\i
start} from the {\i
contingent}. For the real actual is as such the {\i
determinate} actual, and first has its {\i
determinateness as immediate being} in that it is a multiplicity of concretely existing circumstances; but this immediate being as determinateness is also the {\i
negative} of {\ul
}itself, is an in-itself or possibility and so real possibility. As this unity of the two moments, it is the totality of form, but a totality which is {\i
still external to itself}; it is the unity of possibility and actuality in such a way that (1) the manifold concrete existence is possibility {\i
immediately} or positively: it is a possible, something self-identical as such, because it is an actual; (2) inasmuch as this possibility of concrete existence is posited, it is determined as {\i
only} possibility, as the immediate conversion of actuality into its opposite \u8211? or as {\i
contingency}. Hence this possibility which immediate actuality has within in so far as it is condition, is only the in-itself or the possibility of an {\i
other}. Because this in-itself, as shown,{\ul
}{\super
17} sublates itself and this positedness is itself posited, real possibility becomes indeed necessity; but this necessity thus begins from that unity of the possible and the actual which is not yet reflected into itself \u8211? this {\i
presupposing} and the {\i
movement which turns back unto itself} are 11.389still separate \u8211? or {\i
necessity has not yet determined itself out of itself into contingency}. The relativity of real possibility is manifested in the {\i
content} by the fact that the latter is at first only the identity indifferent to form, is therefore distinct from it and a {\i
determinate content} in general. A necessary reality is for this reason any limited actuality which, because of its limitation, is in some other respect also only something {\i
contingent}. In actual fact, therefore, {\i
real necessity} is {\i
in itself also contingency}. \u8211? This first becomes apparent because real necessity, although something necessary {\i
according to form}, is still something limited according to content, and derives its contingency through the latter. But this contingency is to be found also in the form of real necessity because, as shown,{\ul
}{\super
18} real possibility is the necessary only {\i
in itself}, but as posited it is the mutual {\i
otherness} of actuality and possibility. Real necessity thus contains contingency; it is the turning back into itself from the restless {\i
being-the-other}-{\i
of-each-other} of actuality and possibility, but not the turning back from itself to itself. {\i
In itself}, therefore, we have here the unity of necessity and contingency; this unity is to be called {\i
absolute actuality}. {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
C. Absolute necessity\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ Real necessity is determinate necessity; formal necessity does not yet have any content and determinateness in it. The {\i
determinateness} of necessity consists in its having its negation, contingency, within it. This is how it has shown itself to be. {\ul
}But {\i
in its first simplicity} this determinateness is actuality; {\i
determinate} necessity is therefore immediate {\i
actual necessity}. This actuality {\i
which is itself as such necessary}, since it contains necessity as its {\i
in-itself}, is {\i
absolute actuality} \u8211? an actuality which can no longer be otherwise, for its {\i
in-itself} is not possibility but necessity itself. But because this {\i
actuality} is posited {\i
to be} {\i
absolute}, that is to say, {\i
to be itself the unity of itself and possibility}, it is consequently only an {\i
empty} determination, or it is {\i
contingency}. \u8211? This {\i
emptiness} of its determination makes it into a {\i
mere possibility}, one which can just as well be an {\i
other} and is determined as possibility. But this possibility is itself {\i
absolute possibility}, for it is precisely the possibility of being equally determined as possibility 11.390and actuality. For this reason, because it is this indifference towards itself, it is posited as empty, {\i
contingent} determination. Thus real necessity not only contains contingency {\i
implicitly}, but the latter also {\i
becomes} in it; but this {\i
becoming}, as externality, is itself only the {\i
in-itself} of the necessity, because it is only an {\i
immediate determinateness}. But it is not only this but the {\i
necessity's own} becoming \u8211? or the {\i
presupposition} which it had is its own positing. For as real necessity, it is the sublatedness of actuality into possibility and of possibility into actuality; because it is this {\i
simple conversion} of one of these moments into the other, it is also their {\i
positive unity}, for in the other each {\i
rejoins itself}. And so it is {\i
actuality}, yet an actuality which is nothing but this rejoining of form with itself. Its negative positing of these moments is thereby itself {\i
the presupposing} or the positing {\i
of itself as sublated}, or the positing of {\i
immediacy}. But it is precisely in this positing that this actuality is determined as the negative; it rejoins itself from the actuality which was real possibility; this new actuality thus comes to be only out of its in-itself, out of {\i
the negation of itself}. \u8211? Consequently, it is at the same time immediately determined as {\i
possibility}, as {\i
mediated} by virtue of its negation. But accordingly, this possibility is immediately nothing but {\i
this mediating} in which the in-itself, namely the possibility itself and the mediating, both in the same manner, are {\i
positedness}. \u8211? Thus it is necessity which is equally the sublating of this positedness, or the positing of {\i
immediacy} and of the {\i
in-itself}, just as in this very sublating it is the {\i
determining} of it as {\i
positedness}. It is necessity itself, therefore, that determines itself as {\i
contingency}: in its being it repels itself from itself, in this very repelling has only returned to itself, and in this turning back which is its being has repelled itself from itself. Thus has form pervaded in its realization all its distinctions; it has made itself transparent and, as {\i
absolute necessity}, is only this simple {\i
self-identity of being in its negation}, or in essence. \u8211? The distinction itself of {\i
content} and {\ul
}form has thus equally vanished; for that unity of possibility in actuality and actuality in possibility is the {\i
form} which in its determinateness or in positedness is indifferent towards itself: it is the {\i
fact full of content} on which the form of necessity externally ran its course. But necessity is thus this {\i
reflected} identity of the two determinations as indifferent to them, and hence the form determination 11.391of the {\i
in-itself} as against the {\i
positedness}, and this possibility constitutes the limitation of the content which real necessity had. The resolution of this difference is however the absolute necessity whose content is this difference which in this necessity penetrates itself. Absolute necessity is therefore the truth in which actuality and possibility in general as well as formal and real necessity return. \u8211? As we have just seen,{\ul
}{\super
19} it is being which in its negation, in essence, refers itself to itself and is being. It is equally simple immediacy or {\i
pure being} and simple immanent reflection or {\i
pure essence}; it is this, that the two are one and the same. \u8211? The absolutely necessary only {\i
is} because it {\i
is}; it otherwise has neither condition nor ground. \u8211? But it equally is pure {\i
essence}, its {\i
being} the simple immanent reflection; it is {\i
because} it is. As reflection, it has a ground and a condition but has only {\i
itself} for this ground and condition. It is in-itself, but its in-itself is its immediacy, its possibility is its actuality. \u8211? {\i
It is}, {\i
therefore}, {\i
because it is}; as the {\i
rejoining} of being with itself, it is essence; but because this simple is equally immediate simplicity, it is {\i
being}. Absolute necessity is thus the {\i
reflection or form of the absolute}, the unity of being and essence, simple immediacy which is absolute negativity. {\i
On the one hand}, therefore, its differences are not like the determinations of reflection but {\i
an existing manifoldness}, a differentiated actuality in the shape of others independently subsisting over against each other. {\i
On the other hand}, since its connection is that of absolute identity, it is the {\i
absolute conversion} of its actuality into its possibility and its possibility into its actuality. \u8211? Absolute necessity is therefore {\i
blind}. On the one hand, the two different terms determined as actuality and possibility have the shape of {\i
immanent reflection} as {\i
being}; they are therefore {\i
free actualities}, neither of which reflectively shines in the other, nor will either allow in it a trace of its reference to the other; grounded in itself, each is inherently necessary. Necessity as {\i
essence} is concealed in this {\i
being}; the reciprocal contact of these actualities appears, therefore, as an empty externality; the actuality {\i
of the one in the other} is the possibility which is {\i
only possibility}, {\i
contingency}. For being is posited as absolutely necessary, as the self-mediation {\ul
}which is the absolute negation of mediation-through-other, or being which is identical only with being; consequently, an {\i
other} that has actuality in {\i
being}, is therefore determined as something {\i
merely possible}, as {\i
empty positedness}. But this {\i
contingency} is rather absolute necessity; it is the {\i
essence} of those free, inherently necessary actualities. This essence 11.392is {\i
averse to light}, because there is no {\i
reflective shining} in these actualities, no reflex \u8211? because they are grounded purely in themselves, are shaped for themselves, manifest themselves only to {\i
themselves} \u8211? because they are only being. \u8211? But their {\i
essence} will break forth in them and will reveal what {\i
it} is and what {\i
they} are. The {\i
simplicity} of their being, their resting just on themselves, is absolute negativity; it is the {\i
freedom} of their reflectionless{\ul
