Chapter 3 The absolute relation11.393\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ Absolute necessity is not so much the {\i
necessary}, even less {\i
a} necessary, but {\i
necessity} \u8211? being simply as reflection. It is relation because it is a distinguishing whose moments are themselves the whole totality of necessity, and therefore {\i
subsist} absolutely, but do so in such a way that their subsisting is {\i
one} subsistence, and the difference only the {\i
reflective shine} of the movement of exposition, and this reflective shine is the absolute itself. \u8211? Essence as such {\i
is} reflection or a shining; as absolute relation, however, essence is the {\i
reflective shine posited as reflective shine}, one which, as such self-referring, is {\i
absolute actuality}. \u8211? The absolute, first expounded by {\i
external reflection}, as absolute form or as necessity now expounds itself; this self-exposition is its self-positing, and {\i
is} only this self-positing. \u8211? Just as the {\i
light} of nature is not a something, nor is it a thing, but its being is rather only its shining, so manifestation is self-identical absolute actuality. The sides of the absolute relation are not, therefore, {\i
attributes}. In the attribute the absolute reflectively shines only in one of its moments, as in a {\i
presupposition} that {\i
external reflection} has simply assumed. But the {\i
expositor} of the absolute is the {\i
absolute} necessity which, as self-determining, is identical with itself. Since this necessity is the reflective shining posited as reflective shining, the sides of this relation, because they are as shine, are {\i
totalities}; for as shine, the differences are themselves and their opposite, that is, they are the whole; and, conversely, they thus are only shine because they are totalities. Thus this distinguishing, this reflecting shining of the absolute, is only the identical positing of itself. This relation in its immediate concept is the relation of {\i
substance} and {\i
accidents}, the immediate internal disappearing and becoming of the absolute reflective shine. If substance determines itself as a {\i
being-for-itself} over against an {\i
other} or is absolute relation as something real, then we have the {\i
relation of causality}. Finally, when this last relation passes over into {\i
reciprocal causality} by referring itself to itself, we then have the absolute relation also {\i
posited} in accordance with the determination it contains; {\ul
}this {\i
posited unity} of itself in its {\i
determinations}, {\i
which} are {\i
posited as the whole itself} and consequently equally as determinations, is then the {\i
concept}. 11.394 \~ {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
A. The relation of substantiality\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ Absolute necessity is absolute relation because it is not {\i
being} as such but {\i
being} that is {\i
because} it is, being as the absolute mediation of itself with itself. This being is {\i
substance}; as the final unity of essence and being, it is the being in {\i
all} being. It is neither the unreflected immediate, nor something abstract standing behind concrete existence and appearance, but the immediate actuality itself, and it is this actuality as being absolutely reflected into itself, as a {\i
subsisting} that exists in and for itself. \u8211? Substance, as this unity of being and reflection, is essentially the {\i
shining} and the {\i
positedness} of itself. The shining is a {\i
self-referring} shining, thus it {\i
is}; this being is substance as such. Conversely, this being is only the self-identical {\i
positedness}, and as such it is {\i
shining totality}, {\i
accidentality}. This shining is identity as identity of form \u8211? the unity of possibility and actuality. It is {\i
becoming} at first, contingency as the sphere of coming-to-be and passing-away; for in the determination of {\i
immediacy} the connection of possibility and actuality is the {\i
immediate conversion} of the two into each other as {\i
existents}, of each into its other as only an {\i
other} to it. \u8211? But because being is shine, their relation is also one of identical terms or of terms shining in one another, that is, reflection. The movement of accidentality, therefore, exhibits in each of its moments the mutual reflective shine of the {\i
categories} of being and of the {\i
reflective determinations} of essence. \u8211? The immediate {\i
something} has a {\i
content}; its immediacy is at the same time reflected indifference towards the form. This content is determinate, and because this determinateness is one of being, the something {\i
passes over} into an other. But quality is also a determinateness of reflection; as such, it is indifferent {\i
diversity}. But this diversity is animated into {\i
opposition}, and returns to the ground which is the {\i
nothing}, but also {\i
immanent reflection}. This reflection sublates itself; but it is itself also reflected in-itselfness: so it is possibility, and this in-itselfness, in its transition which is equally immanent reflection, is {\i
necessary actuality}. This movement of accidentality is the {\i
actuosity} of substance {\i
as the tranquil coming forth of itself}. It is not active {\i
against} something, but only against itself as a simple unresisting element. The sublating of {\i
a presupposition} is the disappearing shine; only in the act of {\i
sublating} the immediate does this {\ul
}immediate itself come to be, or is that shining; the 11.395beginning that begins from itself is first of all the positing of this itself from which the beginning is made. Substance, as this identity of the reflective shining, is the totality of the whole and embraces accidentality in itself, and accidentality is the whole substance itself. Its differentiation into the {\i
simple identity of being} and the {\i
flux of accidents} within it is one form of its shining. {\i
That simple being} is the formless {\i
substance of the imagination} for which the shine has not determined itself as shine, but which holds on, as on an absolute, to this indeterminate identity that has no truth but only is the determinateness of {\i
immediate} actuality, or equally so of {\i
in-itselfness} or possibility \u8211? form determinations that fall into accidentality. \u8211? The other determination, {\i
the flux of accidents}, is the absolute {\i
form-unity} of accidentality, substance as {\i
absolute power}. \u8211? The ceasing-to-be of the accident is its return as actuality into itself, as into its in-itself or into its possibility; but this, its in-itself, is itself only a positedness and therefore also actuality, and because these form determinations are equally determinations of content, this possible is an actual differently determined also according to content. Substance manifests itself through the actuality, with the content of the latter into which it translates the possible, as {\i
creative power}, and, through the possibility to which it reduces the actual, as {\i
destructive power}; the creating is destructive and the destructing creative, for the negative and the positive, possibility and negativity are in substantial necessity absolutely united. The {\i
accidents} as such \u8211? and there are {\i
several} of them, because plurality is one of the determinations of being \u8211? have {\i
no power} over each other. They are the immediately existent something, or the something that immediately exists for itself; concretely existing things of manifold properties; or wholes consisting of parts, self-subsisting parts; forces in need of reciprocal solicitation and conditioning each other. In so far as such an accidental being seems to exercise a power over an other, that power is that of substance that encompasses them both within itself and, as negativity, posits an inequality of value: one it determines as ceasing-to-be and another as having a different content and as coming-to-be, the one as passing over into its possibility and the other into actuality accordingly \u8211? ever dividing itself into this difference of form and content and ever purifying itself of this one-sidedness, but in this purification ever falling back into determination and division. \u8211? One accident thus drives out another only because its own {\i
subsisting} is this very totality of form and content into which it, as well as its other, equally perishes. {\ul
}Because of this {\i
immediate identity} and presence of substance in the 11.396accidents, there is still no {\i
real} difference present. In this {\i
first} determination, substance is not yet manifested according to its whole concept. When substance, as self-identical being-in-and-for-itself, is differentiated from itself as a totality of {\i
accidents}, it is substance itself, as {\i
power}, that {\i
mediates} the difference. This power is {\i
necessity}, the positive persistence of the accidents in their negativity and their mere positedness in their subsistence; this {\i
middle} is thus the unity of substantiality and accidentality themselves, a middle whose {\i
extremes} have no subsistence of their own. Substantiality is, therefore, only the relation as immediately vanishing; it refers to itself not {\i
as a negative} and, as the immediate unity of power with itself, is in the form only of {\i
its identity}, not of its {\i
negative essence}; only one of its moments, that of negativity or of difference, vanishes altogether; the other moment of identity does not. \u8211? Another way of considering the matter is this. The shine or the accidentality is indeed {\i
in itself} substance by virtue of the power, but is not thus {\i
posited} as this self-identical shine; and therefore substance has only the accidentality, not itself, for its shape or positedness; it is not substance {\i
as} substance. The relation of substantiality is at first, therefore, only this, that substance {\i
manifests} itself as a {\i
formal power} whose differences are not substantial; in fact, substance only is as the {\i
inner} of the accidents, and these only are {\i
in the substance}. Or this relation is only the shining of totality as {\i
becoming}; but it is equally reflection and, for this reason, the accidentality which substance is {\i
in itself} is also {\i
posited} as such; it is thus {\i
determined} as self-referring negativity over against itself, {\i
determined} as self-referring simple {\i
identity} with itself; and it is {\i
substance that exists in and for itself}, {\i
substance endowed with power}. Thus the relation of substantiality passes over into the {\i
relation of causality}. {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
B. The relation of causality\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ Substance is power \u8211? power {\i
reflected into itself}, not transitive power but power that posits {\i
determinations} and {\i
distinguishes them from itself}. As self-referring in its determining, {\i
it} is {\i
itself} that which it posits as a negative or makes into a {\i
positedness}. This positedness is, as such, sublated substantiality, the merely posited, the {\i
effect}; the substance that exists for itself is, however, {\i
cause}. This relation of causality is in the first place only this {\i
relation} of cause and effect; as such, it is the {\i
formal relation of causality}. 11.397{\ul
}{\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
a. Formal causality\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ 1. Cause is {\i
originative} as against the effect. \u8211? As power, substance {\i
is the reflective shining}, or it {\i
has} accidentality. But in this shining, as power, it equally is an immanent reflection; it thus {\i
expounds} its transition, {\i
and this reflective shine is determined as reflective shine}, or the accident is {\i
posited} as being just this, something {\i
posited}. \u8211? But in its determining substance does not proceed from accidentality, as if the latter were an {\i
other} {\i
beforehand} and were determined as determinateness only then, but the two are one actuosity. Substance as power {\i
determines itself}; but this determining is immediately itself the sublation of the determining and a turning back. {\i
It determines itself}: {\i
substance}, that which determines, is thus the {\i
immediate} and that which is itself already determined; in determining {\i
itself} it therefore posits the already determined {\i
as determined}; and thus it has sublated the positedness and has returned into itself. \u8211? Conversely, because this turning back is the negative reference of substance to itself, it is itself a {\i
determining} or the repelling of itself from itself; it is through this turning back that the determinate {\i
comes to be} from which substance seems to begin and now to posit as something which it has found already determined. \u8211? Absolute actuosity is thus {\i
cause} \u8211? the power of substance in {\i
its truth} as the manifestation by which that which is {\i
in itself}, the accident or the positedness, is immediately expounded in its becoming, is {\i
posited as positedness}, {\i
as effect}. \u8211? This effect is, therefore, {\i
first} the same as what the accidentality of the relation of substance is, namely substance as {\i
positedness}; but, {\i
second}, an accident is substantially such only by vanishing, only as transient; but as effect it is positedness as self-identical; in the effect the cause is manifested as the whole substance, that is to say, as reflected into itself in the positedness itself as such. 2. Over against this {\i
positedness} reflected into itself, this determined as determined, there stands substance as the {\i
non-posited} original. Because substance is as absolute power a turning back into itself, yet this turning back is itself a {\i
determining}, it is no longer the mere {\i
in-itself} of its accident but is also posited as this in-itself. Substance has {\i
actuality}, therefore, only as cause. But this actuality in which its {\i
in-itself}, its determinateness in the relation of substantiality, is now posited {\i
as determinateness}, is {\i
effect}; therefore substance has the actuality which it has as cause {\i
only in its effect}. \u8211? This is the {\i
necessity} which is cause. \u8211? It is {\i
actual} substance, because as power substance determines itself; but 11.398it is at the same time cause, because it expounds this determinateness or posits it as positedness and thus posits its actuality as positedness or effect. This is the other of cause, the {\ul
}positedness as against the original and as {\i
mediated} through it. But cause, as necessity, equally sublates this mediating and, in {\i
determining itself} as the originally self-referring term, {\i
as against} the mediated, {\i
turns back} to itself; for positedness is determined {\i
as} positedness, and consequently as self-identical; therefore, cause is truly actual and self-identical only in its effect. \u8211? The effect is therefore {\i
necessary}, because it is the manifestation of the cause or is this necessity which the cause is. \u8211? Only as this necessity is cause self-moving, self-initiating without being solicited by another, {\i
self-subsisting source of production out of itself}; it must {\i
effect}; its originariness is this, that it is because its immanent reflection is a positing that determines and conversely; the two are one unity. {\i
Consequently, an effect contains nothing whatever that the cause does not contain}. Conversely, {\i
a cause contains nothing that is not in its effect}. A cause is cause only to the extent that it produces an effect; to be {\i
cause is nothing but this determination of having an effect}, and {\i
to be effect is nothing but this determination of having a cause}. Cause as such entails its effect, and the effect entails the cause; in so far as a cause has not acted yet or has ceased to act, it is not a cause; and the effect, in so far as its cause is no longer present, is no longer an effect but an indifferent actuality. 3. Now in this {\i
identity} of cause and effect the form distinguishing them respectively, as that which exists in itself and that which is posited, is sublated. The cause {\i
is extinguished} in its effect and the effect too is thereby extinguished, for it only is the determinateness of the cause. Hence this causality which has been extinguished in the effect is an {\i
immediacy} which is indifferent to the relation of cause and effect and comes to it externally. {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
b. The determinate relation of causality\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ 1. The self-{\i
identity} of cause in its effect is the sublation of its power and negativity, hence a unity which is indifferent to differences of form, that is to say, {\i
content}. \u8211? This content, therefore, refers to form (here causality) only {\i
implicitly}. The two are thus posited as {\i
diverse}, and with respect to content the form is itself a causality which is only immediately efficient, a {\i
contingent} causality. 11.399 Further, the content is as thus determined an internally diversified content; and the cause is determined in accordance with its content, and so is therefore also the effect. \u8211? The content, since reflectedness here is also immediate actuality, is to this extent {\i
actual}, but {\i
finite}, {\i
substance}. This is now the {\i
relation of causality in its reality and finitude}. As formal, it is the infinite relation of absolute power, the content of which is pure {\ul
}manifestation or necessity. As finite causality, on the contrary, it has a {\i
given} content and, as an external difference, it runs its course here and there over it, this identical content which in its determination is one and the same substance. Because of this {\i
identity of content}, this causality is an {\i
analytic} proposition. It is {\i
the same fact} that comes up once as cause and then again as effect, in one case as something subsisting on its own and in the other as positedness or determination. Since these determinations of form are an {\i
external reflection}, it is up to the {\i
essentially} tautological consideration of a {\i
subjective} understanding to determine an appearance as effect and to rise from it to its cause in order to comprehend and explain it. The same content is being repeated; there is nothing else in the cause which is not in the effect. \u8211? For instance, rain is the cause of wetness which is its effect; \u8220?the rain makes wet,\u8221? this is an analytical proposition; the same water which is rain is wetness; as rain, this water is only in the form of a subject by itself; as wetness or moisture, it is on the contrary in adjectival form, something posited no longer meant to have a subsistence on its own; and the one determination, just like the other, is external to water. \u8211? Again, the cause of {\i
this color} is a coloring agent, a {\i
pigment} which is one and the same actuality, once in the form of an agent external to it, that is, is externally linked to an agent different from it; but again in the determination, equally external to it, of an effect. \u8211? The cause of an {\i
act} is the inner intention of the subject who is the agent, and this intention is the same in content and value as the existence which it attains through the action. If the {\i
movement} of a body is considered as effect, the cause of this effect is then a {\i
propulsive} force; but it is the same quantum of movement which is present before and after the propulsion, the same concrete existence which the propulsive body contained and which it communicated to the one propelled; and what it communicated, it lost in equal measure. The cause, say the painter or the propulsive body, does have {\i
yet another content} than, in the case of the painter, the colors and the 11.400form combining these into a painting; and, in the other case, the movement of specific strength and direction. But this further content is a contingent side-being which has nothing to do with the cause; whatever other qualities the painter might possess besides being the painter of this painting, this does not enter into the painting; only those of his properties which are displayed in the {\i
effect} are present in him {\i
as cause}; as for the rest, he is not a cause. Likewise, whether the propulsive body is of stone or wood, green, yellow, and so on, all this does not enter into its propulsion and, to this extent, is not a cause. {\ul
}It is worth noting in regard to {\i
this tautology} of the relation of causality that the tautology does not seem to occur whenever it is not the proximate, but the {\i
remote} cause which is at issue. The alteration of form which the basic fact undergoes as it passes through several middle terms hides the identity which it preserves across them. In this proliferation of causes introduced between it and the last effect, that fact is linked to other things and circumstances, so that it is not that first term, which is declared the cause, but all these several causes {\i
together}, that contain the complete effect. \u8211? For instance, if a man developed his talents in circumstances due to the loss of his father who was hit by a bullet in battle, then this shot (or still further back, the war or some cause of the war, and on to infinity) could be adduced as the cause of the man's skillfulness. But it is clear that the shot, for one, is not the cause by itself but only in conjunction with the other efficient determinations. Or more precisely, the shot is not the cause at all, but only a single {\i
moment} that pertained to the {\i
circumstances of the possibility}. But it is the {\i
inadmissible application} of the relation of causality to {\i
the relations of physico-organic and spiritual life} that must be noted above all. Here that which is called the cause does indeed show itself to be of a different content than the effect, {\i
but this is because} anything that has an effect on a living thing is independently determined, altered, and transmuted by the latter, {\i
for the living thing will not let the cause come to its effect}, that is, it sublates it as cause. Thus it is inadmissible to say that nourishment is the {\i
cause} of blood, or that such and such a dish, or chill and humidity, are the {\i
causes} of fever or of what have you; it is equally inadmissible to give the Ionic climate as {\i
the cause} of Homer's works, or Caesar's ambition as the {\i
cause} of the fall of Rome's republican constitution. In {\i
history} in general there are indeed spiritual masses and individuals at play and influencing each other; but it is of the nature of spirit, in a much higher sense than it is of the character of living things, that it will 11.401not {\i
admit another originative principle within itself}, or that it will not let a cause continue to work its causality in it undisturbed but will rather interrupt and transmute it. \u8211? But these relations belong to the {\i
idea}, and will come up for discussion then. \u8211? This much can still be noted here, namely that in so far as the relation of cause and effect is admitted, albeit in an inappropriate sense, the effect cannot be {\i
greater} than the cause. It has become a common witticism in history to let {\i
great effects} arise {\i
from small causes} and to cite as the first cause of an event of far-reaching and profound consequence an {\i
anecdote}. Any such so-called first cause is to be regarded as no more than an {\i
occasion}, an {\i
external stimulus}, of which the {\i
inner spirit} of the event had no need, or could have {\ul
}used a countless number of others, in order to make its first appearance, to give itself a first breath and announce itself. The converse is rather the case. It is {\i
by the spirit} that any such triviality and contingency is {\i
determined in the first place} to be the occasion of spirit. Historical {\i
arabesques} that draw a full-blown figure out of a slender stalk are no doubt an ingenious, but highly superficial, practice. It is true that in the rise of the great out of the small we witness everywhere the conversion that spirit works on the external; but precisely for this reason the external is not the {\i
cause within spirit}; rather, that conversion itself sublates the relation of causality. 2. But this {\i
determinateness} of the relation of causality, that content and form are different and indifferent to each other, extends further. The {\i
determination of form} is also {\i
content determination}; cause and effect, the two sides of the relation, are therefore also {\i
another content}. Or the content, because it is only as the content of a form, has the difference of this form within it and is essentially different. But this form of the content is the relation of causality, which is a content identical in cause and the effect, and consequently the different {\i
content} is {\i
externally connected}, on the one hand {\i
with the cause} and on the other {\i
with the effect}; hence the content itself {\i
does not enter into the effective action} and into the {\i
relation}. This external content is therefore relationless \u8211? an {\i
immediate concrete existence}, or because it is as content the {\i
implicit} identity of cause and effect, it is also {\i
immediate}, {\i
existent} identity. This content is, therefore, {\i
anything at all} which has manifold determinations of its existence, {\i
among them} also this, that it is in {\i
some respect or other} cause or also effect. In it, the form determinations of cause and effect have their {\i
substrate}, that is to say, their essential subsistence \u8211? and each has a particular subsistence (since their identity is their subsistence); but it is a subsistence which is at the same time immediate, not their subsistence as unity of form or as relation. 11.402 But this thing is not only substrate but also substance, for it is identical subsistence only {\i
as subsistence of the relation}. Moreover, the substance is {\i
finite} substance, for it is determined as immediate {\i
over against} its causality. But it has causality at the same time, for it is just as much an identity as this relation.{\ul
}{\super
21} \u8211? Now this substrate is, as cause, negative reference to {\i
itself}. But this \u8220?itself\u8221? to which it refers is, {\i
first}, a positedness because it is determined as {\i
immediately} actual; this positedness, as content, is any determination whatever. \u8211? {\i
Second}, {\i
causality} is external to the substrate and {\i
itself constitutes}, {\i
therefore}, {\i
its positedness}. Now since it is causal substance, its causality consists in negatively referring itself to itself, hence to its positedness and external {\ul
}causality. The effective action of this substance thus begins from something external, frees itself from this external determination, and its turning back into itself is the preservation of its immediate concrete existence and the sublation of the one which is posited, and consequently of its causality as such. Take a stone that moves. It is a cause. Its movement is a determination which it has. But, besides it, it contains yet many other determinations (color, shape, and so on) that do not enter into its causality. Because its immediate concrete existence is separated from its form-connection, namely the form of causality, the latter is something external; the stone's movement and the causality attaching to it is in it only {\i
positedness}. \u8211? But the causality of the stone is also {\i
the stone's own causality}, as follows from the fact that its substantial subsistence is the stone's identical self-reference, but that this is now determined as positedness and is therefore at the same time {\i
negative} self-{\i
reference}. \u8211? Its causality, which is directed against itself as a positedness or as an externality, consists therefore in sublating this and through {\i
its removal} in returning to itself \u8211? to this extent, therefore, in {\i
not} being self-identical {\i
in its positedness} but only in restoring {\i
its originariness}. \u8211? Or again, rain is the cause of wetness, which is the same water as the rain. This water has the determination of being rain and cause because this determination has been posited in it by another; another force, or what have you, has lifted it into the air and compressed it into a mass, the weight of which makes it fall. Its being removed from the earth is a determination alien to its original self-identity, to its gravity; its causality consists in removing such determination and in restoring its original identity; but this means also sublating its causality. We now consider the {\i
second determinateness} of causality which concerns {\i
form}; this relation is {\i
causality external to itself}, as the {\i
originariness} which is within just as much {\i
positedness} or {\i
effect}. This union of opposite determination {\i
in an existent substrate} constitutes the {\i
infinite regress} from cause to 11.403cause. \u8211? We start from an effect; the latter has as effect a cause; but this cause has a cause in turn, and so on. Why does the cause have a cause in turn? That is to say, why is {\i
the same side} which was previously determined {\i
as cause} now determined as {\i
effect} and therefore demands a new cause? \u8211? Because the cause is {\i
something finite}, {\i
a determinate} in general; determined as one moment of the form as against the effect; so it has its determinateness or negation outside it; but for this very reason it is itself {\i
finite}, has {\i
its determinateness within it} and is thereby {\i
positedness} or {\i
effect}. Its identity as this positedness is also posited, but it is a {\i
third term}, the immediate substrate; causality is therefore external to itself, because {\i
its originariness} is here {\ul
}an {\i
immediacy}. The difference of form is therefore a {\i
first determinateness}, not yet determinateness {\i
posited as determinateness}; it is {\i
existent otherness}. Finite reflection, on the one hand, stops short at this immediate, removes the unity of form from it and makes it be cause {\i
in one respect} and effect {\i
in another}; on the other hand, it transfers the unity of form into the {\i
infinite}, and through the endless progression expresses its impotence in attaining and holding fast to this unity. Exactly the same is the case of the {\i
effect}, or rather the {\i
endless progression from effect to effect} is one and the same as the {\i
regression from cause to cause}. Just as in the latter a {\i
cause} becomes an {\i
effect} which has {\i
another} cause in turn, so too, conversely, the {\i
effect} becomes a {\i
cause} which has {\i
another} effect in turn. \u8211? The determinate cause under consideration begins from an externality and returns in its effect back to itself, but not {\i
as cause}; on the contrary, it loses its causality in that process. But, conversely, the effect arrives at a substrate which is substance, an original self-referring subsistence; in it, therefore, that positedness {\i
becomes a positedness}, that is to say, this substance, as the effect is posited in it, {\i
behaves as cause}. But that first effect, the positedness that accrues to the substance {\i
externally}, is {\i
other} than the second which the substance {\i
produces}; for this second effect is determined as the {\i
immanent reflection} of substance whereas the first is in it as an {\i
externality}. \u8211? But because causality is here causality external to itself, it also equally {\i
fails} to return in its effect back to itself but becomes therein {\i
external} to itself; {\i
its} effect becomes again a positedness in a substrate \u8211? as in {\i
another substance} which however equally makes this positedness into a positedness, in other words, manifests itself as cause, again repels its effect from itself, and so on, into bad infinity. 11.404 3. We now have to see what has resulted from the movement of determinate causality. \u8211? Formal causality expires in the effect and the element of {\i
identity} of these two moments {\i
emerges} as a result, but it does so only {\i
as an implicit} unity of cause and effect to which the form connection is external. \u8211? For this reason, the element of identity is {\i
immediate} also with respect to both of the two determinations of immediacy, {\i
first} as {\i
in-itself}, as a {\i
content} on which causality is deployed externally; {\i
second}, as a {\i
concrete existent} substrate in which cause and effect {\i
inhere} as different determinations of form. In this substrate, the two determinations are {\i
implicitly} one, but, on account of this {\i
implicitness} or of the externality of form, each is external to itself and hence, in its {\i
unity} with the other, is also determined as {\i
other} with respect to it. Consequently, the cause has indeed an effect {\i
and is at the same time itself effect}; and the effect not only {\i
has} a cause but {\i
is itself also cause}. But the effect which the cause {\i
has}, and the effect {\i
which it is}, are {\ul
}different \u8211? as are also the cause which the effect {\i
has} and the cause {\i
which it is}. The outcome of the movement of the determinate relation of causality is then this, that the cause {\i
does not just expire} in the effect, and thereby the effect as well, as in formal causality, but that {\i
by expiring} in the effect the cause {\i
comes to be} again; that the effect {\i
vanishes} in the cause, but equally {\i
comes to be} again in it. Each of these determinations {\i
sublates itself in its positing}, and {\i
posits itself in its sublating}; what we have is not an {\i
external transition} of causality from one substrate to another, but its {\i
becoming-other} is at the same time its {\i
own positing}. Causality thus {\i
pre-supposes} itself or {\i
conditions itself}. The previously only {\i
implicit identity}, the substrate, is therefore now {\i
determined as presupposition} or {\i
posited as against the efficient} causality, and the {\i
reflection hitherto only} external to the identity is now {\i
in relation} to it. {\s6 \afs21
