{\ql
c. Action and reaction\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ Causality is a {\i
presupposing} activity. The cause is {\i
conditioned}; it is a negative reference to itself as a presupposed, as an external other which {\i
in itself}, but only {\i
in itself}, is causality itself. This other is, as we have seen,{\ul
}{\super
22} {\i
the substantial identity} into which formal causality passes over, which now has determined itself {\i
as against this causality} as its negative. Or it is the same as the substance of the causal relation, but a substance which is confronted by the power of accidentality as itself {\i
substantial activity}. \u8211? 11.405It is the {\i
passive} substance. \u8211? {\i
Passive} is that which is immediate, or which exists-in-itself but is not also {\i
for itself} \u8211? pure being or essence in just this determinateness of {\i
abstract self-identity}. \u8211? Confronting the passive substance is the negatively self-referring substance, the {\i
efficient} substance. It is cause in as much as in determinate causality it has restored itself out of the effect through the negation of itself \u8211? a reflected being{\ul
}{\super
23} which in its otherness or as an immediate behaves essentially as a {\i
positing activity} and through its negation mediates itself. Here, therefore, causality no longer has a {\i
substrate} in which it {\i
inheres}; it is not a determination of form as against this identity but is itself substance, or in other words, causality alone is at the origin. \u8211? The substrate is the passive substance which causality has presupposed for itself. This cause now {\i
acts}, for it is the negative power {\i
over itself}; at the same time it is its own {\i
presupposition}; thus it acts upon itself as {\i
upon an other}, {\ul
}upon {\i
the passive substance}. \u8211? Hence, it {\i
first sublates the otherness} of this substance and returns in it back to itself; {\i
second}, it {\i
determines} this same substance, posits this sublation of its otherness or the substance's turning back into itself as a {\i
determinateness}. This positedness, because it is at the same time the substance's turning back into itself, is at first {\i
its effect}. But conversely, because as presupposing it determines itself as its other, it then posits the effect in this {\i
other}, in the passive substance. \u8211? Or again, because the passive substance is itself this {\i
double} \u8211? namely a self-subsistent {\i
other}, and at the same time something {\i
presupposed} and already implicitly identical with the efficient cause \u8211? because of this, the action of the passive substance is therefore itself double. It is at once both the sublation of its {\i
determinateness}, namely of its condition, or the sublation of the self-subsistence of the passive substance; and also, in sublating its identity as it sublates this substance, the {\i
pre-supposing} of itself, that is, the {\i
positing or supposing} of itself as {\i
other}. \u8211? Through this last moment, the passive substance is {\i
preserved}; that first sublation of it appears in this respect at the same time also in this way, namely that {\i
only some determinations} are sublated in it, and its identity in the effect with the efficient cause occurs in it externally. To this extent it suffers {\i
violence}. \u8211? Violence is {\i
the appearance of power}, or {\i
power as external}. But power is something external only in so far as in its action, that is, in the positing of itself, the causal substance is at the same time a presupposing, that is, posits itself as sublated. Conversely, the act of violence is therefore equally an act of power. The violent cause acts only on an other which it presupposes; its effect on it is its negative {\i
self}-reference, or the manifestation of {\i
itself}. The passive is the self-subsistent which is only a {\i
posited}, something internally fractured \u8211? an actuality which is condition, though a condition that now is in its truth as an actuality that 11.406is only a possible, or, conversely, an {\i
in-itself} that is only the {\i
determinateness of the in-itself}, is only passive. To that which suffers violence, therefore, not only is it possible to do violence, but violence must be done to it; that which has dominion over an other, only has it because its power is that of the other, a power which in that dominion manifests both itself and the other. Through violence the passive substance is only {\i
posited} as what it is {\i
in truth}, namely, that because it is the simple positive or the immediate substance, for that very reason it is only something {\i
posited}; the \u8220?{\i
pre-}\u8221?{\ul
}{\super
24} that it has as condition is the reflective shine of immediacy that the efficient causality strips off from it. {\ul
}Passive substance, therefore, is only given its due by the action on it of another power. What it {\i
loses} is the {\i
immediacy} it had, the substantiality {\i
alien to it}. What {\i
comes to it} as {\i
an alien something}, namely that it is determined as a {\i
positedness}, is its own determination. \u8211? But now in being determined in its positedness, or {\i
in its own} determination, the result is that it is not sublated but rather that it {\i
only rejoins itself} and {\i
in its being determined} is, therefore, an {\i
originariness}. \u8211? On the one hand, therefore, the passive substance is {\i
preserved} or {\i
posited} by the active, namely in so far as the latter sublates itself; but, on the other hand, it is {\i
the act of the passive substance itself} to rejoin itself and thus to make itself into what is originary and a {\i
cause. The being posited} by an other and its own {\i
becoming} are one and the same. Now, because the passive substance has been converted into a cause, it follows, {\i
first}, that the effect is sublated in it; therein consists its {\i
reaction} in general. As passive substance, it is {\i
in itself} as positedness; also, positedness has been {\i
posited} in it by the other substance, namely in so far as it received its {\i
effect} within it. Its reaction contains, therefore, a twofold aspect. For one, what it is {\i
in itself} is {\i
posited}. And two, what it is as {\i
posited} displays itself as its {\i
in-itself}; it is positedness {\i
in itself}, hence through the other substance it receives an effect within; but, conversely, this positedness is {\i
its} own in-itself, it is thus {\i
its own} effect, it itself displays itself as a cause. {\i
Second}, the reaction is directed at {\i
the first efficient cause}. For the effect which the hitherto passive substance sublates within itself is precisely the effect of that other cause. But a cause has its substantial actuality only in its effect; in as much as this effect is sublated, so is also the causal substantiality of the other cause. This happens first {\i
in itself through itself}, in that the cause makes itself into an effect; its negative determination disappears in this identity and the cause becomes passive; and, second, it happens {\i
through the hitherto passive}, but now reacting substance, which sublates its effect. \u8211? 11.407Now in {\i
determinate causality} the substance acted upon becomes a cause, for it acts {\i
against} the positing of an {\i
effect} in it. But it did not react {\i
against the cause of that effect} but posited its effect rather in {\i
another} substance, and thus there arose the progression to infinity of effects \u8211? for here the cause is only {\i
implicitly} identical with itself in the effect, and hence, on the one hand, it expires into an {\i
immediate} identity as it comes to {\i
rest}, but, on the other hand, it revives in {\i
another} substance. \u8211? In conditioned causality, on the contrary, the cause {\i
refers back to itself} in the effect, for the latter is as a condition, as a {\i
presupposition}, its other, and its act is therefore just as much a {\i
becoming} as a positing and sublating of {\i
the other}. {\ul
}Further, causality behaves in all this as passive substance; but, as we have seen,{\ul
}{\super
25} the latter {\i
becomes} causal through the effect it incurs. That first cause, the one which acts first and receives its effect back into itself as a reaction, thus comes up again as a cause, whereby the {\i
activity} which in finite causality runs into the bad infinite progression is {\i
bent around} and becomes an action that returns to itself, an infinite {\i
reciprocal action}. {\s6 \afs21
