{\ql
Foreword\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ This part of the Logic which contains the {\i
Doctrine of the Concept} and constitutes the third part of the whole is also issued under the particular title of {\i
System of Subjective Logic}. This is for the convenience of those friends of this science who, of the materials covered by logic commonly so called, normally take a greater interest in those treated here than in the first two parts. \u8211? For these earlier parts I could claim the indulgence of the fair critic because of the dearth of previous work that could have afforded me some support, the materials and a guide on how to proceed. In the case of the present part, I can rather claim this indulgence for the opposite reason; for there already exists for the logic of the {\i
concept} a fully ready and well-entrenched, one may even say ossified, material, and the task is to make it fluid again, to revive the concept in such a dead matter. To build a new city in a devastated land has its difficulties, even if there is no lack of material at hand; but even greater are the obstacles, of a different kind, when the task is to give a new layout to an ancient and solidly constructed city, with established rights of ownership and domicile; one must also decide, among other things, not to make use of much otherwise valued stock. \u8211? But above all, it is the greatness of the subject matter itself that may be adduced as an excuse for the imperfection in execution. For what subject matter is there for cognition more sublime than {\i
truth} itself? \u8211? Yet there is no escaping the doubt that it is this very subject matter that needs excuse when the sense in which Pilate put the question, \u8220?What is truth?,\u8221? comes to mind, uttering it as he did, in the words of the poet, \u8220?. . .with the courtier's mien that myopically yet smiling damns the cause of the earnest soul.\u8221?{\ul
}{\super
1} 12.6Pilate's question then carries the meaning, which we may view as a moment of politeness, together with its reminder, that the goal of discovering the truth {\ul
}is, as everyone knows, something that has been given up, long since set aside with a shrug; that the unattainableness of truth is recognized also by philosophers and professional logicians. \u8211? But if in our times the question raised in {\i
religion} regarding the value of things, of insights and actions \u8211? a question that in content carries the same implication as Pilate's \u8211? is once more reclaiming its rightful meaning, then philosophy can well hope that it will no longer occur as so strange if it too once more begins to reassert its goal in its immediate domain, and that, after having lapsed into the ways of other sciences in renouncing truth, once more strives to rise up to that goal. There is no excuse needed for this attempt; but as for the execution of it, I may plead as excuse my official duties and other personal circumstances that have allowed me to work but interruptedly at a science that demands instead, and deserves, undistracted and undivided exertion. {\qr
N\u252?rnberg, July 21, 181612.11\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
Of the concept in general\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ What {\i
the nature of the concept is} cannot be given right away, not any more than can the concept of any other subject matter. It might perhaps seem that, in order to state the concept of a subject matter, the logical element can be presupposed, and that this element would not therefore be preceded by anything else, or be something deduced, just as in geometry logical propositions, when they occur applied to magnitudes and employed in that science, are premised in the form of {\i
axioms}, {\i
underived and underivable} determinations of cognition. Now the concept is to be regarded indeed, not just as a subjective presupposition but as {\i
absolute foundation}; but it cannot be the latter except to the extent that it has {\i
made} itself into one. Anything abstractly immediate is indeed a {\i
first}; but, as an abstraction, it is rather something mediated, the foundation of which, if it is to be grasped in its truth, must therefore first be sought. And this foundation will indeed be something immediate, but an immediate which has made itself such by the sublation of mediation. From this aspect the {\i
concept} is at first to be regarded simply as the {\i
third} to {\i
being} and {\i
essence}, to the {\i
immediate} and to {\i
reflection}. Being and essence are therefore the moments of its {\i
becoming}; but the concept is their {\i
foundation} and {\i
truth} as the identity into which they have sunk and in which they are contained. They are contained in it because the concept is their result, but no longer as {\i
being} and {\i
essence}; these are determinations which they have {\ul
}only in so far as they have not yet returned into the identity which is their unity. Hence the {\i
objective logic}, which treats of {\i
being} and {\i
essence}, constitutes in truth the {\i
genetic exposition of the concept}. More precisely, {\i
substance} already is {\i
real essence}, or {\i
essence} in so far as it is united with {\i
being} and has stepped into actuality. Consequently, the concept has substance for its immediate presupposition; substance is {\i
implicitly} what the concept is {\i
explicitly}. The {\i
dialectical movement} of {\i
substance} through causality and reciprocal affection is thus the immediate {\i
genesis of the concept} by virtue of which its {\i
becoming} is displayed. But 12.12the meaning of its {\i
becoming}, like that of all becoming, is that it is the reflection of something which passes over into its {\i
ground}, and that the at first apparent {\i
other} into which this something has passed over constitutes the {\i
truth} of the latter. Thus the concept is the {\i
truth} of substance, and since {\i
necessity} is the determining relational mode of substance, {\i
freedom} reveals itself to be the {\i
truth of necessity} and {\i
the relational mode of the concept}. The necessary forward course of determination characteristic of substance is the {\i
positing} of that which is {\i
in and for itself}. The concept is now this absolute unity of {\i
being} and {\i
reflection} whereby {\i
being-in-and-for-itself} only is by being equally {\i
reflection} or {\i
positedness}, and {\i
positedness} only is by being equally {\i
in-and-for-itself}. \u8211? This abstract result is elucidated by the exposition of its concrete genesis which contains the nature of the concept but had to precede its treatment. We must briefly sum up here, therefore, the main moments of this exposition (which has been treated in detail in Book Two of the Objective Logic). Substance is the {\i
absolute}, the actual in-and-for-itself: {\i
in itself}, because it is the simple identity of possibility and actuality; absolute, because it is the essence containing all actuality and possibility {\i
within itself}; {\i
for itself}, because it is this identity as absolute {\i
power} or absolutely self-referring {\i
negativity}. \u8211? The movement of substantiality posited by these moments consists in the following stages: 1. Substance, as absolute power or self-referring {\i
negativity}, differentiates itself into a relation in which what are at first only simple moments are {\i
substances} and original {\i
presuppositions}. \u8211? Their specific relation is that of a {\i
passive} substance, of {\i
the originariness of the simple} in-itself which, powerless to posit itself, is only originary positedness, and of an {\i
active} substance, the {\i
self-referring} negativity which has as such posited itself as an other and refers {\i
to it}. This other is precisely the passive substance which the active substance, as originative power, has {\i
presupposed} for itself as its condition. \u8211? This presupposing is to be understood in the sense that the movement of substance is at first in the form of one moment of {\ul
}its concept, that of the {\i
in-itself} \u8211? that the determinateness of one of the {\i
substances} standing in relation is itself also the determinateness of this {\i
relation}. 2. The other moment is the {\i
