{\ql
Division12.29\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ The concept, as considered so far, has demonstrated itself to be the unity of {\i
being} and essence. Essence is the {\i
first negation} of being, which has thereby become {\i
reflective shine}; the concept is the {\i
second negation}, or the negation of this negation, and is therefore being which has been restored once more, but as in itself the infinite mediation and negation of being. \u8211? In the concept, therefore, {\i
being} and {\i
essence} no longer have determination as {\i
being} and {\i
essence}, nor are they only in such a unity in which each would {\i
reflectively shine} in the other. Consequently, the concept does not differentiate itself into these determinations. The concept is the truth of the substantial relation in which being and essence attain their perfect self-subsistence and determination each through the other. The truth of substantiality proved to be the {\i
substantial identity}, an identity that equally is, and only is, {\i
positedness}. Positedness is {\i
determinate existence} and {\i
differentiation}; in the concept, therefore, being-in-and-for-itself has attained a true existence adequate to it, for that positedness is itself being-in-and-for-itself. This positedness constitutes the difference of the concept in the concept itself; and because the concept is immediately being-in-and-for-itself, its {\i
differences} are themselves the {\i
whole concept} \u8211? {\i
universal in their determinateness and identical in their negation}. This is now the concept itself of the concept, but {\i
at first only} the concept of the concept or also itself {\i
only} concept. Since the concept is being-in-and-for-itself by being a positedness, or is absolute substance, and substance manifests the {\i
necessity} of distinct substances as an {\i
identity}, this identity must itself posit what it is. The moments of the movement of the substantial relation through which the concept {\i
came to be} and the reality thereby exhibited are only in the transition to the concept; that reality is not yet the {\i
concept's own} determination, one that has emerged out of it; it fell in the sphere of necessity whereas the reality of the concept can only be its {\i
free} determination, a determinate existence in which the concept is identical with itself and whose moments are themselves concepts {\i
posited through the concept itself}. {\i
At first}, therefore, the concept is only {\i
implicitly} the truth; because it is only something {\i
inner}, it is equally {\i
only something outer}. It is {\i
at first} simply an 12.30{\i
immediate} and in this shape its moments have the form of {\i
immediate}, {\i
fixed determinations}. It appears as the {\i
determinate concept}, as the sphere of mere {\i
understanding}. \u8211? Because this form of immediacy is an existence still inadequate to the nature of the concept, for the concept is {\i
free} and only refers to itself, it is an {\i
external} form in which the concept does not exist {\ul
}in-and-for-itself, but can only count as {\i
something posited} or {\i
subjective}. \u8211? The shape of the {\i
immediate} concept constitutes the standpoint that makes of the concept a subjective thinking, a reflection external to the {\i
subject matter}. This stage constitutes, therefore, {\i
subjectivity}, or the {\i
formal concept}. Its externality is manifested in the {\i
fixed being} of its {\i
determinations} that makes them come up each by itself, isolated and qualitative, and each only externally referred to the other. But the identity of the {\i
concept}, which is precisely their {\i
inner} or {\i
subjective} essence, sets them in dialectical movement, and through this movement their singleness is sublated and with it also the separation of the concept from the subject matter, and what emerges as their truth is the {\i
totality} which is {\i
the objective concept}. {\i
Second}, in its {\i
objectivity} the concept is the {\i
fact itself as it exists in-and-for-itself}. The {\i
formal} concept makes itself into the fact by virtue of the necessary determination of its form, and it thereby sheds the relation of subjectivity and externality that it had to that matter. Or, conversely, objectivity is the {\i
real concept} that {\i
has emerged from its inwardness} and has passed over into existence. \u8211? In this identity with the fact, the concept thus has an existence which is {\i
its own} and {\i
free}. But this existence is still a freedom which is {\i
immediate} and not yet {\i
negative}. Being at one with the subject matter, the concept is {\i
submerged} into it; its differences are objective determinations of existence in which it is itself again the {\i
inner}. As the soul of objective existence, the concept must {\i
give itself} the form of {\i
subjectivity} that it {\i
immediately} had as {\i
formal} concept; and so, {\i
in the form} of the free concept which in objectivity it still lacked, it steps forth over against that objectivity and, over against it, it makes therein the identity with it, which {\i
as objective concept} it has in and for itself, into an identity that is also {\i
posited}. In this consummation in which the concept has the form of freedom even in its objectivity, the {\i
adequate}{\ul
}{\super
27} {\i
concept} is the {\i
idea}. {\i
Reason}, which is the sphere of the idea, is the {\i
self-unveiled}{\ul
}{\super
28} {\i
truth} in which the concept attains the realization absolutely adequate to it, and is free in as much as in this real world, in its objectivity, it recognizes its subjectivity, and in this subjectivity recognizes that objective world. {\ql
