Chapter 1 The concept12.32\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ The faculty of concepts is normally associated with the {\i
understanding}, and the latter is accordingly distinguished from the {\i
faculty of judgment} and from the faculty of syllogistic inferences which is formal {\i
reason}. But it is particularly with {\i
reason} that the understanding is contrasted, and it signifies then, not the faculty of concepts in general, but the faculty of {\i
determinate} concepts, as if, as the prevailing opinion has it, the concept were {\i
only} a {\i
determinate}. When distinguished in this meaning from the formal faculty of judgment and from formal reason, the understanding is accordingly to be taken as the faculty of the {\i
single} determinate concept. For the judgment and the syllogism or reason, as formal, are themselves only {\i
a thing of the understanding},{\ul
}{\super
31} since they are subsumed under the form of the abstract determinateness of the concept. Here, however, we are definitely not taking the concept as just abstractly determined; the understanding is therefore to be distinguished from reason only in that it is the faculty of the concept as such. This universal concept that we now have to consider contains the three moments of {\i
universality}, {\i
particularity}, and {\i
singularity}. The difference and the determinations which the concept gives itself in its process of distinguishing constitute the sides formerly called {\i
positedness}. Since this positedness is in the concept identical with being-in-and-for-itself, each of the moments is just as much the {\i
whole} concept as it is {\i
determinate concept} and {\i
a determination} of the concept. It is at first {\i
pure concept}, or the determination of {\i
universality}. But the pure or universal concept is also only a {\i
determinate} or {\i
particular} concept that takes its place alongside the other concepts. Because the concept is a totality, and therefore in its universality or pure identical self-reference is essentially a determining and a distinguishing, it possesses in itself the norm by which this form of its self-identity, in pervading all the moments {\ul
}and comprehending them within, equally determines itself immediately as being {\i
only the universal} as against the distinctness of the moments. {\i
Second}, the concept is thereby posited as this {\i
particular} or {\i
determinate} concept, distinct from others. {\i
Third}, {\i
singularity} is the concept reflecting itself out of difference into absolute negativity. This is at the same time the moment at which it has stepped out of its identity into its {\i
otherness and becomes judgment}.12.33 \~ {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
A. The universal concept\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ The pure concept is the absolutely infinite, unconditioned and free. It is here, as the {\i
content} of our treatise begins to be the concept itself, that we must look back once more at its genesis. {\i
Essence} {\i
came to be out of being}, and the concept out of essence, therefore also from being. But this becoming has the meaning of a self-{\i
repulsion}, so that {\i
what becomes} is rather the {\i
unconditional} and the {\i
originative}. In passing over into essence, {\i
being} became a {\i
reflective shine} or a {\i
positedness}, and {\i
becoming} or the passing over into an {\i
other} became a {\i
positing}; conversely, the {\i
positing} or the reflection of essence sublated itself and restored itself to a {\i
non-posited}, an {\i
original} being. The concept is the mutual penetration of these moments, namely, the qualitative and the originative existent is only as positing and as immanent turning back, and this pure immanent reflection simply is the {\i
becoming-other} or {\i
determinateness} which is, consequently, no less infinite, self-referring {\i
determinateness}. Thus the concept is {\i
absolute self-identity} by being first just this, the negation of negation or the infinite unity of negativity with itself. This {\i
pure} self-{\i
reference} of the concept, which is such by positing itself through the negativity, is the {\i
universality} of the concept. {\i
Universality} seems incapable of explanation, because it is the {\i
simplest} of determinations; explanation must rely on determinations and differentiations and must apply predicates to its subject matter, and this would alter rather than explain the simple. But it is precisely of the nature of the universal to be a simple that, by virtue of absolute negativity, contains difference and determinateness {\i
in itself} in the highest degree. {\i
Being} is simple as an {\i
immediate}; for this reason we can only {\i
intend} it without being able to say what it is; therefore, it is immediately one with its other, {\i
non-being}. The concept of being is just this, that it is so simple as to vanish into its opposite immediately; it is {\i
becoming}. The {\i
universal} is, on the contrary, a {\i
simple} that is at the same time {\i
all the richer in itself}, for it is the concept. {\ul
}{\i
First}, therefore, it is simple self-reference; it is only {\i
in itself}. But, {\i
second}, this identity is in itself absolute {\i
mediation} but not anything {\i
mediated}. Of the universal which is mediated, that is to say, the {\i
abstract} universal, the one opposed to the particular and the singular, 12.34of that we shall have to speak only in connection with the determinate concept. \u8211? Yet, even the {\i
abstract universal} entails this much, that in order to obtain it there is required the {\i
leaving aside} of other determinations of the concrete. As determinations in general, these determination are {\i
negations}, and {\i
leaving them aside} is a further {\i
negating}. Even in the abstract universal, therefore, the negation of negation is already present. But this double negation comes to be represented as if it were {\i
external} to it, both as if the properties of the concrete that are left out were different from the ones that are retained as the content of the abstraction, and as if this operation of leaving some aside while retaining the rest went on outside them. With respect to this movement, the universal has not yet acquired the determination of {\i
externality}; it is still in itself that absolute negation which is, precisely, the negation of negation or absolute negativity. Accordingly, because of this original unity, the first negative, or the {\i
determination}, is not, to begin with, a restriction for the universal; rather, the latter {\i
maintains itself in it} and its self-identity is positive. The categories of being were, as concepts, essentially these identities of the determinations with themselves in their restriction or their otherness; but this identity was only {\i
implicitly} the concept, was not yet made manifest. Consequently, the qualitative determination perished as such in its other and had as its truth a determination {\i
diverse} from it. The universal, on the contrary, even when it posits itself in a determination, {\i
remains} in it what it is. It is the {\i
soul} of the concrete which it inhabits, unhindered and equal to itself in its manifoldness and diversity. It is not swept away in the {\i
becoming} but {\i
persists} undisturbed through it, endowed with the power of unalterable, undying self-preservation. It also does not simply {\i
shine reflectively} in its other, as does the determination of reflection. This determination, as {\i
something relative}, does not refer only to itself but is a {\i
relating}. It {\i
lets itself be known} in its other, but at first it only {\i
shines reflectively} in it, and this reflective shining of each in the other, or their reciprocal determination, has the form of an external activity alongside their self-subsistence. \u8211? The {\i
universal} is posited, on the contrary, as the {\i
essence} of its determination, as this determination's {\i
own positive nature}. For the determination that constitutes the negative of the universal is in the concept simply and solely a {\i
positedness}; essentially, in other words, it is at the same time the negative of the negative, and only {\ul
}is as this self-identity of the negative which is the universal. To this extent, the universal is also the {\i
substance} of its determinations, but in such a way that what for the substance as such was 12.35an {\i
accident}, is the concept's own self {\i
mediation}, its own {\i
immanent} reflection. But this mediation, which first raises the accidental to {\i
necessity}, is the {\i
manifested} reference; the concept is not the abyss of formless substance, or the necessity which is the {\i
inner} identity of things or circumstances different from each other and reciprocally constricting; rather, as absolute negativity, it is the informing and creative principle, and since the determination is not as limitation but is just as much simply sublated as determination, is positedness, so is the reflective shine the appearance as appearance of {\i
the identical}. The universal is therefore {\i
free} power; it is itself while reaching out to its other and embracing it, but without {\i
doing violence} to it; on the contrary, it is at rest in its other as {\i
in its own}. Just as it has been called free power, it could also be called {\i
free love} and {\i
boundless blessedness}, for it relates to {\i
that which is distinct from it} as {\i
to itself}; in it, it has returned to itself. Mention has just been made of {\i
determinateness}, even though the concept has not yet progressed to it, being at first only as the universal and only self-identical. But one cannot speak of the universal apart from determinateness which, to be more precise, is particularity and singularity. For in its absolute negativity the universal contains determinateness in and for itself, so that, when speaking of determinateness in connection with the universal, the determinateness is not being imported into the latter from outside. As negativity in general, that is, according to the {\i
first immediate} negation, the universal has determinateness {\i
in it} above all {\i
as particularity}; as {\i
a second universal}, as the negation of negation, it is {\i
absolute determinateness}, that is, {\i
singularity} and {\i
concreteness}. \u8211? The universal is thus the totality of the concept; it is what is concrete, is not empty but, on the contrary, has {\i
content} by virtue of its concept \u8211? a content in which the universal does not just preserve itself but is rather the universal's own, immanent to it. It is of course possible to abstract from this content, but what we have then is not the universal element of the concept but the {\i
abstract universal}, which is an isolated and imperfect moment of the concept, void of truth. More precisely, the universal shows itself to be this totality as follows. In so far as the universal possesses determinateness, this determinateness is not only the {\i
first} negation but also the reflection of this negation into itself. According to that first negation, taken by itself, the universal is a {\i
particular}, and in this guise we shall consider it in a moment. In the other determinateness, however, the universal is still essentially universal, and this side we have here still to consider. \u8211? For this determinateness, as it {\ul
}is in the concept, is the total reflection \u8211? a {\i
doubly reflective shine}, both {\i
outwards}, as reflection into the other, and {\i
inwards}, as reflection into itself. The outward shining establishes a distinction with respect to an {\i
other}; the universal accordingly takes on a 12.36particularity which is resolved in a higher universality. In as much as it now is also only a relative universal, it does not lose its character of universality; it preserves itself in its determinateness, not just because it remains indifferent to it \u8211? for then it would be only {\i
posited together} with it \u8211? but because of what has just been called the {\i
inward shining}. The determinateness, as determinate {\i
concept}, {\i
is bent back into itself}; it is the concept's own immanent {\i
character}, a character made essential by being taken up into the universality and by being pervaded by it, just as it pervades it in turn, equal in extension and identical with it. This is the character that belongs to the {\i
genus} as the determinateness which is not separated from the universal. To this extent, it is not an outwardly directed {\i
limitation}, but is {\i
positive}, for by virtue of the universality it stands in free self-reference. Thus even the determinate concept remains in itself infinitely free concept. But in regard to the other side in which the genus is limited because of its determinate character, we have just said that, as a lower genus, it has its resolution in a higher universal. This universal can also be grasped as a genus but as a more abstract one; it always pertains, however, only to the side of the determinate concept which is outwardly directed. The truly higher universal is the one in which this outwardly directed side is redirected inwardly; this is the second negation in which the determinateness is present simply and solely {\i
as} something posited, or {\i
as} reflective shine. Life, the \u8220?I,\u8221? spirit, absolute concept, are not universals only as higher genera, but are rather {\i
concretes} whose determinacies are also not mere species or lower genera but determinacies which, in their reality, are self-contained and self-complete. Of course, life, the \u8220?I,\u8221? finite spirit, are also only determinate concepts. To this extent, however, their resolution is in a universal which, as the truly absolute concept, is to be grasped as the idea of infinite spirit \u8211? the spirit whose {\i
posited being} is the infinite, transparent reality in which it contemplates its {\i
creation} and, in this creation, itself. The true, infinite universal, the one which, immediately in itself, is just as much particularity as singularity, is now to be more closely examined as {\i
particularity}. It {\i
determines} itself freely; the process by which it becomes finite is not a transition, the kind that occurs only in the sphere of being; {\i
it is creative power} as self-referring absolute negativity. As such, it differentiates itself internally, and this is a {\i
determining}, because the differentiating is one with the universality. Accordingly, it is a positing of differences {\ul
}that are themselves universals, self-referring. They become thereby {\i
fixed}, isolated differences. The isolated {\i
subsistence} of the finite that was earlier determined{\ul
}{\super
32} as its 12.37being-for-itself, also as thinghood, as substance, is in its truth universality, the form with which the infinite concept clothes its differences \u8211? a form which is equally itself one of its differences. Herein consists the {\i
creativity} of the concept, a creativity which is to be comprehended only in the concept's innermost core. {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
B. The particular concept\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ {\i
Determinateness} as such belongs to being and the qualitative; as the determinateness of the concept, it is {\i
particularity}. It is not a {\i
limit}, as if it were related to an {\i
other} beyond it, but is rather, as just shown, the universal's own immanent moment; in particularity, therefore, the universal is not in an other but simply and solely with itself. The particular contains the universality that constitutes its substance; the genus is {\i
unaltered} in its species; these do not differ from the universal but only {\i
from each other}. The particular has one and the same universality as the {\i
other} particulars to which it is related. The diversity of these particulars, because of their identity with the universal, is {\i
as such} at the same time universal; it is {\i
totality}. \u8211? The particular, therefore, does not only {\i
contain} the universal but exhibits it also {\i
through its determinateness}; accordingly the universal constitutes a {\i
sphere} that the particular must exhaust. This totality, in as much as the determinateness of the particular is taken as mere {\i
diversity}, appears as {\i
completeness}. In this respect, the species are complete simply in so far as {\i
there are} no more of them. There is no inner standard or principle available for them, for their {\i
diversity} is just the dispersed{\ul
}{\super
33} difference for which the universality, which is for itself absolute unity, is a merely external reflex and an unconstrained, contingent completeness. But diversity passes over into {\i
opposition}, into an {\i
immanent connection} of diverse moments. Particularity, however, because it is universality, is this immanent connection, not by virtue of a transition, but in and for itself. It is totality intrinsically, and {\i
simple} determinateness, essential {\i
principle}. It has no {\i
other} determinateness than that posited by the universal itself and resulting from it in the following manner. The particular is the universal itself, but it is its difference or 12.38reference to an {\i
other}, its {\i
outwardly reflecting shine}; but there is no other at hand from {\ul
}which the particular would be differentiated than the universal itself. \u8211? The universal determines {\i
itself}, and so is itself the particular; the determinateness is {\i
its} difference; it is only differentiated from itself. Its species are therefore only (a) the universal itself and (b) the particular. The universal is as concept itself and its opposite, and this opposite is in turn the universal itself as its posited determinateness; the universal overreaches it and, in it, it is with itself. Thus it is the totality and the principle of its diversity, which is determined wholly and solely through itself. There is, therefore, no other true logical division than this, that the concept sets itself on one side as the {\i
immediate}, indeterminate universality; it is this very indeterminateness that makes its determinateness, or that it is a {\i
particular}. The two are both a particular and are therefore {\i
coordinated}. Both, as particular, are also {\i
determinate as against} the universal, and in this sense they are {\i
subordinated} to it. But even this universal, {\i
as against} which the particular is determined, is for that reason itself also {\i
just one} of the opposing sides. When we speak of {\i
two opposing sides}, we must repeat that the two constitute the particular, not just {\i
together}, as if they were {\i
alike} in being particular only for external reflection, but because their determinateness {\i
over against each other} is at the same time essentially only one determinateness; it is the negativity which in the universal is {\i
simple}. Difference, as it presents itself here, is in its concept and therefore in its truth. All previous difference has this unity in the concept. As it is present immediately in being, difference is the {\i
limit} of an {\i
other}; as present in reflection, it is relative, posited as referring essentially to its other; here is where the unity of the concept thus begins to be {\i
posited}; at first, however, the unity is only a {\i
reflective shine} in an other. \u8211? The true significance of the transitoriness and the dissolution of these determinations is just this, that they attain to their concept, to their truth; being, existence, something, or whole and part, and so on, substance and accidents, cause and effect, are thought determinations on their own; as determinate {\i
concepts}, however, they are grasped in so far as each is cognized in unity with its others or in opposition to them. \u8211? Whole and parts, for example, or cause and effect, and so on, are not yet diverse terms that are determined as {\i
particular} relatively to each other, for although they {\i
implicitly} constitute one concept, their {\i
unity} has not yet attained the form of {\i
universality}; thus the {\i
difference} as well which is in these relations, does not yet have the form of being {\i
one} determinateness. Cause and effect, for example, are not two diverse concepts but only {\i
one determinate} concept, and causality is, like every concept, a {\i
simple concept}. 12.39{\ul
}With respect to completeness, we have just seen that the determinate moment{\ul
}{\super
34} of particularity is {\i
complete} in the difference of the {\i
universal} and the {\i
particular}, and that only these two make up the particular species. To be sure, there are more than two species to be found in any genus in {\i
nature}, and these many species cannot stand in the same relation to each other as we have shown. This is the impotence of nature, that it cannot abide by and exhibit the rigor of the concept and loses itself in a blind manifoldness void of concept. We can {\i
wonder} at nature, at the manifoldness of its genera and species, in the infinite diversity of its shapes, for wonder is {\i
without concept} and its object is the irrational. It is allowed to nature, since nature is the self-externality of the concept, to indulge in this diversity, just as spirit, even though it possesses the concept in the shape of concept, lets itself go into pictorial representation and runs wild in the infinite manifoldness of the latter. The manifold genera and species of nature must not be esteemed to be anything more than arbitrary notions of spirit engaged in pictorial representations. Both indeed show traces and intimations of the concept, but they do not exhibit it in trustworthy copy, for they are the sides of its free self-externality; the concept is the absolute power precisely because it can let its difference go free in the shape of self-subsistent diversity, external necessity, accidentality, arbitrariness, opinion \u8211? all of which, however, must not be taken as anything more than the abstract side of {\i
nothingness}. As we have just seen, the {\i
determinateness} of the particular is {\i
simple} as {\i
principle}, but it is also simple as a moment of the totality, determinateness as against the {\i
other} determinateness. The concept, in determining or differentiating itself, behaves negatively towards its unity and gives itself the form of one of its ideal moments {\i
of being}; as a determinate concept, it has a {\i
determinate existence} in general. But this being no longer has the significance of mere {\i
immediacy}, but has the significance rather of an immediacy which is equal to itself by virtue of absolute mediation, an immediacy that equally contains in itself the other moment of essence or of reflection. This universality, with which the determinate clothes itself, is {\i
abstract universality}. The particular has this universality in it as its essence; but in so far as the determinateness of the difference is {\i
posited} and thereby has being, the universality is form in it, and the determinateness as such is its {\i
content}. Universality becomes form in as much as the difference is something essential, just as in the pure universal it is, on the contrary, only absolute negativity and {\i
not} a difference {\i
posited} as such. {\ul
}Now the determinateness is indeed an {\i
abstraction}, as against the {\i
other} determinateness; but the other determinateness is only universality itself, and this too is therefore 12.40{\i
abstract universality}; and the determinateness of the concept, or particularity, is again nothing more than determinate universality. In this universality, the concept is {\i
outside itself}, and because {\i
it is it}, {\i
the concept}, which is there outside itself, the abstract-universal contains all the moments of the concept. It is (\u945?) universality, (\u946?) determinateness, (\u947?) the {\i
simple} unity of the two; but this unity is {\i
immediate}, and the particularity is not therefore {\i
as} totality. {\i
Implicitly} it is this {\i
totality} also, and {\i
mediation}; it is essentially a reference to the {\i
other excluding it}, or the {\i
sublation of negation}, namely of the {\i
other} determinateness \u8211? an {\i
other} that lingers on only as an intention, for it vanishes immediately revealing itself to be the same as its {\i
other} is supposed to be. Therefore, what makes this universality an abstraction is that the mediation is only a {\i
condition}, or is not {\i
posited} {\i
in it}. Because it is not {\i
posited}, the unity of the abstraction has the form of immediacy, and the content has the form of indifference to its universality, for the content is nothing but this totality which is the universality of absolute negativity. Hence the abstract universal is indeed the {\i
concept}, but the {\i
unconceptualized} concept, the concept not posited as such. When we speak of the {\i
determinate concept}, what we ordinarily mean is precisely just this {\i
abstract universal}. Even by {\i
concept} as such, what is generally understood is only this {\i
unconceptualized} concept, and the {\i
understanding} is designated as its faculty. {\i
Demonstration} belongs to this {\i
understanding} in as much as it {\i
proceeds} by way of {\i
concepts}, that is to say, only in {\i
determinations}. This progression by way of concepts does not therefore reach past finitude and necessity; the highest it reaches is the negative infinite, the abstraction of the highest essence which is itself the determinateness of the {\i
indeterminateness}. Absolute substance, too, although not this empty abstraction but on the contrary a totality according to content, is still abstract, for since it is without absolute form, its innermost truth is not constituted by the concept; although it is the identity of universality and particularity, or of thought and externality, this identity is not the {\i
determinateness} of the concept; there is rather an understanding {\i
outside} it \u8211? an understanding which is contingent precisely because it is outside it \u8211? in which and for which substance exists in diverse attributes and modes. Moreover, abstraction is not as {\i
empty} as it is usually said to be; it is the {\i
determinate} concept; it has some determinateness or other for its content; the highest essence also, the pure abstraction, has the determinateness of indeterminateness, as just mentioned; but indeterminateness is a determinateness because it is supposed to stand {\i
opposite} the 12.41determinate. But the {\ul
}moment one says what it is, its intended meaning sublates itself by itself; for it is spoken of on a par with determinateness, and from this abstraction the concept and its truth are brought out. \u8211? To be sure, any determinate concept is {\i
empty} in so far as it does not contain the totality, but only a one-sided determinateness. Even when it has otherwise concrete content such as, for instance, humankind, the state, animal, etc., it remains an empty concept in as much as its determinateness is not the {\i
principle} of its differentiation; the principle contains the beginning and the essence of its development and realization; any other determinateness of the concept is however otiose. To reproach the concept as such for being empty is to ignore its absolute determinateness which is the difference of the concept and the only true content in the element of the concept. Here we have the circumstance that explains why the understanding is nowadays held in such a low repute and is so much discredited when measured against reason;{\ul
}{\super
35} it is the {\i
fixity} which it imparts to determinacies and consequently to anything finite. This fixity consists in the form of the abstract universality just considered that makes them {\i
unalterable}. For qualitative determinateness, and also the determination of reflection, are essentially {\i
limited}, and because of their limitation they entail a reference to their {\i
other}; hence the {\i
necessity} of their transition and passing away. But the universality which they possess in the understanding gives them the form of immanent reflection and, because this form removes from them the reference to the other, they have become {\i
unalterable}. Now although this eternity belongs to the pure concept by nature, the determinations of the concept are eternal essentialities only according to {\i
form}; but their content is not proportionate to this form and, therefore, they are not truth, or imperishable. Their content is not proportionate to the form because it is not the determinateness itself as universal, that is, not as totality of the difference of the concept, or is not itself the whole form; the form of the limited understanding is for this reason itself imperfect universality, that is to say, {\i
abstract} universality. \u8211? But further, we must pay due respect to the infinite force of the understanding in splitting the concrete into abstract {\ul
}determinacies and plumbing the depth of the difference \u8211? this force which alone is at the same time the mighty power causing the transition of the determinacies. The concrete of {\i
intuition} is a {\i
totality}, but a {\i
sensuous totality}, a real material that subsists in space and time, {\i
part outside part}, each indifferent to the other; surely this lack of unity in a manifold that makes it the content of intuition ought not to be credited as privileging it over the universal of the understanding. The mutability that the manifold exhibits in intuition already points to the universal; but all of the manifold that comes to intuition is just more of the same, an equally {\i
alterable other} \u8211? not the universal that one would expect to 12.42appear and take its place. But least of all should we reckon to the credit of such sciences as for example Geometry and Arithmetic that their material carries an {\i
intuitive element} with it, or imagine that their propositions are grounded by it. On the contrary, the presence of that element renders the material of these sciences of an inferior nature; the intuition of figures or numbers is of no help to the science of figures and numbers; only the {\i
thought} of them produces this science. \u8211? But if by intuition we understand not merely a sensuous material but the {\i
objective totality}, then the intuition is an {\i
intellectual one}, that is, its subject matter is not existence in its externalization but that element in existence which is unalterable reality and truth \u8211? the reality only in so far as it is essentially in the concept and is {\i
determined} by it; the {\i
idea}, of whose more precise nature more will be said later. What intuition as such is supposed to have over the concept is external reality, the reality that lacks the concept and receives value only through the concept. Consequently, since the understanding exhibits the infinite force that determines the universal, or conversely, since it is the understanding that through the form of universality imparts stable subsistence to the otherwise inherent instability of determinateness, then it is not the fault of the understanding if there is no further advance. It is a subjective {\i
impotence of reason} that allows these determinacies to remain so dispersed, and is unable to bring them back to their unity through the dialectical force opposed to that abstract universality, that is to say, through the determinacies' own nature which is their concept. To be sure, the understanding does give them through the form of abstract universality a {\i
rigidity} of being, so to speak, which they do not otherwise possess in the qualitative sphere and in the sphere of reflection; but by thus simplifying them, the understanding at the same time {\i
quickens} them {\i
with spirit}, and it so sharpens them that only at that point, only there, do they also obtain the capacity to dissolve themselves and to pass over into their opposite. The ripest maturity, the highest stage, that anything can attain is the one at which its fall begins. {\ul
}The fixity of the determinacies which the understanding appears to run up against, the form of the imperishable, is that of self-referring universality. But this universality belongs to the concept as its own, and for this reason what is found expressed in it, infinitely close at hand, is the {\i
dissolution} of the finite. This universality directly {\i
contradicts} the determinateness of the finite and {\i
makes explicit} its disproportion with respect to it. \u8211? Or rather, that disproportion is already at hand; the abstract determinate is posited as one with universality and, for this reason, not for itself (for it would then be only a determinate) but, on the contrary, only as the unity of itself and the universal, that is, as concept. Therefore the common practice of separating understanding and reason is to be rejected on all counts. On the contrary, to consider the concept as void of reason 12.43should itself be considered as an incapacity of reason to recognize itself in the concept. The determinate and abstract concept is the {\i
condition}, or rather an {\i
essential moment}, {\i
of reason}; it is form quickened by spirit in which the finite, through the universality in which it refers to itself, is internally kindled, is posited as dialectical and thereby is the {\i
beginning} of the appearance of reason. Since in the foregoing the determinate concept has been presented in its truth, it is only left to indicate {\i
what}, as so presented, it has already been posited {\i
as}. \u8211? Difference, which is an essential moment of the concept but in the pure universal is not yet posited as such, receives its due in the determinate concept. Determinateness in the form of universality is united with the latter to form a simple; determinate universality is self-referring determinateness, determinate determinateness or absolute negativity posited for itself. But self-referring determinateness is {\i
singularity}. Just as universality immediately is particularity in and for itself, no less immediately is particularity also {\i
singularity} in and for itself; this singularity is at first to be regarded as the third moment of the concept, in as much as it is held fast in {\i
opposition} to the other two, but also as the absolute turning back of the concept into itself, and at the same time as the posited loss of itself. {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
Remark\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ {\i
Universality}, {\i
particularity}, and {\i
singularity} are, according to the foregoing, the {\i
three} determinate concepts, that is, if one wants to {\i
count} them. We have already shown that number is a form unsuited to conceptual determinations,{\ul
}{\super
36} but for the determination of the concept itself it is unsuited the most; number, since the unit is its principle, turns the counted {\ul
}into totally separated units indifferent to each other. We have seen from the foregoing that the diverse determinate concepts, rather than falling apart as they do when counted, are only {\i
one} and the same concept. In the customary treatment of logic, a variety of {\i
classifications} and {\i
species} of concepts are adduced. It immediately strikes one as inconsequential that the species are introduced in this way: \u8220?{\i
There are}, as regards quality, quantity, etc., the following concepts.\u8221? The \u8220?there are\u8221? conveys no other justification than that {\i
we find} the named species and that they show up in {\i
experience}. What we have in this manner is an {\i
empirical} logic \u8211? an odd science indeed, an {\i
irrational} cognition of the {\i
rational}. In this the logic sets a very bad precedent for compliance to its own teaching; it allows itself to do the opposite of what it prescribes as a rule, 12.44namely, that concepts should be derived, and scientific propositions (therefore also the proposition: \u8220?There are such and such species of concepts\u8221?) demonstrated. \u8211? In this context, the Kantian philosophy incurs a further inconsequence by {\i
borrowing} the categories for the {\i
transcendental logic}, as so-called root concepts, from the subjective logic where they were assumed empirically.{\ul
}{\super
37} Since the Kantian philosophy admits the latter fact, it is hard to see why transcendental logic resorts to borrowing from such a science rather than directly helping itself from experience. By way of example, concepts are normally classified according to their {\i
clarity}, namely, as {\i
clear} and {\i
obscure}, {\i
distinct} and {\i
indistinct}, {\i
adequate} and {\i
inadequate}. We can also add to the list {\i
perfect} and {\i
redundant} and other suchlike superfluities. \u8211? Now as regards this classification according to {\i
clarity}, it immediately transpires that this standpoint and its connected distinctions are taken from {\i
psychological} and not {\i
logical} determinations. The so-called {\i
clear} concept is supposed to be one that suffices to differentiate one intended object from another. But this cannot be called a concept yet; it is nothing more than a {\i
subjective representation}. What an {\i
obscure} concept might be must be left to itself, for otherwise it would not be obscure but a distinct concept. \u8211? The {\i
distinct} concept is supposed to be one whose {\i
mark} can be given. But then it is, strictly speaking, the {\i
determinate concept}. The mark, when taken in its strict signification, is nothing else than the {\i
determinateness} or the simple {\i
content} of the concept in so far as the latter is distinguished from the form of universality. But the {\i
mark} does not quite have at first this more precise meaning; it is generally taken as only a determination by which a {\i
third party} takes note of a subject matter or of the concept; it can therefore be a very contingent circumstance. It expresses in {\ul
}general not so much the immanence and essentiality of a determination as its reference to an {\i
external} understanding. If the latter really is an understanding, then it has the concept before it and makes a note of it by nothing else than by {\i
what is in the concept itself}. In case the mark is different from the concept, then the mark is a {\i
sign} or some other determination that belongs to the {\i
representation} of the matter at hand, not to its concept. \u8211? What an {\i
indistinct} concept might be, this can be passed over as superfluous. But the {\i
adequate} concept is something higher; it properly denotes the agreement of the concept with reality, and this is not the concept as such but the {\i
idea}. 12.45 If the {\i
mark} of a distinct concept were really to be the determination itself of the concept, then logic would have trouble with the {\i
simple} concept which, according to another classification, is opposed to the {\i
composite}. For if for a simple concept a true, that is, immanent mark is given, then the concept is no longer regarded as simple; and if no mark is given for it, then the concept is not distinct. But the {\i
clear} concept now comes to the rescue. Unity, reality, and suchlike determinations, are supposed to be {\i
simple} concepts, perhaps because logicians were unable to come up with a determination for them and had to be content, therefore, with just a {\i
clear} concept of them, that is to say, with no concept at all. A {\i
definition}, that is, the statement of a concept, requires as a general rule the statement of genus and specific difference. It thus presents the concept, not as something simple, but in {\i
two} enumerable {\i
components}. Yet surely nobody will suppose that the concept is for that reason a {\i
composite}. \u8211? There is an allusion in the mention of the simple concept to {\i
abstract simplicity}, to a unity that does not entail difference and determinateness \u8211? a unity, therefore, that does not pertain to the concept. In As Much as an object is present in representation, especially in memory, or is also an abstract thought determination, it can be quite simple. Even the object that is richest in content, as for example spirit, nature, world, even God, when non-conceptually apprehended in a simple representation of the equally simple expression: spirit, nature, world, God, is of course something simple at which consciousness can stop short without proceeding to extract the proper determination or a defining mark. But the objects of consciousness ought not to remain so simple, ought not to remain representations or abstract thought determinations, but should rather become {\i
conceptualized}, that is, their simplicity should be determined together with their inner difference. \u8211? A {\i
composite} concept, however, is but the equivalent of a wooden iron. We can of course have the concept of a composition; but a composite concept would be something worse than {\i
materialism}, which assumes only the {\i
substance} of the {\i
soul} to be {\ul
}a composite, yet takes {\i
thought} to be {\i
simple}. The uneducated reflection first stumbles upon the notion of composition because it is the most completely {\i
external} connection, the worst form in which things can be considered; even the lowest of natures must be an {\i
inner} unity. That, to top it off, this form of untruest existence would be extended to the \u8220?I,\u8221? to the concept, is more than one should have expected and it must be regarded as an inept form of barbarism. Concepts are further divided into {\i
contrary} and {\i
contradictory}, a principal division. \u8211? If the task of a treatise on the concept were to give 12.46all the {\i
determinate} concepts that there are, then we would have to cite all possible determinations \u8211? for {\i
all} determinations are concepts, hence determinate concepts \u8211? and we would have to list as species of concepts all the categories of {\i
being} as well as all the determinations of {\i
essence}. And this is what is done in the textbooks on logic, where we are told \u8211? in {\i
greater} or {\i
lesser} detail, according to the whim of the author \u8211? that there are {\i
affirmative}, {\i
negative}, {\i
identical}, {\i
conditional}, {\i
necessary} concepts. But these determinations have already been left behind by {\i
the nature of the concept itself} and are therefore misplaced when applied to the concept itself, thus admitting only the kind of superficial nominal definitions that are of no interest here. \u8211? The underlying basis of the distinction between {\i
contrary} and {\i
contradictory} concepts with which we are specifically dealing here is the reflective determination of {\i
diversity} and {\i
opposition}. They are viewed as two particular {\i
species}, each fixed for itself and indifferent towards the other, without any thought being given to the dialectic and the inner nothingness of these differences, as if that which is {\i
contrary} would not equally have to be determined as {\i
contradictory}. The nature and the transition essential to the forms of reflection which they express have been considered in their proper place.{\ul
}{\super
38} In the concept, identity has developed into universality, difference into particularity, opposition (which returns to the ground) into singularity. In these forms, those determinations of reflection are present as they are in their concept. The universal has proved itself to be not only the identical, but at the same time the diverse or {\i
contrary} as against the particular and the singular, and then also to be opposed to them, or {\i
contradictory}; but in this opposition it is identical with them, and it is their true ground in which they are sublated. The same applies to particularity and singularity, which are likewise the totality of the determinations of reflection. Concepts are further divided into {\i
subordinate} and {\i
coordinate} \u8211? a distinction that comes closer to a determination of the concept, namely that of {\ul
}the relation of universality and particularity, in the context of which we did mention them albeit incidentally.{\ul
}{\super
39} But it is customary to consider them like the rest as rigidly fixed relations, and then to produce several otiose propositions regarding them. The most long-winded disquisition in this regard has to do again with the connection of contrariety and contradiction with subordination and coordination. Since {\i
judgment} is {\i
the connection of determinate concepts}, the true relation will have to come first into view only with reference to it. That fashion of {\i
comparing} these determinations without a thought to their dialectic and to the continuing alteration of their determination, or rather to the conjunction in them of opposite determinations, makes the whole 12.47disquisition of what may or may not be {\i
consonant} in them \u8211? as if this consonance or dissonance were something separate by itself and permanent \u8211? into an otiose exercise void of content. \u8211? The great Euler, infinitely fertile and sharp of mind in detecting and arranging the deep relations of algebraic quantities,{\ul
}{\super
40} the dry, prosaic Lambert in particular,{\ul
}{\super
41} and others, have attempted to construct a {\i
notation} for this class of relations between determinations of the concept based on lines, figures, and the like, the general intention being to elevate \u8211? or in fact rather to debase \u8211? the logical modes of relation to the status of a {\i
calculus}. One need only compare the nature of a sign with what the sign ought to indicate immediately to see that even the project of a logical notation is unworkable. The determinations of the concept, universality, particularity, and singularity, certainly are, like lines or the letters of algebra, {\i
diverse}; and they are also {\i
opposed} and allow, therefore, the signs of {\i
plus} and {\i
minus}. But they themselves and especially their connections, even if we stop short at just {\i
subsumption} and {\i
inherence}, are in their essential nature entirely different from algebraic letters, from lines and their connections, from the equality and diversity of magnitudes, the {\b
plus} and {\b
minus}, or the superimposition of lines, or the joining of them in angles and the resulting disposition of space that they enclose. It is characteristic of objects of this kind, as contrasted with the determinations of the concept, that they are mutually {\i
external}, that they have a {\i
fixed} determination. Now when concepts are made to conform to such signs, they cease to be concepts. Their determinations are not inert things, like numbers and lines whose connections lie outside them; they {\ul
}are living movements; the distinguished determinateness of the one side is immediately also internal to the other side; what would be a complete contradiction for numbers and lines is essential to the nature of the concept. \u8211? The higher mathematics, which also proceeds to the infinite and allows itself contradictions, can no longer employ its customary signs for representing such determinations. In order to indicate the still conceptually uncomprehended representation of the {\i
infinite approximation} of two ordinates, or when it equates a curve to an infinite number of infinitely small straight lines, all it does is to design two straight lines {\i
outside each other} or to draw straight lines inside but still {\i
distinct} from a curve; for the infinite, which is the point at issue here, higher mathematics falls back on {\i
pictorial representation}. What first led to this wayward attempt is above all the {\i
quantitative} relation in which {\i
universality}, {\i
particularity}, and {\i
singularity} are supposed to stand to one another: universal means, {\i
more extensive} than 12.48particular and singular; and particular, {\i
more extensive} than singular. The concept is {\i
concrete} and the {\i
richest in determination}, because it is the ground and the {\i
totality} of the previous determinations, of the categories of being and the determinations of reflection; these, therefore, are certain also to come up in it. But its nature is totally misunderstood if such determinations are retained in it in their former abstraction \u8211? if the {\i
wider extent} of the universal is understood to mean that the universal is a {\i
more}, or a greater quantum, than the particular and the singular. As absolute ground, it is the {\i
possibility} of {\i
quantity}, but no less so of {\i
quality}, that is, its determinations are no less qualitatively distinct; therefore they are already viewed in contravention to their truth when they are posited in the form of quantity alone. So, too, a reflective determination is a {\i
relative}, something in which the opposite shines reflectively; unlike a quantum, its relation is not external. But the concept is more than all this; its determinations are determinate {\i
concepts}, themselves essentially the {\i
totality} of all determinations. It is, therefore, entirely inappropriate, in order to grasp such an inner totality, to want to apply numerical and spatial relations in which the terms fall apart; such relations are rather the last and the worst medium that could be used. Natural relations, as for instance \u8220?magnetism\u8221? or \u8220?color tonality\u8221? would make infinitely higher and truer symbols for the purpose. Since the human being has in language a means of designation that is appropriate to reason, it is otiose to look for a less perfect means of representation to bother oneself with. It is essentially only spirit that can grasp the concept as concept, for the latter is not just the property of spirit but its pure self. It is futile to want to fix it by means of spatial figures and algebraic signs for the sake {\ul
}of the {\i
outer eye} and a {\i
non-conceptual}, {\i
mechanical} {\i
manipulation}, such as a {\i
calculus}. Also anything else that might be supposed to serve as symbol, like the symbols for the nature of God, can at best elicit only intimations and echoes of the concept; if, however, one insists on employing such symbols for expressing and cognizing the concept, then it is not their {\i
external nature} which is fit for the task; the reverse relation applies, namely that what in the symbols is the echo of a higher determination is recognized to be such only by virtue of the concept, and it is only by {\i
shedding} the sensuous standbys that were supposed to express it that one comes closer to the concept. 12.49 {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
C. The singular\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ Singularity, as we have seen,{\ul
}{\super
42} is already posited through particularity; this is {\i
determinate universality} and hence self-referring determinateness, the {\i
determinate determinate}. 1. At first, therefore, singularity appears as {\i
the reflection} of the concept out of its determinateness {\i
into itself}. It is the concept's self-{\i
mediation} by virtue of which, since its {\i
otherness} has once more been made into an {\i
other}, it restores itself as self-equal, but in the determination of {\i
absolute negativity}. \u8211? The negative in the universal, by virtue of which this universal is a {\i
particular}, was earlier determined as {\i
a doubly reflective shine}.{\ul
}{\super
43} In so far as the reflective shining is {\i
inward}, the particular remains a universal; through the outward shining, it is a {\i
determinate particular}; the turning back of this side into the universal is twofold, {\i
either} by virtue of an {\i
abstraction} that lets the particular fall away and climbs to a {\i
higher} and the {\i
highest genus}, or by virtue of the {\i
singularity} to which the universality in the determinateness itself descends. \u8211? Here is where the false start is made that makes abstraction stray away from the way of the concept, abandoning the truth. Its higher and highest universal to which it rises is only a surface that becomes progressively more void of content; the singularity which it scorns is the depth in which the concept grasps itself and where it is posited as concept. {\i
Universality} and {\i
particularity} appeared, on the one hand, as moments of the {\i
becoming} of singularity. But it has already been shown that the two are in themselves the total concept; consequently, that in {\i
singularity} they do not pass over into an {\i
other} but that, on the contrary, what is posited in it is what they are in and for themselves.{\ul
}{\super
44} The {\i
universal} is {\i
for itself} because it is absolute mediation in itself, self-reference only as absolute negativity. It is an {\i
abstract} universal in as much as this sublating is an {\i
external} act and so {\ul
}a {\i
dropping off} of the determinateness. This negativity, therefore, attaches indeed to the abstract universal, but it remains {\i
outside} it, as a mere {\i
condition} of it; it is the abstraction itself that holds its universal {\i
opposite} it, and so the universal does not have singularity in itself and remains void of concept. \u8211? Life, spirit, God, as well as the pure concept, are for this reason beyond the grasp of abstraction, for abstraction keeps singularity away from its products, and singularity is the principle of individuality and personality. And so it comes to nothing but lifeless universalities, void of spirit, color, and content. But the unity of the concept is so indissoluble that these products of 12.50abstraction also, though they are supposed to drop singularity, are rather themselves {\i
singulars}. For in elevating the concrete to universality, abstraction grasps the universal as only a determinate universality, and this is precisely the singularity that presented itself as self-referring determinateness. Thus abstraction is a {\i
partitioning} of the concrete and an {\i
isolating} of its determinations; only {\i
singular} properties or moments are picked out by it, for its product must contain what it itself is. But the difference between this singularity of its products and the singularity of the concept is that in the former the singular and the universal differ from each other as {\i
content} and {\i
form} respectively, precisely because the content is not the absolute form, is not the concept itself, or this form is not the totality of form. \u8211? However, this closer consideration shows that the product of abstraction is itself the unity of the singular content and of abstract universality, therefore that it is something {\i
concrete}, the opposite of what it is supposed to be. The {\i
particular}, for the same reason that makes it only a determinate universal, is also a {\i
singular}, and conversely, because the singular is a determinate universal, it is equally a particular. If we stay at this abstract determinateness, then the concept has the three particular determinations of universal, particular, and singular, whereas earlier we gave only the universal and the particular as species of the particular. Because singularity is the turning of the concept as a negative back to itself, this turning back from abstraction, which in the turning is truly sublated, can itself be placed as an indifferent moment {\i
alongside} the others and be counted with them. If singularity is listed as one of the {\i
particular} determinations of the concept, then particularity is the {\i
totality} which embraces them all and, precisely as this totality, it is the concretion of the determinations or singularity itself. But it is a concrete also according to the previously mentioned side, as {\i
determinate universality}; and then it is the {\i
immediate} unity in which none of these moments is posited as distinct or as the determinant, and in this form it will constitute the {\i
middle term of the formal syllogism}. {\ul
}It follows that each of the determinations established in the preceding exposition of the concept has immediately dissolved itself and has lost itself in its other. Each distinction is confounded in the course of the very reflection that should isolate it and hold it fixed. Only a way of thinking that is {\i
merely representational}, for which abstraction has isolated them, is capable of holding the universal, the particular, and the singular rigidly apart. Then they can be counted; and for a further distinction this representation relies on one which is {\i
entirely external to being}, on their {\i
quantity}, and nowhere is such a distinction as inappropriate as here. \u8211? In singularity, the earlier true relation, the {\i
inseparability} of the determinations of the concept, is {\i
posited}; for as the negation of 12.51negation, singularity contains the opposition of those determinations and this opposition itself at its ground or the unity where the determinations have come together, each in the other. Because in this reflection universality is in and for itself, singularity is essentially the negativity of the determinations of the concept, but not merely as if it stood as a third something distinct from them, but because what is now {\i
posited} is that {\i
positedness} is {\i
being-in-and-for-itself}; that is, what is posited is that each of the distinct determinations is the {\i
totality}. The turning back of the determinate concept into itself means that its determination is to be {\i
in its determinateness} the {\i
whole} concept. 2. Singularity is not, however, only the turning back of the concept into itself, but the immediate loss of it. Through singularity, where it is {\i
internal to itself}, the concept becomes {\i
external to itself} and steps into actuality. {\i
Abstraction}, which is the {\i
soul} of singularity and so the self-reference of the negative, is, as we have seen,{\ul
}{\super
45} nothing external to the universal and the particular but is immanent in them, and these are concreted through it, they become a content, a singular. But, as this negativity, singularity is the determinate determinateness, {\i
differentiation} as such, and through this reflection of the difference into itself, the difference becomes fixed; the determining of the particular occurs only by virtue of singularity, for {\i
singularity} is that abstraction which, precisely as singularity, is now {\i
posited abstraction}. The singular, therefore, is as self-referring negativity the immediate identity of the negative with itself; it {\i
exists for itself}. Or it is the abstraction determining the concept as an {\i
immediate}, according to its ideal moment of {\i
being}. \u8211? Thus the singular is a {\i
one} which is qualitative, or a {\i
this}. In accordance with this qualitative character, it is, {\i
first}, the repulsion of itself {\i
from itself} by virtue of which many {\i
other} ones are presupposed; {\i
second}, it is now a negative reference with respect to these presupposed {\i
others}, and to this {\ul
}extent the singular is {\i
exclusive}. Universality, when referred to these singulars as indifferent ones \u8211? and it must be referred to them, for they are a moment of the concept of singularity \u8211? is only their {\i
commonality}. If by the universal one understands that which is {\i
common} to several singulars, the {\i
indifferent} subsistence of these singulars is then taken as the starting point, thus mixing in the immediacy of being into the determination of the concept. The lowest conception one can have of the universal as connected with the singular is this external relation that it has to the latter as a mere {\i
commonality}. The singular, which in the reflective sphere of concrete existence is as a {\i
this}, does not have the {\i
excluding} reference to the other 12.52that is characteristic of qualitative being-for-itself. A {\i
this} is a one {\i
reflected into itself}, without repulsion; or the repulsion is in this reflection one with abstraction, a reflective {\i
mediation} present in the {\i
this} that makes it a {\i
posited} immediacy {\i
pointed at} by someone external to it. The {\i
this is}; it is {\i
immediate}, it is a {\i
this}, however, only in so far as it is {\i
pointed at}. This \u8220?pointing at\u8221? is the reflective movement that takes hold of itself and posits the immediacy, but as something external to itself. \u8211? Now the singular surely is also a {\i
this}, as an immediate which is the result of mediation, but does not have this mediation outside it; it is itself repelling separation, {\i
posited abstraction}, yet is, precisely in its separation, a positive connection. This act of abstraction by the singular is, as the immanent reflection of difference, the first positing of the differences as {\i
self-subsisting}, reflected into themselves. They {\i
exist} immediately; but, further, this separating is reflection in general, the {\i
reflective shining of one in the other}; the differences thus stand in essential relation. They are, moreover, not singulars that just {\i
exist} next to each other; a plurality of this kind belongs to being; the {\i
singularity} that posits itself as determinate does not posit itself in an external difference but in a difference of the concept; singularity thus excludes the {\i
universal} from itself, but since this universal is a moment of it, it refers to it just as essentially. The concept, as this connection of its {\i
self-subsistent} determinations, has lost itself, for the concept itself is no longer the {\i
posited unity} of these determinations, and these no longer are {\i
moments}, the {\i
reflective shining} of the concept, but subsist rather in and for themselves. \u8211? As singularity, the concept returns in determinateness into itself, and therewith the determinate has itself become totality. The concept's turning back into itself is thus the absolute, originative {\i
partition of itself}, that is, as singularity it is posited as {\i
judgment}.{\ul
