{\ql
a. The positive judgment\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ 1. The subject and predicate, as we have just said,{\ul
}{\super
55} are names at first that receive their actual determination only as the judgment runs its course. However, as sides of the judgment \u8211? the judgment being the {\i
posited} determinate concept \u8211? they have the determination of moments of the concept, but, on account of their immediacy, this determination is as yet quite {\i
simple}, {\ul
}still not enriched by mediation and also still caught up in the abstract opposition of {\i
abstract singularity} and {\i
abstract universality}. \u8211? The predicate, to speak of it first, is the {\i
abstract} universal; this abstract is conditioned by mediation, by the sublation of singularity and particularity, but so far such a mediation is here only a {\i
presupposition}. In the sphere of the concept there can be no other immediacy than the one that contains mediation {\i
in and for itself} and has arisen only through its sublation; this is the {\i
immediacy of the universal}. Thus {\i
qualitative being} also is {\i
in its concept} a universal; as {\i
being}, however, the immediacy is not yet {\i
posited} as such; it is only as {\i
universality} that immediacy is the concept determination in which it is {\i
posited} that negativity essentially belongs to it. This connection is given in the judgment in which universality is the predicate of a subject. \u8211? Similarly the subject is an {\i
abstract} singular, or the {\i
immediate} which is supposed {\i
to be such} and therefore the singular as a {\i
something} in general. The subject constitutes, therefore, the abstract side of the judgment, the side in it according to which the concept has passed over into {\i
externality}. \u8211? As these two 12.61concept determinations are determined, so is also their connection, the \u8220?is\u8221? or the copula; it too can have no other meaning than that of an immediate, abstract {\i
being}. It is because of this connection, which still does not contain any mediation or negation, that this judgment is called \u8220?positive.\u8221? 2. The first pure expression of the positive judgment is, therefore, the proposition: {\i
the singular is universal}. This expression must not be put in the form of \u8220?A is B,\u8221? for A and B are totally formless and hence meaningless names, whereas judgment in general, and therefore already the judgment of existence, has determinations of the concept for its extremes. \u8220?A is B\u8221? can stand just as well for any mere {\i
proposition} as for a {\i
judgment}. But what is asserted in every judgment, even one more richly determined in form, is the proposition that has this determined content, namely, \u8220?the singular is universal,\u8221? for every judgment is in principle also an abstract judgment. (Regarding the negative judgment, how far it likewise comes under this expression, of this we shall speak presently.){\ul
}{\super
56} \u8211? However, if no thought is given to the fact that with every judgment, the positive at least, the assertion is made that the singular is universal, this happens either because {\i
no attention} is given to the {\i
determinate form} differentiating subject and object \u8211? for it is taken for granted that the judgment is nothing but the connecting of {\i
two} concepts \u8211? or also likely because the further {\i
content} of the judgment, \u8220?Gaius is learned,\u8221? or \u8220?the rose is red,\u8221? comes drifting in {\ul
}before the mind, and the latter, busy with the picture of Gaius etc., fails to reflect on the form \u8211? even though, at least, such a content as \u8220?Gaius,\u8221? which is the one that usually has to be dragged in as an example, is much less interesting than the form, and is indeed chosen because it is uninteresting, not to divert attention from the form to itself. The objective meaning of the proposition stating that {\i
the singular is universal} conveys, as already incidentally noted,{\ul
}{\super
57} both the perishableness of singular things and their positive subsistence in the concept in general. The concept itself is imperishable, but that which emerges from it in its division is subjected to alteration and to falling back into its {\i
universal} nature. But the universal, conversely, gives itself a {\i
determinate existence}. Just as essence goes out into {\i
reflective shine} in its determinations; or ground into concrete existence in {\i
appearance}; and substance into manifestation in its accidents, so does the universal {\i
resolve} itself into the singular; judgment is this {\i
resolution} of the universal, the {\i
development} of the negativity which, implicitly, it already is. \u8211? This last circumstance is expressed by the converse proposition, \u8220?the universal is singular,\u8221? which is also equally spoken in the positive judgment. The subject, the {\i
immediate singular} at first, is in the judgment itself referred to its {\i
other}, namely the universal; it is thereby posited as the {\i
concrete} \u8211? according to the category of being, as a something 12.62{\i
of many qualities}; or as the concrete of reflection, {\i
a thing of manifold properties}, an {\i
actual of manifold possibilities}, a {\i
substance} of precisely such {\i
accidents}. Because these manifolds here belong to the subject of the judgment, the something, the thing, etc., is in its qualities, properties, or accidents, reflected into itself, or {\i
continues} across them, maintaining itself in them and them in itself. Positedness or determinateness belongs to being which is in and for itself. The subject is therefore inherently the {\i
universal}. \u8211? The predicate, on the contrary, being this {\i
universality} not as real or concrete, but as {\i
abstract}, is in contrast to the subject the {\i
determinateness}; it contains only {\i
one moment} of the subject's totality to the exclusion of the others. On account of this negativity, which as an extreme of the judgment is at the same time self-referring, the predicate is an {\i
abstract singular}. \u8211? For instance, in the proposition, \u8220?the rose is fragrant,\u8221? the predicate expresses only {\i
one} of the {\i
many} properties of the rose; it isolates it, whereas in the subject the property is joined with the others; likewise in the dissolution of the thing, the manifold properties that inhere in it {\i
become isolated} in acquiring self-subsistence as {\i
materials}. From this side, then, the proposition of the judgment says: {\i
the universal is singular}. {\ul
}By juxtaposing this {\i
reciprocal determination} of subject and predicate in the judgment, we thus obtain this twofold result. (1) Immediately, the subject is indeed an existent or the singular, while the predicate is the universal. But because the judgment {\i
connects} the two, and the subject is determined as universal by the predicate, the subject is then the universal. (2) The predicate is determined in the subject, for it is not a determination {\i
in general} but the determination rather {\i
of the subject}. \u8220?The rose is fragrant.\u8221? This fragrance is not some indeterminate fragrance or other, but the fragrance of the rose. The predicate is therefore {\i
a singular}. \u8211? Now since the subject and predicate stand related in the judgment, they should retain the opposition of concept determinations; likewise, in the {\i
reciprocity} of causality, before the latter attains its truth, the two sides are still supposed to remain self-subsistent and mutually opposed as against the equality of their determination. Therefore, when the subject is determined as universal, the predicate should not also be taken in its determination of universality, for then we would have no judgment; it must rather be taken only in its determination of singularity. And if the subject is determined as singular, then the predicate is to be taken as universal. \u8211? If we reflect on the mere identity above, then we have these two identical propositions, \u8220?the singular is singular,\u8221? \u8220?the universal is universal,\u8221? in which the sides of the judgment would have completely fallen apart; only the self-reference of each is expressed 12.63while the reference connecting them to each other is dissolved; and thus the judgment would be sublated. \u8211? Of the two propositions we drew, the first, \u8220?the universal is singular,\u8221? expresses the judgment according to its {\i
content}, as an isolated determination in the predicate and as the totality of determinations in the subject. The other, \u8220?the singular is universal,\u8221? expresses it according to {\i
form} as immediately given through the judgment itself. \u8211? In the immediate positive judgment, the extremes are still simple: form and content are therefore still united. Or, in other words, it does not consist of two propositions; the twofold connection that it yielded immediately constitutes the {\i
one} positive judgment. For its extremes are (a) the self-subsisting abstract determinations of judgment, and (b) each side of the determination is determined through the other by virtue of the copula connecting them. {\i
Implicitly}, however, the difference of form and content is for this reason present in it, as we have seen; and indeed, what the first proposition contains, that the singular is universal, belongs to form, for the proposition expresses the {\i
immediate determinateness} of the judgment. The relation, on the contrary, which the other proposition expresses, that {\i
the universal is singular} or that the subject is determined as universal whereas the predicate is determined as particular or singular, concerns the {\i
content}; {\ul
}for its determinations are only the result of an immanent reflection by virtue of which the immediate determinacies of the judgment are sublated and the form is thereby converted into an identity that has withdrawn into itself and persists over against the distinction of form: it converts itself into content. 3. If now the two propositions, the one of form and the other of content, \~ (Subject) (Predicate) The singular is universal The universal is singular, \~ were to be united because they are contained in the {\i
one} positive judgment, so that both, the subject as well as the predicate, were determined as the unity of singularity and universality, then both the subject and predicate would be the {\i
particular}, and this must be recognized as {\i
implicitly} their inner determination. However, this combination would be arrived at only through an external reflection; moreover, the proposition that results from it, \u8220?the particular is the particular,\u8221? would no longer be a judgment but an empty identical proposition as were the two propositions already found in the positive judgment, \u8220?the singular is singular,\u8221? and \u8220?the universal is universal.\u8221? \u8211? Singularity and universality cannot yet be united into particularity, because in the positive judgment they are still posited as {\i
immediate}. \u8211? Or again, the judgment must still be distinguished according to its form and its content, because the subject and predicate are themselves still distinguished as immediacy and mediated, or because the judgment, according to its connection, is both 12.64the self-subsistence of the connected terms and their reciprocal determination or mediation. {\i
In first place}, then, the meaning of the judgment when considered according to its form is that {\i
the singular is universal}. But in fact such an {\i
immediate singular} is {\i
definitely not} universal; its predicate is of wider extension, does not correspond to it. The subject is a {\i
being existing immediately for itself}, and hence the {\i
opposite} of that abstraction, of that universality posited through mediation that was supposed to be predicated of it. {\i
In second place}, if the judgment is considered according to its {\i
content}, or as the proposition, \u8220?the universal is singular,\u8221? then the subject is a universe of qualities, an infinitely determined concrete universe, and since its determinacies are as yet qualitites, properties, or accidents, its totality is the {\i
bad infinite plurality} of them. Such a subject, therefore, is not at all the one {\i
single} property that its predicate declares. Consequently, both propositions must be {\i
united}, and the positive judgment must be posited as {\i
negative} instead. {\ul
}{\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
b. The negative judgment\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ 1. We spoke earlier of the common notion that whether the content of a judgment is true or false depends solely on the content itself, since logical truth concerns only the form and its only requirement is that such content shall not contradict itself.{\ul
}{\super
58} Nothing else is reckoned as the form of judgment except that the latter is a connection of {\i
two} concepts. But we have seen that these two concepts are not just the relationless determination of a {\i
sum}, but that they relate to each other as {\i
singular} and {\i
universal}.{\ul
}{\super
59} These are the determinations that constitute the truly logical {\i
content} and also, abstracted in that way, the content of the positive judgment; whatever {\i
other content} is in a judgment (\u8220?the sun is round,\u8221? \u8220?Cicero was a great Roman orator,\u8221? \u8220?it is daytime now,\u8221? etc.) does not concern the judgment as such; the judgment only says that the {\i
subject} is {\i
predicate}, or, since these are only names, that {\i
the singular is universal and vice versa}. \u8211? It is because of this {\i
purely logical content} that the positive judgment is {\i
not true} but has its truth in the negative judgment. \u8211? In judgment, so it is required, the content simply ought not to contradict itself; but it does contradict itself in the positive judgment, as we have just seen. \u8211? At any rate, it makes absolutely no difference if that logical content is called form, and by content is understood only the remaining empirical filling, for even then the form would not contain a mere empty identity outside which the content determination would then lie. 12.65The positive judgment has in fact no truth through its form as positive judgment; whoever calls {\i
truth} the {\i
correctness} of an {\i
intuition} or a {\i
perception}, the agreement of {\i
representation} with the subject matter, has for a minimum no expression left for that which is the subject matter and the aim of philosophy. We should at least say of these that they are the truth of reason, and it will surely be granted that such judgments as \u8220?Cicero was a great orator,\u8221? that \u8220?it is daytime now,\u8221? are definitely not truths of reason. But they are not such truths, not because they have an empirical content as it were contingently, but because they are only positive judgments that can have, and ought to have, no other content than an immediate singular and an abstract determinateness. The positive judgment first attains its truth in the negative judgment: {\i
the singular is not} abstractly {\i
universal} \u8211? {\i
but rather}, the predicate of the singular, because it is such a predicate, or because, if considered by itself without reference to the subject, it is an {\i
abstract} universal, is for that very reason itself something determinate; {\i
from the start}, therefore, the {\i
singular} {\ul
}is a {\i
particular}. Furthermore, with respect to the other proposition that the positive judgment contains, the meaning of the negative judgment is that the {\i
universal} is not abstractly {\i
singular} {\i
but} that this predicate, \u8220?singular,\u8221? by the very fact that it is a predicate, or because it refers to a universal subject, is more than just mere singularity, and the {\i
universal}, accordingly, is {\i
from the start} equally a {\i
particular}. \u8211? Since this universal, as subject, is itself in the judgment determination of singularity, the two propositions both reduce to one: \u8220?the singular is a particular.\u8221? We may remark that (a) the {\i
particularity} that here comes to the predicate has already come up for consideration before;{\ul
}{\super
60} here, however, it is not posited by external reflection but has arisen rather as mediated by the negative connection indicated in the judgment. (b) This determination results here only for the predicate. In the {\i
immediate} judgment, the judgment of existence, the subject is the underlying basis; the {\i
determination} seems at first, therefore, {\i
to occur in the predicate}. But in fact this first negation cannot as yet be a determination, or cannot truly be the {\i
positing of the singular}, for such a positing is only a {\i
second moment}, the negative of the negative. {\i
The singular is a particular}: this is the {\i
positive} expression of the negative judgment. This expression, therefore, is not the positive judgment itself, for the latter, because of its immediacy, has an abstraction for its extremes, while the particular, precisely through the positing of the judgment connection, results as the first {\i
mediated} determination. \u8211? But this determination is not 12.66to be taken only as a moment of the extremes, but also as the {\i
determination} of the {\i
connection}, as it truly is from the start; in other words, the judgment is also to be considered as {\i
negative}. This transition is founded on the relation of the extremes and on their connection in the judgment as such. The positive judgment is the connection of the singular and the universal which are such {\i
immediately} and each, therefore, is {\i
not} at the same time what the other is. The connection is therefore just as essentially {\i
separation}, or {\i
negative}; for this reason the positive judgment was to be posited as negative. There was no need, therefore, for the logicians to make such a fuss about the {\i
not} of the negative judgment being attached to the {\i
copula}. In the judgment, the {\i
determination} of the extremes is equally a {\i
determinate connection}. The judgment determination, or the extreme, is not the purely qualitative one of {\i
immediate} being that only stands over against an {\i
other} {\i
outside} it. Nor is it the determination of reflection, which, in accordance with its general form, behaves positively and negatively, posited in either case as exclusive, only {\i
implicitly} identical {\ul
}with the other. The judgment determination, as the determination of the concept, is a universal within, posited as {\i
extending} {\i
continuously} in its other. Conversely, the judgment {\i
connection} is the same determination as the extremes have; for it is precisely this universality and continuous extension of each into the other; in so far as these are distinguished, the connection also has negativity in it. The just stated transition from the form of the {\i
connection} to the form of the {\i
determination} has the {\i
immediate consequence} that the {\i
not} of the copula must just as equally be attached to the predicate and that the latter must be determined as the {\i
not-universal}. But, through a no less immediate consequence, the not-universal is the {\i
particular}. \u8211? If the focus is on the {\i
negative} according to the totally abstract determination of immediate {\i
non-being}, then the predicate is the {\i
totally indeterminate} not-universal. This is the determination which is normally treated in logic in connection with the {\i
contradictory} concepts, and the further point is made \u8211? a point considered important \u8211? that in the {\i
negative} of a concept one should only focus on the negative, taking it as the mere {\i
indeterminate} extent of the {\i
other} of the positive concept. Thus the mere {\i
not-white} would be just as much red, yellow, blue, etc. as black. {\i
White}, however, is an {\i
unconceptualized} determination of intuition; the {\i
not} of white is equally, then, unconceptualized {\i
not-being}, the abstraction that came in for consideration at the very beginning of the Logic where {\i
becoming} was recognized to be its closest truth. To use as an example, in the consideration of judgment determinations, an unconceptualized content of this sort, drawn from intuition and the imagination, 12.67and to take the determinations of {\i
being}, and of {\i
reflection}, as such judgment determinations, is the same {\i
uncritical} practice as when Kant applies the concepts of the understanding to the infinite idea of reason, the so-called {\i
thing-in-itself};{\ul
}{\super
61} the {\i
concept}, to which the {\i
judgment} proceeding from it also belongs, is the true {\i
thing-in-itself} or the {\i
rational}; those other determinations belong to {\i
being} and {\i
essence}; they are not yet forms developed into the shape where they are in their truth, {\i
in the concept}. \u8211? If we stop at white, red, as representations {\i
of the senses}, then we call concept what is only a determination of pictorial representation. This is common practice. But then, surely, the not-white, the not-red, will be nothing positive, just as the not-triangular will be something totally indeterminate, for a determination based as such on number and quantum is essentially {\i
something indifferent}, {\i
void of concept}. Yet, like {\i
non-being} itself, such a sensuous content ought to be {\i
conceptualized}; ought to shed that indifference and abstract immediacy {\ul
}with which it is affected in the blind immobility of pictorial representation. Already in the sphere of immediate existence, the {\i
non-being} which is otherwise void of thought becomes {\i
limit}, and by virtue of this limit the {\i
something} {\i
refers} to an {\i
other} despite itself. In the sphere of reflection, on the other hand, it is the {\i
negative} that {\i
refers essentially} to a {\i
positive}, and is thereby {\i
determined}; a negative is no longer that {\i
indeterminate non-being}, for it is posited to be only to the extent that the positive stands over against it, and as third comes their {\i
ground}; the negative is thus held circumscribed in a sphere within which the {\i
non}-being of one is something {\i
determinate}. \u8211? But it is all the more in the absolutely fluid continuity of the concept that the {\i
not} is immediately a positive, and the {\i
negation} is not just determinateness but is taken up into universality and is posited as identical with it. The non-universal is therefore directly the {\i
particular}. 2. Since negation has to do with the connection of judgment, and we are considering the {\i
negative judgment} still as such, the latter is {\i
in the first instance still a judgment}; we thus have the relation of subject and predicate, or of singularity and universality, and their connection, {\i
the form of the judgment}. The subject, as the immediate underlying basis, remains untouched by the negation; it retains, therefore, its determination of having a predicate, or its reference to the universality. Consequently, what is negated in the predicate is not the universality as such, but the abstraction or the determinateness of the predicate that appeared as {\i
content} in contrast to that universality. \u8211? The negative judgment is not, therefore, total negation; the universal sphere which contains the predicate remains standing; the connection of subject and the predicate is therefore still essentially {\i
positive}; the yet remaining {\i
determination} of the 12.68predicate is no less {\i
connection}. \u8211? When it is said that, for instance, the rose is {\i
not} red, only the {\i
determinateness} of the predicate is thereby denied and thus separated from the universality which equally attaches to it; the universal sphere, {\i
color}, is retained; if the rose is not red, it is nonetheless assumed that it has a color, though another color. From the side of this universal sphere, the judgment is still positive. \u8220?{\i
The singular is a particular}.\u8221? This positive form of the negative judgment immediately expresses that the particular contains universality. In addition, it also expresses that the predicate is not just a universal but also one which is still determinate. The negative form contains the same, for although the rose, for instance, is not red, it is supposed, nevertheless, not only still to retain the universal sphere of color as predicate, but to have {\i
some other determinate color} as well; the {\i
singularity} of determinateness of the rose is therefore only sublated; and not only is the universal sphere left {\ul
}standing but determinateness too is retained, although transformed into an {\i
indeterminate} determinateness, a universal determinateness, that is to say, into particularity. 3. The {\i
particularity} that has resulted as the positive determination of the negative judgment is the term mediating singularity and universality; so the negative judgment is now that which provides in general the mediation for the third step, that of {\i
the reflection of the judgment of existence into itself}. This judgment is according to its objective meaning only the moment of the alteration of accidents, or, in the sphere of existence, of the singularized properties of the concrete. Through this alteration, the full determinateness of the predicate, or the concrete, emerges as posited. \u8220?The singular is particular\u8221? is what the positive expression of the negative judgment says. But the singular is also {\i
not} particular, for particularity is of wider extension than singularity; it is a predicate, therefore, that does not correspond to the subject, one in which the latter, therefore, does not as yet have its truth. \u8220?{\i
The singular is only a singular}\u8221?: this is a negativity that refers to nothing else, be it positive or negative, except itself. \u8211? The rose is not a thing of {\i
some color or other}, but one that only has the one determinate color which is the rose-color. The singular is not an indeterminate determinate but the determinate determinate. This negation of the negative judgment appears, when one starts from its positive form, to be again a {\i
first} negation. But this is not what it is. The negative judgment is again, in and for itself, already the second negation or the negation of negation, and this, what it is in and for itself, is to be posited. To wit: the judgment {\i
negates the determinateness} of the predicate of the positive judgment, its {\i
abstract} universality, or, considered as content, the singular quality that it possesses of the subject. But the negation of the determinateness is already the second negation, hence the infinite turning back of the singularity into itself. With this, therefore, the {\i
restoration} 12.69of the concrete totality of the subject has taken place, or rather, the subject is now for the first time {\i
posited} as singular, for through the negation and the sublation of that negation it is mediated with itself. The predicate, for its part, has thereby passed over from the first universality to absolute determinateness and made itself equal to the subject. Thus the judgment says: \u8220?the singular is singular.\u8221? \u8211? From the other side, since the subject was equally to be taken as {\i
a universal}, and since in the negative judgment the predicate, which as against that subject is the singular, {\i
expanded into particularity}; moreover, since now the negation of this determinateness is equally the {\i
purification} of the universality contained in the predicate, this judgment also says: \u8220?the universal is the universal.\u8221? {\ul
}In these two judgments, which were earlier obtained through external reflection,{\ul
}{\super
62} the predicate is already expressed in its positivity. But the negation of the negative judgment must itself first appear in the form of a negative judgment. It has just been shown that there still remained in this judgment a {\i
positive connection} of subject and predicate as well the {\i
universal sphere} of the latter.{\ul
}{\super
63} From this side, the negative judgment thus contains a universality which is more purified of limitation than was contained by the positive judgment and is for this reason all the more to be negated of the subject as a singular. In this manner, the {\i
whole extent} of the predicate is negated, and there is no longer any positive connection between it and the subject. This is the {\i
infinite judgment}. {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
c. The infinite judgment\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ The negative judgment is as little of a true judgment as the positive. But the infinite judgment which is supposed to be its truth is, according to its negative expression, the {\i
negative infinite}, a judgment in which even the form of judgment is sublated. \u8211? But this is a {\i
nonsensical judgment}. It ought to be {\i
a judgment}, and hence contains a connection of subject and predicate; but any such connection ought not {\i
at the same time to be} there. \u8211? The name of the infinite judgment does indeed occur in the common textbooks of logic, but without any clarification as to its meaning. \u8211? Examples of negatively infinite judgments are easy to come by. It is a matter of picking determinations, one of which does not contain not just the determinateness of the other but its universal sphere as well, and of combining them negatively as subject and predicate, as when we say, for example, that spirit is not red, yellow, etc., is not acid, 12.70not alkali, etc., or that the rose is not an elephant, the understanding is not a table, and the like. \u8211? These judgments are {\i
correct} or {\i
true}, as it is said, and yet, any such truth notwithstanding, nonsensical and fatuous. \u8211? Or, more to the point, they are {\i
not judgments at all}. \u8211? A more realistic example of the infinite judgment is the {\i
evil} action. In {\i
civil litigation}, when a thing is negated as the property of another party, it is still conceded that the same thing would indeed belong to that party if the latter had a right to it. It is only under the title of right that the possession of it is challenged; in the negative judgment, therefore, the universal sphere, \u8220?right,\u8221? is still acknowledged and maintained. But {\i
crime} is the {\i
infinite judgment} that negates, not only the {\i
particular} right, but the universal sphere, the {\i
right as right}. It has {\i
correctness}, in the sense that it {\ul
}is an effective action, but since it stands in a thoroughly negative fashion with respect to the morality that constitutes its sphere, it is nonsensical. The {\i
positive} element of the infinite judgment, the negation of the negation, is the {\i
reflection of singularity} into itself by virtue of which the singularity is first posited as the determinate determinate. \u8220?The singular is singular\u8221? is what the infinite judgment said according to that reflection. In the judgment of existence, the subject is as the {\i
immediate singular}, hence more of just a {\i
something} in general. Through the mediation of the negative and infinite judgment, it is {\i
posited} as singular for the first time. The singular is thus {\i
posited} as {\i
expanding} into {\i
its predicate}, which is identical with it; to the same extent, therefore, universality is also no longer anything {\i
immediate} but a {\i
summing} of distincts. The positively infinite judgment equally says, \u8220?the universal is universal,\u8221? and in this the universal is posited also as a turning back into itself. Now through the reflection of the judgment determinations into themselves, the judgment has sublated itself; in the negatively infinite judgment, the difference is, so to speak, too great for it still to remain a judgment; subject and predicate have no positive connection whatsoever to each other; in the positively infinite judgment, on the contrary, only identity is present, and because of this total lack of difference there is no longer a judgment. More precisely, it is the {\i
judgment of existence} that has sublated itself and, consequently, there is {\i
posited} what the {\i
copula} of the judgment contains, namely that in its identity the qualitative extremes are sublated. But since this unity is the concept, it is immediately torn apart and is a {\i
judgment}, but one whose terms are no longer immediately determined but are reflected into themselves. {\i
The judgment of existence} has passed over into the {\i
judgment of reflection}. 12.71 {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
B. The judgment of reflection\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ In the judgment that has now arisen, the subject is a singular as such; and similarly, the universal is no longer an {\i
abstract} universality, or a {\i
singular property}, but is posited as a universal that has collected itself together into a unity through the connection of different terms, or, regarded from the standpoint of the content of diverse determinations in general, as the {\i
coalescing} of manifold properties and concrete existences. \u8211? If examples of predicates of judgments of reflection are to be given, they must be of another kind than for the judgments of existence. It is only in the judgment of reflection that we first have a {\i
determinate content} strictly speaking, that is, a content as such; for the content is the form determination reflected into {\ul
}identity as distinct from the form in so far as this is a distinct determinateness \u8211? as it still is as judgment. In the judgment of existence, the content is merely an immediate, or abstract, indeterminate content. \u8211? These may therefore serve as examples of judgments of reflection: the human being is {\i
mortal}, things are {\i
perishable}, this thing is {\i
useful}, {\i
harmful}; {\i
hardness}, {\i
elasticity} of bodies, {\i
happiness}, etc., are predicates of this particular kind. They express an essentiality which is however a {\i
relational} determination, or a {\i
comprehensive} universality. This {\i
universality}, which will further determine itself in the movement of the judgment of reflection, is still distinct from the {\i
universality of the concept} as such; although it is no longer the abstract universality of the qualitative judgment, it still has a connection to the immediate from which it proceeds and has the latter at the basis of its negativity. \u8211? The concept determines immediate existence, in the first instance, to {\i
relational determinations} that extend across the diverse multiplicity of concrete existence, so that the true universal is indeed the inner essence of that multiplicity, but is such {\i
in the sphere of appearance}, and this {\i
relative nature} or even its {\i
mark} is not as yet the element of the multiplicity that exists in and for itself. It may seem fitting to define the judgment of reflection as a judgment of {\i
quantity}, just as the judgment of existence was defined also as {\i
qualitative} judgment. But just as the {\i
immediacy} in the latter was not just {\i
there}, but was an immediacy which is also essentially mediated and {\i
abstract}, so, here also, that same immediacy which is now sublated is not just sublated 12.72quality, and therefore not merely {\i
quantity}; on the contrary, just as quality is the most external immediacy, so is quantity, in the same way, the {\i
most external determination} belonging to mediation. Also to be noted concerning the {\i
determination} as it appears in the movement of the judgment of reflection is that, in the judgment of immediate existence, the movement of the determination showed itself in the {\i
predicate}, for this kind of judgment was in the determination of immediacy and its subject, therefore, appeared as the underlying basis. For a similar reason, in the judgment of reflection the onward movement of determination runs its course {\i
in the subject}, for this judgment has the {\i
reflected in-itselfness} for its determination. Hence the essential is here the {\i
universal} or the predicate, and it is the latter, therefore, that constitutes {\i
the basis} against which the subject is to be measured and determined accordingly. \u8211? Yet the predicate also receives a further determination through the further development of the form of the subject, but it receives it {\i
indirectly}, whereas the progression of the subject manifests itself, for the reason just given, as a {\i
direct} advance in determination. {\ul
}As regards the objective signification of the judgment, the singular enters into existence by virtue of its universality, but it does so in an essential determination which is relational, in an essentiality that maintains itself across the manifold of appearance; the subject {\i
is supposed} to be that which is determined in and for itself; this is the determinateness which it has in its predicate. The singular, for its part, is reflected into this predicate which is its universal essence; to this extent, the subject is a concrete existence and a phenomenal something.{\ul
}{\super
64} In this judgment, the predicate no longer {\i
inheres} in the subject, for it is rather {\i
the implicit being} under which the singular subject is {\i
subsumed} as an accidental. If the judgments of existence can also be defined as {\i
judgments of inherence}, then the judgments of reflection are by contrast {\i
judgments of subsumption}. {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
a. The singular judgment\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ Now the immediate judgment of reflection is again, \u8220?the singular is universal,\u8221? but with the subject and predicate in the signification just explained. More accurately, therefore, it can also be expressed thus, \u8220?this is an essential universal.\u8221? But a \u8220?this\u8221? is {\i
not} an essential universal. That {\i
positive} judgment \u8211? positive according to form \u8211? must as judgment be taken negatively. But in as much as the judgment of reflection is not merely something positive, the negation does not directly affect the predicate \u8211? a predicate which does not inhere in the subject but is rather its {\i
implicit being}. On the contrary, it is the subject that is alterable and needs determination. The negative judgment is therefore to be understood as saying: \u8220?{\i
\u8216?not a this\u8217?} is a universal 12.73of reflection\u8221?;{\ul
}{\super
65} such an {\i
in-itself} has a more universal concrete existence than it would have in a \u8220?this.\u8221? Accordingly, the singular judgment has its proximate truth in the {\i
particular judgment}. {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
b. The particular judgment\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ The non-singularity of the subject that must be posited in the first judgment of reflection instead of the subject's singularity is {\i
particularity}. But particularity is determined in the judgment of reflection as {\i
essential singularity}; particularity cannot be, therefore, a {\i
simple, abstract} determination in which the singular would be sublated and the concrete existent dissolved, but is rather only an extension of this singular in external reflection. Thus the subject is: \u8220?these ones,\u8221? or \u8220?a particular number of singulars.\u8221? {\ul
}The judgment, \u8220?some singulars are a universal of reflection,\u8221? appears at first to be a positive judgment, but it is just as well also negative; for \u8220?some\u8221? contains universality and may, accordingly, be regarded as {\i
comprehensive}; but since it is particularity, it is equally disproportionate with respect to universality. The {\i
negative} determination which the subject has obtained through the transition of the singular judgment also is, as we have shown above, the determination of the connection, the copula.{\ul
}{\super
66} \u8211? Implicated in the judgment, \u8220?some humans are happy,\u8221? is {\i
the immediate consequence}: \u8220?{\i
some} humans are {\i
not} happy.\u8221? When {\i
some} things are useful, then, precisely for that reason, there also are {\i
some} that are {\i
not} useful. The positive and the negative judgment no longer fall outside one another, but the particular immediately contains both at the same time, precisely because it is a judgment of reflection. \u8211? But the particular judgment is therefore {\i
indeterminate}. If, in the example of such a judgment, we consider further the subject, \u8220?some humans,\u8221? \u8220?some animals,\u8221? etc., we find that it contains, besides the particular form determination of \u8220?some,\u8221? also the content determination of \u8220?humans,\u8221? etc. By the subject of the singular judgment one could mean, \u8220?this human,\u8221? a singularity that properly pertains to external pointing; it would best be expressed, therefore, by something like \u8220?Gaius.\u8221? But the subject of the particular judgment can no longer be \u8220?some Gaiuses,\u8221? for Gaius is supposed to be a singular as singular. To the \u8220?some,\u8221? therefore, there is added a more universal content, say \u8220?humans,\u8221? \u8220?animals,\u8221? etc. This is not a mere empirical content, but one which is determined by the form of the judgment; it is {\i
universal}, that is, because \u8220?some\u8221? contains universality, and the latter must at the same time be separated from the singulars which the reflected singularity has as a basis. More precisely, this universality is also the {\i
universal nature} or {\i
species} \u8220?human,\u8221? \u8220?animal\u8221? \u8211? the universality which is the result 12.74of the judgment of reflection, but {\i
anticipated}; just as the positive judgment, since it has {\i
the singular} for subject, also anticipates the determination which is the result of the judgment of existence. Thus the subject that contains the singulars, their connection to particularity, and the universal nature, is already posited as the totality of the determinations of the concept. But, to be precise, this consideration is an external one. What is at first already posited in the subject by virtue of its form, in reciprocal {\i
connection}, is the {\i
extension} of the \u8220?this\u8221? to particularity; but this generalization is not commensurate to the \u8220?this\u8221?; the latter is perfectly determinate, but \u8220?some\u8221? is indeterminate. The extension ought to {\ul
}be appropriate to the \u8220?this\u8221? and therefore, in conformity with it, it ought to be {\i
completely determined}; such an extension is totality, or, in the first instance, {\i
universality} in general. This universality has the \u8220?this\u8221? for its basis, for the singular is here the singular reflected into itself; its further determinations run their course, therefore, {\i
outside} it, and just as for this reason particularity determined itself as a \u8220?some,\u8221? so the universality which the subject has attained is an \u8220?allness,\u8221? and thus the particular judgment has passed over into the {\i
universal}. {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
c. The universal judgment\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ The universality of the subject of the universal judgment is the external universality of reflection, \u8220?allness\u8221?; the \u8220?all\u8221? is the all of all the {\i
singulars} in which the singular remains unchanged. This universality is therefore only a {\i
commonality} of self-subsisting singulars, an {\i
association} of such singulars as comes about only by way of {\i
comparison}. \u8211? This is the association that first comes to mind at a subjective level of {\i
representation} when there is talk of universality. The most obvious reason given for viewing a determination as universal is {\i
because it fits many}. \u8211? Also in {\i
analysis} is this conception of universality the one most prevalent, as when, for instance, the development of a function in a {\i
polynomial} is taken to have greater {\i
universal} value than its development in a {\i
binomial}, because the {\i
polynomial} displays {\i
more single terms} than the {\i
binomial}. The demand that the function should be resolved in its full universality would require, strictly speaking, a {\i
pantonomial}, the exhausted infinity. But here is where the limitation of that demand becomes apparent, and where the display of the {\i
infinite} number of terms must rest satisfied with the {\i
ought} it commands, and therefore also with a {\i
polynomial}. But in fact the binomial is already the pantonomial in those cases where the {\i
method} or 12.75the {\i
rule} concerns only the dependence of one member on another, and the dependence of several terms on those that precede them does not particularize itself but remains one and the same underlying function. It is the {\i
method} or the {\i
rule} which is to be regarded as the true {\i
universal}; in the progress of the development or in the development of a polynomial, the rule is only {\i
repeated}, so that it gains nothing in universality through the increased number of terms. We have already spoken earlier of the bad infinity and its deception;{\ul
}{\super
67} the universality of the concept is the {\i
achieved beyond}, whereas that bad infinity remains afflicted with a beyond which is unattainable but remains a mere {\i
progression} to infinity. If it is {\i
allness} {\ul
}that universality brings to mind, a universality that ought to be exhausted in singulars as singulars, then there has been a relapse into that bad infinity; or else it is mere {\i
plurality} which is taken for allness. But plurality, however great it might be, remains inescapably only particularity: it is not allness. \u8211? Yet there is in all this an obscure intimation of the universality of the {\i
concept} as it exists in and for itself; it is the concept that violently strives to reach beyond the stubborn singularity to which pictorial representation clings and beyond the externality of its reflection, passing off allness {\i
as totality} or rather as the category of the in-and-for-itself. This is apparent in other ways as well in the allness which is above all {\i
empirical} universality. In As Much as the singular is presupposed as something immediate and is therefore {\i
pre-given} and externally {\i
picked}, the reflection which collects it into an allness is equally external to it. But because the singular, as a \u8220?this,\u8221? is absolutely indifferent to such a reflection, the universality and the collected singularity cannot combine to form a unity. The empirical allness thus {\i
remains} a {\i
task}; it is an {\i
ought} which, as such, cannot be represented in the form of being. Now an empirically universal proposition \u8211? for nevertheless such are advanced \u8211? rests on the tacit agreement that, if no {\i
instance} of the contrary can be adduced, a {\i
plurality} of cases ought to count for an {\i
allness}; or that a {\i
subjective} allness, namely the {\i
known} cases, may be taken for an {\i
objective} allness. Now a closer examination of the {\i
universal} judgment before us shows that the subject, as we have just noted, contains the achieved universality {\i
as presupposed}; it even contains it as {\i
posited} in it. \u8220?All humans\u8221? expresses, {\i
first}, the {\i
species} \u8220?human\u8221?; {\i
second}, this species in its singularization, but in such a way that the singulars are at the same time 12.76expanded to the universality of the species; conversely, through this conjunction with singularity, the universality is just as perfectly determined as singularity, and the {\i
posited} universality has thereby become {\i
equal} to {\i
what was presupposed}. But, strictly speaking, we should not anticipate {\i
the presupposed} but should rather consider the result for itself in the form determination. \u8211? The singularity, in as much as it is expanded to allness, is {\i
posited} as negativity, and this is identical self-reference. It has not remained, therefore, that first singularity (of Gaius, for instance) but is a determination identical with universality, or the absolute determinateness of the universal. \u8211? That {\i
first} singularity of the singular judgment was not the {\i
immediate singularity} of the positive judgment, but came about through the dialectical movement of the judgment of existence in general;{\ul
}{\super
68} it was already determined {\ul
}to be the {\i
negative identity} of the determinations of that judgment. This is the true presupposition in the judgment of reflection; as contrasted to the positing that runs its course in that judgment, that {\i
first} determinateness of singularity was the latter's {\i
in-itself}; consequently, what singularity is {\i
in itself}, through the movement of the judgment of reflection is now {\i
posited} \u8211? posited, that is, as the identical self-reference of the determinate. Therefore the {\i
reflection} that expanded the singularity to allness is not external to it; on the contrary, it only makes {\i
explicit} what was before {\i
implicit}. \u8211? Hence the result is in truth the {\i
objective universality}. The subject has thus shed the form determination of the judgment of reflection that made its way from the \u8220?this\u8221? to the \u8220?allness\u8221? through the \u8220?some.\u8221? Instead of \u8220?all humans,\u8221? we now have to say \u8220?the human being.\u8221?{\ul
}{\super
69} The universality that has thereby arisen is {\i
the genus}, or the universality which is {\i
concrete} in its universality. The genus does not {\i
inhere} in the subject; it is not {\i
one} property of it or a property at all; it contains all singular determinacies dissolved into its substantial purity. \u8211? Because it is thus posited as this negative self-identity, it is for that reason essentially subject, but one that is no longer {\i
subsumed} under its predicate. Consequently the nature of the judgment of reflection is now altogether altered. This judgment was essentially a judgment of {\i
subsumption}. The predicate was determined, in contrast to its subject, as the {\i
implicit} universal; according to its content, it could be taken as an essentially relational determination or also as a mark \u8211? a determination which makes the subject essentially only an {\i
appearance}. But when determined to {\i
objective universality}, the subject ceases to be subsumed under such a relational determination or the collecting grasp of reflection; with respect to this objective universality, a predicate of this sort is rather a particular. The relation of subject and predicate has thus reversed itself, and to this extent the judgment has at this point sublated itself. 12.77 This sublation of the judgment coincides with what the {\i
determination of the copula} becomes, as we still have to consider;{\ul
}{\super
70} the sublation of the determinations of judgment and their transition into the copula are one and same. \u8211? For in as much as the subject has raised itself to universality, it has become in this determination equal to the predicate which, as the reflected universality, also contains particularity within itself; subject and predicate are therefore identical, that is, in the copula they have come to coincide. This identity is the genus or the nature of a thing in and for itself. In As Much as this identity, therefore, again divides, it is the {\i
inner nature} by {\ul
}virtue of which a subject and predicate are connected to each other. This is a connection of {\i
necessity} wherein the two terms of the judgment are only unessential distinctions. \u8211? That {\i
what belongs to all the singulars of a genus belongs to the genus by nature}, is an immediate consequence. It expresses what we have just seen \u8211? that the subject, e.g. \u8220?all humans,\u8221? sheds its form determination and \u8220?the human being\u8221? is what it should say instead. \u8211? This combination, implicit and explicit, constitutes the basis of a new judgment \u8211? {\i
the judgment of necessity}. {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
C. The judgment of necessity\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ The determination to which universality has advanced is, as we have seen, the {\i
universality that exists in and for itself} or {\i
the objective universality} that in the sphere of essence corresponds to {\i
substantiality}. It is distinguished from the latter because it belongs to the {\i
concept} and for this reason is not only the {\i
inner} but also the {\i
posited} necessity of its determinations, or in other words, the {\i
distinction} is immanent to it, whereas substance has its distinction only in its accidents, does not have it as a principle within it. In the judgment now, this objective universality is {\i
posited} \u8211? {\i
first}, posited with this determinateness as essential to it, immanent to it; {\i
second}, posited with it as diverse from it, a {\i
particularity} for which the said universality constitutes the substantial basis. In this way the universality is determined as {\i
genus} and {\i
species}. {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
a. The categorical judgment\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ The {\i
genus} essentially {\i
divides} or repels itself into {\i
species}; it is genus only in so far as it comprehends the species under it; the species is a species only in so far as, 12.78on the one side, it exists in singulars, and, on the other side, it possesses in the genus a higher universality. \u8211? Now the {\i
categorical judgment} has for predicate such a universality as in it the subject possesses its {\i
immanent nature}. But the categorical judgment is itself the first or the {\i
immediate} judgment of necessity; consequently, the determinateness of the subject, by virtue of which the latter is a singular as contrasted to the genus or the species, belongs to the immediacy of external concrete existence. \u8211? But objective universality also has here only its first {\i
immediate} particularization; on the one hand, therefore, it is itself a determinate genus with respect to which there are higher genera; on the other hand, it is not the most {\i
proximate} genus, that is, its determinateness is not directly the principle of the specific particularity of the subject. But what is {\i
necessary} in {\ul
}it is the {\i
substantial identity} of subject and predicate, in view of which the distinguishing mark of each is only an unessential positedness or even only a name; in its predicate, the subject is reflected into its being-in-and-for-itself. Such a predicate ought not to be classed with the predicates of the preceding judgments. For example, to throw together into one class these judgments: \~ The rose is red, The rose is a plant, or This ring is yellow, It is gold, \~ and thus to take such an external property as the color of a flower as a predicate equal to its vegetable nature, is to overlook a difference which the dullest mind would not miss. \u8211? The categorical judgment, therefore, is definitely to be distinguished from the positive and the negative judgment; in these, what is said of the subject is a {\i
singular accidental} content; in the former, the content is the totality of the form reflected into itself. In this content, therefore, the copula has the meaning of {\i
necessity}, whereas in that of the other two it has only the meaning of abstract, immediate {\i
being}. The {\i
determinateness} of the subject, which makes it a {\i
particular} with respect to the predicate, is at first still something {\i
contingent}; subject and predicate are not connected with {\i
necessity} by the {\i
form} or the {\i
determinateness}; the necessity is therefore still an {\i
inner} one. \u8211? The subject is subject, however, only as a {\i
particular}, and to the extent that it possesses objective universality, it has to possess it essentially in accordance with that at first immediate determinateness. The objective universal, in {\i
determining} itself, that is, in positing itself in a judgment, is in a connection of identity with this repelled {\i
determinateness} as such \u8211? essentially, that is, this determinateness is not to be posited as merely accidental. Only through this {\i
necessity} of its immediate being does the categorical judgment conform to its objective universality and, in this way, has passed over into the {\i
hypothetical judgment}. 12.79 {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
b. The hypothetical judgment\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ \u8220?{\i
If A is, then B is}\u8221?; or \u8220?{\i
The being of A is not its own being but the being of an other, of B}.\u8221? \u8211? What is posited in this judgment is the {\i
necessary connectedness} of immediate determinacies, a connectedness which in the categorical judgment is not yet posited. \u8211? There are here {\i
two} immediate, or externally contingent concrete existences, of which in the categorical judgment there is at first only one, the subject; but since one is external {\ul
}to the other, this other is immediately also external with respect to the first. \u8211? On account of this immediacy, the {\i
content}s of both sides are still indifferent to each other; at first, therefore, this judgment is a proposition of empty form. Now, {\i
first}, the immediacy is as such indeed self-subsistent, a concrete being; but, {\i
second}, what is essential is its connection; this being is therefore just as much mere {\i
possibility}; the hypothetical judgment does not say either that A {\i
is}, or that B {\i
is}, but only that {\i
if} the one is, then the other is; only the connectedness of the extremes is posited as existing, not the extremes themselves. Indeed, each extreme is posited in this necessity as equally the {\i
being of an other}. \u8211? The principle of identity asserts that A is only A, not B; and B is only B, not A. In the hypothetical judgment, on the contrary, the being of finite things is posited through the concept in accordance with their formal truth, namely that the finite is its own being, but equally is not its own being but is the being of an other. In the sphere of being, the finite {\i
alters} and comes to be an other. In the sphere of essence, it is {\i
appearance}; its being is posited to consist in the {\i
reflective shining} of an other in it, and the {\i
necessity} is the {\i
inner} connection not yet posited as such. But the concept is this: that this identity is {\i
posited}; that the existent is not abstract self-identity but {\i
concrete self-identity} and is, immediately within it, the being of an other. The hypothetical judgment can be more closely determined in terms of the relations of reflection as a relation of {\i
ground} and {\i
consequence}, {\i
condition} and {\i
conditioned}, causality etc. Just as substantiality is present in the categorical judgment in the form of its concept, so is the connectedness of causality in the hypothetical judgment. This and the other relations all recur in it, but they are there essentially only as moments of one and the same identity. \u8211? However, in it they are as yet not opposed as singular or particular and universal according to the determinations of the concept, but are only as {\i
moments in general} at first. The hypothetical judgment, therefore, has a shape which is more that of a proposition; 12.80just as the particular judgment is of indeterminate content, so is the hypothetical of indeterminate form, for the determination of its content does not conform to the relation of subject and predicate. \u8211? Yet the being, since it is the being of the other, is for that very reason {\i
in itself} the {\i
unity of itself and the other}, and therefore {\i
universality}; by the same token it is in fact only a {\i
particular}, for it is a determinate being and does not refer in its determinateness merely to itself. But it is not the {\i
simple}, abstract particularity that is posited; on the contrary, through the {\i
immediacy} which the {\i
determinacies possess}, the moments of particularity are differentiated; at the same time, through the unity of these moments as constituted by their connection, the {\ul
}particularity is also their totality. \u8211? In truth, therefore, what is posited in this judgment is universality as the concrete identity of the concept whose determinations do not have any subsistence of their own but are only particularities posited in that identity. So it is the {\i
disjunctive judgment}. {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
c. The disjunctive judgment\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ In the categorical judgment, the concept is objective universality and an external singularity. In the hypothetical, the concept manifests its presence in this externality, in its negative identity. Through this identity, the objective universality and the external singularity obtain the determinateness, now posited in the disjunctive judgment, which in the hypothetical they possess immediately. Hence the disjunctive judgment is objective universality at the same time posited in union with the form. It thus contains, {\i
first}, the concrete universality or the genus in simple form, as the subject; {\i
second}, {\i
the same universality} but as the totality of its differentiated determinations. \u8220?A is either B or C.\u8221? This is the {\i
necessity of the concept} in which, {\i
first}, the self-identity of the two extremes is of the same extent, content, and universality. {\i
Second}, they are differentiated according to the form of conceptual determination, but, because of that identity, this determination {\i
is a mere form}. {\i
Third}, the identical objective universality appears for that reason reflected into itself as against the non-essential form, as a {\i
content} which however has the determinateness of form in it \u8211? once as the simple determination of {\i
genus}; then again, as this determinateness developed in its difference, and in this way it is the particularity of the {\i
species} and their {\i
totality}, {\i
the universality of the genus}. \u8211? The particularity constitutes in its development the {\i
predicate}, because, in containing the whole universal sphere of the subject, and in containing it, however, also in the articulation of particularity, it is to that extent the {\i
greater universal}. Upon closer consideration of this particularization, it is the genus that constitutes {\i
first of all} the substantial universality of the species; the subject is thus B {\i
as well as} 12.81C; this \u8220?as well as\u8221? indicates the {\i
positive} identity of the particular with the universal; this objective universal maintains itself fully in its particularity. {\i
Secondly}, the species {\i
mutually exclude one another}; \u8220?A is {\i
either} B {\i
or} C\u8221?; for they are the {\i
specific difference} of the universal sphere. This \u8220?either or\u8221? is their {\i
negative} connection. In this negative connection they are just as identical as in the positive; the genus is their {\i
unity} as a unity of {\i
determinate} particulars. \u8211? If the genus were an abstract universality, as in the judgments of existence, then the species would also have to be taken as {\i
diverse} and mutually indifferent; this universality, however, is {\ul
}not the external one that arises only through {\i
comparison} and {\i
abstraction} but is, on the contrary, the universality which is immanent to the genus and concrete. \u8211? An empirical disjunctive judgment is without necessity; A is either B or C or D, etc., because the species B, C, D, etc., are {\i
found beforehand}; strictly speaking, therefore, there is no question here of an \u8220?either or,\u8221? for the completeness of these species is only a subjective one; of course, {\i
one} species excludes the {\i
other}, but the \u8220?either or\u8221? excludes {\i
every other} species and excludes within itself an entire sphere. This totality has its {\i
necessity} in the negative unity of the objective universal which has dissolved singularity within itself and possesses, immanent in it, the simple {\i
principle} of differentiation by which the species are {\i
determined} and connected. The empirical species, on the contrary, have their differences in some accidentality or other which is a principle external to them and is not, therefore, {\i
their} principle, and consequently also not the immanent determinateness of the genus; for this reason, they are also not reciprocally connected according to their determinateness. \u8211? Yet it is by virtue of their determinateness that the species constitute the universality of the predicate. \u8211? Here is where the so-called {\i
contrary} and {\i
contradictory} concepts should find their proper place, for the disjunctive judgment is where the essential difference of the concept is posited; but here they also equally find their truth, namely that contrariness and contradictoriness are themselves differentiated both as contraries and as contradictory. Species are contrary in as much as they are merely {\i
diverse}, that is to say, in as much as they possess an immediate existence as subsisting in and for themselves by virtue of the genus which is their nature. They are {\i
contradictory}, in as much as they exclude one another. But each of these determinations is by itself one-sided and void of truth. In the \u8220?either or\u8221? of the disjunctive judgment, their unity is posited as their truth, which is that the independent subsistence of the species as {\i
concrete universality} is itself also the {\i
principle} of the negative unity by which they mutually exclude one another. Through the identity just demonstrated of subject and predicate in accordance with the 12.82negative unity, the genus is determined in the disjunctive judgment as the {\i
proximate genus}. This expression indicates at first the mere quantitative difference of the {\i
more} or {\i
less} determinations which a universal contains as contrasted to a particularity coming under it. On this account, which is the truly proximate genus remains contingent. But then, if the genus is taken as a universal arrived at by the mere abstraction of determinations, it cannot strictly speaking form a disjunctive judgment; for it is contingent whether, as it were, there is still left in it the determinateness that constitutes the principle of the \u8220?either or\u8221?; the genus would {\ul
}not be displayed in the species according to its {\i
determinateness}, and these would only be capable of contingent completeness. In the categorical judgment, the genus stands at first over against the subject only in this abstract form \u8211? is not, therefore, necessarily its proximate genus and, to this extent, is external to it. But when the genus is a concrete, essentially {\i
determined} universality, then, as simple determinateness, it is the unity of the {\i
moments of the concept} \u8211? moments that, only sublated in that simplicity, have their real difference in the species. Hence the genus is the {\i
proximate} genus of a species, for the latter possesses its specific difference in the essential determinateness of the genus, and the species have as such the determination differentiating them in the nature of the genus. What we have just considered constitutes the identity of subject and predicate from the aspect of {\i
determinateness} in general. This is an aspect that was posited by the hypothetical judgment, the necessity of which is an identity of immediate and diverse things and is, therefore, essentially a negative unity. It is this negative unity that in principle separates subject and predicate but is now posited as itself differentiated \u8211? in the subject, as {\i
simple} determinateness; in the predicate, as {\i
totality}. That parting of subject and predicate is the {\i
difference of the concept}; the {\i
totality} of the {\i
species} in the predicate can then be {\i
none other} than this difference. \u8211? The reciprocal {\i
determination of the disjunctive} terms is therefore hereby given. It reduces to the difference of the concept, for it is the concept alone that disjoins itself and manifests in its determination its negative unity. Of course, the species comes up for consideration here only under the aspect of its simple conceptual determinateness, not according to the {\i
shape} in which, proceeding from the idea, it steps into a further self-subsistent {\i
reality}. This reality is of course {\i
dropped} in the simple principle of the genus; but the {\i
essential} differentiation must be a moment of the concept. In the judgment here considered, it is really now the concept's {\i
own} progressive determination that itself {\i
posits} its disjunction, just as was the case for the concept itself, as we saw when it was determined in and for itself and was differentiated into determinate concepts.{\ul
}{\super
71} \u8211? Now because the concept is the universal, the positive as well as the negative 12.83totality of the particulars, for that reason it is immediately itself also {\i
one of its disjunctive members}; the {\i
other} member, however, is this universality resolved into {\i
its particularity}, or the determinateness of the concept {\i
as determinateness}, in which the very universality displays itself as totality. \u8211? If the disjunction of a genus into species has not yet attained this form, this is proof that the disjunction has {\ul
}not risen to the determinateness of the concept and has not proceeded from it. \u8211? {\i
Color} is either violet, indigo, blue, green, yellow, orange, or red; even empirically, the confusion and impurity of such a disjunction are at once apparent; it is a barbarism even from this standpoint. If color is conceived as the {\i
concrete unity} of light and darkness, then this {\i
genus} has within it the {\i
determinateness} that constitutes the {\i
principle} of its particularization into species. Of these, however, one must be the utterly simple color that holds the opposition in balance, contained and negated in the color's intensity; the relation of the opposition of light and darkness must then take its place over against it, and, since this relation is a natural phenomenon, the indifferent neutrality of the opposition must be further added to it. \u8211? Taking for genus such mixtures as violet, and orange, or shades of difference like indigo blue and light blue, betrays a totally inconsiderate procedure that shows too little reflection even for empiricism.{\ul
}{\super
72} \u8211? But this is not the place to discuss the different and more finely determined forms that disjunction may indeed assume in the element of nature or spirit. In the first instance, the disjunctive judgment has the members of the disjunction in the predicate. But the judgment is itself equally disjoined; its subject and predicate are the members of the disjunction; they are the moments of the concept posited in their determinateness but at the same time as identical \u8211? {\i
identical}, (\u945?) in the objective universality which is in the subject as the simple {\i
genus}, and in the predicate as the universal sphere and totality of the moments of the concept; and (\u946?) in the {\i
negative} unity, the developed connectedness of necessity, in accordance with which the {\i
simple determinateness} in the subject has fallen apart into the {\i
difference of the species} and these, in this very difference, have their essential connection and self-identity. This unity, the copula of this judgment in which the extremes have come together through their identity, is thus the concept itself, indeed the concept {\i
as posited}; the mere judgment of necessity has thereby risen to the {\i
judgment of the concept}.12.84 {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
D. The judgment of the concept\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ To know how to form {\i
judgments of existence}, such as \u8220?the rose is red,\u8221? \u8220?the snow is white,\u8221? etc., hardly counts as a sign of great power of judgment. The {\i
judgments of reflection} are more in the nature of {\i
propositions}; to be sure, {\ul
}in the judgment of necessity the subject matter is present in its objective universality, but it is only in the judgment now to be considered that {\i
its connection with the concept is to be found}. The concept is at the basis of this judgment, and it is there with reference to the subject matter, as an {\i
ought} to which reality may or may not conform. \u8211? This is the judgment, therefore, that first contains true adjudication; the predicates, \u8220?good,\u8221? \u8220?bad,\u8221? \u8220?true,\u8221? \u8220?right,\u8221? etc., express that the fact is {\i
measured} against the concept as an {\i
ought} which is simply presupposed, and is, or is not, in {\i
agreement} with it. The judgment of the concept has been called the judgment of {\i
modality}, and has been regarded as containing the form of the connection of subject and predicate as this obtains in an {\i
external understanding}, and as concerned with the value of the copula only {\i
in connection with thought}.{\ul
}{\super
73} Accordingly, judgment is said to be {\i
problematic} when the affirmation or negation is taken as {\i
optional} or {\i
possible}; {\i
assertoric}, when it is taken as {\i
true}, that is, {\i
actual}, and {\i
apodictic} when it is taken as {\i
necessary}. \u8211? It is easy to see why it would be an easy step in this judgment to go outside the judgment itself and to regard its determination as something merely {\i
subjective}. For it is the concept here, the subjective, that comes into play again in judgment and relates to an immediate actuality. But this subjectivity is not to be confused with {\i
external reflection}, which is of course also something subjective but in a different sense than the concept itself; on the contrary, the concept that has again emerged out of the disjunctive judgment is the very opposite of a mere {\i
mode} or {\i
manner}. The earlier judgments are subjective in this sense, for they rest on an abstract one-sidedness in which the concept is lost. But the judgment of the concept is instead objective and, as contrasted with the others, it is the truth, for it rests on the concept precisely in its determinateness as concept, not in some external reflection or with {\i
reference to} some subjective, that is, accidental, {\i
thought}. In the disjunctive judgment, the concept was posited as the identity of universal nature and its particularization, and with that the relation of the judgment was sublated. This {\i
concretion} of universality and particularization is at first a simple result; it must now further develop itself into totality, for its 12.85moments have at first collapsed into it and do not as yet stand over against each other in determinate self-subsistence. \u8211? The shortcoming of that result may also be stated more incisively by saying that although in the disjunctive judgment the objective {\i
universality} has attained completion in {\i
its particularization}, the negative unity of the latter has only retreated {\i
into it} and has not as yet determined itself as the third moment, that of {\ul
}{\i
singularity}. \u8211? But to the extent that the result is itself {\i
negative unity}, it is already this {\i
singularity}; it is then this one determinateness alone that must now {\i
posit} its negativity, that must part itself into {\i
extremes} and in this way concludes its development in the {\i
syllogistic conclusion}.{\ul
}{\super
74} The proximate diremption of this unity is the judgment in which the unity is posited first as subject, as an {\i
immediate singular}, and then as predicate, as the determinate connection of its moments. {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
a. The assertoric judgment\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ The judgment of the concept is at first {\i
immediate}; as such, it is the {\i
assertoric} judgment. The subject is a concrete singular in general, and the predicate expresses this same singular as the {\i
connection} of its {\i
actuality}, its determinateness or {\i
constitution}, to its {\i
concept}. (\u8220?This house is {\i
bad},\u8221? \u8220?this action is {\i
good}.\u8221?) More closely considered, it contains, therefore, (a) that the subject {\i
ought} to be something; its {\i
universal nature} has posited itself as the self-subsistent concept; (b) that {\i
particularity} is {\i
something constituted} or an {\i
external concrete existence}, not only because of its immediacy, but because it expressly differs from its self-subsisting universal nature; its external concrete existence, for its part, because of this self-subsistence of the concept, is also indifferent with respect to the universal and may or may not conform to it. \u8211? This constitution is the {\i
singularity} which in the disjunctive judgment escapes the necessary {\i
determination} of the universal, a determination that exists only as the particularization of the {\i
species} and as the negative {\i
principle} of the genus. Thus the concrete universality that has come out of the disjunctive judgment divides in the assertoric judgment into the form of {\i
extremes} to which the concept itself, as the {\i
posited} unity connecting them, is still lacking. For this reason the judgment is so far only {\i
assertoric}; its {\i
credential} is only a subjective {\i
assurance}. That something is good or bad, right, suitable or not, hangs on an external third. But to say that the connectedness is thus {\i
externally posited} is the same as saying that it is still only {\i
in itself} or {\i
internal}. \u8211? When we say that something is good or bad, etc., we certainly do not mean to say 12.86that it is good only {\i
in a subjective consciousness} but may perhaps be bad in itself, or that \u8220?good and bad,\u8221? \u8220?right,\u8221? \u8220?suitable,\u8221? etc. may not be predicates of the object itself. The merely subjective character of the assertion of this judgment consists, therefore, in the fact that the {\i
implicitly} present connectedness of subject and predicate has not been {\i
posited} yet, or, {\ul
}what amounts to the same thing, that it is only {\i
external}; the copula still is an immediate {\i
abstract being}. Thus the assurance of the assertoric judgment can with right be confronted by an opposing one. When the assurance is given that \u8220?this action is good,\u8221? the opposite, \u8220?this action is bad,\u8221? has equal justification. \u8211? Or, considering the judgment {\i
in itself}, since its subject is an {\i
immediate singular}, in this abstraction it still does not have, posited {\i
in it}, the {\i
determinateness} that would contain its connection with the universal concept; it still is a contingent matter, therefore, whether there is or there is not conformity to the concept. Essentially, therefore, the judgment is {\i
problematic}. {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
b. The problematic judgment\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ The {\i
problematic} judgment is the assertoric judgment in so far as the latter must be taken positively as well as negatively. \u8211? According to this qualitative side, the {\i
particular} judgment is likewise a problematic one, for it has positive just as much as negative value (equally problematic is also the being of the subject and predicate in the {\i
hypothetical} judgment), and also posited through this side is that the singular judgment and the categorical are still something merely subjective. In the problematic judgment as such, however, this positing is more immanent than it is in these others, for in it the {\i
content of the predicate is the connection of the subject to the concept} \u8211? here, therefore, the {\i
determination of the immediate as something contingent} is itself {\i
present}. Whether the predicate ought to be or not to be coupled with a certain subject appears at first only as problematic, and to this extent the indeterminateness falls on the side of the copula. The {\i
predicate} has no determination to gain from this coupling, since it is already the objective, concrete universality. The problematic element falls, therefore, on the immediacy of the {\i
subject}, which is thereby determined as a {\i
contingency}. \u8211? But further, we must not for that reason abstract from the singularity of the subject; purified of such a singularity, the subject would be only a universal, whereas the predicate entails precisely this, that the concept of the subject ought to be posited with reference to its singularity. \u8211? We may not say, \u8220?the house or a house is good,\u8221? but, \u8220?so indeed it is in the way it is made.\u8221? \u8211? The problematic element in the subject itself constitutes its {\i
moment of contingency}, the 12.87{\i
subjectivity of the fact it expresses} as contrasted with its objective nature or its concept, its mere {\i
mode and manner} or its {\i
constitution}. {\ul
}Consequently the {\i
subject} is itself differentiated into its universality or objective nature, that is, its {\i
ought}, and the particularized constitution of immediate existence. It thereby contains the {\i
ground} for {\i
being or not being what it ought to be}. In this way, it is equated with the predicate. \u8211? Accordingly, the {\i
negativity} of the problematic character of the judgment, in as much as it implicates the immediacy of the {\i
subject}, only amounts to this original partition of the latter {\i
into its moments of universal and particular} of which it is already the unity \u8211? a partition which is the judgment itself. One more comment that can be made is that {\i
both} sides of the subject, its concept and the way it is constituted, could each be called its {\i
subjectivity}. The concept is the universal essence of a fact, withdrawn into itself, the fact's negative self-unity; this unity constitutes the fact's subjectivity. But a fact is also essentially {\i
contingent} and has an {\i
external constitution}; this last may also be called its mere subjectivity, as contrasted with the objectivity of the concept. The fact consists just in this, that its concept, as self-negating unity, negates its universality and projects itself into the externality of singularity. \u8211? As this duplicity, the {\i
subject} of the judgment is here posited; the truth of those two opposite meanings of subjectivity is that they are in one. \u8211? The meaning of subjective has itself become problematic by {\i
having lost} the immediate {\i
determinateness} that it had in the immediate judgment and its determinate {\i
opposition} to the {\i
predicate}. \u8211? These opposite meanings of subjectivity that surface even in the ratiocination of ordinary reflection should by themselves at least call attention to the fact that subjectivity has no truth {\i
in one} of them alone. The duplicity of meaning is the manifestation of the one-sidedness of each when taken by itself. When this problematic character of the judgment is thus posited as the character of the {\i
fact}, the fact with its {\i
constitution}, the judgment itself is no longer problematic but {\i
apodictic}. {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
c. The apodictic judgment\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ The subject of the apodictic judgment (\u8220?the house, as so and so constituted, is {\i
good},\u8221? \u8220?the action, as so and so {\i
constituted}, {\i
is} right\u8221?) includes, {\i
first}, the universal, or what it {\i
ought to be}; {\i
second}, its {\i
constitution}; the latter contains the ground why a predicate of the judgment of the concept 12.88does or does not pertain to it, that is, whether the subject corresponds to its concept or not. This judgment is now {\i
truly} objective; or it is the {\i
truth} of the {\i
judgment} in general. Subject and predicate correspond to each other, and have the same concept, and this {\i
content} is itself posited {\i
concrete universality}; that is {\ul
}to say, it contains the two moments, the objective universal or the genus and the {\i
singularized universal}. Here we have, therefore, the universal that is {\i
itself} and continues through {\i
its opposite}, and is a universal only in {\i
unity} with the latter. \u8211? Such a universal, like \u8220?good,\u8221? \u8220?fitting,\u8221? \u8220?right,\u8221? etc., has an {\i
ought} for its ground, and contains at the same time the correspondence of {\i
existence}; it is not the ought or the genus by itself, but this {\i
correspondence} which is the {\i
universality} that constitutes the predicate of the apodictic judgment. The {\i
subject} likewise contains these two moments in {\i
immediate} unity as {\i
fact}. The truth of the latter, however, is that it is internally {\i
fractured} into its {\i
ought} and its {\i
being}; {\i
this is} the {\i
absolute judgment on all actuality}.{\ul
}{\super
75} \u8211? That this original partition, which is the omnipotence of the concept, is equally a turning back into the concept's unity and the absolute connection of \u8220?ought\u8221? and \u8220?being\u8221? to each other, is what makes the actual into {\i
a fact}; the fact's inner connection, this concrete identity, constitutes its {\i
soul}. The transition from the immediate simplicity of the fact to the {\i
correspondence} which is the {\i
determinate} connection of its ought and its being \u8211? the copula \u8211? now shows itself upon closer examination to lie in the particular {\i
determinateness} of the fact. The genus is the universal {\i
existing in and for itself} which, to that extent, appears as unconnected; the determinateness, however, is that which in that universality is reflected {\i
into itself} but at the same time {\i
into an other}. The judgment, therefore, has its {\i
ground} in the constitution of the subject and is thereby {\i
apodictic}. Consequently, we now have the {\i
determinate} and {\i
accomplished} copula which hitherto consisted in the abstract \u8220?is\u8221? but has now further developed into {\i
ground} in general. It first attaches to the subject as {\i
immediate} determinateness, but it is equally the {\i
connection} to the predicate \u8211? a predicate that has no other {\i
content} than this {\i
correspondence} itself, or the connection of the subject to the universality. Thus the form of judgment has passed away, first, because subject and predicate are {\i
in themselves} the same content; but, second, because through its determinateness the subject points beyond itself and connects itself to the predicate; but again, third, {\i
this connecting} has equally passed over into the predicate, only constitutes the content of it, and so it is the connecting as {\i
posited} or the judgment itself. \u8211? The 12.89concrete identity of the concept that was the {\i
result} of the disjunctive judgment and constitutes the {\i
inner} foundation of the judgment of the concept \u8211? the identity that was posited at first only in the predicate \u8211? is thus recovered {\i
in the whole}. {\ul
}On closer examination, the positive factor in this result which is responsible for the transition of the judgment into another form is that, as we have just seen,{\ul
}{\super
76} the subject and predicate are in the apodictic judgment each the whole concept. \u8211? The {\i
unity} of the concept, as the {\i
determinateness} constituting the copula that connects them, is at the same time {\i
distinct} from them. At first, it stands only on the other side of the subject as the latter's {\i
immediate constitution}. But since its essence is {\i
to connect}, it is not only that immediate constitution but the {\i
universal} that {\i
runs through} the subject and predicate. \u8211? While subject and predicate have the same {\i
content}, it is the {\i
form of their connection} that is instead posited through the determinateness of the copula, {\i
the determinateness as a universal} or {\i
the particularity}. \u8211? Thus it contains in itself both the form determinations of the extremes and is the {\i
determinate} connection of the subject and predicate: the {\i
accomplished} copula of the judgment, the copula {\i
replete of content}, the unity of the concept that re-emerges from the judgment wherein it was lost in the extremes. \u8211? {\i
By virtue of this repletion of the copula}, the judgment has become {\i
syllogism}. {\ql
