 2. The second figure of the formal syllogism, U-S-P, does not correspond to this schema, because the S that constitutes the middle term did not 12.114subsume or was not a predicate. In induction this deficiency is eliminated; here the middle term is \u8220?all singulars\u8221?; the proposition, U-S, which contains as the subject the objective universal or the genus set apart as an extreme, has a predicate which is of at least equal extension as the subject and is consequently identical with it for external reflection. Lion, elephant, etc., constitute the {\i
genus} of quadruped; the difference, that {\i
the same} content is posited once in singularity and again in universality, is thus just an {\i
indifferent determination of form} \u8211? an indifference which in the syllogism of reflection is the posited result of the formal syllogism and is posited here through the equality of extension. Induction, therefore, is not the syllogism of mere {\i
perception} or of contingent existence, like the second figure corresponding to it, but the syllogism of {\i
experience} \u8211? of the subjective gathering together of singulars in the genus, {\ul
}and of the conjoining of the genus with a universal determinateness on the ground that the latter is found in all singulars. It also has the objective significance that the immediate genus has determined itself through the totality of singularity as a universal property and possesses its existence in a universal relation or mark. \u8211? But the objective significance of this syllogism, as it was of the others, is at first only its inner concept, and is not as yet posited in it. 3. On the contrary, induction is essentially still a subjective syllogism. The middle terms are the singulars in their immediacy, the collecting of them into a genus through the allness is an {\i
external} reflection. Because of the persisting {\i
immediacy} of the singulars and because of the {\i
externality} that derives from it, the universality is only completeness, or rather, it remains {\i
a task}. \u8211? In induction, therefore, there recurs the {\i
progression} into the bad infinity; {\i
singularity} ought to be posited as {\i
identical} with {\i
universality}, but since the {\i
singulars} are equally posited as {\i
immediate}, the intended unity remains only a perpetual {\i
ought}; it is a unity of {\i
likeness}; the terms which are supposed to be identical are at the same time supposed {\i
not} to be identical. The a, b, c, d, e, constitute the genus only further on, in the infinite; they do not yield a complete experience. The {\i
conclusion} of induction thus remains {\i
problematic}. But induction, by expressing that perception, in order to become experience, {\i
ought} to be carried on {\i
to infinity}, presupposes that the genus is {\i
in and for itself} conjoined with its determinateness. In this, it in fact rather presupposes its conclusion as something immediate, just as the syllogism of allness presupposes the conclusion for one of its premises. \u8211? An experience that rests on induction is assumed as valid {\i
even though} the perception is admittedly {\i
not complete}; it may 12.115be assumed, however, that there is no {\i
counter-instance} to the experience only if the latter is true {\i
in and for itself}. Inference by induction, therefore, is based indeed on an immediacy, but not on the immediacy on which it is supposed to be based, not on a {\i
singularity} that {\i
exists} immediately, but {\i
on one that exists in and for itself}, on the {\i
universal}. \u8211? The fundamental character of induction is that it is a syllogistic inference; if singularity is taken as the essential determination of the middle term, but universality as only the external determination, then the middle term would fall apart into two disjoined parts, and there would be no inference; this externality belongs rather to the extremes. {\i
Singularity} can only be a middle term {\i
if immediately identical} with the {\i
universality}; such a universality is in truth {\i
objective universality}, {\i
the genus}. \u8211? The matter can also be viewed in this way: universality is {\i
external but essential} to the determination of the singularity which is at the basis of the middle term of {\ul
}induction; such an {\i
external} is just as much immediately its opposite, the {\i
internal}. \u8211? The truth of the syllogism of induction is therefore a syllogism that has for its middle term a singularity which is immediately {\i
in itself} universality. This is {\i
the syllogism of analogy}. {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
c. The syllogism of analogy\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ 1. This syllogism has the third figure of the immediate syllogism, S-U-P, for its abstract schema. But its middle term is no longer some single quality or other but a universality which is {\i
the immanent reflection of a concreted term} and is therefore its {\i
nature}; and conversely, since it is thus the universality of a concreted term, it is at the same time in itself this {\i
concreted term}. \u8211? Here, therefore, a singular is the middle term, but a singular taken in its universal nature; there is moreover another singular, an extreme term, which has the same universal nature as the other which is the middle term. For example: \~ {\i
The earth} has inhabitants, The moon is {\i
an earth}, Therefore the moon has inhabitants.{\ul
}{\super
95} \~ 2. Analogy is all the more superficial, the more the universal in which the two extremes are united, and in accordance with which the one extreme becomes the predicate of the other, is a mere {\i
quality} or, since quality is a matter of subjectivity, is some {\i
distinctive mark} or other and the identity of the extremes is therein taken as just a {\i
similarity}. But this kind of superficiality to which a form of understanding or of reason is reduced by being debased to the sphere of mere {\i
representation} should have no place in logic. \u8211? 12.116Also unacceptable is to present the major premise of this syllogism as though it should run: \u8220?That which is similar to an object in one distinctive mark is similar to it in other such marks as well.\u8221? On this formulation, {\i
the form of the syllogism} is expressed in the shape of a content while the empirical content, the content properly so called, is together relegated to the minor premise. So, for example, could also the whole form of the first syllogism be expressed as its major premise: \u8220?That which is subsumed under another thing in which a third thing inheres has that third thing inhering in it too; but now. . .etc.\u8221? But what matters in the syllogism as such is not the {\ul
}empirical content, and to make its own form the content of a major premise makes just as little difference as to take any other empirical content for that purpose. Nothing of consequence follows for the syllogism of analogy from a content that contains nothing but the form peculiar to that syllogism, just as nothing of consequence would have followed for the first syllogism from having as its content the form that makes the syllogism a syllogism. \u8211? What counts is always the form of the syllogism, whether the latter has itself or something else for its empirical content. So the syllogism of analogy is a form peculiarly its own, and it is vacuous not to want to regard it as such on the ground that that form could be made into the content or matter of a major premise whereas matter is no concern in logic. \u8211? What might tempt one to this view in regard to the syllogism of analogy, and perhaps in regard to the syllogism of induction too, is that the middle term in them, and also the extremes, are more determined than they are in the merely formal syllogism, and therefore the determinations of form, since they are no longer simple and abstract, must also take on the appearance of a {\i
content determination}. But that the form determines itself to content is {\i
first of all} a necessary advance on the part of the formal side, and therefore an advance that touches the nature of the syllogism essentially; {\i
secondly}, such a content determination cannot, therefore, be regarded as any other empirical content, and abstraction cannot be made from it. When we consider the syllogism of analogy with its major premise expressed as above, namely, \u8220?if two subject matters agree in one or more properties, then a further property of one also belongs to the other,\u8221? it may seem that this syllogism contains {\i
four terms}, the {\i
quaternio terminorum} \u8211? a circumstance that brings with it the difficulty of how to bring analogy into the form of a formal syllogism. \u8211? There are {\i
two} singulars; for a {\i
third}, a property immediately assumed as common, and, for a {\i
fourth}, the other properties that one singular possesses immediately but the other 12.117first comes to possess only by means of the syllogism. \u8211? This is so because, as we have seen, in the syllogism of analogy {\i
the middle term} is posited as singularity but immediately {\i
also} as the true universality of the singularity. \u8211? In {\i
induction}, the middle term is, apart from the extremes, an indeterminate number of singulars; this syllogism, therefore, required the enumeration of an infinite number of terms. \u8211? In the syllogism of allness the universality in the middle term is still only the external form determination of the allness; in the syllogism of analogy, on the contrary, it is as essential universality. In the above example, the middle term, \u8220?the earth,\u8221? is taken as something concrete which, in truth, is just as much a universal nature or genus as it is a singular. {\ul
}From this aspect, the {\i
quaternio terminorum} would not make analogy an imperfect syllogism. But it would make it so from another aspect; for although the one subject has the same universal nature as the other, it is undetermined whether the determinateness, which is inferred to pertain also to the second subject, pertains to the first because of its {\i
nature in general} or because of its {\i
particularity}; for example, whether the earth has inhabitants as a heavenly body {\i
in general} or only as this {\i
particular} heavenly body. \u8211? Analogy is still a syllogism of reflection in as much as singularity and universality are united in its middle term {\i
immediately}. Because of this immediacy, the {\i
externality} of the unity of reflection is still there; the singular is the genus only {\i
in itself}, {\i
implicitly}; it is not posited in this negativity by which its determinateness would be the genus's own determinateness. For this reason the predicate that belongs to the singular of the middle term is not already the predicate of the other singular, even though the two singulars both belong to the one genus. 3. S-P (\u8220?the moon is inhabited\u8221?) is the conclusion; but the one premise (\u8220?the earth is inhabited\u8221?) is likewise S-P; in so far as S-P is supposed to be a conclusion, it entails the requirement that that premise also be S-P. This syllogism is thus in itself the demand to counter the immediacy that it contains; or again, it presupposes its conclusion. One syllogism of existence has its presupposition in the {\i
other} syllogism of existence. In the syllogisms just considered, the presupposition has been moved into them, because they are syllogisms of reflection. Since the syllogism of analogy is therefore the demand that it be mediated as against the immediacy with which its mediation is burdened, what it demands is the sublation of the moment of {\i
singularity}. Thus there remains for the middle term the objective universal, the {\i
genus} purified of immediacy. \u8211? In the syllogism of analogy the genus was a moment of the middle term only as {\i
immediate presupposition}; since the syllogism itself demands the sublation of the presupposed immediacy, the negation of singularity and hence the universal is no longer immediate but {\i
posited}. \u8211? The syllogism of reflection 12.118contained the {\i
first} negation of immediacy; the second has now come on the scene, and with it the external universality of reflection is determined as existing in and for itself. \u8211? Regarded from the positive side, the conclusion shows itself to be identical with the premises, the mediation to have rejoined its presupposition, and what we have is thus an identity of the universality of reflection by virtue of which it becomes a higher universality. Reviewing the course of the syllogism of reflection, we find that mediation is in general the {\i
posited} or {\i
concrete} unity of the form determinations of the extremes; reflection consists in this positing of the one determination {\ul
}in the other; the mediating middle is thus {\i
allness}. But it is {\i
singularity} that proves to be the essential ground of mediation while universality is only as an external determination in it, as {\i
completeness}. But universality is {\i
essential} to the singular if the latter is to be the conjoining middle term; the singular is therefore to be taken as an {\i
implicitly existing} universal. But the singular is not united with it in just this positive manner but is sublated in it and is a negative moment; thus the universal is the genus posited as existing in and for itself, and the singular as immediate is rather the externality of the genus, or it is an {\i
extreme}. \u8211? The syllogism of reflection, taken in general, comes under the schema P-S-U in which the singular is still as such the essential determination of the middle term; but since its immediacy has been sublated, the syllogism has entered under the formal schema S-U-P, and the syllogism of reflection has thus passed over into the {\i
syllogism of necessity}. {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
C. The syllogism of necessity\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ The mediating middle has now determined itself (1) as {\i
simple} determinate universality, like the particularity in the syllogism of existence, but (2) as {\i
objective} universality, that is to say, one that, like the allness of the syllogism of reflection, contains the whole determinateness of the different extremes; this is a {\i
completed} but {\i
simple} universality, the {\i
universal nature} of the fact, the {\i
genus}. This syllogism is {\i
full of content}, because the {\i
abstract} middle term of the syllogism of existence has posited itself to be {\i
determinate difference}, in the way it is as the middle term of the syllogism of reflection, but this difference has again reflected itself into simple identity. \u8211? This syllogism is for this reason the syllogism of {\i
necessity}, because its middle term is not any adventitious immediate content but is the 12.119immanent reflection of the determinateness of the extremes. These have their inner identity in the middle term, whose content determinations are the form determinations of the extremes. \u8211? Consequently, what differentiates the terms is a form which is {\i
external} and {\i
unessential} and the terms themselves are as moments {\i
of a necessary} existence. This syllogism is at first immediate and formal in the sense that {\i
what holds} the terms {\i
together} is the {\i
essential nature}, as {\i
content}, and this content is in the distinguished terms only in {\i
different form}, and the extremes are by themselves only an {\i
unessential} subsistence. \u8211? The realization of this syllogism is a matter of determining it in such a way that the {\i
extremes} are equally posited as this totality which initially the middle term is, and the {\ul
}{\i
necessity} of the connection, which is at first only the substantial {\i
content}, shall be a connection of the {\i
posited form}. {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
a. The categorical syllogism\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ 1. The categorical syllogism has the categorical judgment for one or for both of its premises. \u8211? Associated with this syllogism, just as with that judgment, is the more specific signification that its middle term is the {\i
objective universality}. Superficially, the categorical syllogism is also taken for nothing more than a mere syllogism of inherence. Taken in its full import, the categorical syllogism is the {\i
first syllogism of necessity}, one in which a subject is conjoined with a predicate through {\i
its substance}. But when elevated to the sphere of the concept, substance is the universal, so posited to be in and for itself that it has for its form or mode of being, not accidentality, as it has in the relation specific to it, but the determination of the concept. Its differences are therefore the extremes of the syllogism, specifically universality and singularity. This universality, as contrasted with the {\i
genus} that more closely defines the middle term, is abstract or is a universal determinateness: it is the accidentality of substance summed up in a simple determinateness which is, however, the substance's essential difference, its {\i
specific difference}. \u8211? Singularity, for its part, is the actual, in itself the concrete unity of genus and determinateness \u8211? though here, in the immediate syllogism, it is immediate singularity at first, accidentality summed up in the form of a subsistence {\i
existing for itself}. \u8211? The connection of this extreme term to the middle term constitutes a categorical judgment; but since the other extreme term also, as just determined, expresses the specific difference of the genus or its determinate principle, this other premise is also categorical. 12.120 2. This syllogism, as the first and therefore immediate syllogism of necessity, comes in the first instance under the schema of the formal syllogism, S-P-U. \u8211? But since the middle term is the essential {\i
nature} of the singular and not just {\i
one or other} of its determinacies or properties, and likewise the extreme of universality is not any abstract universal, nor just any singular quality either, but is rather the universal determinateness of the genus, its {\i
specific difference}, we no longer have the contingency of a subject being conjoined with just {\i
any quality} through just {\i
any middle term}. \u8211? Consequently, since the {\i
connections} of the extremes with the middle term also do not have the external immediacy that they have in the syllogism of existence, we do not have coming into play the demand for proof in the sense in {\ul
}which it occurred in the case of that other syllogism and led to an infinite progression. Further, this syllogism does not presuppose its conclusion for its premises, as in the syllogism of reflection. The terms, in keeping with the substantial content, stand to one another in a connection of identity that exists {\i
in and for itself}; we have here {\i
one} essence running through the three terms \u8211? an essence in which the determinations of singularity, particularity, and universality are only {\i
formal} moments. To this extent, therefore, the categorical syllogism is no longer subjective; in that connection of identity, objectivity begins; the middle term is the identity, full of content, of its extremes, and these are contained in it in their self-subsistence, for their self-subsistence is the said substantial universality which is the genus. The subjective element of the syllogism consists in the indifferent subsistence of the extremes with respect to the concept or the middle term. 3. But there is still a subjective element in this syllogism, for that identity is still the substantial identity or {\i
content} but is not yet {\i
identity of form} at the same time. The identity of the concept still is an {\i
inner} bond and therefore, as connection, still {\i
necessity}; the universality of the middle term is solid, {\i
positive} identity, but is not equally the {\i
negativity of its extremes}. The immediacy of this syllogism, which is not yet {\i
posited} as what it {\i
is in itself}, is more precisely present in this way. The truly immediate element of the syllogism is the {\i
singular}. This singular is subsumed under its genus as middle term; but subsumed under the same genus are also an {\i
indeterminate number of many} other singulars; it is therefore {\i
contingent} that only {\i
this} singular is posited as subsumed under it. \u8211? But further, this contingency does not belong only to an {\i
external reflection} that finds the singular posited in the syllogism to be contingent by {\i
comparison} with others; on the contrary, it is because the singular is itself connected to the middle term as its objectivity universality that it is posited as {\i
contingent}, as a subjective actuality. From the other side, because the subject is an {\i
immediate} singular, it contains 12.121determinations that are not contained in the middle term as the universal nature; it also has, therefore, a concrete existence which is indifferent to the middle term, determined for itself and with a content of its own. Therefore, conversely, this other term also has an indifferent immediacy and a concrete existence distinct from the former. \u8211? The same relation also obtains between the middle term and the other extreme; for this too likewise has the determination of immediacy, hence of a being which is contingent with respect to the middle term. {\ul
}Accordingly, what is posited in the categorical syllogism are, {\i
on the one hand}, extremes that are so related to the middle term that they have objective universality or self-subsistent nature {\i
in themselves}, and are at the same time immediate {\i
actualities}, hence {\i
indifferent} to one another. {\i
On the other hand}, they are equally {\i
contingent}, or their immediacy is as sublated in their identity. But this identity, because of the self-subsistence and totality of the actuality, is only formal, inner identity, and the syllogism of necessity has thereby determined itself to the {\i
hypothetical syllogism}. {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
b. The hypothetical syllogism\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ 1. The hypothetical judgment contains only the necessary {\i
connection} without the immediacy of the connected terms. \u8220?If A is, so is B\u8221?; or, the being of A is also just as much the being of {\i
an other}, of the B; with this, it is not as yet said either that A {\i
is}, or that B {\i
is}. The hypothetical syllogism adds this {\i
immediacy} of being: \~ If A is, so is B, But A is, Therefore B is. \~ The minor premise expresses by itself the immediate being of the A. But it is not only this that is added to the judgment. The conclusion contains the connection of subject and predicate, not as the abstract copula, but as the accomplished {\i
mediating} unity. The being of the A is to be taken, therefore, {\i
not as mere immediacy} but essentially as {\i
middle term of the syllogism}. This needs closer examination. 2. In the first place, the connection of the hypothetical judgment is the {\i
necessity} or the inner {\i
substantial identity} associated with the external diversity of concrete existence \u8211? an identical {\i
content} lying internally as its basis. The two sides of the judgment are both, therefore, not an immediate being, but a being held in necessity, hence one which is at the same time {\i
sublated} or only being as appearance. 12.122The two behave, moreover, as sides of the judgment, as {\i
universality} and {\i
singularity}; the one, therefore, is the above content as {\i
totality of determinations}, the other as {\i
actuality}. Yet it is a matter of indifference which side is taken as universality and which as singularity. That is to say, in as much as the conditions are still the {\i
inner}, {\i
abstract} element of an actuality, they are the {\i
universal}, and it is by {\i
being held together} in one singularity that they step into actuality. Conversely, the conditions are a {\i
dismembered and} {\i
dispersed} appearance that gains {\i
unity} and meaning, and a {\i
universally valid existence}, only in {\i
actuality}. {\ul
}The relation that is here being assumed between the two sides of condition and conditioned may however also be taken to be one of cause and effect, ground and consequence. This is a matter of indifference here. The relation of condition, however, corresponds more closely to the one that obtains in the hypothetical judgment and syllogism in as much as condition is essentially an indifferent concrete existence, whereas ground and cause are inherently a transition; moreover, condition is a more universal condition in that it comprehends both sides of the relation, since effect, consequence, etc., are just as much the condition of cause and ground as these are the condition of them. \u8211? Now A is the {\i
mediating} being in so far as it is, {\i
first}, an immediate being, an indifferent actuality, but, {\i
second}, in so far as it is equally {\i
inherently contingent}, self-sublating being. What translates the conditions into the actuality of the new shape of which they are the conditions is the fact that they are not being as an abstract immediacy, but {\i
being according to its concept} \u8211? {\i
becoming} in the first instance, but more determinedly (since the concept is no longer transition) {\i
singularity} as self-referring {\i
negative} unity. \u8211? The conditions are a dispersed material awaiting and requiring application; this {\i
negativity} is the mediating means, the free unity of the concept. It determines itself as {\i
activity}, for this middle term is the contradiction of {\i
objective universality}, or of the totality of the identical content and {\i
the indifferent immediacy}. \u8211? This middle term is no longer, therefore, merely inner but {\i
existent necessity}; the objective universality contains its self-reference as {\i
simple immediacy}, as {\i
being}. In the categorical syllogism this moment is at first a determination of the extremes; but as against the objective universality of the middle term, it determines itself as {\i
contingency}, hence as something which is only {\i
posited} and also sublated, something that has returned into the concept or into the middle terms as unity, a unity which is now in its objectivity also being. 12.123 The conclusion, \u8220?therefore B is,\u8221? expresses the same contradiction \u8211? that B exists {\i
immediately} but at the same time through an other or as {\i
mediated}. According to its form, it is therefore the same concept that the middle term is, distinguished from {\i
necessity} only as the {\i
necessary}, in the totally superficial form of singularity as contrasted with universality. The absolute {\i
content} of A and B is the same; for {\i
ordinary representation}, they are two different names for the same basic thing, since representation fixes the appearances of the diversified shape of existence and distinguishes the necessary from its necessity; but to the extent that necessity were to be separated from B, the latter would not be the necessary. What we have here, therefore, is the identity of the {\i
mediating term} and the {\i
mediated}. {\ul
}3. The hypothetical syllogism is the first to display {\i
the necessary connection} as a connectedness through {\i
form} or {\i
negative unity}, just as the categorical syllogism displays it through positive unity, the solid {\i
content}, the objective universality. But {\i
necessity} merges with {\i
the necessary}; {\i
the form-activity} of translating the conditioning actuality into the conditioned is {\i
in itself} the unity into which the determinacies of the oppositions previously let free into indifferent existence are {\i
sublated}, and where the difference of A and B is an empty name. The unity is therefore a unity reflected into itself, and hence an {\i
identical} content, and is this content not only {\i
implicitly in itself} but, through this syllogism, it is also {\i
posited}, for the being of A is also not its own being but that of B and {\i
vice versa}, and in general the being of the one is the being of the other and, as determined in the conclusion, their immediate being or indifferent determinateness is a mediated one \u8211? therefore, their externality has been sublated, and what is {\i
posited} is their {\i
unity withdrawn into itself}. The mediation of the syllogism has thereby determined itself as {\i
singularity}, {\i
immediacy}, and {\i
self-referring negativity}, or as a differentiating identity that retrieves itself into itself out of this differentiation \u8211? as absolute form, and for that very reason as objective {\i
universality}, self-identical existent {\i
content}. In this determination, the syllogism is the {\i
disjunctive syllogism}. {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
c. The disjunctive syllogism\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ As the hypothetical syllogism comes in general under the schema of the second figure of the formal syllogism, U-S-P, so the disjunctive comes under the schema of the third, S-U-P. The middle term, however, is a {\i
universality replete with form}; it has determined itself as {\i
totality}, as {\i
developed} 12.124objective universality. The middle term, therefore, is universality as well as particularity and singularity. As that universality, it is in the first place the substantial identity of the genus, but this identity is secondly one in which {\i
particularity} is {\i
included}, but again, included {\i
as equal to it} \u8211? therefore as a universal sphere that contains its total particularity, the genus sorted out in its species, an A which is {\i
B as well as C and D}. But particularization is differentiation and as such equally the {\i
either-or} of B, C, D \u8211? {\i
negative} unity, {\i
the reciprocal} exclusion of the determinations. \u8211? This excluding, moreover, is now not just reciprocal, the determination not merely relative, but is also just as much {\i
self-referring} determination, the particular as {\i
singularity} to the exclusion of the {\i
others}. \~ A is either B or C or D, But A is B, Therefore A is neither C nor D. \~ {\ul
}Or also: \~ A is either B or C or D, But A is neither C nor D, Therefore A is B. \~ A is subject not only in the two premises but also in the conclusion. It is a universal in the first premise and in its predicate the {\i
universal} sphere particularized in the totality of its species; in the second premise, it is as {\i
a determinate}, or as a species; in the conclusion it is posited as the excluding, {\i
singular} determinateness. \u8211? Or again, in the minor it is already exclusive singularity, and in the conclusion it is positively posited as the determinate that it is. Consequently, what as such appears to be meditated is the {\i
universality} of A with the {\i
singularity}. But the {\i
mediating means} is this A which is the {\i
universal} sphere of its particularizations and is determined as a {\i
singular}. {\i
What is posited in the disjunctive} syllogism is thus the truth of the hypothetical syllogism, the unity of the mediator and the mediated, and for that reason the disjunctive syllogism is equally no longer {\i
a syllogism at all}. For the middle term which is posited in it as the totality of the concept itself contains the two extremes in their complete determinateness. The extremes, as distinct from this middle term, are only a positedness to which there no longer accrues any proper determinateness of its own as against the middle term. If we consider the matter with narrower reference to the hypothetical syllogism, we find that there was in the latter a {\i
substantial identity} as the {\i
inner} bond of necessity, and a {\i
negative unity} distinct from it, namely the activity or the form that translated one existence into another. The disjunctive syllogism is in general in the determination of 12.125{\i
universality}, its middle term is the A as {\i
genus} and as perfectly {\i
determined}; also {\i
posited} through this unity is the earlier inner content and, conversely, the positedness or the form is not the external negative unity over against an indifferent existence but is identical with that solid content. The whole form determination of the concept is posited in its determinate difference and at the same time in the simple identity of the concept. In this way the {\i
formalism of the syllogistic inference}, and consequently the subjectivity of the syllogism and of the concept in general, has sublated itself. This formal or subjective factor consisted in that the middle mediating the extremes is the concept as an {\i
abstract} determination and is therefore {\i
distinct} from the terms whose unity it is. In the completion of the syllogism, where the objective universality is equally posited as the totality {\ul
}of the form determinations, the distinction of mediating and mediated has on the contrary fallen away. That which is mediated is itself an essential moment of what mediates it, and each moment is the totality of what is mediated. The figures of the syllogism exhibit each determinateness of the concept {\i
singly} as the middle term, a middle term which is at the same time the concept as an {\i
ought}, the requirement that the mediating factor be the concept's totality. The different genera of the syllogism exhibit instead the stages in the {\i
repletion} or concretion of the middle term. In the formal syllogism the middle is posited as totality only through all the determinacies, but each {\i
singly}, discharging the function of mediation. In the syllogism of reflection, the middle term is the unity gathering together {\i
externally} the determinations of the extremes. In the syllogism of necessity the middle has determined itself as a unity which is just as developed and total as it is simple, and the form of the syllogism, which consisted in the difference of the middle term over against its extremes, has thereby sublated itself. With this the concept in general has been realized; more precisely, it has gained the kind of reality which is {\i
objectivity}. The {\i
first reality} was that the {\i
concept}, in itself negative unity, partitions itself and as {\i
judgment} posits its determinations in determinate and indifferent difference, and in the syllogism it then sets itself over against them. Since it is still in this way the inwardness of this now acquired externality, in the course of the syllogisms this externality is equated with the inner unity; the different determinations return into the latter through the mediation that unites them at first in a third term, and as a result the externality exhibits, in itself, the concept which, for its part, is no longer distinct from it as inner unity. Conversely, however, that determinateness of the concept which was considered as {\i
reality} is equally a {\i
positedness}. For 12.126the identity of the concept's inwardness and externality has been exhibited as the truth of the concept not only in this result; on the contrary, already in the judgment the moments of the concept remain, even in their reciprocal indifference, determinations that have significance only in their connection. The syllogism is {\i
mediation}, the complete concept in its {\i
positedness}. Its movement is the sublation of this mediation in which nothing is in and for itself, but each thing is only through the meditation of an other. The result is therefore an {\i
immediacy} that has emerged through the {\i
sublation of the mediation}, a {\i
being} which is equally identical with mediation and is the concept that has restored itself out of, and in, its otherness. This {\i
being} is therefore a {\i
fact} which is {\i
in and for itself \u8211? objectivity}. {\ql
