{\b
Chapter 1 Mechanism12.133\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ Since objectivity is the totality of the concept that has returned into its unity, an immediate is thereby posited which is in and for itself that totality, and is also {\i
posited} as such, but in it the negativity of the concept has as yet not detached itself from the immediacy of the totality; in other words, the objectivity is not yet posited as {\i
judgment}. In so far as it has the concept immanent in it, the difference of the concept is present in it; but on account of the objective totality, the differentiated moments are {\i
complete} and {\i
self-subsistent objects} that, consequently, even in connection relate to one another as {\i
each standing on its} own, each maintaining itself in every combination as {\i
external}. \u8211? This is what constitutes the character of {\i
mechanism}, namely, that whatever the connection that obtains between the things combined, the connection remains one that is {\i
alien} to them, that does not affect their nature, and even when a reflective semblance of unity is associated with it, the connection remains nothing more than {\i
composition}, {\i
mixture}, {\i
aggregate}, etc. {\i
Spiritual} mechanism, like its {\i
material} counterpart, also consists in the things connected in the spirit remaining external to one another and to spirit. A {\i
mechanical mode of representation}, a {\i
mechanical memory}, a {\i
habit}, a {\i
mechanical mode of acting}, mean that the pervasive presence that is proper to spirit is lacking in what spirit grasps or does. Although its theoretical or practical mechanism cannot take place without its spontaneous activity, without an impulse and consciousness, the freedom of individuality is still lacking in it, and since this freedom does not appear in it, the mechanical act appears as a merely external one. \~ {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
A. The mechanical object\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ The object is, as we have seen, the {\i
syllogism} whose mediation has attained equilibrium and has therefore come {\i
to be} immediate identity. It is therefore in and 12.134for itself a universal \u8211? universality, not in the sense of a commonality {\ul
}of properties, but a universality that pervades particularity and in it is immediate singularity. 1. To begin with, therefore, the object does not differentiate itself into {\i
matter} and {\i
form}, matter being its presumed self-subsistent universal aspect and form the particular and singular instead; according to its concept, any such abstract differentiation of singularity and universality has no place in the object; if regarded as matter, the object must then be taken to be in itself informed matter. One can just as well take it as a thing with properties, as a whole consisting of parts, as substance with accidents, or as determined by the other relations of reflection. But these are all past relations that in the concept have come to an end. The object, therefore, has neither properties nor accidents, for these are separable from the thing or the substance, whereas in the object particularity is absolutely reflected into the totality. In the parts of a whole, there is indeed present that self-subsistence that pertains to the differences of the object, but these differences are at once themselves essentially objects, totalities which, unlike parts, are not such as against the whole. At first, therefore, the object is {\i
indeterminate}, for it has no determinate opposition within, because it is the mediation that has collapsed into immediate identity. in as much as the {\i
concept} is {\i
essentially determined}, the object has in it the determinateness of a {\i
manifold} which, although complete, is otherwise {\i
indeterminate}, that is, {\i
relationless}, one that constitutes a totality also not further determined at first; {\i
sides} or {\i
parts} that may be distinguished within it belong to an external reflection. This totally indeterminate difference thus amounts just to this, that there are {\i
several objects}, each of which only contains its determinateness reflected into its universality and does not reflectively shine {\i
outwardly}. \u8211? Because this indeterminate determinateness is essential to the object, the object is in itself a {\i
plurality}, and must therefore be regarded as a {\i
composite}, an {\i
aggregate}. \u8211? Yet it does not consist of {\i
atoms}, for atoms are not objects because they are not totalities. Leibniz's {\i
monad} would be more of an object. It is a total representation of the world which, shut up within its {\i
intensive subjectivity}, in essence at least is supposed to be a {\i
one}. Yet the monad, determined as an {\i
exclusive one}, is a principle only {\i
assumed} by {\i
reflection}. It is an object, however, both because the ground of its manifold representations \u8211? of the developed, that is, the {\i
posited} determinations of its merely {\i
implicit} totality \u8211? lies {\i
outside it}, and because it is equally a matter of indifference for the monad that it constitutes an object {\i
together with other objects}; in fact, therefore, it is not {\i
exclusive}, not {\i
self-determined for itself}. 12.135 2. Since the object is now a totality of {\i
determinateness}, yet, because of its indeterminateness and immediacy, it is not the totality's {\i
negative unity}, {\ul
}it is {\i
indifferent} towards the {\i
determinations} as {\i
singulars}, determined in and for themselves, just as these are themselves {\i
indifferent} to each other. These, therefore, are not comprehensible from it nor from one another; the object's totality is the form of the overall reflectedness of its manifoldness into a singularity in general which is not in itself determinate. The determinacies, therefore, which are in it do indeed pertain to it; but the {\i
form} that constitutes their difference and combines them into a unity is an external one, indifferent to them; whether it be a {\i
mixture}, or again an {\i
order}, a certain {\i
arrangement} of parts and sides, these are combinations that are indifferent to what they connect. Consequently, like an existence in general, the object has the determinateness of its totality {\i
outside it}, in {\i
other} objects, and these again {\i
outside them}, and so forth to infinity. The immanent turning back of this progression {\i
in infinitum} must indeed be likewise assumed, and it must be represented as a {\i
totality}, as a {\i
world}, but one which is nothing but a universality brought to closure through a singularity that remains indeterminate, a {\i
universe}. Since the object is thus determinate yet indifferent to its determinateness, through itself it points for its determinateness {\i
outside and beyond} itself, constantly to objects for which it is however likewise {\i
a matter of indifference that they do the determining}. Consequently, nowhere is a principle of self-determination to be found. {\i
Determinism}, which is the standpoint that cognition adopts when it assumes as truth the object as we first have it here, assigns for each determination of the object that of another object; but this other object is likewise indifferent both to its determinateness and its determining. \u8211? For this reason determinism is itself so indeterminate as to be bound to an infinite progression; it can halt at will anywhere, and be satisfied there, because the object to which it has progressed, being a formal totality, is shut up within itself and indifferent to its being determined by another. For this reason to {\i
explain} the determination of an object, and to this end to extend the representation of it beyond it, is only an {\i
empty word}, for there is no self-determination in the other object to which the explanation has been extended. 3. Now since the {\i
determinateness} of an object {\i
lies in an other}, there is no determinate diversity separating the two; the determinateness is merely {\i
doubled}, once in the one object and then again in the other; it is something utterly {\i
identical} and the explanation or comprehension is, therefore, a {\i
tautology}. This tautology is an external back and forth movement; since the determinateness fails to obtain from objects that are indifferent to it any proper differentiation and is therefore only identical, there is only {\i
one} 12.136{\i
determinateness} at hand, and that it should be doubled only expresses {\ul
}precisely the externality and vacuity of a difference. But the objects are at the same time {\i
self-subsistent} in regard to one another; in that identity, therefore, they remain utterly {\i
external}. \u8211? Thus there arises the contradiction of a perfect {\i
indifference} of objects to one another and of an {\i
identity of determinateness} of such objects, or of the objects' perfect {\i
externality} in the {\i
identity} of their determinateness. This contradiction is consequently the {\i
negative unity} of a plurality of objects reciprocally repelling each other in the unity. This is the {\i
mechanical process}. {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
B. The mechanical process\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ If objects are regarded only as self-enclosed totalities, they cannot act on one another. Regarded in this way, they are the same as the {\i
monads} which, precisely for that reason, were thought of as having no influence on each other. But the concept of a monad is for just this reason a deficient reflection. For, {\i
in the first place}, the monad is a {\i
determinate} representation of its only {\i
implicit} totality; as a {\i
certain degree} of development and {\i
positedness} of its representation of the world, it is {\i
determinate}; but since it is a self-enclosed totality, it is also indifferent to this determinateness and is, therefore, not its own determinateness but a determinateness {\i
posited through another object}. {\i
In second place}, it is an {\i
immediate} in general, for it is supposed to be just {\i
a mirroring};{\ul
}{\super
11} its self-reference is therefore {\i
abstract universality} and hence an {\i
existence open to others}. \u8211? It does not suffice, in order to gain the freedom of substance, to represent the latter as a totality that, {\i
complete in itself}, would have nothing to receive {\i
from the outside}. On the contrary, a self-reference that grasps nothing conceptually but is only a mirroring{\super
11} is precisely a {\i
passivity} towards the other. \u8211? Likewise the {\i
determinateness}, whether we now take it as the determinateness of a being that {\i
exists} or that {\i
mirrors},{\super
11} as a {\i
degree} of the monad's own internally generated development, is {\i
something external}; the {\i
degree} that the development achieves has its limit in an other. To project the reciprocal influence of substances into a {\i
predetermined harmony} means nothing more than to make it a presupposition, in effect to remove it from the scope of the concept. \u8211? The need to avoid the {\i
interaction} of substances was founded on the moment of absolute {\i
self-subsistence} and {\i
originariness} which was made a fundamental assumption. But since the 12.137{\i
positedness}, the degree of development, does not correspond to this assumed {\i
in-itselfness}, it has for this reason its ground in an {\i
other}. {\ul
}In connection with the relation of substantiality, we showed that it passes over into the relation of causality.{\ul
}{\super
12} But now the existent no longer has the determination of a substance but that of an {\i
object}; the causal relation has come to an end in the concept; the originariness of one substance {\i
vis-\u224?-vis} another has shown itself to be a reflective shine, the substance's action a transition into the opposite substance. This relation has therefore no objectivity. Hence in so far as one object is posited in the form of subjective unity, as efficient cause, this no longer counts as an {\i
originary} determination but as something {\i
mediated}; the active object has this determination only by means of another object. \u8211? {\i
Mechanism}, since it belongs to the sphere of the concept, has that posited within it which proved to be the truth of the relation of causality, namely, that the cause which is supposed to be something existing in and for itself is in fact effect just as well, positedness. In mechanism, therefore, the originary causality of the object is immediately a non-originariness; the object is indifferent to this determination attributed to it; that it is a cause is therefore something accidental to it. \u8211? To this extent, it can be said that the causality of substances is {\i
only the product of representation}. But precisely this causality as product of representation is what {\i
mechanism} is; for mechanism is this, that causality, as {\i
identical} determinateness of a diversity of substances and hence as the foundering into this identity of their self-subsistence, is {\i
mere positedness}; the objects are indifferent to this unity and maintain themselves in the face of it. But this also, their indifferent {\i
self-subsistence}, is a mere {\i
positedness}, and for this reason they are capable of {\i
mixing} and {\i
aggregating}, {\i
and as an} aggregate of becoming {\i
one object}. Through this indifference both to their transition and to their self-subsistence, the substances are {\i
objects}. {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
a. The formal mechanical process\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ The mechanical process is the positing of that which is contained in the concept of mechanism, hence the positing in the first place of a {\i
contradiction}. 1. It follows from the just indicated concept that the interaction of objects is the {\i
positing} of their identical connection. This positing consists simply in giving to the determinateness which is generated the form of {\i
universality} \u8211? and this is {\i
communication}, which occurs without transition into the opposite. \u8211? {\i
Spiritual communication}, which however takes place in an element of universality in the form of universality, is 12.138an {\i
idealized} connection for itself, one in which {\i
a determinateness} {\i
continues} undisturbed from {\ul
}one person to another, generalizing itself unaltered \u8211? like a scent freely spreading in the unresisting atmosphere. But also in the communication between material objects does their determinateness {\i
widen}, so to speak, in an equally idealizing manner; personality is an infinitely more intensive {\i
hardness} than objects possess. The formal totality of an object in general \u8211? a totality indifferent to determinateness and hence not a self-determination \u8211? renders the object indistinct from another object and thus makes interaction at first an unimpeded continuing of the determinateness of the one into the other. Now in the region of the spirit there is an infinitely manifold content capable of communication, for by being taken up into intelligence the content receives this {\i
form} of universality in which it becomes communicable. But that which is a universal not only by virtue of form, but in and for itself, is the {\i
objective} as such, both in the region of the spirit and of the body, and as against it the singularity of external objects, as of persons also, is an unessential factor unable to offer any resistance to it. Laws, morals, rational conceptions in general, are in the region of the spirit communicable entities of this kind; they pervade individuals unconsciously imposing themselves on them. In the region of the body, such entities are motion, heat, magnetism, electricity, and the like, all of which, even when one wants to imagine them as stuffs or materials, must be termed as {\i
imponderable} agents, for they lack that aspect of materiality that grounds {\i
its singularization}. 2. Now although in the interaction of objects their {\i
identical} universality is posited first, it is equally necessary to posit the other moment of the concept, that of {\i
particularity}; the objects thus also demonstrate their {\i
self-subsistence}; they hold themselves outside each other, and in that universality they produce {\i
singularity}. This production is {\i
reaction} in general. To begin with, this reaction is not to be conceived of as a {\i
mere sublation} of action and of the communicated determinateness; what is communicated is as universal positively present in the particular objects and {\i
particularizes itself} only in their diversity. To this extent, therefore, what is communicated remains what it is, only {\i
distributed} among the objects or determined by their particularity. \u8211? The cause gets lost in its other, in the effect; the activity of the causal substance in its action; but the {\i
active object} only becomes a {\i
universal}; its action is from the start not a loss of its determinateness but a {\i
particularization} by virtue of which the object, which was at first that whole {\i
determinateness} present in it as 12.139{\i
single}, now becomes a {\i
species} of it, and the {\i
determinateness} is thereby posited for the first time as a universal. The two \u8211? the raising in communication of the singular determinateness into {\ul
}universality; and the particularization of it in distribution, the reduction of what was only a one to a species \u8211? are one and the same. Now {\i
reaction} is equal to {\i
action}. \u8211? {\i
First}, this is manifested by the other object {\i
taking over} the entire universal; and so it is now active against the first. Thus its reaction is the same as the action, a {\i
reciprocal repulsion} of the {\i
impulse}. {\i
Second}, what is communicated is the objective; it therefore {\i
remains} the substantial determination of the object on the presupposition of their diversity; the universal thus at the same time specifies itself in them, and consequently each object does not simply give back the whole action but possesses its specific share. But, {\i
third}, reaction is a {\i
wholly negative action} in so far as each object, because of the {\i
elasticity of its self-subsistence}, repels within it the positedness of an other and retains its self-reference. The specific {\i
particularity} of the determinateness communicated in the objects, what was before called species, returns to {\i
singularity}, and the object asserts its externality as against the {\i
communicated universality}. The action thereby passes over into {\i
rest}. It proves to be only a {\i
superficial}, transient alteration within the self-enclosed indifferent totality of the object. 3. This return constitutes the {\i
product} of the mechanical process. {\i
Immediately}, the object is {\i
presupposed} as a singular; then as a particular as against another particular; but finally as indifferent towards its particularity, as universal. The {\i
product} is the totality of the concept previously {\i
presupposed} but now {\i
posited}. It is the conclusion in which the communicated universal is united with singularity through the particularity of the object. In rest, however, the {\i
mediation} is posited at the same time as {\i
sublated}; or again, what is posited is that the product is indifferent to this determining of it and that the received determinateness is external in it. Accordingly the product is the same as the object that first enters the process. But at the same time that object is first {\i
determined} through this movement; the mechanical object is, {\i
as such}, {\i
an object only as product}, for what it {\i
is}, is only {\i
by virtue of the mediation of an other} in it. It is as product that it thus is what it was supposed to be in and for itself, a {\i
composite}, a {\i
mixture}, a certain {\i
arrangement} of parts, in general such that its determinateness is not self-determination but something {\i
posited}. Yet the {\i
result} of the mechanical process is {\i
not already there ahead of that process itself}; its {\i
end is not} in its beginning, as in the case of purpose. The product is in the object a determinateness 12.140which is {\i
externally} posited in it. Hence this product is indeed according to its {\i
concept} the same as what the object already is at the beginning. But at the beginning the external determinateness is not yet there as {\i
posited}. The result is therefore {\i
something {\ul
}quite other} than the first existence of the object, and is something utterly accidental for it. {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
b. The real mechanical process\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ The mechanical process passes over into {\i
rest}. That is to say, the determinateness that the object obtains through that process is only an {\i
external} one. Just as external to it is this rest, for although the latter is a determinateness opposed to the {\i
activity} of the object, the two are each indifferent to the object. Rest can also be viewed, therefore, as brought about by an {\i
external cause}, just as much as it was indifferent to the object to be active. Now further, since the determinateness is a {\i
posited} one, and the concept of the object has {\i
gone back to itself through the process of mediation}, the object contains the determinateness as one that is reflected into itself. Hence in the mechanical process the objects and the process itself now have a more closely determined relation. They are not merely diverse, but are {\i
determinedly differentiated} as against one another. Consequently the result of the formal process, on the one hand a determinationless rest, is, on the other hand, through the immanently reflected determinateness, the {\i
distribution} among several objects mechanically relating to one another {\i
of the opposition} which is in the object as such. The object that on the one hand lacks all determination, showing {\i
no elasticity} and {\i
no self-subsistence} in its relations, has, on the other hand, a {\i
self-subsistence impenetrable} to other objects. Objects now have also {\i
as against one another} this more determined opposition of the {\i
self-subsistent singularity} and {\i
the non-self-subsistent universality}. \u8211? The precise difference between any two may be had merely {\i
quantitatively as a difference} in a body of diverse magnitudes of {\i
mass}, or of {\i
intensity}, or in various other ways. But in general the difference cannot be fixed at just this abstract level; also as objects, both are {\i
positively} self-subsistent. Now the first moment of this real {\i
process} is, as before, {\i
communication}. The {\i
weaker} can be seized and invaded by the {\i
stronger} only in so far as it accepts the stronger and constitutes one {\i
sphere} with it. Just as in the material realm the weaker is secured against the disproportionately strong (as a sheet hanging freely in the air is not penetrated by a musket ball; a weak organic receptivity is not as vulnerable to strong stimuli as it is to weak), so is the wholly feeble spirit 12.141safer facing the strong than one who stands closer to the strong. Imagine, if you will, someone dull-witted and ignoble; lofty intelligence will make no impression on such a one, nor will nobility. The one single effective defense {\i
against} reason is not to get {\ul
}involved with it at all. \u8211? To the extent that an object that has no standing of its own is unable to make contact with one which is self-subsistent, and no communication can take place between them, the latter is also unable to offer {\i
resistance}, that is, cannot specify the communicated universal for itself. \u8211? If they were not in the same sphere, their mutual connection would be an infinite judgment and no process would be possible between them. {\i
Resistance} is the precise moment of the overpowering of the one object by the other, for it is the initial moment in the distribution of the communicated universal and in the positing of the self-referring negativity, of the singularity to be established. Resistance is {\i
overpowered} when its determinateness is not {\i
commensurate} to the communicated universal which the object has accepted and which is supposed to be singularized in the latter. The object's relative lack of self-subsistence is manifested in the fact that its {\i
singularity} lacks the {\i
capacity for what is communicated} to it and is therefore shattered by it, for it is unable to constitute itself as {\i
subject} in this universal, cannot make the latter its {\i
predicate}. \u8211? {\i
Violence} against an object is for the latter {\i
something alien} only according to this second aspect. {\i
Power} becomes {\i
violence} when power, an objective universality, is {\i
identical} with the {\i
nature} of the object, yet its determinateness or negativity is not the object's own immanent {\i
negative reflection} according to which the object is a singular. In so far as the negativity of the object is not reflected back into itself in the power, and the latter is not the object's own self-reference, the negativity, as against the power, is only {\i
abstract} negativity whose manifestation is extinction. Power, as {\i
objective universality} and as {\i
violence against the object} is what is called {\i
fate} \u8211? a concept that falls within mechanism in so far as fate is called {\i
blind}, that is, its {\i
objective universality} is not recognized by the subject in its own specific sphere. \u8211? To add a few more remarks on the subject, the fate of a living thing is in general the {\i
genus}, for the genus manifests itself through the fleetingness of the living individuals that do not possess it as genus in their {\i
actual singularity}. Merely animate natures, as mere objects, like other things at lower levels on the scale of being, do not have fate. What befalls them is a contingency; however, {\i
in their concept as objects} they are {\i
self-external}; hence the alien power of fate is simply and solely their {\i
own immediate nature}, externality and contingency itself. Only self-consciousness has fate in a strict sense, because it is {\i
free}, and therefore in the {\i
singularity} of its \u8220?I\u8221? it absolutely exists 12.142{\i
in and for itself} and can oppose itself to its objective universality and {\i
alienate} itself from it. By this separation, however, it excites against itself the mechanical relation of a fate. Hence, for the latter to have violent power over it, it must have given itself some {\ul
}determinateness or other over against the essential universality; it must have committed a {\i
deed}. Self-consciousness has thereby made itself into a {\i
particular}, and this existence, like abstract universality, is at the same time the side open to the communication of its alienated essence; it is from this side that it is drawn into the process. A people without deeds is without blame; it is wrapped up in objective, ethical universality, is dissolved into it, is without the individuality that moves the unmoved, that gives itself a determinateness on the outside and an abstract universality separated from the objective universality; yet in this individuality the subject is also divested of its essence, becomes an {\i
object} and enters into the relation of {\i
externality} towards its nature, into that of mechanism. {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
c. The product of the mechanical process\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ The product of {\i
formal} mechanism is the object in general, an indifferent totality in which {\i
determinateness} is as {\i
posited}. The object has hereby entered the process as {\i
a determinate thing}, and, in the extinction of this process, the result is, on the one hand, {\i
rest}, the original formalism of the object, the negativity of its determinateness-for-itself. But, on the other hand, it is the sublation of the determinateness, the {\i
positive reflection} of it into itself, the determinateness that has withdrawn into itself, or the {\i
posited totality of the concept}, the {\i
true singularity} of the object. The object, determined at first in its indeterminate universality, then as {\i
particular}, is now determined as an {\i
objective singular}, so that in it that {\i
reflective semblance of singularity}, which is only a self-subsistence {\i
opposing} itself to the substantial universality, is sublated. This resulting immanent reflection, the objective oneness of the objects, is now a oneness which is an individual self-subsistence \u8211? the {\i
center}. Secondly, the reflection of negativity is the universality which is not a fate standing over against determinateness, but a rational fate, immanently determined \u8211? a universality that {\i
particularizes itself from within}, the difference that remains at rest and fixed in the unstable particularity of the objects and their process; it is the {\i
law}. This result is the truth, and consequently also the foundation, of the mechanical process. 12.143 {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
C. Absolute mechanism\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
a. The center\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ The empty manifoldness of the object is now gathered first into objective singularity, into the simple self-determining {\i
middle point}. Secondly, {\ul
}in so far as the object retains as an immediate totality its indifference to determinateness, the latter too is present in it as unessential or as an {\i
outside-one-another} of many objects. As against this immediate totality, the prior or the essential determinateness constitutes the {\i
real middle term} between the many interacting objects; it unites them {\i
in and for themselves} and is their objective universality. Universality exhibited itself first in the relation of {\i
communication}, as present only through {\i
positing}; as {\i
objective} universality, however, it is the pervading immanent essence of the objects. In the material {\i
world} it is the {\i
central body} which is the {\i
genus} or rather the {\i
individualized} universality of the single objects and their mechanical process. The unessential single bodies relate to one another by {\i
impact} and {\i
pressure}; this kind of relation does not hold between the central body and the objects of which it is the essence; for their externality no longer constitutes their fundamental determination. Hence their identity with the central body is rather rest, namely the {\i
being at their center}; this unity is their concept existing in and for itself. It nevertheless remains only an {\i
ought}, since the objects\u8217? externality, still posited at the same time, does not conform to that unity. The {\i
striving} which the objects consequently have towards the center is their absolute universality, one which is not posited through {\i
communication}; it constitutes the true {\i
rest}, itself {\i
concrete} and not {\i
posited from the outside}, into which the process of instability must find its way back. \u8211? It is for this reason an empty abstraction to assume in mechanics that a body set in motion would go on moving in a straight line to infinity if it did not lose movement because of external resistance. {\i
Friction}, or whatever other form resistance takes, is only a phenomenon of {\i
centrality}; it is the latter that in principle brings the body back to itself, since that against which the body rubs and incurs friction has its power of resistance only because it is united with the center. \u8211? In things {\i
spiritual} the center, and the union with it, assume higher forms; but the unity of the concept and the reality of that unity, which is here 12.144in a first instance mechanical centrality, must there too constitute the fundamental determination. The central body has therefore ceased to be a mere {\i
object}, for in the latter the determinateness is something unessential, whereas now the central body no longer has only {\i
being-in-itself}, {\i
in-itselfness}, but also has the {\i
being-for-itself} of the objective totality. For this reason it can be regarded as an {\i
individual}. Its determinateness is essentially different from a mere {\i
order} or {\i
arrangement} and {\i
external combination} of diverse parts; as a determinateness that exists in and for itself it is an {\i
immanent} form, a self-determining principle to which the objects inhere and in virtue of which they are bound together in a true One. {\ul
}But this central individual is at first only a {\i
middle term} that as yet has no true extremes; as the negative unity of the total concept it dirempts itself rather into such extremes. Or again: the previously non-self-subsistent, self-external objects become likewise determined as individuals by the retreat of the concept; the self-identity of the central body, still a {\i
striving}, is burdened by an {\i
externality} to which, in being taken up into the body's {\i
objective singularity}, the latter is communicated. The objects, through this centrality of their own, are positioned outside the original center and are themselves centers for the non-self-subsistent objects. These second centers and the non-self-subsistent objects are brought into unity by the absolute middle term. But the relative individual centers themselves also constitute the middle term of {\i
a second syllogism}. This middle term is on the one hand subsumed under a higher extreme, the objective {\i
universality} and {\i
power} of the absolute center; on the other hand, it subsumes under it the non-self-subsistent objects whose superficiality and formal singularization it supports. \u8211? These non-self-subsistent objects are in turn the middle term of a {\i
third syllogism}, {\i
the formal syllogism}, for since the central individuality obtains through them the externality by virtue of which, in {\i
referring to itself}, is also {\i
strives} towards an absolute middle point, those non-self-subsistent objects are the link between absolute and relative central individuality. The formal objects have for their essence the identical {\i
gravity} of their immediate central body in which they inhere as in their subject and the extreme of singularity; through the externality which they constitute, this immediate central body is subsumed under the absolute central body; they therefore are the formal middle term of {\i
particularity}. \u8211? But the absolute individual is the objectively universal middle term that brings into unity and holds firm the inwardness of the relative individual and its externality. \u8211? Similarly, the {\i
government}, the 12.145{\i
individual citizens}, and the {\i
needs} or the {\i
external life} of these, are also three terms, of which each is the middle term of the other two. The {\i
government} is the absolute center in which the extreme of the singulars is united with their external existence; the {\i
singulars} are likewise the middle term that incites that universal individual into external concrete existence and transposes their ethical essence into the extreme of actuality. The third syllogism is the formal syllogism, the syllogism of reflective shine in which the singular citizens are tied by their {\i
needs} and external existence to this universal absolute individuality; this is a syllogism that, as merely subjective, passes over into the others and has its truth in them. This totality, whose moments are themselves the completed relations of the concept, the {\i
syllogisms} in which each of the three different objects runs {\ul
}through the determination of the middle term and the extreme, constitutes {\i
free mechanism}. In it the different objects have objective universality for their fundamental determination, the {\i
pervasive} gravity that persists self-identical in the {\i
particularization}. The connections of {\i
pressure}, {\i
impact}, {\i
attraction}, and the like, as also of {\i
aggregations} or {\i
mixtures}, belong to the relation of externality which is at the basis of the third of the three syllogisms. {\i
Order}, which is the merely external determinateness of the objects, has passed over into immanent and objective determination. This is the {\i
law}. {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
b. The law\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ In law, the more specific difference of the {\i
idealized reality of objectivity} versus the {\i
external reality} comes into view. The object, as the {\i
immediate} totality of the concept, does not yet possess an externality differentiated from the concept, and the latter is not posited for itself. Now that through the mediation of the process the object has withdrawn into itself, there has arisen the opposition of {\i
simple centrality} as against an {\i
externality} now determined {\i
as} externality, that is, one {\i
posited} as not existing in and for itself. That moment of identity or idealization of individuality is, on account of the reference to externality, an {\i
ought}; it is the unity of the concept, determined in-and-for-itself and self-determining, to which that external reality does not correspond, and therefore does not go past the mere {\i
striving} towards it. But individuality is, {\i
in and for itself}, {\i
the concrete principle of negative unity}, and as such is itself {\i
totality}; it is a unity that dirempts itself into the {\i
specific differences of the concept} while abiding within its self-equal universality; it is thus the 12.146central point {\i
expanded} inside its pure ideality {\i
by difference}. \u8211? This reality that corresponds to the concept is the {\i
idealized} reality, distinct from the reality that is only a striving; it is difference, earlier a plurality of objects but now in its essential nature, and taken up into pure universality. This real ideality is the {\i
soul} of the hitherto developed objective totality, {\i
the identity of the system which is now determined in and for itself}. The objective {\i
being-in-and-for-itself} thus manifests itself more precisely in its totality as the negative unity of the center, a unity that divides into {\i
subjective individuality} and {\i
external objectivity}, maintains the former in the latter and determines it in an idealized difference. This self-determining unity that absolutely reduces external objectivity to ideality is a principle of {\i
self-movement}; the {\i
determinateness} of this animating principle, which is the difference of the concept itself, is the {\i
law}. \u8211? Dead mechanism was the mechanical process of objects above considered that immediately {\ul
}appeared as self-subsisting, but precisely for that reason are in truth non-self-subsistent and have their center outside them; this process that passes over into {\i
rest} exhibits either {\i
contingency} and indeterminate difference or {\i
formal uniformity}. This uniformity is indeed a {\i
rule}, but not {\i
law}. Only free mechanism has a {\i
law}, the determination proper to pure individuality or to {\i
the concept existing for itself}. As difference, the law is in itself the inexhaustible source of a self-igniting fire and, since in the ideality of its difference it refers only to itself, it is {\i
free necessity}. {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
c. Transition of mechanism\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ This soul is however still immersed in its body. The {\i
now determined} but {\i
inner} concept of objective totality is free necessity in the sense that the law has not yet stepped in opposite its object; it is {\i
concrete} centrality as a universality {\i
immediately} diffused in its objectivity. Such an ideality does not have, therefore, the {\i
objects themselves} for its determinate difference; these are {\i
self-subsistent individuals} of the totality, or also, if we look back at the formal stage, non-individual, external {\i
objects}. The law is indeed immanent in them and it does constitute their nature and power; but its difference is shut up in its ideality and the objects are not themselves differentiated in the idealized non-indifference of the law. But the object possesses 12.147its essential self-subsistence solely in the idealized centrality and its laws; it has no power, therefore, to put up resistance to the judgment of the concept and to maintain itself in abstract, indeterminate self-subsistence and remoteness. Because of the idealized difference which is immanent in it, its existence is a {\i
determinateness posited by the concept}. Its lack of self-subsistence is thus no longer just a {\i
striving} towards a {\i
middle point}, with respect to which, precisely because its connection with it is only that of a striving, it still has the appearance of a self-subsistent external object; it is rather a striving towards the {\i
object determinedly opposed to it}; and likewise the center has itself for that reason fallen apart and its negativity has passed over into {\i
objectified opposition}. Centrality, therefore, is now the reciprocally negative and tense {\i
connection} of these objectivities. Thus free mechanism determines itself to chemism. {\ql
