{\b
Chapter 2 Chemism12.148\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ In objectivity as a whole chemism constitutes the moment of judgment, of the difference that has become objective, and of process. Since it already begins with determinateness and positedness, and the chemical object is at the same time objective totality, the course it follows next is simple and perfectly determined by its presupposition. \~ {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
A. The chemical object\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ The chemical object is distinguished from the mechanical in that the latter is a totality indifferent to determinateness, whereas in the chemical object the determinateness, and hence the {\i
reference to other}, and the mode and manner of this reference, belong to its nature. \u8211? This determinateness is at the same time essentially a {\i
particularization}, that is, it is taken up into universality; thus it is a {\i
principle} \u8211? a {\i
determinateness which is universal}, not only the determinateness of the {\i
one singular object} but also of the {\i
other}. In the chemical object there is now, therefore, a distinction in its concept, between the inner totality of the two determinacies and the determinateness that constitutes the nature of the singular object in its {\i
externality} and {\i
concrete existence}. Since in this way the object is {\i
implicitly} the whole concept, it has within it the {\i
necessity} and the {\i
impulse} to sublate its opposed, {\i
one-sided subsistence}, and to bring itself in existence to the {\i
real whole} which it is according to its concept. Regarding the expression \u8220?chemism\u8221? for the said relation of the non-indifference of objectivity, it may be further remarked that the expression is not to be understood here as though the relation were only to be found in that form of elemental nature that strictly goes by that name. Already the meteorological relation must be regarded as a process whose parts have more the nature of physical than chemical elements.{\ul
}{\super
13} In animate things, {\ul
}the sex relation 12.149falls under this schema, and the schema also constitutes the {\i
formal} basis for the spiritual relations of love, friendship, and the like. On closer examination, the chemical object is at first a {\i
self-subsistent} totality in general, one reflected into itself and therefore distinct from its reflectedness outwards \u8211? an indifferent {\i
basis}, the individual not yet determined as non-indifferent; the person, too, is in the first instance a basis of this kind, one that refers only to itself. But the immanent determinateness that constitutes the object's {\i
non-indifference} is, {\i
first}, reflected into itself in such a manner that this retraction of the reference outwards is only a formal abstract universality; the outwards reference is thus a determination of the object's immediacy and concrete existence. From this side the object does not return, {\i
within it}, to individual totality: the negative unity has its two moments of opposition in {\i
two particular objects}. Accordingly, a chemical object is not comprehensible from itself, and the being of one object is the being of another. \u8211? But, {\i
second}, the determinateness is absolutely reflected into itself and is the concrete moment of the individual concept of the whole which is the universal essence, the {\i
real genus} of the particular objects. The chemical object, which is thus the contradiction of its immediate positedness and its immanent individual concept, is a {\i
striving} to sublate the immediate determinateness of its existence and to give concrete existence to the objective totality of the concept. Hence it does still remain a non-self-subsistent object, but in such a way that it is by nature in tension with this lack of self-subsistence and initiates the {\i
process} as a self-determining. {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
B. The process\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ 1. It begins with the presupposition that the objects in tension, as much as they are tensed against themselves, just as much are they by that very fact at first tensed against each other \u8211? a relation which is called their {\i
affinity}. Each stands through its concept in contradiction to its concrete existence's own one-sidedness and each consequently strives to sublate it, and in this there is immediately posited the striving to sublate the one-sidedness of the other and, through this reciprocal balancing and combining, to posit a reality conformable to the concept that contains both moments. Since each is posited within it as self-contradictory and self-sublating, they are held apart from each other and from their reciprocal complementation only by {\i
external violent force}. The middle term whereby these extremes are now concluded into a unity is, {\i
first}, the {\i
implicitly existent} nature of both, the whole concept containing both within. But, {\i
second}, since in concrete existence the two 12.150stand over against each other, their absolute unity is also a {\ul
}still formal element that concretely {\i
exists distinc}t from them \u8211? the element of {\i
communication} wherein they enter into external {\i
community} with each other. Since the real difference belongs to the extremes, this middle term is only the abstract neutrality, the real possibility of those extremes \u8211? the {\i
theoretical element}, as it were, of the concrete existence of the chemical objects, of their process and its result. In the realm of bodies, {\i
water} fulfills the function of this medium; in that of spirit, in as much as there is in it an analog of such a relation, the {\i
sign} in general, and {\i
language} more specifically, can be regarded as fulfilling it. The relation of the objects, as mere communication in this element, is on the one hand a tranquil coming-together, but on the other it is equally a {\i
negative relating}, for in communication the concrete concept which is their nature is posited in reality, and the {\i
real differences} of the object are thereby reduced to its unity. Their prior self-subsistent determinateness is thus sublated in the union that conforms to the concept, which is one and the same in both; their opposition and tension are thereby blunted, with the result that in this reciprocal complementation the striving attains its tranquil {\i
neutrality}. The process is in this way {\i
dissolved}; since the contradiction between concept and reality has been resolved, the extremes of the syllogism have consequently lost their opposition and have ceased to be extremes as against each other and the middle term. The product is {\i
something neutral}, that is, something in which the ingredients, which can no longer be called objects, are no longer in tension and therefore no longer have the properties that accrued to them in tension, though in the product the {\i
capacity} for their prior self-subsistence and tension is retained. For the negative unity of the neutral product proceeds from a {\i
presupposed non-indifference}; the {\i
determinateness} of the chemical object is identical with its objectivity; it is original. Through the process just considered, this non-indifference is only {\i
immediately} sublated; the determinateness, therefore, is not as yet absolutely reflected into itself, and consequently the product of the process is only a formal unity. 2. In this product the tension of opposition, and the negative unity which is the activity of the process, are now indeed dissolved. But since this unity is essential to the concept and has also itself come into concrete existence, it is still present but has stepped {\i
outside} the neutral object. The process does not spontaneously re-start itself, for it had non-indifference only as its {\i
presupposition} \u8211? it did not posit it. \u8211? This self-subsistent negativity outside the object, the concrete existence of the {\i
abstract} singularity whose being-for-itself has its reality in the {\i
non-indifferent object}, is in itself now in {\ul
}tension with its abstraction, an inherently restless activity 12.151outwardly bent on consuming. It connects {\i
immediately} with the object whose tranquil neutrality is the real possibility of an opposition to this neutrality; the same object is now the {\i
middle term} of the prior formal neutrality, now concrete in itself and determined. The more precise immediate connection of the {\i
extreme of negative unity} with the object is in that the latter is determined by it and is thereby disrupted. This disruption may at first be regarded as the restoration of the opposition of the objects in tension with which chemism began. But this determination does not constitute the other extreme of the syllogism but belongs to the immediate connection of the differentiating principle with the middle in which this principle gives itself its immediate reality; it is the determinateness which the middle term, besides at the same time being the universal nature of the subject matter, possesses in the disjunctive syllogism, whereby that object is both objective universality and determinate particularity. The {\i
other extreme} of the syllogism stands opposed to the external {\i
self-subsistent extreme} of singularity; it is, therefore, the equally self-subsisting extreme of {\i
universality}; hence the disruption that the real neutrality of the middle term undergoes in it is that it breaks up into moments that are not non-indifferent but, on the contrary, {\i
neutral}.{\ul
}{\super
14} Accordingly these moments are, on the one side, the abstract and indifferent{\ul
}{\super
15} {\i
base}, and, on the other, this base's {\i
activating} principle which, separated from it, equally attains the form of indifferent objectivity. This disjunctive syllogism is the totality of chemism in which the same objective whole is exhibited as self-standing {\i
negative} unity; then, in the middle term, as {\i
real} unity; and finally as the chemical reality resolved into its {\i
abstract} moments. In these moments the determinateness has not reached its {\i
immanent reflection} in {\i
an other} as in the neutral product, but has in itself returned into its abstraction, an {\i
originally determined element}. 3. These elemental objects are therefore liberated from chemical tension; in them, the original basis of that {\i
presupposition} with which chemism began has been {\i
posited} through the real process. Now further, their inner {\i
determinateness} is as such essentially the contradiction of their {\i
simple indifferent subsistence} and themselves as {\i
determinateness}, and is the outward impulse that disrupts itself and posits tension in its determined object and in an {\i
other}, {\i
in order that the object may have something} to which it can relate as non-indifferent, with which it can neutralize itself and give to its simple {\ul
}determinateness an existent reality. Consequently, on the one hand chemism has gone back to its beginning in which 12.152objects in a state of reciprocal tension seek one another and then combine in a neutral product by means of a formal and external middle term; and, on the other hand, by thus going back to its {\i
concept}, chemism sublates itself and has gone over into a higher sphere. {\s6 \afs21
{\b
{\ql
c. Transition of chemism\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ Even ordinary chemistry shows examples of chemical alterations in which a body, for example, imparts a higher oxidation to one part of its mass and thereby reduces another part to a lower degree of the same, at which degree alone it can enter into a neutral combination with another differing body brought into contact with it, a combination to which it would not have been receptive at that other first immediate degree. What happens here is that the object does not connect with another in accordance with an immediate, one-sided determinateness, but, in accordance with the inner totality of an original {\i
relation}, {\i
posits the presupposition} which it needs for a real connection and thereby gives itself a middle term by virtue of which it unites its concept with its reality in conclusion; it is a singularity determined in and for itself, the concrete concept as the principle of the {\i
disjunction} into extremes whose {\i
re-union} is the activity of that {\i
same} negative principle that thereby returns to its first determination, but returns to it {\i
objectified}. Chemism is itself {\i
the first negation of the indifferent} objectivity and of the {\i
externality} of determinateness; it is still burdened, therefore, by the immediate self-subsistence of the object and with externality. Consequently it is not yet for itself that totality of self-determination that proceeds from it and in which it is rather sublated. \u8211? The three syllogisms that have resulted constitute its totality. The first has formal neutrality for its middle term and for extremes the objects in tension. The second has for its middle term the product of the first, real neutrality; and for extremes the disrupting activity and its product, the indifferent element. But the third is the self-realizing concept that posits for itself the presupposition by virtue of which the process of its realization is conditioned \u8211? a syllogism that has the universal for its essence. Yet, on account of the immediacy and externality by which the chemical objectivity is still determined, {\i
these three syllogisms fall apart}. The first process whose product is the neutrality of the tensed objects is extinguished in this product and is re-activated only by a differentiation that comes to it from outside; conditioned by an 12.153immediate presupposition, the process is exhausted in it. \u8211? The excretion out of the neutral product {\ul
}of the non-indifferent extremes, as also their decomposition into their abstract elements, must likewise proceed from {\i
conditions} and stimulations of activity brought in from the outside. But the two essential moments of the process, neutralization on the one hand and dissolution and reduction on the other, since they too are bound together in one and the same process and the {\i
union} blunting the tension of the extremes is also a {\i
separation} into these, constitute on account of the still underlying externality {\i
two diverse} sides; the extremes that are separated in that same process are other than the objects or matters uniting in it; in so far as the former proceed from it again as non-indifferent, they must turn outwards; their renewed neutralization is a process other than the one that took place in the first. But these various processes, which have demonstrated themselves to be necessary, are equally so many {\i
stages} by which {\i
externality} and {\i
conditionality} are sublated, and from which the concept emerges as determined in and for itself, a totality unconditioned by externality. In the first process, what is sublated is the externality of the mutually non-indifferent extremes that constitute the whole reality, or the distinction between the {\i
implicitly} determinate concept and its {\i
existing} determinateness. Sublated in the second process is the externality of the real unity, union as merely {\i
neutral}. Or more precisely, the formal activity sublates itself in bases that are equally formal, neutral{\ul
}{\super
16} determinacies whose {\i
inner concept} is now the absolute activity that has withdrawn into itself and now realizes itself internally, that is, {\i
posits} the determinate difference within itself and through this {\i
mediation} constitutes itself as real unity; this is a mediation which is thus the concept's own mediation, its self-determination and, considering that in it the concept reflects itself back into itself, an immanent {\i
presupposing}. The third syllogism, which on the one hand is the restoration of the preceding processes, sublates on the other hand the last remaining moment of {\i
indifferent} bases: it sublates the whole abstract external {\i
immediacy} that becomes in this way the concept's own moment of self-mediation. The concept that has thus sublated as external all the moments of its objective existence, and has posited them in its simple unity, is thereby completely liberated from the objective externality to which it refers only as an unessential reality. This objective free concept is {\i
purpose}. {\ql
