Chapter 2 The idea of cognition12.192\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ Life is the immediate idea, or the idea as its still internally unrealized {\i
concept}. In its {\i
judgment}, the idea is {\i
cognition} in general. The concept is {\i
for itself} as concept in as much as it freely and concretely exists as abstract universality or a genus. As such, it is its pure self-identity that internally differentiates itself in such a way that the differentiated is not an {\i
objectivity} but is rather equally liberated into subjectivity or into the form of simple self-equality; consequently, the object facing the concept is the concept itself. Its {\i
reality} in general is the {\i
form of its existence}; all depends on the determination of this form; on it rests the difference between what the concept is {\i
in itself}, or as {\i
subjective}, and what it is when immersed in objectivity, and then in the idea of life. In this last, the concept is indeed distinguished from its external reality and posited {\i
for itself}; however, this being-for-itself which it now has, it has only as an identity that refers to itself as immersed in the objectivity subjugated to it, or to itself as indwelling, substantial form. The elevation of the concept above life consists in this, that its reality is the concept-form liberated into universality. Through this judgment the idea is doubled, into the subjective concept whose reality is the concept itself, and the objective concept which is as life. \u8211? {\i
Thought}, {\i
spirit}, {\i
self-consciousness}, are determinations of the idea in as much as the latter has itself as the subject matter, and its {\i
existence}, that is, the determinateness of its being, is its own difference from itself. The {\i
metaphysics of the spirit} or, as was more commonly said in the past, {\i
of the soul}, revolved around the determinations of substance, simplicity, immateriality. These were determinations for which spirit was supposed to be the ground, but as a subject drawn from {\i
empirical} consciousness, and the question then was which predicates agreed with the perceived facts. But this was a procedure that could go no further than the procedure of physics, which reduces the world of appearance to general laws and determinations of reflection, for it is spirit still as {\i
phenomenal} that is taken as the foundation. In fact, in so far as scientific stringency goes, it also {\ul
}had to fall short of physics. For not only is spirit infinitely richer than nature; since its essence is constituted by the absolute unity {\i
in the concept} of opposites, and in its 12.193appearance, therefore, and in its connection with externality, it exhibits contradiction at its most extreme form, it must be possible to adduce an experience in support of each of the opposite determinations of reflection, or, starting from experiences, to proceed by way of formal inference to the opposite determinations. Since the predicates immediately drawn from the appearances still belong to empirical psychology, so far as metaphysical consideration goes, all that is in truth left are the entirely inadequate determinations of reflection. \u8211? In his critique of {\i
rational psychology},{\ul
}{\super
20} Kant insists that, since this metaphysics is supposed to be a rational science, the least {\i
addition} of anything drawn from perception to the {\i
universal representation} of self-consciousness would alter it into an {\i
empirical} science, thus compromising its rational purity and its independence from all experience. \u8211? Accordingly, all that is left on this view is the simple representation \u8220?I,\u8221? a representation entirely devoid of content, of which one cannot even say that it is a {\i
concept}, but must say that it is a {\i
mere consciousness}, one that {\i
accompanies every concept}. Now, as Kant argues further, this \u8220?I,\u8221? or, if you prefer, this \u8220?{\i
it}\u8221? ({\i
the thing}) that thinks, takes us no further than the representation of a transcendental subject of thoughts = x, a subject which is known only through the thoughts that are its predicates, and of which, taken in isolation, we cannot {\i
ever} have {\i
the least concept}. This \u8220?I\u8221? has the associated {\i
inconvenience} that, as Kant expresses it, in order to judge anything about it, {\i
we must} every time {\i
already make use of it}, for it is not so much {\i
one representation} by which a particular object is distinguished, as it is rather a {\i
form} of representation in general, in so far as representation can be said to be cognition. \u8211? Now {\i
the paralogism} that rational psychology incurs, as Kant expresses it, consists in this: that {\i
modes} of self-consciousness in thinking are converted into {\i
concepts of the understanding}, as if they were the concepts of an {\i
object}; that that \u8220?I think\u8221? is taken to be a {\i
thinking being}, a {\i
thing-in-itself}; that in this way, because I am present in consciousness always as a {\i
subject}, am indeed as a {\i
singular subject}, {\i
identical} in all the manifoldness of representation, and distinguishing myself from this manifoldness as external to it, the illegitimate inference is thereby drawn that I am a {\i
substance}, and a qualitatively {\i
simple being} on top of that, and a {\i
one}, and a being that {\i
concretely exists independently} of the things of space and time.{\ul
}{\super
21}{\ul
}I have cited this position in some detail because one can clearly recognize in it both the nature of the former {\i
metaphysics of the soul} and also, more to the point, of the {\i
Critique} that put an end to it. \u8211? The former was intent on determining the {\i
abstract essence} of the soul; it went about this starting from observation, and then converting the latter's empirical generalizations, and the determination of purely {\i
external} 12.194reflection attaching to the singularity of the actual, into the form of the {\i
determinations of essence} just cited. \u8211? What Kant generally has in mind here is the state of the metaphysics of his time which, as a rule, stayed at these one-sided determinations with no hint of dialectic; he neither paid attention to, nor examined, the genuinely {\i
speculative} ideas of older philosophers on the concept of spirit. In his {\i
critique} of those determinations he then simply abided by the Humean style of skepticism; that is to say, he fixes on how the \u8220?I\u8221? appears in self-consciousness, but from this \u8220?I,\u8221? since it is its essence ({\i
the thing in itself}) that we want to cognize, he removes everything empirical; nothing then remains but this appearance of the \u8220?I think\u8221? that accompanies all representations and of which we do {\i
not have the slightest concept}. \u8211? It must of course be conceded that, as long as we are not engaged in {\i
comprehending} but confine ourselves to a simple, fixed representation or to a name, we do not have the slightest concept of the \u8220?I,\u8221? or of anything whatever, not even of the concept itself. \u8211? Peculiar indeed is the thought (if one can call it a thought at all) that I must {\i
make use} of the \u8220?I\u8221? in order to judge the \u8220?I.\u8221? The \u8220?I\u8221? that {\i
makes use} of self-consciousness as a means in order to judge: this is indeed an x of which, and also of the relation involved in this \u8220?making use,\u8221? we cannot possibly have the slightest concept. But surely it is laughable to label the nature of this self-consciousness, namely that the \u8220?I\u8221? thinks itself, that the \u8220?I\u8221? cannot be thought without the \u8220?I\u8221? thinking it, an {\i
awkwardness} and, as if it were a fallacy, a {\i
circle}. The awkwardness, the circle, is in fact the relation by which the eternal nature of self-consciousness and of the concept is revealed in immediate, empirical self-consciousness \u8211? is revealed because self-consciousness is precisely {\i
the existent} and therefore {\i
empirically perceivable} pure {\i
concept}; because it is the absolute self-reference that, as parting judgment, makes itself into an intended object and consists in simply making itself thereby into a circle. \u8211? This is an {\i
awkwardness} that a stone does not have. When it is a matter of thinking or judging, the stone does not stand in its own way; it is dispensed from the burden of making use of itself for the task; something else outside it must shoulder that effort. The defect, which these surely barbarous notions place in the fact that in thinking the \u8220?I\u8221? the latter cannot be left out as a subject, then also appears the other way around, in that the \u8220?I\u8221? occurs {\i
only} as the {\i
subject of {\ul
}consciousness}, or in that I can {\i
use} myself only as a {\i
subject}, and no {\i
intuition} is available by which the \u8220?I\u8221? would be {\i
given} as an {\i
object}; but the concept of a thing capable of existence only as a subject does not as yet carry any objective reality with it. \u8211? Now if external intuition as determined in time and space is required for objectivity, and it is this objectivity that 12.195is missed, it is then clear that by objectivity is meant only sensuous reality. But to have risen above such a reality is precisely the condition of thinking and of truth. Of course, if the \u8220?I\u8221? is not grasped conceptually but is taken as a mere representation, in the way we talk about it in everyday consciousness, then it is an abstract self-determination, and not the self-reference that has itself for its subject matter. Then it is only {\i
one} of the extremes, a one-sided subject without its objectivity; or else just an object without subjectivity, which it would be were it not for the awkwardness just touched upon, namely that the thinking subject will not be left out of the \u8220?I\u8221? as object. But as a matter of fact this awkwardness is already found in the other determination, that of the \u8220?I\u8221? as subject; the \u8220?I\u8221? does think {\i
something}, whether itself or something else. This inseparability of the two forms in which the \u8220?I\u8221? opposes itself to itself belongs to the most intimate nature of its concept and of the concept as such; it is precisely what Kant wants to keep away in order to retain what is only a {\i
representation} that does not internally differentiate itself and consequently, of course, is void of concept. Now this kind of conceptual void may well oppose itself to the abstract determinations of reflection or to the categories of the previous metaphysics, for in one-sidedness it stands at the same level with them, though these are in fact on a higher level of thought; but it appears all the more lame and empty when compared with the profounder ideas of ancient philosophy concerning the concept of the soul or of thinking, as for instance the truly speculative ideas of Aristotle.{\ul
}{\super
22} If the Kantian philosophy subjected the categories of reflection to critical investigation, all the more should it have investigated the abstraction of the empty \u8220?I\u8221? that he retained, the supposed idea of the thing-in-itself. The experience of the awkwardness complained of is itself the empirical fact in which the untruth of that abstraction finds expression. The Kantian critique of rational psychology only refers to Mendelssohn's proof of the persistence of the soul,{\ul
}{\super
23} and I now also cite its refutation of that proof because of the oddness of what it adduces against it. Mendelssohn's proof is based on the {\i
simplicity} of the soul, by virtue of which it is supposed {\ul
}to be incapable of alteration in time, {\i
of transition into an other}. Qualitative simplicity is in general the form of {\i
abstraction} earlier considered; as {\i
qualitative} determinateness, it was investigated in the sphere of being and it was then proved that the qualitative, which is as such abstractly self-referring determinateness, is precisely for that reason dialectical, mere transition into an other.{\ul
}{\super
24} In the case of the concept, however, it was shown that, when considered in connection with persistence, indestructibility, imperishableness, it is that which exists for itself, which is eternal, just because it is not {\i
abstract} but {\i
concrete} simplicity \u8211? because it is not a determinateness 12.196that refers to itself abstractly but is the unity {\i
of itself and its other}, and it cannot therefore pass over into this other as if it thereby altered in it; it cannot precisely because it is itself the {\i
other}, the determinateness, and hence in this passing over it only comes to itself.{\ul
}{\super
25} \u8211? Now the Kantian critique opposes to this {\i
qualitative} determination of the unity of the concept a quantitative one. As it says, although the soul is not a manifold of reciprocally external parts and contains no {\i
extensive} magnitude, yet consciousness has {\i
a degree}, and the soul, like {\i
every concretely existing being}, is an {\i
intensive magnitude}; with this magnitude, however, there is posited the possibility of a transition into nothing through {\i
gradual vanishing}.{\ul
}{\super
26} \u8211? Now what is this refutation but the application to spirit of a category {\i
of being}, of {\i
intensive magnitude} \u8211? a determination that has no truth in itself but on the contrary is sublated in the concept? Metaphysics \u8211? even one that restricted itself to the fixed concepts of the understanding without rising to speculation, to the nature of the concept and of the idea \u8211? did have for its aim {\i
the cognition of truth}; it did probe its subject matter to ascertain whether they were {\i
something true} or not, whether substances or phenomena. The triumph of the Kantian critique over this metaphysics consists, on the contrary, in side-lining any investigation that would have {\i
truth} for its aim and this aim itself; it simply does not pose the one question which is of interest, namely whether a determinate subject, in this case the {\i
abstract \u8220?I\u8221? of representation}, has truth in and for itself. But to stay at appearances and at the mere representations of ordinary consciousness is to give up on the concept and on philosophy. Anything beyond that is branded by the Kantian critique as high-flown, something to which reason has no claim. As a matter of fact, the concept does fly high, rising above what has no concept, and the immediate justification for going beyond it is, for one thing, the concept itself, and, for another, {\ul
}on the negative side, the untruth of appearance and of representation, and also of such abstractions as the thing-in-itself and the said \u8220?I\u8221? which is not supposed to be an object to itself. In the context of this logical exposition, it is from the {\i
idea of life} that the idea of spirit has emerged, or what is the same thing, that has demonstrated itself to be the truth of the idea of life. As this result, the idea possesses its truth in and for itself, with which one may then also compare the empirical reality or the appearance of spirit to see how far it accords with it. We have seen regarding {\i
life} that it is the idea,{\ul
}{\super
27} but at the same time it has shown itself not to be as yet the true presentation or the true mode of its existence. For in life, the reality of the idea is {\i
singularity}; {\i
universality} or the genus is the {\i
inwardness}. The truth of life as absolute negative unity 12.197consists, therefore, in this: to sublate the abstract or, what is the same, the immediate singularity, and as {\i
identical} to be self-identical, as genus, to be self-equal. Now this idea is {\i
spirit}. \u8211? In this connection, we may further remark that spirit is here considered in the form that pertains to this idea as logical. For the idea also has other shapes which we may now mention in passing; in these it falls to the concrete sciences of spirit to consider it, namely as {\i
soul}, {\i
consciousness, and spirit as such}. The name \u8220?soul\u8221? was used formerly to mean singular finite spirit in general, and rational or empirical {\i
psychology} was supposed to be synonymous with {\i
doctrine of spirit}. The expression, \u8220?soul,\u8221? evokes an image of it as if it were a {\i
thing} like other things. One enquires regarding its {\i
seat}, the spatial location from which its {\i
forces} operate; still more, how this thing can be {\i
imperishable}, subjected to the conditions of {\i
temporality} yet exempt from alteration in it. The system of {\i
monads} elevates matter by making all of it in principle a soul;{\ul
}{\super
28} on this way of representing it, the soul is an atom like the atoms of matter; the atom that rises from a cup of coffee as vapor is capable in favorable circumstances of developing into a soul; only the {\i
greater} obscurity of its ideation distinguishes it from the kind of thing that is manifestly soul. \u8211? The concept {\i
that is for itself} is necessarily also in {\i
immediate existence}; in this substantial identity with life, immersed in its externality, the concept is the subject matter of {\i
anthropology}. But even anthropology would find alien a metaphysics in which this form of {\i
immediacy} is made into a {\i
soul-thing}, into an atom like the atoms of matter. \u8211? To anthropology must be left only that obscure region where spirit, under influences which were once called {\i
sidereal} and {\i
terrestrial}, lives as a natural spirit in {\i
sympathy} with nature and has presentiments of the latter's {\ul
}alterations in {\i
dreams} and {\i
presentiments}, and indwells the brain, the heart, the liver, and so forth. To the liver, according to Plato, God gave the gift of prophesy above which the self-conscious human is exalted, so that even the irrational part of the soul would be provided for by his bounty and made to share in higher things.{\ul
}{\super
29} To this irrational side belongs further the behavior of figurative representation, and of higher spiritual activity in so far as the latter is subject to the play of an entirely corporeal constitution, of external influences and particular circumstances. This lowest of the concrete shapes in which spirit is sunk into materiality{\ul
}{\super
30} has the one immediately superior to it in consciousness. In this form the free concept, as the \u8220?{\i
I}\u8221? {\i
existing for itself}, is withdrawn from objectivity, but it refers to the latter as {\i
its other}, a subject matter that confronts it. 12.198Since spirit is here no longer as soul, but, in the {\i
certainty} that it has of itself, the {\i
immediacy of being} has for it the significance rather of {\i
a negative}, its identity with itself in the objectivity confronting it is at the same time still only a {\i
reflective shining}, for that objectivity still also has the form of a {\i
being that exists in itself}. This stage is the subject matter of {\i
the phenomenology of spirit} \u8211? a science that stands midway between the science of the natural spirit and of the spirit as such. It considers spirit as it exists {\i
for itself}, but at the same time as {\i
referring to its other} \u8211? an other which, as we have just said, is thereby determined both as an object existing {\i
in itself} and as a negative. The science thus considers spirit as {\i
appearing}, as exhibiting itself in its contrary. But the higher truth of this form is {\i
spirit for itself}. For this spirit, the subject matter{\ul
}{\super
31} which for consciousness exists {\i
in itself} has the form of its own determination, the form of {\i
representation} in general; this spirit, which acts on the subject matter's determinations as on its own, on feelings, on representations and thought, is thus infinite in itself and in its form. The consideration of this stage belongs to the {\i
doctrine of spirit} proper, which would embrace the subject matter of ordinary {\i
empirical psychology} but which, in order to be the science of spirit, must not go about its work empirically but must be conceived scientifically. \u8211? At this stage spirit is {\i
finite} spirit in so far as the {\i
content} of its determinateness is an immediately given content; the science of this finite spirit has to display the course along which it liberates itself from this determinateness and goes on to grasp its truth, the infinite spirit. The {\i
idea of spirit} which is the subject matter of {\i
logic} already stands, on the contrary, inside pure science; it has no need, therefore, to observe {\ul
}spirit's tracing that course, to see how it gets entangled with nature, with immediate determinateness, with matter, or in other words with pictorial representation; this is what the other three sciences investigate. The idea of spirit has this course already behind it, or what is the same, it has it rather ahead of it \u8211? behind in so far as logic is taken as {\i
the final} science; ahead in so far as it is taken as {\i
the first science} from which the idea first passes over into nature. In the logical idea of spirit, therefore, the \u8220?I\u8221? is from the start in the way it has emerged from the concept of nature as the truth of nature, the free concept which in its judgment is itself the subject matter confronting it, {\i
the concept as its idea}. Also in this shape, however, the idea is still not consummated. Although the idea is indeed the free concept that has itself as its subject matter, it is nonetheless {\i
immediate}, and just because it is immediate, it is still the idea in its {\i
subjectivity}, and hence in its finitude in general. It is the {\i
purpose} that ought to realize itself, or the {\i
absolute idea} itself still in its 12.199{\i
appearance}. What the idea {\i
seeks} is the {\i
truth}, this identity of the concept itself and reality; but at first it only {\i
seeks} it; for it is here as it is {\i
at first}, still something {\i
subjective}. Consequently, although the subject matter that is for the concept is here also a given subject matter, it does not enter into the subject as affecting it, or as confronting it with a constitution of its own as subject matter, or as a pictorial representation; on the contrary, the subject transforms it into a {\i
conceptual determination}; it is the concept which is the active principle in it \u8211? which therein refers itself to itself, and, by thus giving itself its reality in the object, finds {\i
truth}. Initially, therefore, the idea is one extreme of a syllogism, the concept that as purpose has itself at first for its subjective reality; the other extreme is the restriction of the subjective, the objective world. The two extremes are identical in that they are the idea. Their unity is, first, that of the concept, a unity which in the one extreme is only {\i
for itself} and in the other only {\i
in itself}. Second, it is reality, abstract in the one extreme and in the other in its concrete externality. \u8211? This unity is now {\i
posited} through cognition, and, because the latter is the subjective idea which as purpose proceeds from itself, it is at first only a {\i
middle term}. \u8211? The knowing subject, through the determinateness of its concept which is the abstract being-for-itself, refers to an external world; nevertheless, it does this in the absolute certainty of itself, in order to elevate its implicit reality, this formal truth, to real truth. It has the {\i
entire essentiality} of the objective world in its concept; its process consists in positing for itself the concrete reality of that world as identical with the {\i
concept}, and conversely in positing the latter as identical with objectivity. {\ul
}Immediately, the idea of appearance is the {\i
theoretical idea}, cognition as such. For to the concept that exists for itself, the objective world immediately has the form of {\i
immediacy} or of {\i
being}, just as that concept is to itself at first only the abstract concept of itself, is still shut up within itself. The concept is therefore only as {\i
form}, of which only its simple determinations of {\i
universality} and {\i
particularity} are the reality that it possesses within, while the singularity or the {\i
determinate determinateness}, the content, is received by it from the outside. \~ {\s6 \afs21
