First Principles of Architecture}, the Eighth Theorem, runs as follows: Theorem: A window must be wide enough that two persons can comfortably stand at it side by side. 
{\line }
\~\~\~\~Proof: For it is common for one to stand at a window with somebody else to view the outside. Now since the fabric architect should in every respect satisfy the main intentions of the owner of the fabric (\u167?1), he must also construct the window in such a manner that two persons can comfortably stand at it side by side. {\i
Q.E.D}.{\ul
}{\super
61} 
{\line }
\~\~\~\~From the same author's {\i
Principles of Fortification}, the Second Theorem: 
{\line }
\~\~\~\~Theorem: If the enemy encamps in the vicinity, and the attempt at a rush to relieve the siege is expected, a line of circumvallation must be drawn around the whole siege. Proof: Lines of circumvallation prevent anyone from penetrating into an area from outside (\u167?311). But those who wish to relieve the area of siege will attempt to penetrate the area from outside. If, therefore, one wants to keep them out, a line of circumvallation must be drawn around the area. Therefore, if the enemy encamps in the vicinity and the attempt at a rush to relieve the siege is expected, the area must be enclosed in lines of circumvallation. {\i
Q.E.D}.{\ul
