{\b
Chapter 3 The absolute idea12.236\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\~ The absolute idea has shown itself to be the identity of the theoretical and the practical idea, each of which, of itself still one-sided, possesses the idea only as a sought-for beyond and unattained goal; each is therefore a {\i
synthesis of striving}, each possessing as well as {\i
not} possessing the idea within it, passing over from one thought to the other without bringing the two together but remaining fixed in the contradiction of the two. The absolute idea, as the rational concept that in its reality only rejoins itself, is by virtue of this immediacy of its objective identity, on the one hand, a turning back to {\i
life}; on the other hand, it has equally sublated this form of its immediacy and harbors the most extreme opposition within. The concept is not only {\i
soul}, but free subjective concept that exists for itself and therefore has {\i
personality} \u8211? the practical objective concept that is determined in and for itself and is as person impenetrable, atomic subjectivity \u8211? but which is not, just the same, exclusive singularity; it is rather explicitly {\i
universality} and {\i
cognition}, and in its other has {\i
its own} objectivity for its subject matter. All the rest is error, confusion, opinion, striving, arbitrariness, and transitoriness; the absolute idea alone is {\i
being}, imperishable {\i
life}, {\i
self-knowing truth}, and is {\i
all truth}. It is the sole subject matter and content of philosophy. Since it contains {\i
all} determinateness within it, and its essence consists in returning through its self-determination and particularization back to itself, it has various shapes, and the business of philosophy is to recognize it in these. Nature and spirit are in general different modes of exhibiting {\i
its existence}, art and religion its different modes of apprehending itself and giving itself appropriate existence. Philosophy has the same content and the same purpose as art and religion, but it is the highest mode of apprehending the absolute idea, because its mode, that of the concept, is the highest. Hence it seizes those shapes of real and ideal finitude, as well of infinity and holiness, and comprehends them and itself. The derivation and cognition of these particular modes are now the further business of the particular {\ul
}philosophical sciences. Also the {\i
logicality} of the absolute idea 12.237can be called a {\i
mode} of it; but {\i
mode} signifies a {\i
particular} kind, a {\i
determinateness} of form, whereas the logicality of the idea is the universal mode in which all particular modes are sublated and enveloped. The logical idea is the idea itself in its pure essence, the idea which is enclosed in simple identity within its concept and in {\i
reflective shining} has as yet to step into a form-determinateness. The Logic thus exhibits the self-movement of the absolute idea only as the original {\i
word}, a word which is an {\i
utterance}, but one that in being externally uttered has immediately vanished again. The idea is, therefore, only in this self-determination of {\i
apprehending} {\i
itself}; it is in {\i
pure thought}, where difference is not yet {\i
otherness}, but is and remains perfectly transparent to itself. \u8211? The logical idea thus has itself, as the {\i
infinite form}, for its content \u8211? form that constitutes the opposite of {\i
content} in as much as the latter is the form determination that has withdrawn into itself and has been so sublated in identity that this concrete identity stands over against the identity developed as form; the content has the shape of an other and of something given as against the form that as such stands simply in {\i
reference}, and whose determinateness is posited at the same time as reflective shine. \u8211? More exactly, the absolute idea itself has only this for its content, namely that the form determination is its own completed totality, the pure content. Now the {\i
determinateness} of the idea and the entire course traversed by this determinateness has constituted the subject matter of the science of logic, and out of this course the absolute idea has come forth {\i
for itself}; thus to be for itself, however, has shown itself to amount to this, namely that determinateness does not have the shape of a {\i
content}, but that it is simply as {\i
form}, and that accordingly the idea is the absolutely {\i
universal idea}. What is left to be considered here, therefore, is thus not a content as such, but the universal character of its form \u8211? that is, {\i
method}. {\i
Method} may appear at first to be just the {\i
manner} in which cognition proceeds, and this is in fact its nature. But as method this manner of proceeding is not only a {\i
modality} of {\i
being determined in and for itself}; it is a modality of cognition, and as such is posited as determined by the {\i
concept} and as form, since form is the soul of all objectivity and all otherwise determined content has its truth in form alone. If the content is again assumed as given to the method and of a nature of its own, then method, so understood, is just like the logical realm in general a merely {\i
external} form. But against this assumption appeal can be made, not only to the fundamental concept of what constitutes logic, but to the entire logical course in which all the shapes of a given content and of objects came up for consideration. This course has shown the transitoriness and the untruth of {\ul
}all such shapes; also that no given object is capable of being the foundation to which the absolute form would relate as only an external and accidental 12.238determination; that, on the contrary, it is the absolute form that has proved itself to be the absolute foundation and the ultimate truth. For this course the method has resulted as the {\i
absolutely self-knowing concept}, as the {\i
concept that has} the absolute, both as subjective and objective, {\i
as its subject matter}, and consequently as the pure correspondence of the concept and its reality, a concrete existence that is the concept itself. Accordingly, what is to be considered as method here is only the movement of the {\i
concept} itself. We already know the nature of this movement, but it now has, {\i
first}, the added significance that the {\i
concept is all}, and that its movement is the {\i
universal absolute activity}, the self-determining and self-realizing movement. The method is therefore to be acknowledged as the universal, internal and external mode, free of restrictions, and as the absolutely infinite force to which no object that may present itself as something external, removed from reason and independent of it, could offer resistance, or be of a particular nature opposite to it, and could not be penetrated by it. It is therefore {\i
soul and substance}, and nothing is conceived and known in its truth unless {\i
completely subjugated to the method}; it is the method proper to each and every fact because its activity is the concept. This is also the truer meaning of its {\i
universality}; according to the universality of reflection, it is taken only as the method for all things; but according to the universality of the idea, it is both the manner of cognition, of the concept {\i
subjectively} aware of itself, and the {\i
objective} manner, or rather the {\i
substantiality} {\i
of things \u8211?} that is, of concepts as they first appear as {\i
others} to {\i
representation} and {\i
reflection}. It is therefore not only the highest {\i
force} of reason, or rather its {\i
sole} and absolute {\i
force}, but also reason's highest and sole {\i
impulse} to find and recognize {\i
itself through itself in all things}. \u8211? {\i
Second}, here we also have the {\i
distinction} of the {\i
method from the concept as such}, the {\i
particularization} of the method. As the concept was considered for itself, it appeared in its immediacy; the {\i
reflection}, or {\i
the concept considering it}, fell on the side of {\i
our} knowledge. The method is this knowledge itself, for which the concept is not only as subject matter but is as its own subjective act, the {\i
instrument} and the means of cognitive activity, distinct from this activity and yet the activity's own essentiality. In cognition as enquiry, the method likewise occupies the position of an {\i
instrument}, as a means that stands on the side of the subject, connecting it with the object. The subject in this syllogism is one extreme, the object is the other, and in conclusion the subject unites through its method with the object without however {\i
uniting with itself} there. The extremes remain diverse, because {\ul
}subject, method, and object are not posited as {\i
the one identical concept}; the syllogism is therefore always the 12.239formal syllogism; the premise in which the subject posits the form on its side as its method is an {\i
immediate} determination and contains therefore the determinations of the form \u8211? as we have seen, of definition, division, and so forth{\ul
}{\super
71} \u8211? as matters of fact {\i
found ready-made in the subject}. In true cognition, on the contrary, method is not only an aggregate of certain determinations, but the determinateness in-and-for-itself of the concept, and the concept is the middle term only because it equally has the significance of the objective; in the conclusion, therefore, the objective does not attain only an external determinateness by virtue of the method, but is posited rather in its identity with the subjective concept. 1. Accordingly, what constitutes the method are the determinations of the concept itself and their connections, and these we must now examine in the significance that they have as determinations of the method. \u8211? In this, we must begin from the {\i
beginning}. We spoke of this beginning at the very beginning of the Logic,{\ul
}{\super
72} and also in connection with subjective cognition,{\ul
}{\super
73} and we showed that, when not performed arbitrarily and in the absence of categorial sensitivity, though it may seem to present many difficulties, it is nevertheless of an extremely simple nature. Because it is the beginning, its content is an {\i
immediate}, but one that has the meaning and the form of {\i
abstract universality}. Or be it a content of {\i
being}, or of {\i
essence} or of the {\i
concept}, in as much as it is {\i
something immediate}, it is {\i
assumed}, {\i
found in advance}, {\i
assertoric}. But first of all it is not an immediate of {\i
sense-intuition} or of {\i
representation}, but of {\i
thought}, which because of its immediacy can also be called a supersensuous, {\i
inner intuiting}. The immediate of sense-intuition is a {\i
manifold} and a {\i
singular}. Cognition, on the contrary, is a thinking that conceptualizes; its beginning, therefore, is also {\i
only in the element of thought}, a {\i
simple} and a {\i
universal}. \u8211? We spoke of this form earlier, in connection with definition.{\ul
}{\super
74} At the beginning of finite cognition universality is likewise recognized as an essential determination, but only as thought \u8211? and concept determination in opposition to being. In fact this {\i
first} universality is an {\i
immediate} universality, and for that reason it has equally the significance of {\i
being}, for being is precisely this abstract self-reference. Being has no need of further derivation, as if it came to the abstract element of definition only because taken from the intuition of the senses or elsewhere, and in so far as it can be pointed at. This pointing and deriving involve a {\ul
}mediation that is more than a mere beginning, and is a mediation of a kind that does not belong to the comprehension of thought, but is rather the 12.240elevation of representation, of empirical and ratiocinative consciousness, to the standpoint of thinking. According to the currently accepted opposition of thought, or concept, and being, it passes as a very important truth that no being belongs as yet to thought as thought, and that being has a ground of its own independent of thought. But the simple determination of {\i
being} is in itself so poor that, if for that reason alone, not much fuss ought to be made about it; the universal is immediately itself this immediate because, as abstract, it is also the abstract self-reference which is being. In fact, the demand that being should be exhibited has a further, inner meaning in which more is at issue than just this abstract determination; implied in it is the demand for {\i
the realization of the concept}, a realization that is missing at the {\i
beginning} itself but is rather the goal and the business of the entire subsequent development of cognition. Further, in as much as the {\i
content} of the beginning is to be justified and authenticated as something true or correct by being exhibited in inner or outer perception, it is no longer the {\i
form} of universality as such that is meant, but its determinateness, about which more in a moment. The authentication of the {\i
determinate content} with which the beginning is made seems to lie {\i
behind} it, but is in fact to be regarded as an advance, in so far as it is a matter of conceptual cognition. The beginning, therefore, has for the method no other determinateness than that of being the simple and universal; this is precisely the {\i
determinateness} that makes it deficient. Universality is the pure, simple concept, and the method, as the consciousness of this concept, is aware that universality is only a moment and that in it the concept is still not determined in and for itself. But with this consciousness that would want to carry the beginning further only for the sake of method, the method is only a formal procedure posited in external reflection. Where the method, however, is the objective and immanent form, the immediate character of the beginning must be a lack {\i
inherent} in the beginning itself, which must be endowed with the impulse to carry itself further. But in the absolute method the universal has the value not of a mere abstraction but of the objective universal, that is, the universal that is {\i
in itself} the {\i
concrete totality}, but a totality as yet not {\i
posited}, not yet {\i
for itself}. Even the abstract universal is as such, when considered conceptually, that is, in its truth, not just anything {\i
simple}, but is, as {\i
abstract}, already {\i
posited} afflicted by a {\i
negation}. For this reason also {\i
there is} nothing so simple and so abstract, be it in {\i
actuality} or in {\i
thought}, as is commonly imagined. Anything as simple as that is a mere {\i
presumption} that {\ul
}has its ground solely in the lack of awareness of what is actually there. \u8211? We said earlier that the beginning is made with the immediate;{\ul
}{\super
75} the {\i
immediacy of the universal} is the same as what is here 12.241expressed as the {\i
in-itself} that is without {\i
being-for-itself}. \u8211? One may well say, therefore, that every beginning must be made with the {\i
absolute}, just as every advance is only the exposition of it, in so far as {\i
implicit in existence} is the concept.{\ul
}{\super
76} But because the absolute exists at first only {\i
implicitly}, {\i
in} {\i
itself}, it equally is {\i
not} the absolute nor the posited concept, and also not the idea, for the {\i
in-itself} is only an abstract, one-sided moment, and this is what they are. The advance is not, therefore, a kind of {\i
superfluity}; this is what it would be if that which is at the beginning were already the absolute; the advance consists rather in this, that the universal determines itself and is the universal {\i
for itself}, that is, equally a singular and a subject. Only in its consummation is it the absolute. It may also be mentioned that a beginning which is {\i
in itself} a concrete totality may as such also be {\i
free} and its immediacy have the determination of an {\i
external existence}; the {\i
germ} of {\i
anything living}, and {\i
subjective} purpose in general, have shown themselves to be such beginnings; hence both are themselves {\i
impulses}. The non-spiritual and inanimate, on the contrary, are the concrete concept only as {\i
real possibility}; {\i
cause} is the highest stage in which the concrete concept has, as the beginning in the sphere of necessity, an immediate existence; but it is not yet a subject that maintains itself as such in the course of its effective realization. The {\i
sun}, for instance, and in general all things inanimate, are determinate concrete existences in which real possibility remains an {\i
inner} totality; the moments of the latter are not {\i
posited} in them in subjective form and therefore, in so far as they are realized, they attain concrete existence through {\i
other} corporeal individuals. 2. The concrete totality which makes the beginning possesses as such, within it, the beginning of the advance and development. As concrete, it is {\i
differentiated in itself}, but because of its {\i
initial immediacy}, this first {\i
differentiation} is to start with a {\i
diversity}. However, as self-referring universality, as subject, the immediate is also the {\i
unity} of this diversity. \u8211? This reflection is the first stage of the forward movement \u8211? the emergence of {\i
non-indifference}, {\i
judgment}, and {\i
determining} in general. Essential is that the method find, and recognize, the {\i
determination} of the universal within it. Whatever in this abstractive generation of the universal is left out of the concrete is then picked up, still externally, by the finite cognition of the {\ul
}understanding. This is how the latter operates. The absolute method, on the contrary, does not behave in this manner of external reflection but takes the determinate from its subject matter, for it is itself its immanent principle and its soul. \u8211? This is what Plato demanded of cognition, 12.242that {\i
it should consider things in and for themselves}; on the one hand, that it should consider them in their universality; on the other hand, that it should not stray away from them while it grasps at circumstances, examples, and comparisons, but, on the contrary, should keep only them in view before it and bring to consciousness what is immanent in them. \u8211? To this extent the method of absolute cognition is {\i
analytic}. That the method finds the further determinations of its initial universal simply and solely in this universal, constitutes the concept's absolute objectivity, of which the method is the certainty. \u8211? Equally so, however, is the method {\i
synthetic}, for its subject matter, while immediately determined as the {\i
simple universal}, through the determinateness which it has in its very immediacy and universality, proves to be an {\i
other}. Yet this connection in diversity that the subject matter is thus in itself, is no longer a synthesis as understood in finite cognition; the no less thoroughly analytic determination of the subject matter, the fact that the connection is within the {\i
concept}, already distinguishes it fully from the latter synthesis. This no less synthetic than analytic moment of the {\i
judgment} through which the initial universal determines itself from within itself as the {\i
other of itself} is to be called the {\i
dialectical moment}. {\i
Dialectic} is one of those ancient sciences that have been the most misjudged in the metaphysics of the moderns, and in general also by popular philosophy, both ancient and recent. Diogenes La\u235?rtius said of Plato that, just as Thales was the founder of natural philosophy and Socrates of moral philosophy, so Plato was the founder of the third of the sciences that belong to philosophy, of {\i
dialectic} \u8211? a contribution for which he was highly esteemed by the ancients but that often goes quite unnoticed by those who have the most to say about him.{\ul
}{\super
77} Dialectic has often been regarded as an {\i
art}, as if it rested on a subjective {\i
talent} and did not belong to the objectivity of the concept. What shape it received in Kantian philosophy, and with what result, has already been indicated in representative examples of that philosophy's view.{\ul
}{\super
78} It must be regarded as an infinitely important step that dialectic is once more being {\ul
}recognized as necessary to reason, although the result that must be drawn from it is the opposite than Kant drew. When dialectic is not presented, as it generally is, as something incidental, it usually assumes the following more precise form. It is shown of a subject matter or other (for instance: world, movement, point, and so on) that a certain determination accrues to it (for instance, in the order of the just mentioned examples: finitude in space or time, being at {\i
this} place, absolute negation of space), also the opposite determinations can then just as necessarily be shown to accrue to it (for example: infinity in space and time, not-being at this place, reference to space and hence spatiality). The older Eleatic school directed its dialectic especially against motion; Plato commonly did it against accepted notions and 12.243concepts of his time, in particular those of the Sophists, but also against the pure categories and the determinations of reflection; the later and more sophisticated form of skepticism extended it not only to the immediate so-called facts of consciousness and the maxims of ordinary life, but also to all scientific concepts. Now the conclusion drawn from this kind of dialectic is in general the {\i
contradiction} and {\i
nullity} of the asserted claims. But this can happen in two ways \u8211? either in the objective sense, that the {\i
subject matter} that thus contradicts itself internally cancels itself and is a non-thing (this was, for instance, the conclusion of the Eleatics, who denied the {\i
truth} of the world, of movement, of the point); or in the subjective sense, that {\i
cognition is deficient}. Now understood in this last subjective sense, the conclusion may be taken in two further ways. It may mean that it is this dialectic itself that generates the artifice of an illusion. This is the common view of the so-called healthy common sense that takes its stand on the evidence of the {\i
senses} and on {\i
customary notions} and {\i
claims}, at times quietly, like Diogenes the cynic did, who demonstrated the vacuity of the dialectic of motion by silently walking up and down; but often by getting itself all worked up, declaring that dialectic is mere foolery or, when important ethical matters are at issue, the criminal attempt at unsettling essentially solid norms and providing excuses for the wicked \u8211? a view we see directed in the Socratic dialectic against that of the Sophists, with an ire that, turned into the opposite direction, even cost Socrates his life. As for the vulgar refutation that opposes to thinking, as Diogenes did, {\i
sensuous consciousness} and in this latter believes that it finds the truth, this we must leave to itself; but in so far as dialectic sublates ethical determinations, we must have confidence in reason that it will know how to reinstate them, but reinstate them in their truth and in the consciousness of their right, though also of their limitations. \u8211? Yet another view is that the result of subjective nullity has nothing to do with dialectic {\ul
}itself, but that it affects the cognition against which it is directed and, in the view of skepticism and likewise of the Kantian philosophy, {\i
cognition in general}. The fundamental prejudice here is that dialectic has {\i
only a negative result} \u8211? a point about which more in a moment. First, regarding the said {\i
form} in which dialectic usually makes its appearance, it is to be observed that according to that form the dialectic and its result affect a {\i
subject matter} which is previously assumed or also the subjective {\i
cognition} of it, and declare either the latter or the subject matter to be null and void, while, on the contrary, no attention is given to the {\i
determinations} which are exhibited in the subject matter as in a {\i
third} thing and presupposed as valid for themselves. To have called attention to this uncritical procedure has been the infinite merit of the Kantian 12.244philosophy, and in so doing to have given the impetus to the restoration of logic and dialectic understood as the examination of {\i
thought determinations in and for themselves}. The subject matter, as it is apart from thought and conceptualization, is a picture representation or also a name; it is in the determinations of thought and of the concept that it {\i
is} what it {\i
is}. In fact, therefore, everything rests on these determinations; they are the true subject matter and content of reason, and anything else that might be understood by subject matter and content in distinction from them has meaning only through them and in them. It must not therefore be taken as the fault of a subject matter or of the cognition that these determinations, because of what they are and the way they are externally joined, prove to be dialectical. On this assumption, the subject matter and the cognition are imagined to be a subject on which the {\i
determinations} are brought to bear, in the form of predicates, properties, or self-subsistent universals, as fixed and independently correct, so that these determinations are brought into dialectical relations and incur contradiction only by extraneous and contingent conjunction in and by a third thing. But this kind of external and fixed subject of imagination and understanding, and also these abstract determinations, cannot be regarded as {\i
ultimates}, as secure and permanent substrates. On the contrary, they are to be regarded as themselves immediate, precisely the kind of presuppositions and starting points which, as we have shown above,{\ul
}{\super
79} must succumb to dialectic in and for themselves, because they are to be taken as {\i
in themselves} the concept. The same applies to all oppositions that are assumed as fixed, as for example the finite and the infinite, the singular and the universal. These are not in contradiction through some external conjoining; on the {\ul
}contrary, as an examination of their nature shows, they are a transition in and for themselves; the synthesis and the subject in which they appear is the product of their concept's own reflection. If a consideration that avoids the concept stops short at their external relation, isolates them and leaves them as fixed presuppositions, it is the concept that, on the contrary, will fix its sight on them, move them as their soul and bring out their dialectic. Now this is the very standpoint indicated above{\ul
}{\super
80} from which a universal {\i
prius}, {\i
considered in and for itself}, proves to be the other of itself. Taken quite generally, this determination can be taken to mean that what is at first {\i
immediate} is therewith posited as {\i
mediated}, as {\i
referred} to an other, or that the universal is posited as a particular. The {\i
second} universal that has thereby arisen is thus the {\i
negative} of that first and, in view of subsequent developments, the {\i
first negative}. From this negative side, the immediate has {\i
perished} in the other; but the other is essentially not an {\i
empty negative}, the {\i
nothing} 12.245which is normally taken to be the result of dialectic, but is rather the {\i
other of the first}, the {\i
negative} of the {\i
immediate}; it is therefore determined as the {\i
mediated} \u8211? {\i
contains} as such the {\i
determination of the first} in it. The first is thus essentially {\i
preserved} and contained also in the other. \u8211? To hold fast to the positive in {\i
its} negative, to the content of the presupposition in the result, this is the most important factor in rational cognition; what is more, it takes only the simplest of reflections to be convinced of the absolute truth and necessity of this requirement, and as for examples of proofs that testify to this, the whole Logic consists of such proofs. So what we now have, taken first or also immediately, is the {\i
mediated}, also a {\i
simple} determination, for the {\i
first} has perished in it, and only the {\i
second} is therefore at hand. Now since the first is {\i
contained} in the second, and this second is the truth of the first, this unity of the two can be expressed in the form of a proposition in which the immediate is placed as the subject but the mediated as its predicate; for example, \u8220?the finite is infinite,\u8221? \u8220?one is many,\u8221? \u8220?the singular is the universal.\u8221? The inadequacy of the form of such propositions and judgments is however obvious. In connection with {\i
judgment} it was shown that its form in general, and most of all the immediate form of the {\i
positive} judgment, is incapable of holding within its grasp the speculative content and the truth.{\ul
}{\super
81} Its closest complement, the {\i
negative} judgment, would have to be brought in at least in equal measure. In judgment the first, as subject, conveys the reflective semblance of an independent subsistence, whereas it is in fact sublated in the predicate as in its other; this negation is indeed contained in the content of the above {\ul
}propositions, but their positive form contradicts the content; consequently, what is contained in them is not posited \u8211? whereas this was precisely the intent behind the use of a proposition. The second determination, the {\i
negative} or {\i
mediated} determination, is moreover at the same time {\i
the one that mediates}. At first it may be taken as a simple determination, but its truth is that it is a {\i
reference} or {\i
relation}; for it is the negative, {\i
but the negative of the positive}, and it includes this positive within itself. It is the {\i
other}, therefore, not of a one to which it is indifferent; in that case it would not be an {\i
other}, nor a reference or relation. It is rather the {\i
other in itself}, the {\i
other of an other}; hence it includes {\i
its} own other within itself and is consequently the {\i
contradiction}, the {\i
posited dialectic}, {\i
of itself}. \u8211? Because the first or the immediate is the concept {\i
in itself} or {\i
implicitly}, and therefore is the negative also only {\i
implicitly}, the dialectical moment in it consists in the positing of the {\i
difference} that is {\i
implicitly} contained in it. 12.246The second is on the contrary itself the {\i
determinate}, the {\i
difference} or relation; hence the dialectical moment consists in its case in the positing of the {\i
unity} contained within it. \u8211? For this reason, if the negative, the determinate, relation, judgment, and all the determinations falling under this second moment, do not appear by themselves already as contradiction, as dialectical, this is solely a defect on the part of thinking that fails to bring its thoughts together. For the material, the {\i
opposed} determinations in {\i
one connection}, are already {\i
posited}, already present for thought. But formal thinking makes identity its law, lets the contradictory content that it has before it fall into the sphere of representation, in space and time, where the contradictory is held in {\i
external moments}, next to and following each other, parading before consciousness without reciprocal contact. The firm principle that formal thinking lays down for itself here is that contradiction cannot be thought. But in fact the thought of contradiction is the essential moment of the concept. Formal thought does in fact think it, only it at once looks away from it and stating its principle it only passes over from it into abstract negation. Now the negativity just considered constitutes the {\i
turning point} of the movement of the concept. It is the {\i
simple point of the negative} self-{\i
reference}, the innermost source of all activity, of living and spiritual self-movement; it is the dialectical soul which everything true possesses and through which alone it is true; for on this subjectivity alone rests the sublation of the opposition between concept and reality, and the unity which is truth. \u8211? The {\i
second} negative at which we have arrived, the negative of the negative, is this sublating of contradiction, and it too, just like contradiction, is not an {\i
act of external reflection}; for it is on the contrary the {\i
innermost}, {\ul
}{\i
objective moment} of the life of spirit by virtue of which a {\i
subject} is a {\i
person}, is {\i
free}. \u8211? The {\i
self-reference of the negative} is to be regarded as the {\i
second premise} of the entire syllogism. If the terms {\i
analytic} and {\i
synthetic} are used as opposites, the {\i
first} premise may be regarded as the {\i
analytic} moment, for in it the immediate relates to its other immediately and therefore {\i
passes over}, or rather has passed over, into it \u8211? though this connection, as already remarked, is for this very reason also synthetic, for it is {\i
its} other that it passes over into. The second premise considered here may be defined as {\i
synthetic}, because it is the connection of the {\i
differentiated}, {\i
as differentiated}, to {\i
that from which it is differentiated}. \u8211? Just as the first premise is the moment of {\i
universality} and {\i
communication}, so is the second determined by {\i
singularity} \u8211? a singularity which in referring to the other is at first exclusive, for itself, and different. 12.247The negative appears as the {\i
mediating} factor, because it holds itself and the immediate of which it is the negation within itself. In so far as these two determinations are taken as referring to each other externally in some relation or other, the negative is only the {\i
formal} mediating factor; but, as absolute negativity, the negative moment of absolute mediation is the unity which is subjectivity and soul. In this turning point of the method, the course of cognition returns at the same time back into itself. This negativity is as self-sublating contradiction the {\i
restoration} of the {\i
first immediacy}, of simple universality; for the other of the other, the negative of the negative, is immediately the {\i
positive}, the {\i
identical}, the {\i
universal}. In the whole course, if one at all cares {\i
to count}, this {\i
second} immediate is {\i
third} to the first immediate and the mediated. But it is also {\i
third} to the first or formal negative and to the absolute negativity or second negative; now in so far as that first negative is already the second term, the term counted as {\i
third} can also be counted as {\i
fourth}, and instead of a {\i
triplicity}, the abstract form may also be taken to be a {\i
quadruplicity}; in this way the negative or the {\i
difference} is counted as a {\i
duality}. \u8211? The third or the fourth is in general the unity of the first and the second moment, of the immediate and the mediated. \u8211? That it is this {\i
unity}, or that the entire form of the method is a {\i
triplicity}, is indeed nothing but the merely superficial, external side of cognition; but to have also demonstrated this superficiality, and to have done it in the context of a specific application (for the abstract form of number has been around for a long time, as is well known, but without conceptual comprehension and therefore without any result) is again to be regarded as an infinite merit of the Kantian philosophy.{\ul
}{\super
82} The {\i
syllogism}, or the threefold, has always been recognized to be the universal {\ul
}form of reason; but it has had in general the value of a wholly external form that does not determine the nature of the content; moreover, since in its formalism it gets caught up in the understanding's determination of mere {\i
identity}, it lacks the essential {\i
dialectical} moment of {\i
negativity}; and yet this moment enters into the triplicity of the determinations, because the third term is the unity of the two first determinations and these, since they are diverse, can be in unity only {\i
as sublated}. \u8211? Formalism, it is true, has also seized hold of triplicity, attending to its empty {\i
schema}; the shallow nonsense and the barrenness of the so-called {\i
construction} of modern philosophy, that consists in nothing but fastening that formal schema everywhere for the sake of external order, with no concept or immanent determination, 12.248has rendered that form tedious and has given it a bad name. Yet the insipidity of this use cannot rob it of its inner worth, and the fact that the shape of reason was discovered, albeit without conceptual comprehension at first, is always to be highly valued. Now, on closer examination, the {\i
third} is the immediate, but the immediate {\i
through sublation of mediation}, the simple through the {\i
sublating of difference}, the positive through the sublating of the negative; it is the concept that has realized itself through its otherness, and through the sublating of this reality has rejoined itself and has restored its absolute reality, its {\i
simple} self-reference. This {\i
result} is therefore the {\i
truth}. It is {\i
just as much} immediacy {\i
as} mediation \u8211? though these forms of judgments, that the third {\i
is} immediacy and mediation, or that it {\i
is the unity} of the two, are not capable of grasping it, for it is not a dormant third but, exactly like this unity, self-mediating movement and activity. \u8211? Just as that with which we began was the {\i
universal}, so the result is the {\i
singular}, the {\i
concrete}, the {\i
subject}; what the former is {\i
in itself}, the latter is now equally {\i
for itself}: the universal is {\i
posited} in the subject. The two first moments of triplicity are {\i
abstract}, untrue moments that are dialectical for that very reason, and through this their negativity make themselves into the subject. {\i
For us} at first, the concept itself is {\i
both} the universal that exists in itself {\i
and} the negative that exists for itself, and also the third term that exists in and for itself, the {\i
universal} that runs through all the moments of the syllogism; but this third is the conclusion in which the concept mediates itself with itself through its negativity and is thereby posited {\i
for itself} as the {\i
universal} and the {\i
identity of its moments}. Now this result, as the whole that has withdrawn into itself and is {\i
identical} with itself, has given itself again the form of {\i
immediacy}. Consequently, it is now itself all that {\i
the starting point} had determined itself to be. As simple self-reference it is a universal, and in this universal the {\i
negativity} that {\ul
}constituted its dialectic and mediation has likewise withdrawn into {\i
simple determinateness}, which can again be a beginning. It may seem at first that this cognition of the result is an analysis of it and would therefore have to dissect these determinations again, and the course that it went through in order to come to be \u8211? the course that we have examined. But if the subject matter were in fact treated analytically in this manner, it would belong to that stage of the idea considered above,{\ul
}{\super
83} a mode of cognition that searches for its subject matter and only states of it what it {\i
is}, without the necessity of its concrete identity and of its concept. But the method of truth that comprehends the subject matter, though analytic as we have seen, since it remains strictly within the concept, is however equally synthetic, for through 12.249the concept the subject matter is determined as dialectical and as other.{\ul
}{\super
84} On the new foundation that the result has now constituted as the subject matter, the method remains the same as in the preceding subject matter. The difference concerns solely the status of the foundation as such; although it is certainly still a foundation, its immediacy is only {\i
form}, since it was a result as well; hence its determinateness as content is no longer something merely taken up but is {\i
deduced} and {\i
proved}. It is here that the {\i
content} of cognition first enters as such into the circle of consideration, because as deduced it now belongs to the method. The method itself expands with this moment into a {\i
system}. \u8211? With respect to content, the beginning has to be for the method at first wholly indeterminate; to this extent the method appears as the merely formal soul, for which and by which the beginning was determined simply and solely only according to form, that is to say, as the immediate and universal. In the course of the movement we have indicated, the subject matter has received a {\i
determinateness} for itself \u8211? and this determinateness is a {\i
content}, for the negativity that has withdrawn into simplicity is the sublated form, and stands as simple determinateness over against its development, and in the first instance against its very opposition to universality. Now since this determinateness is the proximate truth of the indeterminate beginning, it denounces the incompleteness of the latter, and it also denounces the method itself which, starting from that beginning, was only formal. This can now be expressed as the henceforth determinate demand that the beginning, since as against the determinateness of the result it is itself something determinate, ought to be taken not as immediate, but as mediated and deduced. This may appear as the demand for an infinite {\i
retrogression} in proof and deduction; just as from the newly obtained {\ul
}beginning a result likewise emerges as the method runs its course, so that the movement would roll on {\i
forwards} to infinity as well. It has been repeatedly shown that the infinite progression as such belongs to a reflection void of concept; the absolute method, which has the concept for its soul and content, cannot lead into it. Even such beginnings as {\i
being}, {\i
essence}, {\i
universality}, may seem at first to be of the kind that possess the full universality and complete absence of content that is required for an entirely formal beginning, such as the beginning is supposed to be, and therefore not to require or allow, as absolutely first beginnings, further regress. Since they refer purely to themselves, they are immediate and indeterminate, and so they do not of course have in them the difference which is straightaway posited in some other beginning between the universality of its form and its content. But the very indeterminacy which these logical beginnings have as their sole content 12.250is what constitutes their determinateness; this determinateness consists in their negativity, as sublated mediation; the particularity of this negativity gives a particularity also to their indeterminacy, and it is by virtue of it that {\i
being}, {\i
essence}, and {\i
universality}, are differentiated. Now the determinateness that accrues to them when taken for themselves is their {\i
immediate determinateness}, and this is just as immediate as that of any content and in need, therefore, of derivation; for the method it is a matter of indifference whether the determinateness is taken as determinateness of {\i
form} or of {\i
content}. That it gives itself a determination by the first of its results does not mean that, in fact, it is thereby set on a new footing; it remains neither more nor less formal than before. For since the method is the absolute form, the concept that knows itself and everything as concept, there is no content that would stand out over against it and determine it as a one-sided external form. Hence, just as the lack of content of the said beginnings does not make them absolute beginnings, so too it is not the content that would as such lead the method into the infinite progress forwards or backwards. In one respect, the {\i
determinateness} that the method generates for itself in its result is the moment through which it is self-mediation and converts {\i
the immediate into a mediated beginning}. But conversely, it is through that determinateness that this mediation of the method runs its course; it goes {\i
through} a {\i
content}, as through a seeming {\i
other} of itself, back to its beginning, in such a way that it does not merely restore that beginning, albeit as {\i
determinate}, but that the result is equally the sublated determinateness, and hence also the restoration of the first immediacy in which it began. This it accomplishes as {\i
a system of totality}. We now have to consider it in this determination. {\ul
}The determinateness which was the result is, as we have shown,{\ul
}{\super
85} itself a new beginning because of the form of simplicity into which it has withdrawn; since this beginning is distinguished from the one preceding it by this very determinateness, cognition rolls onwards from content to content. First of all, this forward movement determines itself in that it begins from simple determinacies, and the following become ever {\i
richer and more concrete}. For the result contains its beginning and its course has enriched it with a new determinateness. The {\i
universal} constitutes the foundation; the advance is not to be taken, therefore, as a {\i
flowing} from {\i
other} to {\i
other}. In the absolute method, the concept {\i
maintains} itself in its otherness, the universal in its particularization, in judgment and reality; at each stage of further determination, the universal elevates the whole mass of its preceding content, not only not losing anything through its dialectical advance, or leaving it behind, but, on the contrary, carrying with itself all that it has gained, inwardly enriched and compressed. 12.251 This {\i
expansion} may be regarded as the moment of content, and in the whole as the first premise; the universal is {\i
communicated} to the wealth of content, is immediately received in it. But the relation has also a second, negative or dialectical side. The enrichment proceeds in the {\i
necessity} of the concept, it is contained by it, and every determination is a reflection into itself. Each new stage of {\i
exteriorization}, that is, of {\i
further determination}, is also a withdrawing into itself, and the greater the {\i
extension}, just as dense is the {\i
intensity}. The richest is therefore the most concrete and the {\i
most subjective}, and that which retreats to the simplest depth is the mightiest and the most all-encompassing. The highest and most intense point is the {\i
pure personality} that, solely by virtue of the absolute dialectic which is its nature, equally embraces and holds {\i
everything within itself}, for it makes itself into the supremely free \u8211? the simplicity which is the first immediacy and universality. It is in this manner that each step of the {\i
advance} in the process of further determination, while getting away from the indeterminate beginning, is also a {\i
getting back closer} to it; consequently, that what may at first appear to be different, the {\i
retrogressive grounding} of the beginning and the {\i
progressive further determination} of it, run into one another and are the same. The method, which thus coils in a circle, cannot however anticipate in a temporal development that the beginning is as such already something derived; sufficient for an immediate beginning is that it be simple universality. In As Much as this is what it is, it has its complete condition; and {\ul
}there is no need to deprecate the fact that it may be accepted only {\i
provisionally} and {\i
hypothetically}. Whatever might be adduced against it \u8211? about the limitations of human cognition; about the need to reflect critically on the instrument of cognition before getting to the fact itself \u8211? all these are themselves {\i
presuppositions}, {\i
concrete determinations} that as such carry with them the demand for mediation and grounding. Therefore, since they formally have no advantage over {\i
beginning} with the fact itself as they protest against, and, because of their more concrete content, are on the contrary all the more in need of derivation, singling them out for special attention is to be considered as empty presumption. They have an untrue content, for they make into something incontestable and absolute what is known to be finite and untrue, namely a {\i
restricted} cognition determined as {\i
form} and {\i
instrument in opposition to} its {\i
content}; this untrue cognition is itself also the form, the retroactive search for grounds. \u8211? The method of truth also knows that the beginning is incomplete, because it is a beginning; but at the same time it knows that this incompleteness is necessary, because truth is but the coming-to-oneself through the negativity of immediacy. The impatience 12.252that would {\i
merely} transcend the {\i
determinate} \u8211? be it called beginning, object, the finite, or in whatever other form it is otherwise taken \u8211? in order that one would find oneself immediately in the absolute, has nothing before it as cognition but the empty negative, the abstract infinite. Or what it has before it is a {\i
presumed} absolute, presumed because not {\i
posited}, not {\i
comprehended}; comprehended it will be only through the {\i
mediation} of cognition, of which the universal and immediate are a moment, and as for the truth itself, it resides only in the extended course of mediation and at the end. \u8211? To meet the subjective need and the impatience that come with not knowing, one may well provide an overview of the {\i
whole in advance} \u8211? by means of a division for reflection that, in the manner of finite cognition, gives the particular of the universal as {\i
already there}, to be waited for as the science progresses. Yet this affords nothing more than a picture for {\i
representation}; for the true transition from the universal to the particular and to the whole which is determined in and for itself and in which that first universal is in truth itself again a moment \u8211? this transition is alien to the division of reflection and is the exclusive mediation of science itself. By virtue of the nature of the method just indicated, the science presents itself as a {\i
circle} that winds around itself, where the mediation winds the end back to the beginning which is the simple ground; the circle is thus a {\i
circle of circles}, for each single member ensouled by the method is reflected into itself so that, in returning to the beginning it is at the same time the beginning of a new member. Fragments of this chain are the single sciences, {\ul
}each of which has a {\i
before} and an {\i
after} \u8211? or, more accurately said, {\i
has in possession} only the {\i
before} and in its conclusion {\i
points to its after}. So the logic also has returned in the absolute idea to this simple unity which is its beginning; the pure immediacy of being, in which all determination appears at first as extinguished or removed by abstraction, is the idea that through mediation, that is, the sublation of mediation, has come to the likeness corresponding to it. The method is the pure concept that only relates to itself; it is, therefore, the {\i
simple self-reference} which is being. But it now is also the {\i
fulfilled concept}, the {\i
concept that comprehends} itself {\i
conceptually}, being as the {\i
concrete} and just as absolutely {\i
intensive} totality. \u8211? In conclusion, there remains only this to be said of this idea, that in it, {\i
in the first place}, the {\i
science of logic} has apprehended its own concept. In the sphere of being, at the beginning of its {\i
content}, its concept appears as a knowledge external to that content in subjective reflection. But in the idea of absolute cognition, the concept has become the idea's own content. The idea is itself the pure concept that has itself as its subject matter and which, as it runs itself as subject matter through the totality of its determinations, builds itself up to the entirety of its 12.253reality, to the system of science, and concludes by apprehending this conceptual comprehension of itself, hence by sublating its position as content and subject matter and cognizing the concept of science. \u8211? {\i
In second place}, this idea is still logical; it is shut up in pure thought, the science only of the divine {\i
concept}. Its systematic exposition is of course itself a realization, but one confined within the same sphere. Because the pure idea of cognition is to this extent shut up within subjectivity, it is the {\i
impulse} to sublate it, and pure truth becomes as final result also the {\i
beginning of another sphere and science}. It only remains here to indicate this transition. The idea, namely, in positing itself as the absolute {\i
unity} of the pure concept and its reality and thus collecting itself in the immediacy of {\i
being}, is in this form as {\i
totality} \u8211? {\i
nature}. \u8211? This determination, however, is nothing that {\i
has become}, is not a {\i
transition}, as was the case above when the subjective concept in its totality {\i
becomes objectivity}, or the {\i
subjective purpose becomes life}.{\ul
}{\super
86} The pure idea into which the determinateness or reality of the concept is itself raised into concept is rather an absolute {\i
liberation} for which there is no longer an immediate determination which is not equally {\i
posited} and is not concept; in this freedom, therefore, there is no transition that takes place; the simple being to which the idea determines itself remains perfectly transparent to it: it is the idea that in its determination remains {\ul
}with itself. The transition is to be grasped, therefore, in the sense that the idea {\i
freely discharges}{\ul
}{\super
87} itself, absolutely certain of itself and internally at rest. On account of this freedom, the {\i
form of its determinateness} is just as absolutely free: the {\i
externality of space and time} absolutely existing for itself without subjectivity. \u8211? In As Much as this externality is only in the abstract determinateness of being and is apprehended by consciousness, it is as mere objectivity and external life; within the idea, however, it remains in and for itself the totality of the concept, and science in the relation of divine cognition to nature. But what is posited by this first resolve of the pure idea to determine itself as external idea is only the mediation out of which the concept, as free concrete existence that from externality has come to itself, raises itself up, completes this self-liberation {\i
in the science of spirit}, and in the science of logic finds the highest concept of itself, the pure concept conceptually comprehending itself. \~ {\ql
